/* The heavy half of the astro cards — GENERATED, never hand-edited.
 *
 * abstract / sources / why / quote / wiki / image / places, for the cards whose ids begin `astro-`. None of it is
 * read until a reader REVEALS a card in this collection, so it is fetched then (bundle
 * `cardExtra:astro`) rather than downloaded by every visitor before they can flip one.
 *
 * IT STAGES ONTO A QUEUE rather than assigning, for the reason glossary-extra.js does: app.js
 * snapshots PRISTINE_CARDS at boot, which is BEFORE this file lands, so a plain assignment would
 * leave the editor's revert baseline empty and Revert would DELETE a shipped abstract rather than
 * restore it. cardExtraIngest drains the queue and re-seeds that baseline.
 *
 * An id data.js does not carry is not resurrected — the light half decides what a card is. */
(function () {
  var CARD_EXTRA = {
    "astro-001": {"questions":["Egyptians timing the dawn rising of Sirius and Chinese observers recording comets and sunspots were practising an early form of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> thousands of years before anyone owned a telescope.","Unable to touch a star or rerun the history of a galaxy, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> learns almost everything it knows from the light and other signals that happen to reach the Earth."],"abstract":"<b>Astronomy</b> is the study of the objects that lie beyond the Earth and of the processes by which those objects interact with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also an attempt to organise what is learned into a single history of the universe, from its beginning in the Big Bang to the present day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its subjects run from the Sun and planets through the stars and the gas between them to galaxies and the universe as a whole, the range of the International Astronomical Union's divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost none of these objects can be handled, so astronomy learns about them from the light and other signals they send, interacting with them directly only to a limited degree inside the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is also among the oldest sciences, since Babylonian, Assyrian and Egyptian sky-watchers already knew the approximate length of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Egyptians of 3,000 years ago adopted a calendar of 365 days and kept careful track of the dawn rising of Sirius, while Chinese observers recorded comets, bright meteors and dark spots on the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Babylonian tablets written between about 350 and 50 BCE predict the position of Jupiter by arithmetic, even working out the area under a graph, a method once thought to have been invented in 14th-century Oxford.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Many cultures also read their own figures into the stars, so that the pattern the Greeks called Orion the hunter was seen in ancient China as a supreme warrior named Shen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Modern astronomy asks larger questions, grouped by the survey that set American priorities for the 2020s under worlds and suns in context, new messengers and new physics, and cosmic ecosystems, while NASA asks how the universe works and whether we are alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> New instruments keep changing the account, and the James Webb Space Telescope can peer back over 13.5 billion years to the first galaxies, which is why astronomy is best read as a progress report rather than a finished list of facts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why is astronomy described as a progress report rather than a finished body of facts?","a":"Because its account of the universe keeps changing as new techniques and instruments let astronomers probe the universe more deeply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same textbook stresses that the universe itself evolves over long periods, so the story being told is a history that is still being filled in, not a fixed catalogue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can astronomers so rarely test an idea by handling the object itself?","a":"Because the objects are out of reach. Direct interaction with cosmic phenomena is possible only to a limited degree inside the Solar System, so nearly all evidence has to come from light and other signals, interpreted through models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did ancient peoples watch the sky so carefully?","a":"Because the sky kept time. The Egyptians tracked the dawn rising of Sirius, whose yearly cycle matched the flooding of the Nile, and used a calendar of 365 days, while the Chinese kept their own calendar and long records of comets, meteors and sunspots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Nature of Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 1.1 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-1-the-nature-of-astronomy. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Ancient Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 2.2 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-2-ancient-astronomy. [Open access]","International Astronomical Union, “Divisions,” https://www.iau.org/IAU/Iau/Science/Divisions.aspx. [Open access]","Sibylle Anderl, “Astronomy and Astrophysics,” in <i>The Oxford Handbook of Philosophy of Science</i>, ed. Paul Humphreys (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2016), https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780199368815.013.45. [Paywalled]","National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine, <i>Pathways to Discovery in Astronomy and Astrophysics for the 2020s</i> (Washington, DC: National Academies Press, 2023), https://doi.org/10.17226/26141. [Open access]","NASA Science, “NASA Astrophysics,” https://science.nasa.gov/astrophysics/. [Open access]","Mathieu Ossendrijver, “Ancient Babylonian Astronomers Calculated Jupiter’s Position from the Area under a Time-Velocity Graph,” <i>Science</i> 351, no. 6272 (2016): 482–84, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aad8085. [Paywalled]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “What Are Constellations?,” SpaceBook, https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/what-are-constellations/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “James Webb Space Telescope,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/. [Open access]"],"wiki":"Astronomy"},
    "astro-002": {"questions":["Whether a body circling the Sun is ruled a planet, a dwarf planet or a small Solar System body, it remains an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> studied by astronomy under written definitions.","From the Moon at about 384,000 kilometres (239,000 miles) to quasars billions of light-years off, every natural <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beyond the atmosphere falls within what astronomy studies."],"abstract":"An <b>astronomical object</b> is any naturally occurring body or structure in space beyond the Earth, from a planet or star to a dust cloud or a cluster of galaxies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The nearest is the Moon, about 384,000 kilometres (239,000 miles) away, and beyond it lies the Sun, about 150 million kilometres (93 million miles) away and about 1.5 million kilometres (930,000 miles) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the simplest sense a planet is a body of significant size that orbits a star and makes no light of its own, while a star is an enormous ball of glowing gas powered by nuclear reactions deep within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stars gather into clusters and into galaxies of billions, and the space between them holds thin gas and dust, the raw material of future stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Galaxies form groups, clusters and superclusters, and farther still lie quasars, brilliant galactic centres powered by gas falling towards massive black holes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Sorting so mixed a family is part of the work, and the third Gaia data release alone gave class probabilities, such as star, galaxy or quasar, for about 1.5 billion sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Within the Solar System the classes have strict written definitions, and a planet is a body that orbits the Sun, has enough mass for its own gravity to pull it into a nearly round shape, and has cleared the neighbourhood around its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A dwarf planet passes the first two tests but not the third, and a small Solar System body is one that is too small to be a dwarf planet and is not a comet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beyond the Solar System the class of planets keeps growing, with more than 6,000 planets around other stars confirmed by 2026, while the International Astronomical Union organises its members by divisions for the Sun, planetary systems, stars, interstellar matter, and galaxies and cosmology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yet no object is studied alone, and the American survey of priorities for the 2020s gave a whole theme, cosmic ecosystems, to linking stars, galaxies and the gas between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why do astronomers write formal definitions for kinds of object?","a":"Because the objects in space are so varied that a loose name hides real differences. The definitions of planet, dwarf planet and small Solar System body each set out tests a body must pass, such as being nearly round or having cleared its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are quasars visible at such great distances?","a":"Because they are extraordinarily bright: each is the centre of a galaxy where gas falling towards a massive black hole is heated to millions of degrees. That brilliance makes quasars the most distant beacons astronomers can see.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does interstellar gas count as an object worth studying?","a":"Because it is the raw material of future stars. The thin gas and dust between the stars gathers into enormous clouds, and new generations of stars form from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A whole theme of the 2020s survey links stars, galaxies and this gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “A Tour of the Universe,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 1.6 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-6-a-tour-of-the-universe. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Universe on the Large Scale,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 1.7 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-7-the-universe-on-the-large-scale. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Glossary,” definitions consistent with the 2023 edition of <i>The Astronomical Almanac</i>, https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","International Astronomical Union, “Divisions,” https://www.iau.org/IAU/Iau/Science/Divisions.aspx. [Open access]","National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine, <i>Pathways to Discovery in Astronomy and Astrophysics for the 2020s</i> (Washington, DC: National Academies Press, 2023), https://doi.org/10.17226/26141. [Open access]","A. Vallenari, A. G. A. Brown, T. Prusti, et al., for the Gaia Collaboration, “<i>Gaia</i> Data Release 3: Summary of the Content and Survey Properties,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 674 (2023): A1, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202243940. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Chapter 1: The Solar System,” in <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter1-1/. [Open access]"],"wiki":"Astronomical object"},
    "astro-003": {"questions":["Once spectroscopy let a physicist find sodium in the Sun in 1860, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could explain what the stars were made of instead of only charting where they stood.","Today the words astronomy and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are used almost interchangeably, although the second once meant explaining the sky with physics rather than simply describing it."],"abstract":"<b>Astrophysics</b> is the application of physics to cosmic phenomena, the attempt to explain what stars, galaxies and the matter between them are made of and how they work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The objects it studies exist under conditions far more extreme than any laboratory can produce, in temperature, pressure, size and time, and gravity often dominates them in a way it never does on a laboratory bench.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its methods are those of physics carried out at a distance, applying existing physical theories to the many different environments the universe provides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sun and the other stars, for instance, are understood as enormous balls of glowing gas whose energy comes from nuclear reactions deep within them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It also reaches what cannot be seen: gravity acting on visible stars and gas reveals dark matter of unknown make-up, and in the standard model of the universe dark energy makes up roughly 70 per cent of the total energy density.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> An older distinction made astronomy the description of the sky and astrophysics its explanation, but the two words are now often used interchangeably.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The change came largely through spectroscopy, the spreading of light into its colours, which in 1860 let a physicist identify the signature of sodium in the Sun for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Helium was found in the spectrum of the Sun before it was found on Earth, and its name comes from 'helios', the Greek word for ‘Sun’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the largest scale, maps of the microwave background from the Planck satellite are fitted by a physical model of the whole universe with just six parameters, five of them measured to better than one per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The pairing survives in the title of the American survey of priorities for the 2020s, which takes in dark matter, dark energy and cosmic inflation, in the International Astronomical Union's division for high-energy phenomena and fundamental physics, and in NASA's question of how black holes and gravity shape the universe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why was spectroscopy such a turning point for the study of the stars?","a":"Because the lines in a spectrum reveal what a glowing gas is made of. Once sodium was identified in the Sun in 1860, astronomers found many other elements in the Sun and stars, and only by knowing what stars were made of could they build theories of why they shine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can astrophysics not simply copy the methods of laboratory physics?","a":"Because its objects lie beyond reach and under conditions no laboratory can reproduce, with extreme temperatures, pressures and scales and gravity dominant. Without the chance to interact with them, it relies on observing many cases and on models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is dark matter treated as an astrophysical question?","a":"Because it is detected through physics: its gravity pulls on the stars and gas that can be seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What it is made of remains unknown, and the 2020s survey placed its nature among the central questions for the coming decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"sources":["Sibylle Anderl, “Astronomy and Astrophysics,” in <i>The Oxford Handbook of Philosophy of Science</i>, ed. Paul Humphreys (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2016), https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780199368815.013.45. [Paywalled]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Spectroscopy in Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 5.3 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/5-3-spectroscopy-in-astronomy. [Open access]","National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine, <i>Pathways to Discovery in Astronomy and Astrophysics for the 2020s</i> (Washington, DC: National Academies Press, 2023), https://doi.org/10.17226/26141. [Open access]","International Astronomical Union, “Divisions,” https://www.iau.org/IAU/Iau/Science/Divisions.aspx. [Open access]","NASA Science, “NASA Astrophysics,” https://science.nasa.gov/astrophysics/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “A Tour of the Universe,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 1.6 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-6-a-tour-of-the-universe. [Open access]","Christopher Smeenk and George Ellis, “Philosophy of Cosmology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, first published 2017, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/cosmology/. [Open access]","N. Aghanim, Y. Akrami, F. Arroja, et al., for the Planck Collaboration, “<i>Planck</i> 2018 Results. I. Overview and the Cosmological Legacy of <i>Planck</i>,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 641 (2020): A1, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201833880. [Open access]"],"wiki":"Astrophysics"},
    "astro-004": {"questions":["Treating the universe as a cosmic laboratory, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lets nature supply the varied conditions and stages of development that a scientist at a bench would otherwise have to arrange.","Beyond the Solar System, unlike the asteroid Bennu from which a spacecraft brought back grains in 2023, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must work without ever touching or changing the objects it studies."],"abstract":"<b>Observational astronomy</b> is the side of astronomy that gathers its evidence by recording the light and other signals that arrive from objects in space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the only way to work, because the objects cannot be brought into a laboratory: no one can put a group of stars into a test tube or order another comet for study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instead of experimenting, astronomers test an idea by observing many examples of the kind of object they want to understand and noting carefully how the examples vary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New instruments let those examples be seen in finer detail, as when the James Webb Space Telescope peers back over 13.5 billion years to the first galaxies, and an idea then passes or fails much as it would against a laboratory result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The International Astronomical Union keeps a whole division for the facilities, technologies and data science on which this work depends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Because astronomers cannot interact with what they study, causes have to be established without staging an experiment, and some philosophers have doubted whether such a science can claim to describe reality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reply from practice is that the universe is a cosmic laboratory, full of objects at every stage of development and in every kind of setting, so nature supplies the variety an experimenter would otherwise arrange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Large surveys make that comparison possible on a vast scale, and the third data release of the Gaia mission gave parallaxes and proper motions for about 1.5 billion sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of astronomy is also a historical science, since what is observed has already happened and cannot be changed, and cosmology is the extreme case, with only one universe and nothing similar to compare it with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The main exception is the Solar System, where direct contact is possible to a limited degree, and the OSIRIS-REx spacecraft delivered a sample of the asteroid Bennu to Earth on 24 September 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why does comparing many objects stand in for an experiment?","a":"Because the universe already contains objects at every stage and in every setting. By sampling and modelling that variety, astronomers can draw general conclusions without controlling anything themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each new set of observations then tests an idea much as a laboratory result would.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is astronomy also called a historical science?","a":"Because what astronomers observe has already happened and cannot be changed, in the same way that a geologist cannot alter what happened to the Earth. The astronomer works like a detective arriving after the event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a sample from an asteroid matter so much to astronomers?","a":"Because it is one of the rare cases where an object from space can be handled directly. Contact is possible only to a limited degree inside the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Bennu sample delivered in 2023 may show whether asteroids brought water and other ingredients for life to the early Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Nature of Science,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 1.2 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-2-the-nature-of-science. [Open access]","Sibylle Anderl, “Astronomy and Astrophysics,” in <i>The Oxford Handbook of Philosophy of Science</i>, ed. Paul Humphreys (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2016), https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780199368815.013.45. [Paywalled]","A. Vallenari, A. G. A. Brown, T. Prusti, et al., for the Gaia Collaboration, “<i>Gaia</i> Data Release 3: Summary of the Content and Survey Properties,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 674 (2023): A1, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202243940. [Open access]","NASA Science, “OSIRIS-REx,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/osiris-rex/. [Open access]","International Astronomical Union, “Divisions,” https://www.iau.org/IAU/Iau/Science/Divisions.aspx. [Open access]","NASA Science, “James Webb Space Telescope,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/. [Open access]","Christopher Smeenk and George Ellis, “Philosophy of Cosmology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, first published 2017, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/cosmology/. [Open access]"],"wiki":"Observational astronomy"},
    "astro-005": {"questions":["Astronomers write 500,000,000 as 5 × 10⁸ in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which the small raised exponent counts how many places the decimal point has been moved.","With roughly 10⁴ stars within 100 light-years of the Sun and some 10⁷ within 1,000, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lets astronomers write such counts as powers of ten instead of long strings of zeros."],"abstract":"<b>Scientific notation</b> is a way of writing very large or very small numbers as a figure multiplied by a power of ten, so that 500,000,000 is written as 5 × 10⁸.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The small raised number, called the exponent, records how many places the decimal point has been moved, which removes the long strings of zeros that make such figures hard to read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also called powers-of-ten notation, and astronomers rely on it to cover a range of sizes running from atomic nuclei to clusters of galaxies without resorting to phrases such as 'a billion trillion'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The metric International System of Units has its own named prefixes for powers of ten, which since 2022 have run from quecto, 10⁻³⁰, to quetta, 10³⁰.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A NASA tour of cosmic distances deliberately leaves the notation out, writing each distance in kilometres in full so that the reader watches the zeros pile up at every step outward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The scale of the universe is what makes the notation necessary: the Moon is about 384,000 kilometres (239,000 miles) away, and the Sun is about 400 times farther.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Light crosses the distance from the Sun to the Earth in about 8.3 minutes, yet Proxima Centauri, the nearest star beyond the Sun at about 4.2 light-years, is some 265,000 times as far away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Within 100 light-years of the Sun there are roughly 10⁴ stars, within 1,000 light-years some 10⁷, and a sphere 100,000 light-years in radius takes in the whole Milky Way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The nearest large galaxy, Andromeda, lies about 2.5 million light-years away and is itself some 220,000 light-years wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Even the Orion Nebula, at about 1,400 light-years, lies roughly 1.34 × 10¹⁶ kilometres (8.3 × 10¹⁵ miles) away, a figure far easier to grasp in scientific notation than in full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why do astronomers need a special way of writing numbers?","a":"Because their distances are on a scale few people meet elsewhere. Even the nearest star beyond the Sun is more than 40 trillion kilometres away, and writing the zeros out makes such figures hard to read or compare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Powers of ten remove the zeros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a NASA tour of distances avoid scientific notation on purpose?","a":"Because its aim is to make the size of the universe felt. By writing every distance in kilometres in full, it lets the reader watch the number of zeros grow with each step away from the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were new metric prefixes added in 2022?","a":"Because some scientific communities needed to express quantities beyond the old range. The 2022 decision added ronna and quetta for 10²⁷ and 10³⁰, and ronto and quecto for their reciprocals, partly to head off unofficial names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Numbers in Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 1.4 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-4-numbers-in-astronomy. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “A Tour of the Universe,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 1.6 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-6-a-tour-of-the-universe. [Open access]","Bureau International des Poids et Mesures, <i>The International System of Units (SI)</i>, 9th ed. (Sèvres: BIPM, 2019), https://doi.org/10.59161/AUEZ1291. [Open access]","Maggie Masetti, “The Cosmic Distance Scale,” Imagine the Universe!, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/features/cosmic/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Chapter 1: The Solar System,” in <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter1-1/. [Open access]","NASA Space Place, “What Is a Light-Year?,” https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/light-year/en/. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Units for Distance and Size in the Universe,” SpaceBook, https://lco.global/spacebook/distance/units-distance-and-size-universe/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “What Is a Light-Year?,” Exoplanets, https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/what-is-a-light-year/. [Open access]"],"wiki":"Scientific notation"},
    "astro-006": {"questions":["Astronomers who timed the transits of Venus across the Sun in 1761 and 1769 hoped to measure the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in kilometres and so fix the true scale of the Solar System.","Sedna comes no closer to the Sun than 76.4 times the length of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the yardstick that astronomers use for distances among the planets and their neighbours."],"abstract":"The <b>astronomical unit</b> is a unit of length used mainly within the Solar System, equal by definition to exactly 149,597,870,700 metres, or about 150 million kilometres (93 million miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began as the average distance between the Earth and the Sun, which ranges from about 147.1 million kilometres (91.4 million miles) at perihelion, the closest point, to about 152.1 million kilometres (94.5 million miles) at aphelion, the farthest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its symbol is au, which the International System of Units lists among non-SI units in wide use, and the mean semi-major axis of the Earth's orbit is given as 1.00000011 au, a shade more than the unit itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Light takes about 8.3 minutes to cross one astronomical unit, so the Sun is always seen as it was a little over eight minutes earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The unit keeps Solar System figures manageable: Jupiter lies about 5.2 au from the Sun and Pluto about 39.5 au, while the distant body Sedna comes no closer than 76.4 au and swings out to about 961 au.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Finding its length in kilometres was long a central problem, and measuring it was one of the main aims of observing the rare transits of Venus across the face of the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only seven transits of Venus had been observed with telescopes by 2012, falling in 1639, in 1761 and 1769, in 1874 and 1882, and in 2004 and 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Radar echoes bounced off Venus in the 1960s refined the value, which mattered because steering a spacecraft depends on knowing it accurately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Years of careful analysis of radar measurements eventually fixed its length to a precision of about one part in a billion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was formerly defined through the Gaussian gravitational constant, and after astronomers had proposed tying it to the metre instead, the International Astronomical Union fixed it in 2012 as an exact number of metres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why was the astronomical unit redefined as a fixed number of metres?","a":"Because the older definition rested on the Gaussian gravitational constant, a quantity tied to the Sun's gravity, rather than on the metre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Astronomers had proposed a fixed relation to the metre instead,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and since 2012 the unit has been exactly 149,597,870,700 metres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the transits of Venus once so valuable?","a":"Because timing Venus as it crossed the face of the Sun offered a way to measure the astronomical unit in kilometres, which had been one of the main goals of studying these rare events since the first telescopic observation in 1639.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does spacecraft navigation need the astronomical unit to be known precisely?","a":"Because a spacecraft is steered across distances measured in it. That is why radar echoes from Venus in the 1960s, which refined its value, were so important for missions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"sources":["Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Glossary,” definitions consistent with the 2023 edition of <i>The Astronomical Almanac</i>, https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Bureau International des Poids et Mesures, <i>The International System of Units (SI)</i>, 9th ed. (Sèvres: BIPM, 2019), https://doi.org/10.59161/AUEZ1291. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Fundamental Units of Distance,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 19.1 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-1-fundamental-units-of-distance. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Chapter 1: The Solar System,” in <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter1-1/. [Open access]","Costantino Sigismondi, “Transits of Venus and the Astronomical Unit: Four Centuries of Increasing Precision,” arXiv:1301.0296 (2013), https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.0296. [Open access]","N. Capitaine and B. Guinot, “The Astronomical Units,” arXiv:0812.2970 (2008), https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.2970. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Units for Distance and Size in the Universe,” SpaceBook, https://lco.global/spacebook/distance/units-distance-and-size-universe/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]"],"wiki":"Astronomical unit"},
    "astro-007": {"questions":["With the nearest star beyond the Sun about 4.25 of them away, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns more than 40 trillion kilometres (25 trillion miles) into a number small enough to remember.","Because the International Astronomical Union recommends basing it on the Julian year, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a set length of about 63,200 astronomical units, or 0.307 parsecs."],"abstract":"A <b>light-year</b> is the distance that light travels through a vacuum in one year, about 9.46 trillion kilometres (5.88 trillion miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Despite the word 'year' in its name it measures distance, not time, much as a cinema might be described as twenty minutes from the town centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because a year can be defined in several ways, the International Astronomical Union recommends the Julian year as its basis, and the speed of light itself is an exact value of 299,792,458 metres per second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One light-year is about 63,200 astronomical units or 0.307 parsecs, and astronomers often use it for the distances between stars, although distances beyond the Solar System are just as often given in parsecs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Light moves at about 300,000 kilometres (186,000 miles) per second, yet even at that speed it needs about 8.3 minutes to arrive from the Sun, and in a full year it covers about 63,000 au.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> The unit earns its place beyond the Solar System, where kilometres become unwieldy: the nearest star beyond the Sun is about 4.25 light-years away, more than 40 trillion kilometres (25 trillion miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other stars visible to the unaided eye lie hundreds to thousands of light-years away, the TRAPPIST-1 system of seven planets orbits a red dwarf about 40 light-years off, and the Andromeda galaxy is some 220,000 light-years wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A distance in light-years also gives the age of the light, so Andromeda, about 2.5 million light-years away, is seen as it was 2.5 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The James Webb Space Telescope can peer back over 13.5 billion years, but at such depths the expansion of the universe means the light's travel time no longer equals the object's present distance in light-years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup> Textbooks for general readers usually keep to light-years, while many professional astronomers still use parsecs in technical papers and at meetings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why does a unit with 'year' in its name measure distance?","a":"Because it is the distance light covers in a year. People do the same in everyday speech when they give a place as so many minutes away; the time stands for the distance travelled in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the exact length of a light-year depend on which year is meant?","a":"Because there are several ways to define a year, which leaves the distance slightly ambiguous. The International Astronomical Union recommends the Julian year as the basis to settle it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is a distance in light-years also a statement about the past?","a":"Because the light has been travelling for that many years. Andromeda, about 2.5 million light-years away, is seen as it was 2.5 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many professionals nonetheless prefer parsecs in their technical work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"sources":["Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Glossary,” definitions consistent with the 2023 edition of <i>The Astronomical Almanac</i>, https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Numbers in Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 1.4 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-4-numbers-in-astronomy. [Open access]","NASA Science, “What Is a Light-Year?,” Exoplanets, https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/what-is-a-light-year/. [Open access]","NASA Space Place, “What Is a Light-Year?,” https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/light-year/en/. [Open access]","National Institute of Standards and Technology, “CODATA Value: Speed of Light in Vacuum,” 2022 CODATA recommended values, https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?c. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Fundamental Units of Distance,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 19.1 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-1-fundamental-units-of-distance. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Units for Distance and Size in the Universe,” SpaceBook, https://lco.global/spacebook/distance/units-distance-and-size-universe/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Chapter 1: The Solar System,” in <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter1-1/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “James Webb Space Telescope,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Distance and Lookback Time,” SpaceBook, https://lco.global/spacebook/cosmology/distance-and-lookback-time/. [Open access]"],"wiki":"Light-year"},
    "astro-008": {"questions":["Named as a blend of 'parallax' and 'arcsecond', the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> makes a star's distance simply one divided by its parallax, so a parallax of 0.1 arcsecond means ten units away.","Stated in a 2015 International Astronomical Union resolution as exactly 648,000 divided by pi astronomical units, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes to about 3.0857 × 10¹⁶ metres, a little over three light-years."],"abstract":"A <b>parsec</b> is the distance at which one astronomical unit spans an angle of one arcsecond, which is the same as the distance of a star whose annual parallax is one arcsecond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One parsec is about 206,265 astronomical units, about 3.26 light-years, or roughly 31 trillion kilometres (19 trillion miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A resolution of the International Astronomical Union in 2015 states it as exactly 648,000 divided by pi astronomical units, which makes it about 3.0857 × 10¹⁶ metres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name is a blend of 'parallax' and 'arcsecond', so it describes exactly how the unit is defined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Larger distances use the kiloparsec, a thousand parsecs, and the megaparsec, a million, and one recent catalogue of binary stars built from Gaia data reaches out to about one kiloparsec from the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The unit is convenient because a star's distance in parsecs is simply one divided by its parallax in arcseconds, so a parallax of 0.1 arcsecond means 10 parsecs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Parallax is the small yearly shift in a nearby star's apparent position as the Earth moves around the Sun, and the farther away the star, the longer the baseline needed to detect it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the ground, parallaxes smaller than about 0.01 arcsecond are very hard to measure through the atmosphere, which limits such telescopes to stars within about 100 parsecs, while the Milky Way is about 30,000 parsecs across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The European Space Agency's Gaia spacecraft, launched on 19 December 2013 to the second Lagrange point of the Sun–Earth–Moon system, scans the whole sky again and again, and its third data release lists parallaxes for about 1.5 billion sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two stars bound together share near-identical parallaxes, a fact one study used to test how reliable the published uncertainties on Gaia's parallaxes are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why is the parsec defined through an angle?","a":"Because stellar distances are measured through parallax, the small yearly shift of a star as the Earth orbits the Sun. A unit built on one arcsecond of parallax turns that angle straight into a distance: one divided by the parallax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the parsec be given to many decimal places?","a":"Because it is stated as exactly 648,000 divided by pi astronomical units, and the astronomical unit has itself been an exact number of metres since 2012, which gives about 3.0857 × 10¹⁶ metres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do pairs of bound stars help check Gaia's parallaxes?","a":"Because the two stars of a bound pair lie at almost the same distance and so share near-identical parallaxes. Comparing them showed that published uncertainties are generally reliable for faint stars but too small for brighter ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"sources":["Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Glossary,” definitions consistent with the 2023 edition of <i>The Astronomical Almanac</i>, https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","E. E. Mamajek et al., “IAU 2015 Resolution B2 on Recommended Zero Points for the Absolute and Apparent Bolometric Magnitude Scales,” arXiv:1510.06262 (2015), https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.06262. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Surveying the Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 19.2 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-2-surveying-the-stars. [Open access]","T. Prusti, J. H. J. de Bruijne, A. G. A. Brown, A. Vallenari, et al., for the Gaia Collaboration, “The <i>Gaia</i> Mission,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 595 (2016): A1, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201629272. [Open access]","A. Vallenari, A. G. A. Brown, T. Prusti, et al., for the Gaia Collaboration, “<i>Gaia</i> Data Release 3: Summary of the Content and Survey Properties,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 674 (2023): A1, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202243940. [Open access]","Kareem El-Badry, Hans-Walter Rix, and Tyler M. Heintz, “A Million Binaries from <i>Gaia</i> eDR3: Sample Selection and Validation of <i>Gaia</i> Parallax Uncertainties,” <i>Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society</i> 506, no. 2 (2021): 2269–95, https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stab323. [Paywalled]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Units for Distance and Size in the Universe,” SpaceBook, https://lco.global/spacebook/distance/units-distance-and-size-universe/. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Stellar Parallax,” SpaceBook, https://lco.global/spacebook/distance/parallax-and-distance-measurement/. [Open access]"],"wiki":"Parsec"},
    "astro-009": {"questions":["Radio replies from Apollo astronauts reached Mission Control almost three seconds late, a delay set by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over the round trip of about 768,000 kilometres (477,000 miles).","Fixed at exactly 299,792,458 metres per second when the metre was redefined, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means that sunlight reaching the Earth left the Sun about 8.3 minutes earlier."],"abstract":"The <b>speed of light</b> in a vacuum is exactly 299,792,458 metres per second, about 300,000 kilometres (186,000 miles) every second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The figure is exact because since 1983 the metre itself has been defined by fixing this value, so the speed of light carries no uncertainty at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Light takes about 1.3 seconds to travel between the Earth and the Moon, which is why radio conversations with the Apollo astronauts had a delay of almost three seconds for the round trip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From the Sun, about 150 million kilometres (93 million miles) away, light takes about 8.3 minutes, so the Sun is always seen as it was 8.3 minutes ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Information about the universe arrives almost entirely as some form of light, so this one speed limits how soon a distant event can be known and gives every observer a visual horizon, the most distant matter from which light can have arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> That light takes time to travel at all was first shown in 1676 from eclipses of the moons of Jupiter, which came several minutes late when Jupiter and the Earth were far apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> The limit is also a gift, because the farther away an object is, the longer its light has been travelling and the earlier the moment it shows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A star 500 light-years away is seen by light that left it 500 years ago, and the Andromeda galaxy, about 2.5 million light-years away, appears as it was 2.5 million years in the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Looking billions of light-years out means seeing billions of years into the past, which is one reason telescopes keep growing, and the James Webb Space Telescope can peer back over 13.5 billion years to the first galaxies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The deepest view that light allows is the cosmic microwave background, which formed about 380,000 years after the Big Bang, when the young universe stopped being opaque like a fog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why is the speed of light an exact number with no uncertainty?","a":"Because since 1983 the metre has been defined by fixing the speed of light at 299,792,458 metres per second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The value is therefore exact by definition, as the 2022 recommended values of the physical constants record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do astronomers see distant galaxies as they were long ago?","a":"Because light takes time to cross space. The farther away an object is, the longer its light has travelled, so looking billions of light-years out means seeing billions of years into the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Andromeda appears as it was 2.5 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can light not show us anything earlier than the cosmic microwave background?","a":"Because for roughly the first 380,000 years the universe was filled with hot plasma in which light kept colliding with free electrons and could not travel far. The background radiation is the furthest back in time light lets us explore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"sources":["National Institute of Standards and Technology, “CODATA Value: Speed of Light in Vacuum,” 2022 CODATA recommended values, https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?c. [Open access]","Bureau International des Poids et Mesures, <i>The International System of Units (SI)</i>, 9th ed. (Sèvres: BIPM, 2019), https://doi.org/10.59161/AUEZ1291. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Consequences of Light Travel Time,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 1.5 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-5-consequences-of-light-travel-time. [Open access]","NASA Space Place, “What Is a Light-Year?,” https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/light-year/en/. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Planck and the Cosmic Microwave Background,” https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Planck/Planck_and_the_cosmic_microwave_background. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “A Tour of the Universe,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 1.6 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/1-6-a-tour-of-the-universe. [Open access]","Christopher Smeenk and George Ellis, “Philosophy of Cosmology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, first published 2017, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/cosmology/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “James Webb Space Telescope,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “The Speed of Light,” SpaceBook, https://lco.global/spacebook/light/speed-light/. [Open access]"],"wiki":"Speed of light"},
    "astro-010": {"questions":["Because the sky shows only directions, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may be either a true binary or a chance alignment, and only a measured distance such as a parallax can tell which it is.","When two stars line up by chance although they lie far apart along the line of sight, the resulting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is called optical, unlike a binary whose stars truly orbit each other."],"abstract":"A <b>double star</b> is any pair of stars that appear close together in the sky, whether or not they are physically connected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some are true binaries, two stars held together by gravity and orbiting each other, while others are chance alignments of stars that actually lie at different distances from us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A pair of the second kind, which look close together but do not orbit each other, is called an optical double.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Washington Double Star Catalog kept by the United States Naval Observatory, the world's principal database of such pairs, listed about 158,000 systems in October 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Telling the two kinds apart takes more than positions, and one recent catalogue of more than a million pairs estimated for each the probability that it was only a chance alignment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Optical doubles are a reminder that the night sky is a projection: looking up, the sky seems a great dome with every star at the same distance on its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In reality the stars lie at greatly varying distances, the dome surviving only as a convenient way to track where objects appear, and how big anything looks depends on its distance as much as on its true size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Astronomers formalise this as the celestial sphere, an imaginary sphere of arbitrary radius on which bodies are treated as lying, so a position on it gives a direction but no distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A constellation is likewise an area of that sphere identified by a grouping of stars, and the stars of one grouping need not be near one another in space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The missing third dimension has to be measured separately, and Gaia parallaxes for about 1.5 billion sources now let astronomers test pairs, since bound partners share near-identical parallaxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why can two stars look close together when they are not?","a":"Because the sky shows only directions. The stars seem to sit on a dome at one distance, but in reality they lie at greatly varying distances, so two stars far apart in space can line up along almost the same direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a parallax settle whether a pair is real?","a":"Because parallax gives each star's distance. Stars bound together lie at nearly the same distance and share near-identical parallaxes, so a large difference shows a chance alignment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gaia now provides parallaxes for about 1.5 billion sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do astronomers still use a celestial sphere if the stars are not on one?","a":"Because it is a convenient way of recording where objects appear. Its radius is arbitrary and it can be centred on the observer or the Earth, so it describes directions without claiming anything about distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 2.1 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Glossary,” definitions consistent with the 2023 edition of <i>The Astronomical Almanac</i>, https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Measuring Stellar Masses,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, section 18.2 (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/18-2-measuring-stellar-masses. [Open access]","United States Naval Observatory, “The Washington Double Star Catalog,” hosted by Georgia State University, accessed 2026, http://www.astro.gsu.edu/wds/. [Open access]","Kareem El-Badry, Hans-Walter Rix, and Tyler M. Heintz, “A Million Binaries from <i>Gaia</i> eDR3: Sample Selection and Validation of <i>Gaia</i> Parallax Uncertainties,” <i>Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society</i> 506, no. 2 (2021): 2269–95, https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stab323. [Paywalled]","A. Vallenari, A. G. A. Brown, T. Prusti, et al., for the Gaia Collaboration, “<i>Gaia</i> Data Release 3: Summary of the Content and Survey Properties,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 674 (2023): A1, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202243940. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Positions and Sizes of Cosmic Objects,” SpaceBook, https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/using-angles-describe-positions-and-apparent-sizes-objects/. [Open access]"],"wiki":"Double star"},
    "astro-011": {"questions":["Astronomical twilight ends when the Sun's centre sinks 18 degrees below the horizon, and after that the scattered sunlight in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is fainter than starlight and other natural sources.","That the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is dark in every direction, rather than as bright as the Sun's surface, is evidence that the stars are finite in number and each shines for only a limited time."],"abstract":"The <b>night sky</b> is the sky as seen once the Sun has sunk far enough below the horizon that the air overhead is no longer lit by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By day, sunlight is scattered in every direction by the gases and particles of the atmosphere, and blue light is scattered most, which is why a clear daytime sky is bright and blue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That scattered light hides the stars, which go on circling overhead through the day even though the glare makes them hard to see.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After sunset the sky darkens through civil, nautical and astronomical twilight, and once the Sun's centre is 18 degrees below the horizon the scattered sunlight left is fainter than starlight and the other natural sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even then a moonless sky far from towns is not perfectly black, because the stars and the Milky Way add their light, and so does airglow, light given off by atoms high in the atmosphere that sunlight has energised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Zodiacal light, sunlight scattered by dust spread through the plane of the Solar System, adds a fainter glow along the band of the zodiac.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> The darkness that is left raises a deeper question, because in a universe that went on for ever, filled with stars that had always shone, every line of sight should end on the surface of a star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The puzzle is known as Olbers' paradox, after the astronomer in Bremen who set it out in 1823, although he was not the first to notice it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The resolution now accepted, worked out in the 1960s and supported by observations in the 1980s, is that the stars are finite in number and each shines for a limited time, so their light, spread through a gigantic volume, cannot fill the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Seen with other instruments the sky is not quite dark after all, since the cosmic microwave background arrives from every direction and galaxies add a much fainter background light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Fabio Falchi, Pierantonio Cinzano, Dan Duriscoe, Christopher C. M. Kyba, Christopher D. Elvidge, Kimberly Baugh, Boris A. Portnov, Nataliya A. Rybnikova, and Riccardo Furgoni, “The New World Atlas of Artificial Night Sky Brightness,” <i>Science Advances</i> 2, no. 6 (2016): e1600377, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Rise, Set, and Twilight Definitions,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/RST_defs. [Open access]","NASA Space Place, “Why Is the Sky Blue?,” NASA Science, https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/blue-sky/en/. [Open access]","Simon Driver, Jonathan Biteau, Alberto Domínguez, David Valls-Gabaud, Hervé Dole, José Fonseca, and Juan Garcia-Bellido, “Why Is the Sky Dark at Night? The 200-Year History of a Question That Transformed Our Understanding of the Universe,” University of Western Australia, 19 June 2023, first published in <i>The Conversation</i>, https://www.uwa.edu.au/news/article/2023/june/why-is-the-sky-dark-at-night-the-200-year-history-of-a-question-that-transformed-our-understanding-of-the-universe. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Joy Ng, narr., “The Secrets behind Earth’s Multi-colored Glow,” transcript, NASA Scientific Visualization Studio, https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/vis/a010000/a012900/a012902/script_26187_01.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Cosmic Microwave Background,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 29.4, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/29-4-the-cosmic-microwave-background. [Open access]","Jeremie Lasue, Anny-Chantal Levasseur-Regourd, and Jean-Baptiste Renard, “Zodiacal Light Observations and Its Link with Cosmic Dust: A Review,” <i>Planetary and Space Science</i> 190 (2020): 104973, https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.07480. [Open access]","European Southern Observatory, “Zodiacal Glow Lightens Paranal Sky,” Picture of the Week, 2 December 2013, https://www.eso.org/public/images/potw1348a/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the daytime sky bright when space itself is dark?","a":"Because the brightness belongs to the air, not to space. Sunlight is scattered in every direction by the gases and particles of the atmosphere, with blue light scattered most, so the whole sky glows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That glow hides the stars, which are still overhead by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do astronomers wait for astronomical twilight to end?","a":"Because only then is the scattered sunlight in the sky fainter than starlight and the other natural sources. That happens when the Sun's centre is 18 degrees below the horizon; at the end of civil and nautical twilight, 6 and 12 degrees down, the sky is still noticeably lit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a dark night sky say something about the universe?","a":"Because an endless, unchanging universe full of stars would leave no gaps between them, and every direction would be as bright as the Sun's surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The darkness fits instead a finite number of stars, each shining for a limited time, whose light is spread through an enormous volume.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Night sky"},
    "astro-012": {"questions":["A world atlas published in 2016 found that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hides the Milky Way from more than one-third of humanity, including about 60 per cent of Europeans.","More than 51,000 naked-eye star counts made by volunteers between 2011 and 2022 showed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brightening the sky by an estimated 7 to 10 per cent a year."],"abstract":"<b>Light pollution</b> is the brightening of the night by artificial light, and its most visible form is artificial skyglow, the glow made when light from the ground is scattered in the atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Near cities the air scatters the glare of lamps into a general illumination that hides the faintest stars and limits how far telescopes can see.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is one reason the best observatory sites are high, dry and dark, ideally at least about 160 kilometres (100 miles) from the nearest large city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A world atlas published in 2016 estimated that more than 80 per cent of the world's population, and more than 99 per cent of people in the United States and Europe, live under light-polluted skies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same atlas found the Milky Way hidden from more than one-third of humanity, including about 60 per cent of Europeans and nearly 80 per cent of North Americans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first such atlas, published in 2001, had already put about two-thirds of the world's population under polluted skies by its own threshold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The problem is growing, and between 2011 and 2022 more than 51,000 naked-eye star counts sent in by volunteers showed fewer and fewer stars visible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That loss is best explained by the sky brightening by 7 to 10 per cent a year in the band the eye sees, faster than satellite measurements of emissions suggest, a gap put down to changes in the colour and angle of modern lighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Darkness matters beyond astronomy, because ecosystems rely on natural cycles of light and dark, artificial light can disrupt how living things use light as a resource and as information, and campaigners warn of effects on energy use and health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A newer concern is the growing number of large satellite constellations in low orbits, which the International Astronomical Union says harm both astronomy and humanity's enjoyment of the night sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Fabio Falchi, Pierantonio Cinzano, Dan Duriscoe, Christopher C. M. Kyba, Christopher D. Elvidge, Kimberly Baugh, Boris A. Portnov, Nataliya A. Rybnikova, and Riccardo Furgoni, “The New World Atlas of Artificial Night Sky Brightness,” <i>Science Advances</i> 2, no. 6 (2016): e1600377, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377. [Open access]","Christopher C. M. Kyba, Yiğit Öner Altıntaş, Constance E. Walker, and Mark Newhouse, “Citizen Scientists Report Global Rapid Reductions in the Visibility of Stars from 2011 to 2022,” <i>Science</i> 379, no. 6629 (2023): 265–68, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abq7781. [Paywalled]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Telescopes Today,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 6.2, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/6-2-telescopes-today. [Open access]","NSF NOIRLab, “About Globe at Night,” Globe at Night, https://www.globeatnight.org/about. [Open access]","IAU Centre for the Protection of the Dark and Quiet Sky from Satellite Constellation Interference, “Our Mission Is to Protect the Sky,” International Astronomical Union, https://cps.iau.org/. [Open access]","P. Cinzano, F. Falchi, and C. D. Elvidge, “The First World Atlas of the Artificial Night Sky Brightness,” <i>Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society</i> 328, no. 3 (2001): 689–707, https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0108052. [Open access]","US National Park Service, “Night Skies,” https://www.nps.gov/subjects/nightskies/index.htm. [Open access]","Kevin J. Gaston, Jonathan Bennie, Thomas W. Davies, and John Hopkins, “The Ecological Impacts of Nighttime Light Pollution: A Mechanistic Appraisal,” <i>Biological Reviews</i> 88, no. 4 (2013): 912–27, https://doi.org/10.1111/brv.12036. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does light from the ground brighten the sky overhead?","a":"Because the atmosphere scatters it. Light sent upwards or sideways from streets and buildings is scattered by the air, and part of it comes back down as artificial skyglow, the most visible effect of light pollution, which hides the faintest stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are big observatories built so far from cities?","a":"Because a city's lights make the sky above it glow, and that glow limits how faint a telescope can see. The best sites are high, dry and dark, ideally at least about 160 kilometres (100 miles) from the nearest large city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did star counts fall faster than satellite data predicted?","a":"Because the volunteers' counts implied the sky brightening by 7 to 10 per cent a year in the band the eye sees, faster than satellites showed. The study's authors put the gap down to changes in the colour of the light emitted and in the angle at which it leaves the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Light pollution"},
    "astro-013": {"questions":["Because a planet shows a small disc rather than a point, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of its many separate rays averages out, so it usually shines more steadily than the stars around it.","Measured with instruments, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of starlight is called scintillation, and it is caused mostly by turbulence high in the atmosphere rather than by the air near the telescope."],"abstract":"<b>Twinkling</b> is the rapid flickering in brightness of a star seen through the Earth's atmosphere, which astronomers measure as scintillation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It happens because the air is turbulent, and moving pockets of it bend starlight so that part of the time the light reaches the eye and part of the time some of it misses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above the atmosphere the light of the stars is steady, which is one reason for putting telescopes in space, where the detail they see is limited only by their size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The turbulence casts a pattern of 'flying shadows' across the ground, and a star's brightness at any one spot changes as winds carry the pattern along and as it reshapes itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A planet usually twinkles far less than a star, because it shows a small disc whose many points twinkle separately and average out, while a star is a single point even in a telescope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Twinkling is related to seeing, the blurring of a star's image by the same turbulent air, which astronomers measure by the tiny angle the blur covers on the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two are not the same thing, because scintillation is caused mainly by turbulence high in the atmosphere, so a night can be steady for brightness measurements while the seeing is poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scintillation grows stronger as a star is observed farther from the point overhead, where its light crosses more air, and it is weaker in a bigger telescope, which averages over more of the shadow pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sites differ, and measurements on the Antarctic plateau at Dome C found less high-altitude turbulence than at two Chilean observatories, promising about 3.6 times better precision in brightness measurements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The blurring can be corrected: adaptive optics uses computer-controlled deformable mirrors, guided by a bright natural star or an artificial one made with a laser, to undo the distortion in real time and approach a telescope's sharpest possible images in the near infrared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Telescopes Today,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 6.2, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/6-2-telescopes-today. [Open access]","J. Osborn, D. Föhring, V. S. Dhillon, and R. W. Wilson, “Atmospheric Scintillation in Astronomical Photometry,” <i>Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society</i> 452, no. 2 (2015): 1707–16, https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.06921. [Open access]","Dainis Dravins, Lennart Lindegren, Eva Mezey, and Andrew T. Young, “Atmospheric Intensity Scintillation of Stars, I. Statistical Distributions and Temporal Properties,” <i>Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific</i> 109 (1997): 173, https://doi.org/10.1086/133872. [Paywalled]","John Blanton and Scott Chase, “Why Do Stars Twinkle Whereas Planets Do Not?,” <i>The Physics FAQ</i>, 1992, https://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/General/twinkle.html. [Open access]","European Southern Observatory, “Adaptive Optics,” ESO, https://www.eso.org/public/teles-instr/technology/adaptive_optics/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Observations outside Earth’s Atmosphere,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 6.5, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/6-5-observations-outside-earths-atmosphere. [Open access]","Paul Hickson, “Atmospheric and Adaptive Optics,” <i>The Astronomy and Astrophysics Review</i> 22 (2014): 76, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00159-014-0076-9. [Paywalled]","S. L. Kenyon, J. S. Lawrence, M. C. B. Ashley, J. W. V. Storey, A. Tokovinin, and E. Fossat, “Atmospheric Scintillation at Dome C, Antarctica: Implications for Photometry and Astrometry,” <i>Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific</i> 118, no. 844 (2006): 924–32, https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0604538. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do stars twinkle but planets usually do not?","a":"Because a star is so distant that it is a single point even in a telescope, so when turbulence bends its light away the star seems to dim. A planet shows a small disc made of many points, and their separate flickers average out into a steadier light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do stars not twinkle when seen from space?","a":"Because the twinkling is made by the air, not by the star. Turbulent pockets of the atmosphere bend the light on its way down; above the atmosphere nothing bends it, and the starlight is steady.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a night of poor seeing still be good for measuring brightness?","a":"Because scintillation comes mostly from turbulence high in the atmosphere, while much of the blurring that makes seeing poor can come from lower down. A star's brightness can therefore stay steady enough for precise measurement while its image is blurred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Twinkling"},
    "astro-014": {"questions":["In 2009 a dark impact scar on Jupiter was first spotted in images from a home observatory in New South Wales, a famous success for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a NASA telescope later confirmed.","Contributions from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to planetary science have grown fast, with skilled observers monitoring planets and studying asteroids and comets using instruments, detectors and software of their own."],"abstract":"<b>Amateur astronomy</b> is the observation and study of the sky by people who are not professional astronomers, using instruments, detectors, software and methods of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Amateur contributions to professional publications in planetary astronomy have grown exponentially over recent decades, according to a review of collaborations between the two groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The review names the monitoring of planets and interplanetary matter, the study of asteroids and comets and measurements of Kuiper Belt objects and exoplanets among the fields where such work regularly leads to papers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of the best-known finds come from patient watching of one planet, as when a dark scar left by an impact was spotted on Jupiter in July 2009 in images taken at a home observatory in Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> NASA's Infrared Telescope Facility on Mauna Kea in Hawaii later confirmed the scar and a plume of particles in Jupiter's upper atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> An amateur need not own a telescope at all, since much modern work is done by volunteers examining data that professional instruments have already gathered, and more than 650 of NASA's citizen scientists have co-authored publications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In the Galaxy Zoo project, about 100,000 members of the public made more than 40 million classifications of the shapes of nearly a million galaxies, and their results agreed with professional classifications of the same galaxies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sungrazer Project began in 2000 because amateurs were already spotting comets in images from the SOHO solar spacecraft, and its volunteers, many with no formal scientific training, helped find most of SOHO's 5,000 comets by 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those finds have made SOHO the most prolific comet finder in history, with more than half of all the comets known today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Volunteers also measure the sky itself, and more than 51,000 naked-eye star counts sent in between 2011 and 2022 showed the night sky brightening year by year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["O. Mousis, R. Hueso, J.-P. Beaulieu, S. Bouley, B. Carry et al., “Instrumental Methods for Professional and Amateur Collaborations in Planetary Astronomy,” <i>Experimental Astronomy</i> 38, nos. 1–2 (2014): 91–191, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10686-014-9379-0. [Paywalled]","Chris J. Lintott, Kevin Schawinski, Anže Slosar, Kate Land, Steven Bamford et al., “Galaxy Zoo: Morphologies Derived from Visual Inspection of Galaxies from the Sloan Digital Sky Survey,” <i>Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society</i> 389, no. 3 (2008): 1179–89, https://arxiv.org/abs/0804.4483. [Open access]","Robert Nemiroff and Jerry Bonnell, “Jupiter’s Impact Scar,” Astronomy Picture of the Day, NASA, 23 July 2009, https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap090723.html. [Open access]","Vanessa Thomas, “ESA, NASA Solar Observatory Discovers Its 5,000th Comet,” NASA Science, 27 March 2024, https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/heliophysics/esa-nasa-solar-observatory-discovers-its-5000th-comet/. [Open access]","NSF NOIRLab, “About Globe at Night,” Globe at Night, https://www.globeatnight.org/about. [Open access]","Christopher C. M. Kyba, Yiğit Öner Altıntaş, Constance E. Walker, and Mark Newhouse, “Citizen Scientists Report Global Rapid Reductions in the Visibility of Stars from 2011 to 2022,” <i>Science</i> 379, no. 6629 (2023): 265–68, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abq7781. [Paywalled]","Sungrazer Project, “SOHO-5000!,” US Naval Research Laboratory, 27 March 2024, https://sungrazer.nrl.navy.mil/soho5000. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Citizen Science,” https://science.nasa.gov/citizen-science/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can amateurs still contribute to professional astronomy?","a":"Because much of the work needs many patient observers as well as big telescopes. Amateurs regularly contribute to monitoring planets, studying asteroids and comets and measuring Kuiper Belt objects and exoplanets, and their share of professional papers in planetary astronomy has grown exponentially.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Galaxy Zoo ask the public to sort galaxies?","a":"Because there were nearly a million galaxies to classify by eye, far too many for a handful of professionals. About 100,000 volunteers made more than 40 million classifications over the internet, and their results agreed with professional classifications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why have volunteers found so many comets in SOHO images?","a":"Because the NASA-funded Sungrazer Project lets anyone report comets they find in SOHO's images. Volunteer hunters, many with no formal scientific training, have found most of the 5,000 comets discovered in SOHO data, making it the most prolific comet finder in history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Amateur astronomy"},
    "astro-015": {"questions":["Babylonian scribes read signs in the sky as messages from the gods, and from that celestial divination grew <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Greek writers later applied to the fate of every individual at birth.","In 1666 the new Académie des Sciences in Paris refused to admit members sympathetic to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, confirming a separation from astronomy that had been growing since the late 16th century."],"abstract":"<b>Astrology</b> is the ancient system in which the positions of the Sun, Moon and planets among the stars of the zodiac are taken to hold the key to events on Earth and to people's lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of Mesopotamian celestial divination, in which scribes read signs in the sky as messages from the gods about what lay in store for humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That tradition ran for nearly 2,000 years, from omen texts of the late Old Babylonian period through the great omen series <i>Enūma Anu Enlil</i> to the horoscopes written under Persian, Seleucid and Parthian rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the 2nd century BCE Greek astrologers had widened the idea so that the sky at the moment of a person's birth was thought to shape that person's character and fortune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Precession has since carried the constellations about one sign away from the dates the signs were given, and astrologers split over whether to tie the zodiac to the seasons or to the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> For centuries the two subjects were closely tied, and the Latin word 'astrologia' regularly meant ‘astronomy’ until the end of the Middle Ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ptolemy drew the line himself nearly 2,000 years ago, calling the study of the motions of the Sun, Moon and stars, the subject of his <i>Almagest</i>, a science desirable in itself, and the study of their effects, set out in his <i>Tetrabiblos</i>, a less self-sufficient method.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cicero reports that Eudoxus, whom he calls easily the first in astronomy, held that no reliance should be placed on astrologers forecasting a life from the stars at birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From the end of the 16th century astronomers began firmly to reject astrology, and in 1666 the new Académie des Sciences in Paris refused to admit members who sympathised with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Astrology has no known force through which the planets could shape a life, statistical tests such as double-blind trials of natal charts have found no evidence that it predicts anything, and philosophers treat it as a standard case of pseudoscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Astrology and Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.3, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-3-astrology-and-astronomy. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Ptolemy, <i>Tetrabiblos</i> 1.1, trans. Frank Egleston Robbins, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Ptolemy/Tetrabiblos/1A*.html. [Open access]","Francesca Rochberg, “Personal Celestial Divination: The Babylonian Horoscopes,” in <i>The Heavenly Writing: Divination, Horoscopy, and Astronomy in Mesopotamian Culture</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2004), https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/heavenly-writing/personal-celestial-divination-the-babylonian-horoscopes/481E4E18C78D40097BC8368BC2C859AD. [Paywalled]","Observatoire de Paris, “Astrology and Science: A Complicated Divorce,” in <i>Sharing the Sky: Astronomers and Astrologers in the West</i>, online exhibition, Bibliothèque numérique de l’Observatoire de Paris, https://bibnum.obspm.fr/astrology-and-science-a-complicated-divorce. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Divination</i> 2.42.87, trans. W. A. Falconer, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cicero/de_Divinatione/2*.html. [Open access]","Observatoire de Paris, “The Axial Precession,” in <i>Sharing the Sky: Astronomers and Astrologers in the West</i>, online exhibition, Bibliothèque numérique de l’Observatoire de Paris, https://bibnum.obspm.fr/the-axial-precession. [Open access]","Sven Ove Hansson, “Science and Pseudo-Science,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, substantive revision 28 July 2025, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pseudo-science/. [Open access]","Shawn Carlson, “A Double-Blind Test of Astrology,” <i>Nature</i> 318, no. 6045 (1985): 419–25, https://doi.org/10.1038/318419a0. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did astrology and astronomy share a history for so long?","a":"Because both rested on the same observations of the sky. The Babylonians used their knowledge of astronomy to guide their rulers, and in Latin the word 'astrologia' regularly meant ‘astronomy’ until the end of the Middle Ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Ptolemy treat astrology as a lesser method?","a":"Because he held that the motions of the heavens could be known by demonstration, while their effects on earthly things shared the weakness and unpredictability of material qualities. He kept the second as a real but less self-sufficient branch of prediction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are newspaper sun signs out of step with the constellations?","a":"Because of precession. The constellations of the zodiac slide westward along the ecliptic, going once round the sky in about 26,000 years, so since the signs were named they have slipped by about one sign, while most astrologers kept the old dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Western astrologers mostly tie the signs to the seasons, while many in the East and in India tie them to the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Astrology"},
    "astro-016": {"questions":["Some early Greeks thought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a real shell of transparent crystal, with the stars set in it like tiny jewels, turning around a motionless Earth.","Right ascension and declination locate a star on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> much as longitude and latitude locate a city on the Earth, with the celestial equator in place of the equator."],"abstract":"The <b>celestial sphere</b> is an imaginary sphere of arbitrary radius on which the Sun, Moon, planets and stars can all be treated as if they lay, with a radius so large that no part of the Earth is noticeably closer to any star than another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It may be centred on the observer, on the Earth's centre or anywhere else, since it records only directions, while the real objects lie at greatly varying distances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On a clear night far from city lights the sky looks like a great hollow dome with the stars all at the same distance on its surface, and the dome seems to turn, carrying stars up in the east and down in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some early Greeks took this literally and thought of the sky as a real sphere of transparent crystal with the stars set in it like tiny jewels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle argued that the shape of the heaven must of necessity be spherical, the shape he thought most appropriate to its substance and first in nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It is now known that the turning is the Earth's daily rotation on its axis, yet the sphere remains a convenient way of keeping track of where things are in the sky, and planetariums use it to simulate the night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Because the whole sphere seems to turn together, the stars keep their places relative to one another through the night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Astronomers project the Earth's poles and equator onto it to make a grid, measuring declination north or south of the celestial equator and right ascension eastwards from the vernal equinox.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Right ascension can be given in hours as well as degrees, because the sphere appears to make one turn of 360 degrees in 24 hours, so each hour is worth 15 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Today directions on the sphere are fixed by the International Celestial Reference System, adopted by the International Astronomical Union and tied to very distant radio sources, mostly quasars, not expected to show measurable motion of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Earth and Sky,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 4.1, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-1-earth-and-sky. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “International Celestial Reference System (ICRS),” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/ICRS_doc. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>On the Heavens</i> 2.4, trans. J. L. Stocks, Internet Classics Archive, https://classics.mit.edu/Aristotle/heavens.2.ii.html. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Celestial Sphere Defined,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s2.htm. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Reference Markers,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s4.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do astronomers keep using a sphere they know is imaginary?","a":"Because it is a simple way to record directions. The stars lie at greatly varying distances, but for keeping track of where they appear it is enough to place them all on one sphere of any radius and give each a position on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the celestial sphere seem to turn once a day?","a":"Because the Earth turns on its axis once a day, carrying the observer with it. The sky therefore appears to rotate the other way, bringing stars up in the east and setting them in the west while they keep their places relative to one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are distant quasars used to fix directions on the sphere?","a":"Because they are so far away that they are not expected to show any measurable motion of their own, unlike the stars of our galaxy. A frame tied to their radio positions, now measured to better than a thousandth of an arcsecond, is steadier than one built on stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Celestial sphere"},
    "astro-017": {"questions":["Polaris lies close to the north <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at this point in the Earth's history, but the slow change in the direction of the axis called precession will carry the pole away from it.","An observer standing at the Earth's North Pole would see the north <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> directly overhead, with every visible star circling it and none ever rising or setting."],"abstract":"A <b>celestial pole</b> is either of the two points where the Earth's axis, extended outwards, meets the celestial sphere, one above the North Pole and one above the South Pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the Earth turns on that axis, the whole sky appears to rotate in the opposite direction around these two points, which themselves do not seem to move.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long-exposure photographs show this as star trails, arcs of light curving around the pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In precise work the pole is tied to one particular axis, though the different possible axes all lie within a tenth of an arcsecond of one another, a tiny fraction of a degree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Halfway between the two poles, 90 degrees from each, lies the celestial equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The height of the pole above the horizon equals the observer's latitude, so from San Francisco, at 38 degrees north, the north celestial pole stands 38 degrees above the northern horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At the North Pole it would be directly overhead and every star would circle it without rising or setting, while at the equator both poles lie on the horizon and every star rises and sets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stars closer to the pole than the observer's latitude never set, and apart from these circumpolar stars every star rises in the east and sets in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The pole is not fixed among the stars, because the direction of the Earth's axis slowly changes in a cycle of about 25,800 years called axial precession, first noticed by Hipparchus in the 2nd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Polaris, the star that moves least as the northern sky turns, is near the north pole only at this point in the Earth's history, and a couple of thousand years ago the stars nearest it were Kochab and Pherkad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Astrology and Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.3, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-3-astrology-and-astronomy. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","NASA Science Editorial Team, “Milankovitch (Orbital) Cycles and Their Role in Earth’s Climate,” NASA Science, 27 February 2020, https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/earth-science/milankovitch-orbital-cycles-and-their-role-in-earths-climate/. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Rise, Set, and Twilight Definitions,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/RST_defs. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Reference Markers,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s4.htm. [Open access]","Observatoire de Paris, “The Axial Precession,” in <i>Sharing the Sky: Astronomers and Astrologers in the West</i>, online exhibition, Bibliothèque numérique de l’Observatoire de Paris, https://bibnum.obspm.fr/the-axial-precession. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the pole's height equal the observer's latitude?","a":"Because the pole lies along the Earth's axis. At the North Pole, latitude 90 degrees, it is overhead; at the equator, latitude 0, it sits on the horizon; in between it stands at an angle equal to the latitude, 38 degrees at San Francisco.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some stars never set?","a":"Because they lie close enough to the celestial pole that their daily circle around it never dips below the horizon. For an observer at 38 degrees north, every star within 38 degrees of the north celestial pole stays up day and night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why will Polaris not always be the pole star?","a":"Because the direction of the Earth's axis slowly changes, a motion called axial precession that takes about 25,800 years to complete a cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the axis turns, the celestial pole moves among the stars, and a couple of thousand years ago the stars nearest it were Kochab and Pherkad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Celestial pole"},
    "astro-018": {"questions":["On the day of an equinox the centre of the Sun's disc crosses the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that point is then above the horizon for 12 hours everywhere on the Earth.","For an observer standing on the Earth's equator the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passes straight overhead through the zenith, and every star spends exactly half of each day above the horizon."],"abstract":"The <b>celestial equator</b> is the great circle on the celestial sphere that lies in the same plane as the Earth's equator, halfway between the two celestial poles and 90 degrees from each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Put simply, it is the Earth's equator projected outwards onto the sky, though the formal definition ties it to the plane perpendicular to the Earth's defined pole of rotation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the base line of the sky's coordinates, since declination is measured north of it as positive and south of it as negative, just as latitude is measured from the Earth's equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Right ascension, the other coordinate, is counted eastwards along it from the vernal equinox, one of the two points where the Sun's yearly path crosses it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the Earth's equator and its orbit both slowly change direction, a coordinate system built on them must state the date of the equator and equinox it uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> From anywhere on the Earth the celestial equator meets the horizon due east and due west, but how high it arches depends on latitude, and from the North Pole it would lie along the horizon itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the Earth's equator it passes straight overhead through the zenith, the celestial poles sit on the northern and southern horizon, and every star is above the horizon for exactly half of each day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sun crosses it twice a year, because the Sun's yearly path is tilted by about 23.5 degrees to it, so for half the year the Sun lies north of it and for half the year south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crossings come at the equinoxes, around 21 March and 22 September, when the centre of the Sun's disc is above the horizon for 12 hours everywhere on the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Day and night are nevertheless not quite equal then, because sunrise and sunset are timed by the Sun's upper edge and the atmosphere bends its light, so equal day and night fall a few days away from the equinoxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “Earth and Sky,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 4.1, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-1-earth-and-sky. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “International Celestial Reference System (ICRS),” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/ICRS_doc. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Length of Day and Night at the Equinoxes,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/equinoxes. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Reference Markers,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s4.htm. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Motion of Our Star the Sun,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s5.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is declination measured from the celestial equator?","a":"Because it is the sky's version of the Earth's equator. Declination works like latitude, counted positive to the north and negative to the south, so Polaris, close to the north celestial pole, has a declination of almost +90 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Sun cross the celestial equator twice a year?","a":"Because its yearly path, the ecliptic, is tilted by about 23.5 degrees to the equator, a result of the tilt of the Earth's axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two great circles cross at two points, the equinoxes, and the Sun passes each of them once a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are day and night not exactly equal at an equinox?","a":"Because sunrise and sunset are timed by the upper edge of the Sun, not its centre, and the atmosphere bends sunlight so that the Sun appears higher than it is. Both effects lengthen the day, so the dates of equal day and night fall a few days away from the equinoxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Celestial equator"},
    "astro-019": {"questions":["Because the Earth's axis is tilted, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is inclined to the celestial equator by about 23.4 degrees, which is why the Sun stands high at noon in summer and low in winter.","The Moon's orbit is tilted about 5 degrees to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which it crosses at two nodes, and the Sun's passage from one node to the other sets the spacing of eclipse seasons."],"abstract":"The <b>ecliptic</b> is the apparent path of the Sun around the celestial sphere over a year and, in a stricter sense, the mean plane of the orbit of the Earth and Moon around the centre of mass of the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sun seems to move along it because the Earth is going round the Sun, so that through the year the Sun is seen against different background stars, shifting about one degree eastwards each day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That daily shift is why the Sun rises about 4 minutes later each day relative to the stars, and the Earth must turn a little more than once to bring the Sun back up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ecliptic runs through the constellations of the zodiac, which the ancients thought special because the Sun, Moon and planets pass in front of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Moon's orbit is tilted to it by a mean angle of about 5.1 degrees, so the Moon is always found close to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The ecliptic is inclined to the celestial equator because the Earth's axis is tilted, today by about 23.4 degrees, from the perpendicular to its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> This angle is called the obliquity of the ecliptic, and its present value is approximately 23.44 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It is not fixed, since over the last million years it has varied between 22.1 and 24.5 degrees in a cycle of about 41,000 years, and it is now slowly decreasing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tilt means the Sun lies north of the celestial equator for half the year and south of it for the other half, high in the northern sky in June and low in December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two circles cross at the vernal and autumnal equinoxes, while the Moon's path crosses the ecliptic at two nodes, and the Sun takes about 173 days to travel from one node to the other, the interval between eclipse seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Eclipses and the Moon’s Orbit,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center Eclipse Web Site, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEhelp/moonorbit.html. [Open access]","Observatoire de Paris, “Glossary,” in <i>Sharing the Sky: Astronomers and Astrologers in the West</i>, online exhibition, Bibliothèque numérique de l’Observatoire de Paris, https://bibnum.obspm.fr/glossary. [Open access]","NASA Science Editorial Team, “Milankovitch (Orbital) Cycles and Their Role in Earth’s Climate,” NASA Science, 27 February 2020, https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/earth-science/milankovitch-orbital-cycles-and-their-role-in-earths-climate/. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Motion of Our Star the Sun,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s5.htm. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the Sun appear to move against the stars?","a":"Because the Earth is travelling round the Sun. As the Earth moves along its orbit, the Sun is seen against a different part of the background sky, shifting about one degree eastwards a day and completing the circuit of the ecliptic in a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the ecliptic tilted to the celestial equator?","a":"Because the Earth's axis is tilted from the perpendicular to its orbit, by about 23.4 degrees today, so the plane of the equator and the plane of the orbit meet at that angle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tilt itself varies between about 22.1 and 24.5 degrees over some 41,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the Moon and the zodiac always near the ecliptic?","a":"Because the Moon's orbit is tilted only about 5.1 degrees to the ecliptic, so it never strays far from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The zodiac is simply the band of constellations through which the ecliptic runs, in front of which the Sun makes its yearly path.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ecliptic"},
    "astro-020": {"questions":["The Sun reaches the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only for places in the tropics, as in Hawaii in May and July, when upright objects briefly cast no shadow at all.","Starlight from near the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crosses the least air, which is why scintillation, the measured twinkling of a star, is weakest high overhead and grows as a star sinks towards the horizon."],"abstract":"The <b>zenith</b> is the point on the celestial sphere directly above an observer, the top of the apparent dome of the sky, which always looks like a great sphere centred on whoever is watching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Strictly, the astronomical zenith is where a plumb line from the observer's position would point if it were extended upwards for ever, and the point directly opposite, beneath the feet, is the nadir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the dome of the sky meets the ground is the horizon, which from the sea or a flat plain looks like a circle around the viewer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In astronomy the horizon is also defined as a plane at right angles to the line from the observer through the zenith, so that the zenith lies exactly 90 degrees from it, and the meridian is the arc running from the north point of the horizon through the zenith to the south point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A star's altitude is its angle above the horizon and its zenith distance is its angle from the zenith, and the two always add up to 90 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Which part of the sky passes through the zenith depends on latitude, and from the Earth's equator the celestial equator itself runs straight overhead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the North Pole the north celestial pole would stand at the zenith, so the stars would circle it without ever rising or setting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sun can reach the zenith only within the tropics, between about 23 degrees north and south, where it passes directly overhead at noon on one or two days a year, and never anywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In Hawaii the moment is called Lāhainā Noon and falls in May and July, when upright objects briefly cast no shadow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Light from near the zenith crosses the least air, so scintillation, the flicker that turbulent air adds to a star's light, is smallest overhead and grows as a star sinks towards the horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, US Naval Observatory, “Rise, Set, and Twilight Definitions,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/RST_defs. [Open access]","Bishop Museum, “Lāhainā Noon,” Planetarium: Astronomy Resources, https://www.bishopmuseum.org/lahaina-noon/. [Open access]","J. Osborn, D. Föhring, V. S. Dhillon, and R. W. Wilson, “Atmospheric Scintillation in Astronomical Photometry,” <i>Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society</i> 452, no. 2 (2015): 1707–16, https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.06921. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Reference Markers,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s4.htm. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “The Seasons,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 4.2, https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-2-the-seasons. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Celestial Sphere Defined,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s2.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does every observer have a different zenith?","a":"Because the zenith is defined by the observer's own vertical, the direction a plumb line points when extended upwards. Move across the Earth's curved surface and that direction changes, carrying the zenith to a different point on the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the overhead Sun limited to the tropics?","a":"Because only there does the Sun's path ever carry it to the point directly above. In the tropics it passes overhead twice a year, as in Hawaii in May and July, when upright objects cast no shadow; nowhere else on the planet does this happen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do astronomers prefer to observe objects high in the sky?","a":"Because light from near the zenith passes through the least air. Scintillation, the flicker that turbulent air adds to starlight, increases with distance from the zenith, so brightness measurements are steadiest for objects high overhead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Zenith"},
    "astro-021": {"questions":["Because a star's position in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> changes through the night and from one place to another, catalogues list right ascension and declination instead of altitude and azimuth.","A star seen in the south-west has an azimuth between 180 and 270 degrees in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where due east lies at 90 degrees and the zenith at an altitude of 90."],"abstract":"The <b>horizontal coordinate system</b> fixes the position of an object in the sky by two angles measured from the observer's own surroundings, its altitude and its azimuth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Altitude is the angle of an object above or below the horizon, measured along the great circle that runs from the zenith, the point straight overhead, down through the object.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It runs from 0 degrees at the horizon to 90 degrees at the zenith, and the zenith distance, the angle from the point overhead, is simply 90 degrees minus the altitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Azimuth is the angle measured along the horizon, usually from true north and clockwise, so that due east is 90 degrees, due south 180 and due west 270, though some southern-hemisphere conventions count from the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A star reaches its greatest altitude of the night as it crosses the meridian, the arc that joins the north point of the horizon, the zenith and the south point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The system is the natural way to point out a star to a friend, but its numbers belong to one place and one moment only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As the Earth turns, every star's altitude and azimuth change through the night, and two observers in different places looking at the same star at the same instant record different values.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almanacs and catalogues therefore give positions in right ascension and declination, which are tied to the Earth's equator and are meant to be independent of the observer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To turn such a position into altitude and azimuth, an observer needs their latitude and longitude and the sidereal time, from which the local hour angle and then the two horizon angles follow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conversion also runs the other way: the transit circle at Greenwich measured a star's angle above the horizon as it crossed the meridian and turned it into a declination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “Computing Altitude and Azimuth from Greenwich Apparent Sidereal Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/alt_az. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “Glossary,” definitions consistent with the 2023 edition of <i>The Astronomical Almanac</i>, https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Coordinates,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s6.htm. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Reference Markers,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s4.htm. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “Airy's Transit Circle and the Dawn of the Universal Day,” https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/time/airys-transit-circle-dawn-universal-day. [Open access]","Christopher Palma, “Definitions,” in <i>ASTRO 801: Planets, Stars, Galaxies, and the Universe</i> (University Park: Pennsylvania State University), https://www.e-education.psu.edu/astro801/content/l1_p2.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do star catalogues not list positions by altitude and azimuth?","a":"Because those two numbers belong to one observer at one moment. As the Earth turns, a star's altitude and azimuth change through the night, and observers in different places see the same star at different values.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Right ascension and declination are tied to the Earth's equator instead, so they do not depend on where or when a star is seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What does an observer need to find a star's altitude and azimuth?","a":"Because the horizon system is local, the observer has to supply their own place and time. With their latitude and longitude, the star's right ascension and declination and the Greenwich sidereal time, they can work out the local hour angle and then the altitude and azimuth with two standard formulas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is one altitude in the sky the same as the observer's latitude?","a":"Because the celestial pole lies on the extension of the Earth's axis, its height above the horizon changes by one degree for each degree an observer moves north or south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The result is that the pole stands at an altitude equal to the observer's latitude, which is why measuring it tells a navigator where they are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Horizontal coordinate system"},
    "astro-022": {"questions":["Counting right ascension in hours, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> makes one hour equal to 15 degrees, so that two stars an hour apart cross the observer's meridian an hour apart in time.","Sirius is listed at right ascension 6 hours 45 minutes and declination −16 degrees 43 minutes in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a frame whose axes are now fixed by distant quasars."],"abstract":"The <b>equatorial coordinate system</b> gives the position of an object by two angles, right ascension and declination, which work on the celestial sphere much as longitude and latitude work on the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Declination is the angle north or south of the celestial equator, the Earth's equator projected onto the sky, positive to the north and negative to the south, so that the north celestial pole lies at +90 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Right ascension is measured eastward along the celestial equator from a chosen zero point, the vernal equinox, where the Sun's yearly path crosses the equator heading north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is usually counted in hours, minutes and seconds rather than degrees, because the sky turns once a day: 24 hours make the full circle, so one hour equals 15 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A position is written with right ascension first, so Sirius is listed at about 6 hours 45 minutes and −16 degrees 43 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The great advantage of the system is that a star's coordinates do not depend on the observer's place or time, which is why catalogues, magazines and planetarium software all use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hours have a practical meaning, since two stars one hour apart in right ascension cross an observer's meridian one hour apart in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Transit instruments relied on exactly this, timing a star's passage across the meridian and measuring its angle above the horizon to fix its position relative to other stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The coordinates still drift slowly, because precession moves both the equator and the equinox, so catalogue positions are given for a stated epoch such as J2000.0.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Since an International Astronomical Union resolution of 1997 the reference has been the International Celestial Reference System, whose axes are fixed by distant extragalactic sources yet agree with the equator and equinox of J2000.0 to better than 0.1 arcsecond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.1 Earth and Sky,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-1-earth-and-sky. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Cosmic Coordinates,” <i>SpaceBook</i>, https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/equatorial-coordinate-system/. [Open access]","Solar System Dynamics Group, Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Right Ascension,” Glossary, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/glossary/ra.html. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Coordinates,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s6.htm. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “Airy's Transit Circle and the Dawn of the Universal Day,” https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/time/airys-transit-circle-dawn-universal-day. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “International Celestial Reference System (ICRS),” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/ICRS_doc. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “Glossary,” definitions consistent with the 2023 edition of <i>The Astronomical Almanac</i>, https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","George H. Kaplan, <i>The IAU Resolutions on Astronomical Reference Systems, Time Scales, and Earth Rotation Models: Explanation and Implementation</i>, U.S. Naval Observatory Circular 179 (Washington, DC: U.S. Naval Observatory, 2005), https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0602086. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is right ascension measured in hours rather than degrees?","a":"Because the sky appears to turn once a day, a full circle of right ascension can be set equal to 24 hours, so each hour is 15 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That makes the number useful at the telescope: stars one hour apart in right ascension cross the observer's meridian one hour apart in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do star positions come with a date such as J2000.0?","a":"Because the frame itself moves. Precession, driven mainly by the Moon's pull, slowly shifts both the celestial equator and the equinox, so right ascension and declination change over the years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A position therefore has to say which date's equator and equinox it is measured from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the axes now fixed by quasars rather than by the Earth?","a":"Because quasars are so distant that they show no measurable motion across the sky, a frame built on their radio positions is steadier than one built on stars or on the Earth's moving equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The International Celestial Reference System was aligned to agree with the old J2000.0 coordinates, so catalogues kept their numbers when it was adopted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Equatorial coordinate system"},
    "astro-023": {"questions":["Precession has carried the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of the constellation it is named after and into Pisces, close to the border with Aquarius, over the last four thousand years.","The zero of right ascension at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives in modern catalogues because the reference system based on distant quasars was deliberately aligned with the equinox of J2000.0."],"abstract":"The <b>First Point of Aries</b> is the vernal equinox thought of as a point on the sky: the place on the celestial sphere where the Sun crosses the celestial equator from south to north each March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of the two places where the ecliptic, the Sun's yearly path, meets the celestial equator, and the other is the autumnal equinox.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two circles are tilted about 23.5 degrees to each other and cross at opposite points of the sky, which the Sun reaches around 21 March and 22 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Astronomers made the March point the origin of right ascension, the sky's counterpart of the Greenwich meridian, from which positions are counted eastward along the celestial equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No star marks it, so its place has always had to be worked out from the observed motions of the Sun and planets and carried in catalogues of star positions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The name records an older sky: around 2000 BCE, when Babylon was a great power, the point lay in Aries, and by about the 1st century CE it had moved into Pisces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The drift is precession, the slow wobble of the Earth's axis with a period of about 26,000 years, which shifts the equinox westward and changes stellar coordinates by roughly 1.4 degrees a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Today the point lies near the border of Pisces and Aquarius, which is why every traditional catalogue position has to say which date's equator and equinox it uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That moving origin became a weakness, and the International Celestial Reference System, built from 1997 on the radio positions of distant quasars, does not depend on the equinox, although the equinox itself has not been discarded and still underlies sidereal time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> For continuity its axes were set to agree with the equinox of J2000.0 to better than 0.1 arcsecond, so the old zero point survives in the numbers although the Earth's motion no longer defines it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Solar System Dynamics Group, Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Vernal Equinox,” Glossary, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/glossary/verneq.html. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “Glossary,” definitions consistent with the 2023 edition of <i>The Astronomical Almanac</i>, https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “International Celestial Reference System (ICRS),” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/ICRS_doc. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Cosmic Coordinates,” <i>SpaceBook</i>, https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/equatorial-coordinate-system/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.1 Earth and Sky,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-1-earth-and-sky. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Coordinates,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s6.htm. [Open access]","George H. Kaplan, <i>The IAU Resolutions on Astronomical Reference Systems, Time Scales, and Earth Rotation Models: Explanation and Implementation</i>, U.S. Naval Observatory Circular 179 (Washington, DC: U.S. Naval Observatory, 2005), https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0602086. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is it called the First Point of Aries if it lies in Pisces?","a":"Because the name is older than the present sky. Around 2000 BCE the vernal equinox lay in Aries, but precession, the slow wobble of the Earth's axis, has since moved it westward into Pisces, and it now sits near the border with Aquarius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why must an old star catalogue say which equinox it uses?","a":"Because the equinox moves. Precession shifts both the celestial equator and the ecliptic, so the point where they cross, the zero of right ascension, slowly slides along the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A position is only meaningful when it names the date of the equator and equinox it is measured from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did astronomers stop defining the zero point by the equinox?","a":"Because the equinox depends on the moving planes of the Earth's equator and orbit, which can only be pinned down through imperfect theories of the Earth's motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The International Celestial Reference System instead uses distant quasars as fixed markers, while keeping its axes aligned with the J2000.0 equinox so that older coordinates still fit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"First point of Aries"},
    "astro-024": {"questions":["The Sun's transit across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marks local solar noon, the moment a sundial reads twelve, and gives morning and afternoon their Latin names, before and after it.","Clock stars timed as they crossed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the cross-hairs of the Greenwich transit circle were once used to check the standard clock and keep Greenwich Mean Time."],"abstract":"The <b>celestial meridian</b> of an observer is the great circle on the sky that runs from the north point of the horizon up through the zenith and down to the south point, the one vertical circle that passes through both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It is the sky's continuation of the observer's own meridian of longitude, a circle through the Earth's rotational poles and the point directly overhead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stays fixed to the observer's horizon while the stars turn past it, and it shifts against the stars whenever the observer changes place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A star crosses the meridian twice in each turn of the sky, and the crossing nearer the zenith is called upper culmination, or transit, while the one farther away is lower culmination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For a circumpolar star, which circles the pole without setting, the lower crossing is also called culmination below the pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At transit an object stands at its highest point of the day, and for observers at low and middle latitudes that moment falls about midway between its rising and setting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sun's transit is local solar noon, which is why the morning is 'ante meridiem', ‘before the meridian’, and the afternoon 'post meridiem', after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Observatories built transit instruments to watch this crossing, timing the moment a star passed the meridian and measuring its angle above the horizon to fix its position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Stars whose positions were already known could be used the other way round, as clock stars whose predicted transits checked the observatory's standard clock, and in this way Greenwich Mean Time was set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1884 a conference in Washington agreed that the cross-hairs of the Airy Transit Circle at Greenwich, first used in 1851, would mark longitude 0 degrees for the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nick Strobel, “Reference Markers,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s4.htm. [Open access]","Solar System Dynamics Group, Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Meridian,” Glossary, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/glossary/meridian.html. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “Glossary,” definitions consistent with the 2023 edition of <i>The Astronomical Almanac</i>, https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “Rise, Set, and Twilight Definitions,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/RST_defs. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “Airy's Transit Circle and the Dawn of the Universal Day,” https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/time/airys-transit-circle-dawn-universal-day. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “Airy Transit Circle Telescope,” https://www.rmg.co.uk/royal-observatory/attractions/airy-transit-circle-telescope. [Open access]","Christopher Palma, “Definitions,” in <i>ASTRO 801: Planets, Stars, Galaxies, and the Universe</i> (University Park: Pennsylvania State University), https://www.e-education.psu.edu/astro801/content/l1_p2.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a star highest in the sky when it crosses the meridian?","a":"Because stars move in arcs parallel to the celestial equator, rising in the east and setting in the west, and the meridian runs through the top of each arc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At transit a body is at its highest point of the day, about midway between rising and setting for observers at low and middle latitudes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did observatories time stars crossing the meridian?","a":"Because one crossing gave two measurements: the time of transit and the star's angle above the horizon, from which its position in the sky could be worked out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Once positions were known, the predicted transits of clock stars could be used to check and set the observatory's standard clock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does longitude 0 degrees run through Greenwich?","a":"Because an international conference in Washington in 1884 agreed that the cross-hairs in the eyepiece of the Airy Transit Circle would define longitude 0 degrees and the start of the universal day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The telescope had been in use since 1851 and was known as an instrument of great accuracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Meridian (astronomy)"},
    "astro-025": {"questions":["The nocturnal, used by navigators between about 1400 and 1800, read the time from the turning of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> such as the pointer stars of the Big Dipper around Polaris.","At the North Pole every star is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that never rises or sets, while at the equator none is, since every star there spends half its time below the horizon."],"abstract":"A <b>circumpolar star</b> is one that, seen from a given latitude, circles the celestial pole without ever setting below the horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name means ‘around the pole’, and such a star stays above the horizon by day as well as by night, although sunlight hides it in the daytime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether a star qualifies depends on the observer, because the celestial pole stands at an altitude equal to the observer's latitude, so every star within that many degrees of the pole never sets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At 38 degrees north, for example, stars within 38 degrees of the north celestial pole are always up, while those within 38 degrees of the south celestial pole never rise at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second group is also called circumpolar, in the opposite sense, and the two caps of sky are known as the north and south circumpolar zones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The zones grow and shrink with latitude, so that at the poles every constellation is circumpolar, while at the equator all of them rise and set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From most of the continental United States the Big Dipper, the Little Dipper and Cassiopeia never set, while the Southern Cross stays hidden in the southern zone, and from Britain the Big Dipper can likewise be seen all year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Depending on latitude, a northern observer can see up to nine circumpolar constellations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because they turn about the pole once every sidereal day of 23 hours, 56 minutes and 4 seconds, circumpolar stars make a clock, turning anticlockwise for an observer facing north, with the Big Dipper's two pointer stars sweeping round Polaris like an hour hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Between about 1400 and 1800 mathematicians, astronomers and navigators read that clock with an instrument called a nocturnal, and seamen could find their local time with it to within about 15 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Reference Markers,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s4.htm. [Open access]","Louise Devoy, “What Is a Nocturnal? How to Tell Time by the Stars,” Royal Museums Greenwich, 30 October 2023, https://rmg.co.uk/stories/blog/astronomy/what-nocturnal. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “What Are Constellations?,” https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/space-astronomy/constellations. [Open access]","Kat Troche, “February’s Night Sky Notes: Constant Companions: Circumpolar Constellations, Part I,” NASA Science, 1 February 2024, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/skywatching/night-sky-network/feb2024-night-sky-notes/. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “Astronomy without a Telescope: What Can I See in the Night Sky?,” https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/space-astronomy/astronomy-naked-eye. [Open access]","Christopher Palma, “Day and Night,” in <i>ASTRO 801: Planets, Stars, Galaxies, and the Universe</i> (University Park: Pennsylvania State University), https://www.e-education.psu.edu/astro801/content/l1_p3.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does it depend on latitude whether a star is circumpolar?","a":"Because the height of the celestial pole above the horizon equals the observer's latitude, and only stars closer to the pole than that angle can swing round it without touching the horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Move north and the pole rises, so more stars become circumpolar; move towards the equator and fewer do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could circumpolar stars be used to tell the time?","a":"Because they never set and turn about the pole at a steady rate, once every sidereal day of about 23 hours 56 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pointer stars of the Big Dipper move round Polaris like the hand of a clock, and a nocturnal lined up on them gave the hour to within about 15 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are there no circumpolar stars at the equator?","a":"Because from the equator both celestial poles lie on the horizon, so every star rises straight up in the east and sets straight down in the west, above the horizon for exactly half of each turn of the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the poles the opposite is true, and every constellation is circumpolar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Circumpolar star"},
    "astro-026": {"questions":["Sailing south towards the Canary Islands, at a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 28 degrees north, navigators found the Big Dipper too low and used nocturnals scaled for the higher pointer stars of the Little Bear.","Which constellations are seasonal and which never set depends on an observer's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that at the poles every constellation is circumpolar and at the equator every one rises and sets."],"abstract":"<b>Latitude</b> is a place's angular distance north or south of the Earth's equator, measured along its meridian from 0 degrees at the equator to 90 degrees at either pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its partner is longitude, counted east or west from the prime meridian through Greenwich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the stars are so distant, latitude alone decides which part of the celestial sphere an observer can ever see and how it appears to turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rule behind this is simple: the celestial pole stands above the horizon at an altitude equal to the observer's latitude, so measuring its height is an easy way of finding latitude, a fact navigators still use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Latitude also enters every calculation of a star's altitude and azimuth, and even day length: on the day of an equinox the day is about 7 minutes longer than the night up to about 25 degrees of latitude and 10 minutes or more at 50 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> At the North Pole the north celestial pole is at the zenith and the celestial equator lies along the horizon, so only the northern half of the sky is ever visible and no star rises or sets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the equator both celestial poles sit on the horizon, every star rises and sets, and each spends exactly half of every turn of the sky above the horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In between, stars within the observer's latitude of the visible pole never set, those as close to the hidden pole never rise, and the rest climb from the horizon at 90 degrees minus the latitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which constellations are seasonal therefore changes from place to place, and a northern observer may see anything up to nine circumpolar constellations depending on latitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The effect mattered at sea: sailors heading south towards the Canary Islands, at 28 degrees north, found the Big Dipper harder to see and used nocturnals scaled for the higher pointer stars of the Little Bear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.1 Earth and Sky,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-1-earth-and-sky. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Reference Markers,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s4.htm. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “What Are Constellations?,” https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/space-astronomy/constellations. [Open access]","Louise Devoy, “What Is a Nocturnal? How to Tell Time by the Stars,” Royal Museums Greenwich, 30 October 2023, https://rmg.co.uk/stories/blog/astronomy/what-nocturnal. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “Computing Altitude and Azimuth from Greenwich Apparent Sidereal Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/alt_az. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “Length of Day and Night at the Equinoxes,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/equinoxes. [Open access]","Kat Troche, “February’s Night Sky Notes: Constant Companions: Circumpolar Constellations, Part I,” NASA Science, 1 February 2024, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/skywatching/night-sky-network/feb2024-night-sky-notes/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the height of the celestial pole equal the observer's latitude?","a":"Because the pole lies straight above the Earth's geographic pole. For every degree an observer moves south from the North Pole, the north celestial pole moves one degree down from the zenith towards the horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the time the observer reaches their home latitude, the pole stands that many degrees above the northern horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can an observer at the North Pole see only half the sky?","a":"Because there the celestial equator lies along the horizon, so everything south of it is permanently below the horizon. The stars that are visible all circle the pole overhead without rising or setting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the distance to the stars not change what each latitude sees?","a":"Because the celestial sphere is in effect infinitely far away, only the direction of the observer's horizon matters, and that direction is set by latitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Observers on a round world only about 16 kilometres (10 miles) across would see exactly the same pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Latitude"},
    "astro-027": {"questions":["The paper roll from Dunhuang that plots 1,339 stars in 257 asterisms holds the oldest complete <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the whole sky known from any civilisation, probably drawn in the early Tang dynasty.","Turning a planisphere, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mounted as a rotating wheel, shows which stars and constellations are above the horizon at any chosen date, hour and place."],"abstract":"A <b>star chart</b> is a map of the sky on which stars and other objects can be found, and on most modern charts the grid lines are lines of right ascension and declination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making one means flattening a curved sphere onto a flat sheet, and any such projection distorts the sky somewhere, most of all near the poles, where the lines of right ascension converge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chart-makers have long dealt with this by drawing the sky in pieces, as bands around the equator in one projection and a separate polar map in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Brightness is usually shown as well, and the magnitude scale survives in large part because books, star charts and astronomy apps still use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A planisphere, or star wheel, is a chart that can be rotated to show which stars and constellations are visible at a given time and place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest complete star atlas known from any civilisation is a Chinese paper roll from the Silk Road town of Dunhuang, now held in the British Library.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It plots 1,339 stars in 257 asterisms on twelve maps in a near-cylindrical projection and one circumpolar map, drawn to within about 1.5 to 4 degrees for the brightest stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its date is debated, but the fullest study argues for 649–684 CE, in the early Tang dynasty, rather than the previously proposed date of about 940 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Europe printed celestial atlases flourished from the late 15th to the mid-19th century, and the <i>Uranometria</i> of 1603, usually called the first modern star atlas, plotted Tycho Brahe's star positions on grids across 51 charts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Later atlases tried other choices: the <i>Firmamentum</i> of 1690 drew the constellations as they would look on a globe, seen from outside, and the <i>Atlas coelestis</i> of 1729 was the largest and most accurate star atlas published until then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Nick Strobel, “Coordinates,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s6.htm. [Open access]","Jean-Marc Bonnet-Bidaud, Francoise Praderie, and Susan Whitfield, “The Dunhuang Chinese Sky: A Comprehensive Study of the Oldest Known Star Atlas,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 12 (2009): 39–59, https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.3034. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “17.1 The Brightness of Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/17-1-the-brightness-of-stars. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “What Are Constellations?,” https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/space-astronomy/constellations. [Open access]","William B. Ashworth Jr., “Johann Bayer,” Scientist of the Day, Linda Hall Library, 7 March 2016, https://www.lindahall.org/about/news/scientist-of-the-day/johann-bayer. [Open access]","Linda Hall Library, <i>Out of This World: The Golden Age of the Celestial Atlas</i>, digital exhibition, https://www.lindahall.org/experience/digital-exhibitions/out-of-this-world/. [Open access]","Linda Hall Library, “The Innovations of Hevelius, 1690–1731,” in <i>Out of This World: The Golden Age of the Celestial Atlas</i>, digital exhibition, https://www.lindahall.org/experience/digital-exhibitions/out-of-this-world/22-the-innovations-of-hevelius-1690-1731/. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “Who Was John Flamsteed, the First Astronomer Royal?,” https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/space-astronomy/who-was-john-flamsteed-first-astronomer-royal. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can't a flat star chart show the whole sky without distortion?","a":"Because the sky is mapped as the inside of a sphere, and projecting a curved surface onto a flat sheet always stretches some part of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dunhuang atlas already shows chart-makers choosing different projections for different parts of the sky to keep the error small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do many charts give the polar sky a map of its own?","a":"Because the lines of right ascension all converge at the celestial poles, so a grid that works well near the equator crowds and distorts the polar region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dunhuang atlas used twelve near-cylindrical maps for the band around the sky and a separate circumpolar map in a different projection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the <i>Uranometria</i> called the first modern star atlas?","a":"Because its 51 charts plotted star positions from Tycho Brahe's recent observations carefully onto grids, rather than drawing pictures of the constellations alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It also introduced the practice of naming bright stars with Greek letters, as in alpha Centauri.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Star chart"},
    "astro-028": {"questions":["Traditionally credited to Hipparchus, who sorted stars by eye into six classes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first survives in the star catalogue of Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i>, written in Alexandria in about 137 CE.","Sirius at about −1.46 and the Sun at about −26.7 sit below zero on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had to be extended into negative numbers once the brightest objects were measured precisely."],"abstract":"The <b>magnitude scale</b> is the system astronomers use to express how bright a star or other object appears, and it runs backwards: the larger the number, the fainter the object.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tradition credits the Greek astronomer Hipparchus, around 150 BCE, with sorting the stars into six classes by eye, calling the brightest first magnitude and the faintest he could see sixth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The earliest surviving list in that system is the catalogue in Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i> of about 137 CE, with magnitudes for over a thousand stars, and whether Ptolemy or Hipparchus mainly observed it is still debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 1856 the scale was strictly defined, after measurements showed that first-magnitude stars send about 100 times as much light as sixth-magnitude ones, so that five magnitudes now equal a factor of exactly 100.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One magnitude is therefore a factor of the fifth root of 100, about 2.512, which makes the scale logarithmic, and because brightness varies with wavelength a magnitude is often given for one band, such as the visual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Once measured precisely, the old first-magnitude stars proved unequal, and the scale had to run past zero into negative numbers for the brightest objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky, has a visual magnitude of about −1.46, while the faintest stars visible to the unaided eye under ideal skies are near magnitude 6.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Venus at its brightest reaches about −4.4 and the Sun about −26.7, while the faintest objects recorded by the largest telescopes lie around magnitude 30.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many astronomers find the system awkward, and radio, infrared, X-ray and gamma-ray astronomers mostly express brightness as energy received per square metre per second instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It survives because it is built into catalogues, charts and the literature, and in 2015 the International Astronomical Union fixed an agreed zero point for its bolometric, all-wavelength form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “17.1 The Brightness of Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/17-1-the-brightness-of-stars. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Magnitude System,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/starprop/s4.htm. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “Glossary,” definitions consistent with the 2023 edition of <i>The Astronomical Almanac</i>, https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Christopher Palma, “The Magnitude System,” in <i>ASTRO 801: Planets, Stars, Galaxies, and the Universe</i> (University Park: Pennsylvania State University), https://www.e-education.psu.edu/astro801/content/l4_p5.html. [Open access]","E. E. Mamajek et al., “IAU 2015 Resolution B2 on Recommended Zero Points for the Absolute and Apparent Bolometric Magnitude Scales,” arXiv:1510.06262 (2015), https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.06262. [Open access]","Tomoko Fujiwara and Hitoshi Yamaoka, “Magnitude Systems in Old Star Catalogues,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 8, no. 1 (2005): 39–47, https://doi.org/10.3724/sp.j.1440-2807.2005.01.04.","Bradley E. Schaefer, “The Thousand Star Magnitudes in the Catalogues of Ptolemy, Al Sufi, and Tycho Are All Corrected for Atmospheric Extinction,” <i>Journal for the History of Astronomy</i> 44, no. 1 (2013): 47–74, https://doi.org/10.1177/002182861304400103. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the magnitude scale run backwards?","a":"Because it began as a ranking. The ancient catalogues, traditionally traced to Hipparchus, called the brightest stars first magnitude and the faintest he could see sixth magnitude, so a bigger number meant a fainter star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later astronomers kept the old order when they made the scale precise rather than abandoning a long-used system, and the steps in old naked-eye catalogues turn out to be close to the modern ratio of 2.512.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is one magnitude a factor of about 2.512 in brightness?","a":"Because 19th-century measurements found that first-magnitude stars send about 100 times as much light as sixth-magnitude ones, and astronomers fixed five magnitudes as exactly a factor of 100.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The number that multiplied by itself five times gives 100 is about 2.512, so each single step is that factor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some astronomers avoid magnitudes altogether?","a":"Because the backwards, logarithmic scale is easy to misread, and newer branches of astronomy never adopted it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Radio, infrared, X-ray and gamma-ray astronomers usually give the energy received per square metre each second instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-029": {"questions":["Subtracting a star's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from its apparent magnitude gives the distance modulus, from which the star's distance in parsecs follows once its true luminosity is known from its type.","Defined for an imagined distance of 1 astronomical unit from both the Sun and the Earth, an asteroid's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> differs from a star's, which assumes a distance of 10 parsecs."],"abstract":"<b>Absolute magnitude</b> is the magnitude a star would have if it were seen from a standard distance of 10 parsecs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It measures what a star actually gives out, its luminosity, whereas apparent magnitude measures only how bright it looks from the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The difference matters because apparent brightness falls with the square of distance, so of two identical stars the one twice as far away looks four times dimmer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A faint star may therefore be feeble but close or brilliant but very far away, and the two cases can be told apart only once its distance is known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A star exactly 10 parsecs away has equal apparent and absolute magnitudes, while a nearer star looks brighter than its absolute magnitude and a farther one fainter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Apparent minus absolute magnitude is called the distance modulus, and it equals five times the logarithm of the distance in parsecs, minus five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Measure a star's apparent magnitude and its distance by parallax and its absolute magnitude follows; know its absolute magnitude from stars of its type instead, and the distance follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sun, overwhelming at an apparent visual magnitude of about −26.7, would be a modest star of absolute magnitude about 4.8 seen from 10 parsecs, and Sirius would be about 1.5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stars span an enormous range, from absolute magnitudes of about −8 for the most luminous to about +19 for the feeblest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The term means something different for asteroids, whose absolute magnitude is their brightness at 1 astronomical unit from both the Sun and the Earth, and in 2015 the International Astronomical Union fixed a zero point for the bolometric scale that gives the Sun about 4.74.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nick Strobel, “Magnitude System,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/starprop/s4.htm. [Open access]","Christopher Palma, “The Magnitude System,” in <i>ASTRO 801: Planets, Stars, Galaxies, and the Universe</i> (University Park: Pennsylvania State University), https://www.e-education.psu.edu/astro801/content/l4_p5.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “17.1 The Brightness of Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/17-1-the-brightness-of-stars. [Open access]","Solar System Dynamics Group, Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “H (Absolute Magnitude),” Glossary, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/glossary/h.html. [Open access]","E. E. Mamajek et al., “IAU 2015 Resolution B2 on Recommended Zero Points for the Absolute and Apparent Bolometric Magnitude Scales,” arXiv:1510.06262 (2015), https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.06262. [Open access]","Christopher Palma, “Luminosity and Apparent Brightness,” in <i>ASTRO 801: Planets, Stars, Galaxies, and the Universe</i> (University Park: Pennsylvania State University), https://www.e-education.psu.edu/astro801/content/l4_p4.html. [Open access]","Rob Zondag, “Stellar Distances,” ESA Science & Technology, https://sci.esa.int/web/education/-/35616-stellar-distances. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can't a star's apparent brightness tell us how luminous it is?","a":"Because brightness falls with the square of distance, a dim star may be a weak one nearby or a powerful one far away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only when the distance is known can the dimming be allowed for, and absolute magnitude does exactly that by imagining every star at the same 10 parsecs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does absolute magnitude help to measure distances?","a":"Because the gap between apparent and absolute magnitude, the distance modulus, depends only on distance. If a star's type tells us its absolute magnitude, comparing that with how bright it looks gives the distance in parsecs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every such distance depends on how well stars of that type have first been calibrated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the International Astronomical Union set a zero point in 2015?","a":"Because there had long been no internationally agreed zero point for the absolute and apparent bolometric magnitude scales, which cover all wavelengths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The chosen zero point gives the Sun a bolometric absolute magnitude of about 4.74, the value most often used in recent literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Absolute magnitude"},
    "astro-030": {"questions":["Multiplying an object's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in arcseconds by its distance and dividing by 206,265 gives its true diameter, a relation astronomers call the small-angle formula.","The bright ring around the black hole in the galaxy M87 has an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of only about 42 microarcseconds, resolved by linking radio telescopes around the world into one array."],"abstract":"<b>Angular size</b> is how large an object appears in the sky, measured as the angle its diameter covers at the observer's eye rather than as a length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A degree, one 360th of a circle, is divided into 60 arcminutes and each arcminute into 60 arcseconds, the units in which most angular sizes are given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both the Sun and the full Moon are about half a degree across, roughly the width of the little finger held at arm's length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Moon's angular diameter is about 31 arcminutes, and almanacs take the Sun's radius on the sky as 16 arcminutes when they calculate the times of sunrise and sunset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Angular size alone says nothing about true size, since a thumb held at arm's length can cover the whole full Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> True size, distance and angular size are tied together by the small-angle formula: the linear size equals the angular size in arcseconds times the distance, divided by 206,265.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Any two of the three quantities give the third, so an object at a known distance with a measured angular size can be given a real diameter, and the formula only holds for small angles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the Moon's distance from the Earth varies, so does its angular size, and an eclipse near its farthest point leaves it too small to cover the Sun, so that a bright ring remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The angles astronomers measure can be tiny: from the ground a star's parallax shift is hard to measure below about 0.01 arcsecond, yet the bright ring around the black hole in the galaxy M87, reported in 2019, is about 42 microarcseconds across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Resolving it needed the Event Horizon Telescope, a worldwide network of radio dishes working together as a single array at a wavelength of 1.3 millimetres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Las Cumbres Observatory, “Positions and Sizes of Cosmic Objects,” <i>SpaceBook</i>, https://lco.global/spacebook/using-angles-describe-positions-and-apparent-sizes-objects. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Angles,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, https://www.astronomynotes.com/nakedeye/s3.htm. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Astronomical Applications Department, U.S. Naval Observatory, “Length of Day and Night at the Equinoxes,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/equinoxes. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Types of Solar Eclipses,” https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/types/. [Open access]","Kazunori Akiyama et al. for the Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration, “First M87 Event Horizon Telescope Results. I. The Shadow of the Supermassive Black Hole,” <i>The Astrophysical Journal Letters</i> 875, no. 1 (2019): L1, https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/ab0ec7. [Open access]","Rob Zondag, “Stellar Distances,” ESA Science & Technology, https://sci.esa.int/web/education/-/35616-stellar-distances. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a thumb at arm's length cover the full Moon?","a":"Because what the eye judges is angle, not length. A thumb close to the eye and the Moon far away can cover the same angle, about half a degree, although the Moon is vastly larger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How large an object looks depends on both its real size and its distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are some solar eclipses a ring rather than total?","a":"Because the Moon's angular size changes with its distance. When an eclipse happens with the Moon at or near its farthest point from the Earth, it appears smaller than the Sun and cannot cover it, leaving a bright ring around a dark disc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How was a size of 42 microarcseconds measured?","a":"Because no single telescope can see detail that small, the Event Horizon Telescope linked radio dishes around the world into one very long baseline interferometry array, observing at a wavelength of 1.3 millimetres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The resulting image of M87's black hole showed a ring 42 microarcseconds across, with an uncertainty of about 3.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Angular diameter"},
    "astro-031": {"questions":["Measured along an arc of a great circle and worked out with spherical trigonometry, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of two stars is stated in degrees, arcminutes and arcseconds.","With an outstretched hand, a finger covering about 1° and a palm about 10°, an observer can estimate the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between two stars without any instrument at all."],"abstract":"The <b>angular separation</b> of two objects in the sky is the angle between the directions in which they are seen, and it is the only measure of how far apart they are that an observer can read straight off the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the stars seem to lie on a great dome, astronomers say how far apart two of them look in degrees, with 360° making a full circle around the sky and 180° spanning the dome from one horizon to the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A degree is divided into 60 arcminutes and an arcminute into 60 arcseconds, the same units used for latitude and longitude on the Earth, and the full Moon covers about 31 arcminutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> These units are not part of the International System of Units but are formally accepted for use with it, one degree being π/180 of a radian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the smallest angles it measures, astronomy also uses the milliarcsecond and the microarcsecond, thousandths and millionths of an arcsecond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A rough separation can be judged with an outstretched arm, on which a finger covers about 1° of sky and the width of the palm about 10°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the sky, as on any sphere, the shortest path between two points is an arc of a great circle, so the sides of a triangle drawn on the celestial sphere are measured as angles rather than lengths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Given the coordinates of two stars, their separation is worked out with the cosine formula of spherical trigonometry, the curved counterpart of the rule used for a flat triangle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Angular separation says nothing by itself about how far apart two objects really are, because the stars only seem to sit on a dome and in fact lie at greatly varying distances from us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Precision has grown enormously, and the Gaia spacecraft measured the positions of faint stars to about 24 microarcseconds, comparable to the width of a human hair seen from 1,000 kilometres (620 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Positions and Sizes of Cosmic Objects,” <i>SpaceBook</i>, https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/using-angles-describe-positions-and-apparent-sizes-objects/. [Open access]","National Institute of Standards and Technology, <i>The International System of Units (SI)</i>, NIST Special Publication 330, 2019 ed., section 4, https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-330/sp-330-section-4. [Open access]","Vik Dhillon, “The Geometry of the Sphere,” PHY105 lecture notes, University of Sheffield, 2009, https://vikdhillon.staff.shef.ac.uk/teaching/phy105/celsphere/phy105_geometry.html. [Open access]","Vik Dhillon, “Spherical Trigonometry,” PHY105 lecture notes, University of Sheffield, 2009, https://vikdhillon.staff.shef.ac.uk/teaching/phy105/celsphere/phy105_trigonometry.html. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Gaia Factsheet,” 15 August 2025, https://www.esa.int/articles/about-us/directorates/science/gaia/gaia-factsheet. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Chapter 2: Reference Systems,” page one, <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter2-1/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are distances on the sky given in degrees rather than kilometres?","a":"Because the eye cannot see how far away the stars are: they only appear to lie on a dome, and in fact sit at greatly varying distances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What an observer can measure is the angle between two directions, and a full circle around the sky is 360°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A degree is then split into 60 arcminutes and each arcminute into 60 arcseconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does astronomy need units far smaller than a degree?","a":"Because many of the angles it cares about are tiny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The SI rules accept the degree, minute and second for use and note that astronomy measures small angles in arcseconds, milliarcseconds and microarcseconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gaia, for example, fixed the positions of faint stars to about 24 microarcseconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a flat triangle not be used for widely separated stars?","a":"Because the sky is treated as a sphere, on which the shortest route between two points is an arc of a great circle and the sides of a triangle are angles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cosine formula of spherical trigonometry relates those sides and angles, and it gives the separation of two stars from their coordinates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Angular distance"},
    "astro-032": {"questions":["The smaller the angle of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across a fixed baseline, the farther away the object must be, which turns a measured angle into a distance by simple triangulation.","During a transit Venus shows a horizontal <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of about 30 arcseconds, a shift between observers that made the transits of 1761 and 1769 useful for measuring the Sun's distance."],"abstract":"<b>Parallax</b> is the apparent shift in the direction of an object caused by a change in the position of the observer, and it is measured as an angle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is familiar from everyday life: from a moving train nearby trees seem to slide past distant hills, and a hand held up before the face jumps against the background when each eye is closed in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The astronomers' almanac defines parallax as the difference in apparent direction of an object seen from two places, or equally as the angle at the object covered by the line joining them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That line is the baseline, and the baseline with the two lines of sight makes a long, thin triangle whose length is the distance to the object.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rule that follows holds for any parallax: the smaller the angle, the farther away the object must be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Astronomers name a parallax by its baseline, so a geocentric or diurnal parallax compares the direction seen from the Earth's surface with the direction from its centre, while annual parallax compares the view from the Earth with the view from the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The solar parallax is defined as the angle that the Earth's equatorial radius would cover seen from a distance of one astronomical unit, a way of stating the Sun's distance as an angle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> During a transit Venus shows a horizontal parallax of about 30 arcseconds, and the transits of 1761 and 1769 gave astronomers their first good value for the distance of the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Greek astronomers looked for a seasonal shift in the stars and found none, which left two choices: either the Earth was not moving or the stars were immensely far away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stellar parallaxes are indeed tiny, usually a fraction of an arcsecond even for the nearest stars, and Gaia measured star positions to about 24 microarcseconds, millionths of an arcsecond, which brings far more distant stars within reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “19.2 Surveying the Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-2-surveying-the-stars. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.2 Ancient Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-2-ancient-astronomy. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Stellar Parallax,” <i>SpaceBook</i>, https://lco.global/spacebook/distance/parallax-and-distance-measurement/. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “2004 and 2012 Transits of Venus,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, Goddard Space Flight Center, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/transit/venus0412.html. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Gaia Factsheet,” 15 August 2025, https://www.esa.int/articles/about-us/directorates/science/gaia/gaia-factsheet. [Open access]","National Institute of Standards and Technology, <i>The International System of Units (SI)</i>, NIST Special Publication 330, 2019 ed., section 4, https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-330/sp-330-section-4. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a parallax tell how far away something is?","a":"Because the baseline and the two lines of sight form a triangle, and the parallax is the angle that the baseline covers as seen from the object.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> If the object were farther away the triangle would be longer and skinnier and the angle smaller, so measuring the angle across a known baseline gives the distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Greek astronomers fail to see the stars shift?","a":"Because the shift is far too small for the eye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greeks looked for it in spring and again in autumn and saw nothing, so they concluded either that the Earth was still or that the stars were unimaginably far off, and most chose a fixed Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even for the nearest stars the parallax is usually a fraction of an arcsecond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do astronomers speak of several kinds of parallax?","a":"Because a parallax depends on its baseline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The radius of the Earth gives the geocentric or diurnal parallax used for the Moon, the planets and the Sun, while the Earth's orbit gives the annual parallax of the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Venus in transit shows a horizontal parallax of about 30 arcseconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Parallax"},
    "astro-033": {"questions":["Measured as the hour angle of the vernal equinox, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs through 24 hours in one turn of the Earth against the stars, so its clocks gain nearly four minutes a day.","Because a given star crosses the meridian at the same moment of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> every night, observatories plan their work by it rather than by ordinary solar clocks."],"abstract":"<b>Sidereal time</b> is a system of timekeeping based on the rotation of the Earth measured against the distant stars rather than against the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strictly, it is the hour angle of the vernal equinox, the angle measured westward through which that point of the sky has turned past an observer's meridian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the equinox crosses a meridian the sidereal time there is zero, and it then advances through 24 sidereal hours in one turn of the Earth relative to the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That turn takes about 23 hours 56 minutes 4 seconds of ordinary clock time, so sidereal time gains about 3 minutes 56 seconds on clock time each day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is the same for every place on one meridian whatever the latitude, and the local value is found by adding the longitude, turned into hours at 15° to the hour, to the sidereal time at Greenwich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The definition observers find most useful is that local sidereal time equals the right ascension of whatever is crossing the meridian at that moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An object's hour angle measures the time since it crossed the meridian, and local sidereal time is the sum of its right ascension and its hour angle, a rule that holds for any star, planet or spacecraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An observer who knows the sidereal time can therefore tell at once which objects are near the meridian and high in the sky, where the air disturbs the view least.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A star crosses the meridian at the same sidereal time every night, while on solar clocks it rises about 4 minutes earlier each day, so astronomers prefer sidereal time for planning observations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> For precise work there are two versions, mean sidereal time, referred to an equinox moved only by precession, and apparent sidereal time, which includes nutation as well, and they never differ by more than about 1.2 seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Sidereal Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/data/siderealtime. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Computing Approximate Sidereal Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/GAST. [Open access]","Vik Dhillon, “Sidereal Time,” PHY105 lecture notes, University of Sheffield, 2009, https://vikdhillon.staff.shef.ac.uk/teaching/phy105/celsphere/phy105_sidereal.html. [Open access]","Vik Dhillon, “The Equatorial Coordinate System,” PHY105 lecture notes, University of Sheffield, 2009, https://vikdhillon.staff.shef.ac.uk/teaching/phy105/celsphere/phy105_equatorial.html. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Solar Time vs. Sidereal Time,” <i>SpaceBook</i>, https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/sidereal-time/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.3 Keeping Time,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-3-keeping-time. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does sidereal time run ahead of clock time?","a":"Because it follows the Earth's turn against the stars, which takes about 23 hours 56 minutes 4 seconds of clock time, so sidereal time gains about 3 minutes 56 seconds a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ordinary clocks follow the Sun instead, and since the Earth also moves along its orbit it must turn about 1° more than a full circle to bring the Sun back to noon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is sidereal time tied so closely to right ascension?","a":"Because the vernal equinox is the zero point of both, so when the equinox crosses the meridian the sidereal time is zero and a star then crossing has a right ascension of zero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In general local sidereal time equals an object's right ascension plus its hour angle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does longitude change the sidereal time but latitude does not?","a":"Because sidereal time is an hour angle measured from the local meridian, and all places with the same longitude share one meridian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moving east or west changes the meridian, so the local value is found by adding the longitude, divided by 15 to turn degrees into hours, to the Greenwich sidereal time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sidereal time"},
    "astro-034": {"questions":["Divided into twelve equal signs of 30° in Babylonia around the end of the 5th century BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave astronomers a scale for stating positions by sign and degree.","Because precession has carried the first point of Aries into Pisces, the equal signs of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> no longer match the 13 constellations that the Sun's path now crosses."],"abstract":"The <b>zodiac</b> is the band of sky centred on the ecliptic, the Sun's apparent yearly path, within which the Sun, the Moon and the planets are always found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is about 18° wide, because the paths of the planets around the Sun and of the Moon around the Earth all lie in nearly the same plane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word shares its root with 'zoo' and means a collection of animals, since many of the star patterns along the band reminded early observers of creatures such as a fish or a goat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ecliptic that runs down its middle is both the mean plane of the Earth's orbit and the apparent path of the Sun around the celestial sphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the Earth goes round the Sun, the Sun appears to pass in turn in front of the constellations along the band, and those visible at night in any season are the ones lying opposite it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The signs of the zodiac are not the constellations themselves but a division of the band into twelve equal parts of 30°, an idea invented in Babylonia around the end of the 5th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> This uniform zodiac let astronomers state a position by sign and degree, and it had spread widely through Greek astronomy by the time Geminos wrote his <i>Introduction to the Phenomena</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ecliptic longitude, measured along the ecliptic from the first point of Aries, is the modern descendant of the same scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That starting point is not fixed, since the slow precession of the Earth's axis, a cycle of about 26,000 years, drags it backwards along the ecliptic by about 50 arcseconds a year, and it now lies in Pisces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The equal signs and the unequal constellations have therefore drifted apart, and measured against the exact boundaries of the 88 official constellations the Sun's path today crosses 13, with Ophiuchus between Scorpius and Sagittarius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","J. M. Steele, “Geminos and Babylonian Astronomy,” in <i>Instruments – Observations – Theories: Studies in the History of Astronomy in Honor of James Evans</i>, ed. Alexander Jones and Christián Carman (2020), 205–12, https://archive.nyu.edu/bitstream/2451/61288/57/12.%20Steele.pdf. [Open access]","Vik Dhillon, “The Ecliptic Coordinate System,” PHY105 lecture notes, University of Sheffield, 2009, https://vikdhillon.staff.shef.ac.uk/teaching/phy105/celsphere/phy105_ecliptic.html. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Vik Dhillon, “The Equatorial Coordinate System,” PHY105 lecture notes, University of Sheffield, 2009, https://vikdhillon.staff.shef.ac.uk/teaching/phy105/celsphere/phy105_equatorial.html. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Chapter 2: Reference Systems,” page one, <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter2-1/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.2 Ancient Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-2-ancient-astronomy. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “The Seasons and the Earth's Orbit,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/seasons_orbit. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “What Are Constellations?,” <i>SpaceBook</i>, https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/what-are-constellations/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the Sun, Moon and planets always found in such a narrow band?","a":"Because the orbits of the planets around the Sun and of the Moon around the Earth all lie in nearly the same plane, like circles drawn on one huge sheet of paper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seen from the Earth, that plane is the ecliptic, the mean plane of the Earth's orbit, and every one of these bodies stays within about 9° of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the signs no longer match the constellations of the same names?","a":"Because the signs are twelve equal 30° divisions of the band, while the constellations are star patterns of unequal size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The signs are counted from the first point of Aries, which precession moves about 50 arcseconds a year and which now lies in Pisces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Sun's path also crosses a thirteenth constellation, Ophiuchus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Babylonian astronomers divide the band into equal parts?","a":"Because a uniform scale let them give the position of the Sun, the Moon or a planet as a sign and a number of degrees within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The scheme passed into Greek astronomy and is still echoed in ecliptic longitude, measured from the first point of Aries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Zodiac"},
    "astro-035": {"questions":["Defined in 1958 after radio surveys showed the old equator missed the Galaxy's hydrogen plane, modern <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put the north galactic pole at a declination of +27.4°.","In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a star's latitude is measured from the galactic equator, which meets the celestial equator at about 63°, and its longitude from the galactic centre."],"abstract":"The <b>galactic coordinates</b> give positions on the sky with the plane of the Milky Way as their equator, in place of the Earth's equator used by right ascension and declination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Galactic latitude, written b, measures angles north or south of the galactic equator up to 90° at the galactic poles, while galactic longitude, written l, runs along that equator from 0° to 360°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Longitude is counted from the direction of the galactic centre, in the constellation of Sagittarius, and increases along the galactic equator in the same sense as right ascension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The system is centred on the Sun, which is treated as lying in the galactic plane even though it is thought to sit a little above the principal plane of the Galaxy's hydrogen gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Sun lies inside a flat disc of stars about 100,000 light-years across, seen from Earth as the band of the Milky Way, along which the galactic equator runs at about 63° to the celestial equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The present system was defined in 1958 by a sub-commission of the International Astronomical Union, replacing an older one whose pole was misplaced by about 1.5° and whose longitudes began some 32° from the galactic centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Radio surveys had shown the strongest emission near the galactic centre lying more than a degree south of the old equator, and the new equator was set by the flat plane of neutral hydrogen mapped at a wavelength of 21 centimetres (8 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The definition fixed the north galactic pole at a right ascension of 12h 49m and a declination of +27.4° for the equinox of 1950, values the sub-commission declared exact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Star positions are now given in a newer reference system, and since no IAU-sanctioned transformation from the old definition into it is exact, the Gaia mission adopted the conventions of the earlier Hipparcos Catalogue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One proposal is to redefine the system on the modern frame, with the compact radio source Sagittarius A* at the Galaxy's dynamical centre as the zero of longitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Vik Dhillon, “The Galactic Coordinate System,” PHY105 lecture notes, University of Sheffield, 2009, https://vikdhillon.staff.shef.ac.uk/teaching/phy105/celsphere/phy105_galactic.html. [Open access]","A. Blaauw, C. S. Gum, J. L. Pawsey, and G. Westerhout, “The New I.A.U. System of Galactic Coordinates (1958 Revision),” <i>Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society</i> 121, no. 2 (1960): 123–31, https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/121.2.123. [Open access]","J.-C. Liu, Z. Zhu, and H. Zhang, “Reconsidering the Galactic Coordinate System,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 526 (2011): A16, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201014961. [Open access]","Alexey Butkevich and Lennart Lindegren, “Transformations of Astrometric Data and Error Propagation,” in <i>Gaia Data Release 2 Documentation</i>, release 1.2, European Space Agency, https://gea.esac.esa.int/archive/documentation/GDR2/Data_processing/chap_cu3ast/sec_cu3ast_intro/ssec_cu3ast_intro_tansforms.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “25.1 The Architecture of the Galaxy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/25-1-the-architecture-of-the-galaxy. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Chapter 2: Reference Systems,” page two, <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter2-2/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why use the plane of the Milky Way instead of the Earth's equator?","a":"Because for studying the structure and motions of the Galaxy it is more convenient to have the equator in the galactic plane and the pole at the galactic pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Sun lies inside the Galaxy's flat disc, so that plane shows on the sky as the band of the Milky Way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the older system replaced in 1958?","a":"Because radio surveys showed it was out of line with the Galaxy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its pole was displaced by about 1.5° from the pole of the plane of neutral hydrogen, and its longitudes started about 32° from the galactic centre, so the IAU sub-commission set a new pole and put the zero of longitude towards the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is converting modern positions into galactic coordinates awkward?","a":"Because the 1958 definition was written in terms of the equinox of 1950, which cannot be transformed exactly into the modern reference system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gaia therefore adopted the Hipparcos conventions, and some astronomers have suggested redefining the system directly on the modern frame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Galactic coordinate system"},
    "astro-036": {"questions":["At the equator every star rises straight up and sets straight down, while at the North Pole the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the sky carries the stars in circles parallel to the horizon.","Long-exposure photographs turn the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the stars into curved trails around a celestial pole, each arc recording part of the daily turning of the sky."],"abstract":"<b>Diurnal motion</b> is the apparent daily motion of the Sun, the Moon, the planets and the stars across the sky from east to west, caused by the rotation of the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Watched for a few hours, the stars rise on the eastern horizon, cross the dome of the sky and set in the west, just as the Sun and Moon do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole sky seems to turn together, so a group of stars such as the Big Dipper keeps its shape through the night while it wheels around the pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The turning carries the sky through 15° every hour, which is why right ascension can be given in hours, one hour equalling 15° of rotation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The motion could be explained either by a sky turning around a fixed Earth or by an Earth turning under a fixed sky, and since the 17th century it has generally been accepted that it is the Earth that turns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The pattern depends on the observer's latitude: at the North Pole the stars circle parallel to the horizon and never set, while at the equator every star rises straight up in the east and sets straight down in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In between, the celestial pole stands above the horizon at an altitude equal to the observer's latitude, the stars rise and set at a slant, and those close enough to the pole never set at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An object's hour angle, the time since it crossed the observer's meridian, grows steadily from 0° to 360° in a day, and every star is highest when it crosses that meridian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The stars complete their daily circuit a little faster than the Sun, so a given star crosses the meridian about 3 minutes 56 seconds earlier by the clock each day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Long-exposure photographs record the motion directly, as arcs traced by the stars around a celestial pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Vik Dhillon, “The Equatorial Coordinate System,” PHY105 lecture notes, University of Sheffield, 2009, https://vikdhillon.staff.shef.ac.uk/teaching/phy105/celsphere/phy105_equatorial.html. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Chapter 2: Reference Systems,” page two, <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter2-2/. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Sidereal Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/data/siderealtime. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.1 Earth and Sky,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-1-earth-and-sky. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the whole sky turn together?","a":"Because the motion belongs to the observer, not to the stars: it is caused by the rotation of the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The objects on the sky keep their positions relative to one another, so a group such as the Big Dipper keeps its shape while the whole sky wheels round the pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the turning sky look different at different latitudes?","a":"Because the celestial pole stands above the horizon at an altitude equal to the observer's latitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the North Pole it is overhead and the stars circle without setting; at the equator it lies on the horizon and every star rises and sets vertically; in between the stars rise at a slant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a star cross the meridian earlier each day?","a":"Because the Sun moves about 1° eastward among the stars every day as the Earth orbits, so the stars complete a circuit slightly faster than the Sun does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the clock a given star therefore crosses the meridian about 3 minutes 56 seconds earlier each day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Diurnal motion"},
    "astro-037": {"questions":["Ancient eclipse records show that the slowing of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has lengthened the mean solar day by about 1.8 milliseconds per century, less than tidal friction alone predicts.","Defining east as the direction in which the ground is carried, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also flattens the planet, so its equatorial radius exceeds its polar radius by about 21 kilometres (13 miles)."],"abstract":"<b>Earth's rotation</b> is the turning of the planet on its own axis, the motion that produces the cycle of day and night, as distinct from its revolution around the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relative to the Sun the Earth turns once in 24 hours of mean solar time, but relative to the distant stars one turn takes about 23.9345 hours, since the Earth must turn about 1° further each day to face the Sun again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The axis is tilted by about 23.44° to the plane of the orbit, and the spin also defines directions on the ground, since east is the direction towards which the Earth rotates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rotation makes the planet slightly oblate, its equatorial radius of about 6,378 kilometres (3,963 miles) exceeding its polar radius by some 21 kilometres (13 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pull of the Moon and the Sun on that equatorial bulge makes the axis precess, tracing out a circle in space over about 26,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> That it is the Earth and not the sky that turns was generally accepted from the 17th century, but an unambiguous demonstration came only in 1851, when a pendulum some 60 metres (200 feet) long was set swinging under the dome of the Panthéon in Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within a few minutes the plane of its swing was seen to turn, because the Earth was turning beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rotation is not perfectly steady, so timekeepers distinguish UT1, set by the Earth's actual rotation, from the atomic timescale UTC, which is kept within 0.9 seconds of it by occasional leap seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Timings of eclipses recorded over the past 2,700 years show that the length of the mean solar day has grown by an average of about 1.8 milliseconds per century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is less than the 2.3 milliseconds per century expected from tidal friction alone, and the difference is put down to other effects such as the rebound of the land since the ice ages and coupling between the core and the mantle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Chapter 2: Reference Systems,” page one, <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter2-1/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 15 November 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.1 Earth and Sky,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-1-earth-and-sky. [Open access]","F. R. Stephenson, L. V. Morrison, and C. Y. Hohenkerk, “Measurement of the Earth's Rotation: 720 BC to AD 2015,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society A</i> 472, no. 2196 (2016): 20160404, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.2016.0404. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Universal Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/UT. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.3 Keeping Time,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-3-keeping-time. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Solar Time vs. Sidereal Time,” <i>SpaceBook</i>, https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/sidereal-time/. [Open access]","Vik Dhillon, “The Equatorial Coordinate System,” PHY105 lecture notes, University of Sheffield, 2009, https://vikdhillon.staff.shef.ac.uk/teaching/phy105/celsphere/phy105_equatorial.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a turn against the stars shorter than 24 hours?","a":"Because the Earth is also moving along its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A turn measured against the distant stars takes about 23.9345 hours, while the 24-hour day is measured against the Sun, which the Earth must turn a little further to face again after moving along its path.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The difference adds up to one whole day over a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a swinging pendulum prove that the Earth turns?","a":"Because a pendulum keeps swinging in the same plane unless something pushes it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Paris demonstration of 1851 the plane of swing appeared to turn within minutes, which was explained by the Earth turning beneath the pendulum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are leap seconds added to civil time?","a":"Because the Earth's rotation is irregular while atomic clocks are not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> UTC is kept within 0.9 seconds of UT1, the time set by the Earth's actual rotation, by inserting a second when needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over the long term the day is also lengthening, by about 1.8 milliseconds per century on average.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Earth's rotation"},
    "astro-038": {"questions":["Changing the Sun's distance by only about 3% over the year, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is too nearly circular to cause the seasons, which come instead from the tilt of the axis.","Pulled by Jupiter and Saturn, the shape of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> swings between nearly circular and slightly elliptical over about 100,000 years, a cycle matched by the recent ice ages."],"abstract":"<b>Earth's orbit</b> is the path the planet follows around the Sun, at an average distance, the semi-major axis, of about 149.6 million kilometres (93 million miles), almost exactly one astronomical unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The path is an ellipse very close to a circle, with an eccentricity of about 0.0167, so the distance ranges from about 147.1 million kilometres (91.4 million miles) at perihelion to 152.1 million kilometres (94.5 million miles) at aphelion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Perihelion falls in early January and aphelion about six months later, and the Earth's speed along the orbit ranges from about 29.3 to 30.3 kilometres (18.2 to 18.8 miles) per second, carrying it about 1° around the Sun each day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> One circuit measured against the stars, the sidereal year, takes about 365.256 days, while the tropical year from equinox to equinox, which the calendar follows, is about 20 minutes shorter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The anomalistic year, from one perihelion to the next, is about 25 minutes longer than the tropical year, so the date of perihelion creeps later by about a day every 58 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The distance changes by only about 3% over the year, which is too little to cause the seasons, and the Earth is in fact closest to the Sun during the northern winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The seasons come instead from the tilt of the axis, which stays nearly fixed in direction in space while the Earth travels round the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> The shape of the orbit still matters a little, making northern summer about 4.5 days longer than northern winter, and each January about 6.8% more sunlight reaches the Earth than each July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over long spans the gravity of Jupiter and Saturn stretches the orbit from nearly circular to slightly more elliptical and back again, in a cycle of about 100,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Together with slow changes in the tilt and direction of the axis, these cycles shift where and when sunlight falls, and for about the last 800,000 years the ice ages have come at intervals matching the 100,000-year cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Chapter 2: Reference Systems,” page one, <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter2-1/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 15 November 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.2 The Seasons,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-2-the-seasons. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “The Seasons and the Earth's Orbit,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/seasons_orbit. [Open access]","NASA Science Editorial Team, “Milankovitch (Orbital) Cycles and Their Role in Earth's Climate,” NASA Science, 27 February 2020, https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/earth-science/milankovitch-orbital-cycles-and-their-role-in-earths-climate/. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Las Cumbres Observatory, “Solar Time vs. Sidereal Time,” <i>SpaceBook</i>, https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/sidereal-time/. [Open access]","NASA Space Place, “What Causes the Seasons?,” NASA Science, https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/seasons/en/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is it not hottest when the Earth is nearest the Sun?","a":"Because the distance changes by only about 3% over the year, which is not enough to drive the seasons, and perihelion actually falls in early January, during the northern winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The seasons come from the tilt of the Earth's axis, which points the same way in space all year, so each hemisphere leans towards the Sun in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do astronomers count several different kinds of year?","a":"Because a year depends on what the Earth's return is measured against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Against the stars it takes about 365.256 days; from equinox to equinox, the year of the seasons, about 20 minutes less; and from perihelion to perihelion about 25 minutes more than the seasonal year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the shape of the orbit matter for climate?","a":"Because it changes how much sunlight arrives at different times of year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Today about 6.8% more reaches the Earth in January than in July, and over about 100,000 years the orbit stretches and relaxes under the pull of Jupiter and Saturn, a rhythm the ice ages of the last 800,000 years have followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Earth's orbit"},
    "astro-039": {"questions":["In ancient Egypt the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Sirius, some 70 days after the star was last seen at dusk, heralded the Nile flood and began the calendar year.","Hesiod's advice to begin the harvest when the Pleiades rise at dawn depended on their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a reappearance after forty days hidden that returns at the same season every year."],"abstract":"A <b>heliacal rising</b> is the first rising of a star in the dawn sky, shortly before sunrise, after a spell in which it has been lost in the Sun's glare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The spell of invisibility comes from the star's conjunction with the Sun, when it lies in nearly the same direction as the Sun and so is above the horizon only in daylight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After that first glimpse the star rises about 4 minutes earlier by the clock on each following morning, so it climbs higher into the dark before dawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The date depends on latitude, coming later farther north, and it drifts over the centuries with precession: Sirius reappeared over Memphis in early July in antiquity but now returns in early August in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In ancient Egypt the heliacal rising of Sirius, some 70 days after the star was last seen in the west at sunset, heralded the Nile flood and marked the start of the calendar year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The stars change with the season because the Sun appears to move about 1° eastward among them each day, completing a circuit of the sky in a year as the Earth goes round it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stars near the Sun's position are hidden in daylight, so the constellations seen at night are always those lying in the opposite direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the clock a star rises about 4 minutes earlier each day, roughly 2 hours earlier each month, so a constellation that rises at sunset in winter rises with the Sun by summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A given star crosses the meridian about 3 minutes 56 seconds earlier in solar time each day, the gap between the Earth's turn against the stars and its turn against the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Farmers used the cycle as a calendar, and the Greek poet Hesiod advised starting the harvest when the Pleiades rise and cutting the grapes when Dawn sees Arcturus, noting that the Pleiades lie hidden for forty nights and days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["M. Heydari-Malayeri, “Heliacal Rising,” <i>An Etymological Dictionary of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i>, Paris Observatory, https://dictionary.obspm.fr/terms/heliacal-rising/. [Open access]","M. Heydari-Malayeri, “Heliacal Rising of Sirius,” <i>An Etymological Dictionary of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i>, Paris Observatory, https://dictionary.obspm.fr/terms/heliacal-rising-of-sirius/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.3 Keeping Time,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-3-keeping-time. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Works and Days</i> 383–387, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0132%3Acard%3D383. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Works and Days</i> 609–611, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0132%3Acard%3D609. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Sidereal Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/data/siderealtime. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do different constellations fill the night sky in different seasons?","a":"Because the Sun appears to move about 1° east among the stars each day as the Earth orbits it, and the stars near the Sun are hidden in daylight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At night we see the stars opposite the Sun, and since each star rises about 4 minutes earlier every day, the view shifts by about 2 hours a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the heliacal rising of Sirius matter so much in Egypt?","a":"Because it came at the season of the Nile flood and was taken as its herald, and it marked the start of the Egyptian calendar year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It followed some 70 days in which the star could not be seen, after it had last been glimpsed low in the west at sunset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could farmers use risings of stars as a calendar?","a":"Because a star's first appearance at dawn returns at the same season every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hesiod told farmers to reap when the Pleiades rose and to plough when they set, noting that they are hidden for forty days and reappear as the year comes round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He timed the grape harvest by Arcturus seen at dawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Heliacal rising"},
    "astro-040": {"questions":["At the June <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Sun stands overhead at noon on the Tropic of Cancer, while everywhere within about 23° of the North Pole has 24 hours of daylight.","Defined as the moment the Sun's apparent ecliptic longitude reaches 90° or 270°, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brings the longest day to one hemisphere and the shortest to the other."],"abstract":"A <b>solstice</b> is either of the two moments in the year when the Sun is at its farthest north or south of the celestial equator, and also either of the two points on the ecliptic where this happens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Astronomers define them as the points where the Sun's apparent ecliptic longitude is 90° and 270°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word comes from the Latin <i>solstitium</i>, ‘Sun stands still’, because the Sun's drift north or south comes to a halt before it changes direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Although a solstice is usually spoken of as a day, it is really an exact instant that falls during that day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the year is about 365¼ days long, the solstices and equinoxes generally fall about six hours later each year, jumping back by a day in leap years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They exist because the Earth's axis is tilted by about 23.44° to its orbit, so the ecliptic is inclined to the celestial equator and the Sun moves north and south across the sky through the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the June solstice the Sun stands overhead at noon on the Tropic of Cancer, near latitude 23° north, and every place within about 23° of the North Pole has sunlight for 24 hours while the region inside the Antarctic Circle has none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Six months later, at the December solstice, the roles are reversed: the Sun passes overhead on the Tropic of Capricorn and the Southern Hemisphere, now tilted towards the Sun, has its longest day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The shortest day does not bring the earliest sunset, since at latitude 40° north the earliest sunset comes around 8 December and the latest sunrise around 5 January, a gap caused by the difference between clock time and Sun time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many cultures marked the solstices, and the stones of Stonehenge frame the midsummer sunrise and the midwinter sunset, though whether marking the solstice was its purpose is not clear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.2 The Seasons,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-2-the-seasons. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “When Is the Summer Solstice?,” Royal Observatory Greenwich, https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/time/summer-solstice. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Sunrise and Sunset Times Near the Solstices,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/rs_solstices. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 15 November 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Chapter 2: Reference Systems,” page one, <i>Basics of Space Flight</i>, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter2-1/. [Open access]","NASA Space Place, “What Causes the Seasons?,” NASA Science, https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/seasons/en/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the Sun seem to stand still at a solstice?","a":"Because its daily drift north or south comes to a halt before it changes direction, which is what the Latin <i>solstitium</i>, ‘Sun stands still’, describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Through the year the Sun moves north and south of the celestial equator because the ecliptic is tilted to it by about 23.5°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the earliest sunset not on the shortest day?","a":"Because clocks keep a uniform average of Sun time, while the Sun's own timing varies through the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In December the Sun runs slow against the clock, so at latitude 40° north the earliest sunset comes around 8 December and the latest sunrise around 5 January, although the shortest day is the solstice itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the hemispheres' solstices reversed?","a":"Because when the Northern Hemisphere is tilted most towards the Sun, the Southern is tilted most away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In June the Sun is overhead on the Tropic of Cancer and the Arctic has continuous daylight; in December it is overhead on the Tropic of Capricorn and the Antarctic has the midnight Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Solstice"},
    "astro-041": {"questions":["Although every place gets roughly twelve hours of sunshine, day is a few minutes longer than night on the date of an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because refraction lifts the Sun's disc into view early.","At the North Pole the Sun rises at the March <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and sets at the September one, giving six months of daylight followed by six months of darkness."],"abstract":"An <b>equinox</b> is either of the two points on the celestial sphere where the ecliptic, the Sun's yearly path, crosses the celestial equator, and also the moment when the Sun passes through one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the ecliptic is tilted by about 23.5° to the celestial equator, the Sun spends half the year north of the equator and half south of it, and the equinoxes, halfway between the solstices, are its two crossings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On those dates the Sun is overhead at noon on the equator, favours neither hemisphere and gives every place on Earth roughly twelve hours of daylight and twelve of night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At the North Pole the Sun rises at the March equinox and sets at the September one, so each pole has six months of sunshine followed by six months of darkness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The equinoxes do not cut the year into equal halves, because the Earth's orbit is slightly elliptical: in the northern hemisphere spring currently lasts about three days longer than autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> The equal split is only approximate, because sunrise and sunset are timed from the edge of the Sun's disc rather than its centre, and because refraction lifts the disc into view before it has geometrically risen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As a result the day on an equinox is about seven minutes longer than the night at latitudes up to about 25 degrees, and ten minutes or more at latitude 50 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Astronomers take the equinoxes, around 20 March and 22 September, as the starts of spring and autumn, and leap years make their dates shift by a day or two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By convention the equinoxes are named for the northern seasons, so the March equinox is called the spring equinox even though autumn is then beginning in the southern hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The points themselves are not fixed among the stars: precession carries them slowly westward along the ecliptic, which is why the March equinox, still called the First Point of Aries, now lies in Pisces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §4.2, “The Seasons” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-2-the-seasons. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Length of Day and Night at the Equinoxes,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/equinoxes. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Earth’s Seasons: Equinoxes, Solstices, Perihelion, and Aphelion,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/data/Earth_Seasons. [Open access]","Jeremy Tatum, <i>Celestial Mechanics</i>, §6.7, “Precession” (LibreTexts Physics, University of Victoria), https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Astronomy__Cosmology/Celestial_Mechanics_(Tatum)/06%3A_The_Celestial_Sphere/6.07%3A_Precession. [Open access]","Royal Meteorological Society, “The Difference between Meteorological and Astronomical Seasons,” MetMatters, 19 February 2025, https://www.rmets.org/metmatters/difference-between-meteorological-and-astronomical-seasons. [Open access]","MeteoSwiss, Federal Office of Meteorology and Climatology, “Calendar, Astronomical and Meteorological Seasons,” https://www.meteoswiss.admin.ch/weather/weather-and-climate-from-a-to-z/calendar-astronomical-and-meteorological-seasons.html. [Open access]","NASA Science Editorial Team, “Milankovitch (Orbital) Cycles and Their Role in Earth’s Climate,” NASA Science, 27 February 2020, https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/earth-science/milankovitch-orbital-cycles-and-their-role-in-earths-climate/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are day and night not exactly equal at an equinox?","a":"Because sunrise and sunset are counted from the edge of the Sun's disc rather than its centre, and the atmosphere bends sunlight over the horizon, so the Sun is seen for several minutes longer than twelve hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the March equinox called the spring equinox everywhere?","a":"Because the equinoxes and solstices are conventionally named after the seasons of the northern hemisphere, even though the March equinox begins autumn south of the equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the equinox point drift against the stars?","a":"Because precession turns the Earth's axis slowly, carrying the equinox westward along the ecliptic in a cycle of about 25,800 years, so the point once in Aries is now in Pisces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Equinox"},
    "astro-042": {"questions":["Over the last million years the Earth's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has wandered between about 22.1° and 24.5° in a cycle of some 41,000 years, making the seasons harsher or milder.","Calculations suggest the Moon steadies the Earth's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, keeping it within about 1.3° of 23.3°, where without the Moon it might vary chaotically and drive dramatic changes in climate."],"abstract":"<b>Axial tilt</b>, also called obliquity, is the angle between a body's axis of rotation and the perpendicular to its orbital plane, or equivalently between the planes of its equator and its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the Earth it is about 23.44°, the angle between the planes of the equator and the ecliptic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Other planets differ widely: Mars is tilted by about 25°, Jupiter by about 3° and Uranus by about 98°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Earth's axis stays nearly fixed in direction as the planet orbits, so for half the year the northern hemisphere leans towards the Sun and for the other half away from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The tilt is not constant: over the last million years it has varied between about 22.1° and 24.5° in a cycle of some 41,000 years, and today it is slowly decreasing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Many people have believed that the seasons come from the Earth's changing distance from the Sun, but that distance varies by only about 3%, and the Earth is closest to the Sun in early January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Distance also cannot explain why the two hemispheres have opposite seasons at the same time, which the tilt explains at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hemisphere leaning towards the Sun receives its rays more directly, so the same sunlight is spread over a smaller area, and it also has longer days, so the Sun has more hours in which to heat it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A larger tilt makes the seasons more extreme and a smaller one makes them milder, and with no tilt at all there would be no seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Calculations of the Earth's spin suggest that the Moon steadies the tilt, keeping its variations to about 1.3° either side of 23.3°, whereas without the Moon it might swing chaotically over a far wider range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §4.2, “The Seasons” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-2-the-seasons. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “The Seasons and the Earth’s Orbit,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/seasons_orbit. [Open access]","NASA Science Editorial Team, “Milankovitch (Orbital) Cycles and Their Role in Earth’s Climate,” NASA Science, 27 February 2020, https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/earth-science/milankovitch-orbital-cycles-and-their-role-in-earths-climate/. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Planetary Fact Sheet – Metric,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 18 March 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/. [Open access]","J. Laskar, F. Joutel, and P. Robutel, “Stabilization of the Earth’s Obliquity by the Moon,” <i>Nature</i> 361, no. 6413 (1993): 615–17, https://doi.org/10.1038/361615a0. [Paywalled]","MeteoSwiss, Federal Office of Meteorology and Climatology, “Calendar, Astronomical and Meteorological Seasons,” https://www.meteoswiss.admin.ch/weather/weather-and-climate-from-a-to-z/calendar-astronomical-and-meteorological-seasons.html. [Open access]","NASA Space Place, “What Causes the Seasons?,” https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/seasons/en/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the seasons in the two hemispheres opposite?","a":"Because the axis keeps nearly the same direction in space all year, so when the northern hemisphere leans towards the Sun the southern leans away, and six months later the roles are reversed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does winter sunlight heat the ground less?","a":"Because the low winter Sun's rays strike the ground at a slant and spread over a wider area, and the Sun is also above the horizon for fewer hours each day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Moon thought to matter for the tilt?","a":"Because calculations show that its pull keeps the Earth out of a chaotic zone, holding the tilt within about 1.3° of its mean, while without it the angle could have varied enormously and driven dramatic changes in climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Axial tilt"},
    "astro-043": {"questions":["Plotting the equation of time against the Sun's declination gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a curve printed on many globes and some sundials so that clock time can be read from sunlight.","The unequal loops of the Earth's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> come from its elliptical orbit, for the tilt of the axis alone would draw a symmetric figure eight centred exactly on the celestial equator."],"abstract":"The <b>analemma</b> is the lopsided figure eight traced over a year by the Sun's position in the sky when it is observed from the same place at the same clock time each day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its long dimension comes from the Sun's seasonal change in declination, its angle north or south of the celestial equator, from −23.44° to +23.44° and back, and its narrow width of a few degrees from a small variation in the time at which the Sun crosses the meridian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That variation is the equation of time, the difference between apparent solar time, which a sundial reads, and mean solar time, which a clock keeps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plotted as a graph, with the equation of time across and the Sun's declination up, the same figure eight appears, and it is printed on many globes and some sundials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Clocks follow a fictitious mean Sun that moves at a steady rate, because the real Sun's daily progress varies through the year and apparent solar time does not advance uniformly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One cause is the 23.4° tilt of the Earth's axis, which gives the Sun's path a north–south component around the equinoxes and slows its east–west motion then, an effect worth up to about 10 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other is the elliptical orbit: the Earth moves fastest at perihelion in early January, and this alone shifts solar time by up to about 7.5 minutes either way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two effects are of similar size, so neither is the main cause, and together they make solar noon range from about 16 minutes before clock noon to about 14 minutes after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tilt alone would draw a symmetric figure eight centred on the celestial equator, and it is the eccentricity of the orbit that makes the two loops unequal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On Mars, with a similar tilt but a more elliptical orbit, the analemma is pear-shaped, as a martian year of images from the Opportunity rover showed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “The Equation of Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/eqtime. [Open access]","Charles H. Holbrow, “Build Your Own Analemma,” arXiv preprint 1302.0765 (2013), https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.0765. [Open access]","Alejandro Gangui, Cecilia Lastra, and Fernando Karaseur, “On Times and Shadows: The Observational Analemma,” <i>The Physics Teacher</i> 56, no. 6 (2018): 367–69, https://doi.org/10.1119/1.5051148. [Paywalled]","David W. Hughes, B. D. Yallop, and C. Y. Hohenkerk, “The Equation of Time,” <i>Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society</i> 238, no. 4 (1989): 1529–35, https://articles.adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1989MNRAS.238.1529H. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §4.3, “Keeping Time” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-3-keeping-time. [Open access]","Robert Nemiroff and Jerry Bonnell, “Opportunity’s Mars Analemma,” Astronomy Picture of the Day, NASA, 16 May 2014, https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-may-16-opportunitys-mars-analemma/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the analemma tall and thin?","a":"Because the Sun's declination swings from −23.44° to +23.44° over the year, while the clock-time shift that gives the figure its width amounts to only a few degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do a sundial and a clock disagree?","a":"Because a sundial follows the real Sun, whose daily motion varies with the tilt of the axis and the ellipse of the orbit, while a clock follows a mean Sun moving at a steady rate; the gap can reach about 16 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the two loops of the figure eight different sizes?","a":"Because of the eccentricity of the Earth's orbit; with the tilt alone the figure would be a symmetric eight centred on the celestial equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Analemma"},
    "astro-044": {"questions":["Comparing his star positions with records some 150 years older, Hipparchus found that the celestial pole had moved, his discovery of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that slowly shifts every star's coordinates.","The Sun and Moon pull on the Earth's equatorial bulge, and the resulting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries the March equinox westward along the ecliptic by about 50 arcseconds a year."],"abstract":"The <b>precession of the equinoxes</b> is the slow, steady change in the direction of the Earth's axis of rotation, which traces out a cone like the axis of a wobbling top.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the celestial poles and equator follow the axis, the north celestial pole circles the pole of the ecliptic at a radius of about 23.4°, and the equinoxes, where equator and ecliptic cross, slide westward along the ecliptic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A full circuit takes about 26,000 years, which corresponds to a westward shift of the equinox of about 50.2 arcseconds a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The cause is the Earth's shape: the planet bulges at the equator, and the gravitational pulls of the Sun and Moon on that bulge exert a torque on the spinning Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Astronomers describe precession with formal models, and since 1 January 2009 the International Astronomical Union's recommended model has been the theory known as P03.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its most visible effect is on the pole star: Polaris lies near the north celestial pole today, but about 5,000 years ago the pole was near Thuban in Draco, and in about 14,000 years Vega will be the North Star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same drift moves the coordinate grid itself, so the right ascension and declination of every star change continually and must be referred to the equinox and equator of a stated date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The March equinox, named the First Point of Aries when it lay in that constellation, has been carried right across Aries and now lies near the western edge of Pisces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the tropical year is measured from the moving equinox and the sidereal year against the background stars, the two differ, the sidereal year being about 20 minutes longer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The discovery is credited to Hipparchus, who found that the north celestial pole had moved over the previous century and a half, and his rate of about 46 arcseconds a year compares well with the modern 50.26.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §2.2, “Ancient Astronomy” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-2-ancient-astronomy. [Open access]","Jeremy Tatum, <i>Celestial Mechanics</i>, §6.7, “Precession” (LibreTexts Physics, University of Victoria), https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Astronomy__Cosmology/Celestial_Mechanics_(Tatum)/06%3A_The_Celestial_Sphere/6.07%3A_Precession. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “The Seasons and the Earth’s Orbit,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/seasons_orbit. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","IERS Conventions Centre, “Transformation between the International Terrestrial Reference System and the Geocentric Celestial Reference System,” chapter 5 of <i>IERS Conventions (2010)</i>, updated 10 August 2012, https://iers-conventions.obspm.fr/conventions/content/chapter5/icc5.pdf. [Open access]","NASA Science Editorial Team, “Milankovitch (Orbital) Cycles and Their Role in Earth’s Climate,” NASA Science, 27 February 2020, https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/earth-science/milankovitch-orbital-cycles-and-their-role-in-earths-climate/. [Open access]","J. J. O’Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Hipparchus,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Hipparchus/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the Earth's axis precess at all?","a":"Because the Earth bulges at the equator, and the Sun and Moon pull on that bulge, producing a torque that makes the spinning axis wobble like a top's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why will Polaris not always be the pole star?","a":"Because the axis traces a circle on the sky about every 26,000 years; the pole was near Thuban about 5,000 years ago and will be near Vega in about 14,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the First Point of Aries no longer in Aries?","a":"Because precession has carried the March equinox westward across that constellation over the centuries, and it now lies near the western boundary of Pisces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Axial precession"},
    "astro-045": {"questions":["The obliquity swings by about 9.2 arcseconds and the equinox by about 17.2 arcseconds along the ecliptic, the components of the Earth's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in obliquity and in longitude respectively.","Very long baseline interferometry reveals a further <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Earth's axis, driven by its liquid core, with a period of about 430 sidereal days that cannot be predicted far ahead."],"abstract":"<b>Nutation</b> is an oscillation in the direction of the rotation pole of a spinning body acted on by external gravitational forces, and for the Earth it is a small nodding laid over the slow circling of precession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word comes from the Latin <i>nutare</i>, ‘to nod’, and a spinning top shows a similar motion, although the Earth's nodding has a different cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The torques that drive it vary because the Earth's orbit is elliptical and the Moon's orbit is both elliptical and inclined, by about 5° to the ecliptic, so the pull of the Sun and Moon on the equatorial bulge is never steady.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nutation of the Earth's pole is described by two components, one in obliquity, the tilt of the equator to the ecliptic, and one in longitude, the position of the equinox along the ecliptic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because of it the equinox does not move along the ecliptic at a uniform speed, and the obliquity of the ecliptic rises and falls quasi-periodically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The largest term depends on the nodes of the Moon's orbit, which regress around the ecliptic every 18.6 years, so to a first approximation both components repeat on that period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its amplitude is about 17.2 arcseconds in longitude and about 9.2 arcseconds in obliquity, tiny beside the tilt of about 23.4° itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since 1 January 2003 the International Astronomical Union has recommended the precession–nutation model IAU 2000A for users who need the pole's motion to about 0.2 milliarcseconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Comparisons with radio observations made by very long baseline interferometry still show leftover wobbles with amplitudes as high as about 0.3 milliarcseconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The main residual is the free core nutation of the Earth's liquid core, with a period of about 430 sidereal days, which behaves irregularly and cannot be predicted far ahead, so it is tracked from observations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jeremy Tatum, <i>Celestial Mechanics</i>, §6.8, “Nutation” (LibreTexts Physics, University of Victoria), https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Astronomy__Cosmology/Celestial_Mechanics_(Tatum)/06%3A_The_Celestial_Sphere/6.08%3A_Nutation. [Open access]","Jeremy Tatum, <i>Celestial Mechanics</i>, §6.7, “Precession” (LibreTexts Physics, University of Victoria), https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Astronomy__Cosmology/Celestial_Mechanics_(Tatum)/06%3A_The_Celestial_Sphere/6.07%3A_Precession. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","IERS Conventions Centre, “Transformation between the International Terrestrial Reference System and the Geocentric Celestial Reference System,” chapter 5 of <i>IERS Conventions (2010)</i>, updated 10 August 2012, https://iers-conventions.obspm.fr/conventions/content/chapter5/icc5.pdf. [Open access]","Zinovy Malkin, “Empiric Models of the Earth’s Free Core Nutation,” <i>Solar System Research</i> 41, no. 6 (2007): 492–97, https://doi.org/10.1134/S0038094607060044. [Paywalled]","David R. Williams, “Moon Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 11 January 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/moonfact.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the main nutation repeat every 18.6 years?","a":"Because its largest term depends on the nodes of the Moon's orbit, which regress around the ecliptic in 18.6 years, changing how the Moon pulls on the Earth's equatorial bulge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Earth's nodding not the same as a top's?","a":"Because a top's free nodding is damped out in a flexible body like the Earth; the Earth's nutation is forced instead, by the changing torques of the Sun and Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why must the free core nutation be observed rather than calculated?","a":"Because it depends on poorly known properties of the Earth's core and behaves irregularly, so it cannot be predicted theoretically far ahead at the accuracy needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nutation"},
    "astro-046": {"questions":["A faint star photographed in 1916 shifting about 10.3 arcseconds a year almost due north still holds the largest known <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, crossing a full Moon's width in about 200 years.","Gaia's third data release lists parallaxes and two-component <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for about 1.5 billion sources, measured from space by a satellite that scans the whole sky again and again."],"abstract":"<b>Proper motion</b> is the projection of a star's motion through space onto the celestial sphere, the part of its velocity relative to the Solar System that runs across the line of sight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Star catalogues usually tabulate it as changes in right ascension and declination per year or per century, and it is quoted in arcseconds per year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It cannot be found from a spectrum, which reveals only radial velocity, the motion towards or away from the observer, and the two together describe a star's velocity through space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Since proper motion is an angle, a nearby star shows a larger yearly shift than a distant one moving across the line of sight at the same speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The shifts are tiny: even the star with the largest proper motion takes about 200 years to move by the width of the full Moon, so no change is noticed with the naked eye in a lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Stellar motions were first discovered in the 18th century by Edmond Halley, who compared the star catalogues of his day with the one compiled by Hipparchus some two thousand years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1916 photographs revealed a faint star of the 11th photographic magnitude moving about 10.3 arcseconds a year almost due north, now called Barnard's star, which at about six light-years is the closest single star to the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Modern measurements come from space, where the Gaia satellite of the European Space Agency has scanned the whole sky repeatedly since it began scientific operations in July 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its third data release, based on 34 months of observations, gives parallaxes and proper motions for about 1.5 billion sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Combined with distances and radial velocities, such measurements let astronomers reconstruct the past paths of stars through the Milky Way and project them millions of years ahead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §17.4, “Using Spectra to Measure Stellar Radius, Composition, and Motion” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/17-4-using-spectra-to-measure-stellar-radius-composition-and-motion. [Open access]","E. E. Barnard, “A Small Star with Large Proper-Motion,” <i>The Astronomical Journal</i> 29 (1916): 181, https://articles.adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1916AJ.....29..181B. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Two Million Stars on the Move,” 21 April 2017, https://www.esa.int/About_Us/ESAC/Two_million_stars_on_the_move. [Open access]","A. Vallenari, A. G. A. Brown, T. Prusti, et al., for the Gaia Collaboration, “<i>Gaia</i> Data Release 3: Summary of the Content and Survey Properties,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 674 (2023): A1, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202243940. [Open access]","European Southern Observatory, “Scientists Discover Planet Orbiting Closest Single Star to Our Sun,” press release eso2414, 1 October 2024, https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso2414/. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “The Future of the Orion Constellation,” 9 June 2017, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Gaia/The_future_of_the_Orion_constellation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a spectrum not reveal proper motion?","a":"Because a spectrum measures only motion along the line of sight, the radial velocity; motion across the sky changes a star's position rather than its spectrum, so it is found by comparing positions over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do nearby stars tend to show larger proper motions?","a":"Because proper motion is an angle: two stars moving across our line of sight at the same speed sweep out different angles, and the closer one shifts more each year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Halley compare his positions with an ancient catalogue?","a":"Because the shifts are too small to see over a human lifetime with the naked eye, so a gap of about two thousand years, back to Hipparchus, made them stand out; today a few years of precise measurement suffice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Proper motion"},
    "astro-047": {"questions":["Some 50,000 years from now the handle of the Big Dipper will be visibly more bent, one sign that the so-called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are not fixed at all over deep time.","A video built from Gaia and Hipparcos measurements shows Orion rearranged over 450,000 years, with Betelgeuse leaving the field, revealing how the patterns of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are only temporary."],"abstract":"The <b>fixed stars</b> were, for ancient astronomers, the stars that keep the same patterns among themselves through many generations, as opposed to the wandering stars, or planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over a single night even the planets seem fixed relative to the stars, and only from day to day do the Sun, the Moon and the planets visibly shift among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stars are in fact moving through the Milky Way, but their changes of position are too small and slow to be noticed with the naked eye over a human lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each star's shift across the sky, its proper motion, is measured in arcseconds per year, and even the fastest takes about 200 years to move the width of the full Moon, though the Gaia satellite now measures it for about 1.5 billion sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The whole sky also changes for a second reason, precession, which slowly turns the Earth's axis in a cycle of about 25,800 years and so changes which star lies near the celestial pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Around 2500 BCE, when the pyramids were built, the pole lay near Thuban, Alpha Draconis, rather than Polaris, so the sky turned around a different point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over tens of thousands of years proper motions reshape the patterns themselves: some 50,000 years from now the handle of the Big Dipper will be unmistakably more bent than it is now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A video based on measurements by the Gaia and Hipparcos satellites projects the sky around Orion over the next 450,000 years, during which the constellation's brightest stars are rearranged into a new pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In it Betelgeuse moves out of the field in about 100,000 years and the Hyades cluster of Taurus drifts across the frame, revealing how ephemeral constellations are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Such projections are linear extrapolations of measured velocities, a reasonable first approximation over a few million years, and a companion video carries two million stars five million years ahead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §2.1, “The Sky Above” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §17.4, “Using Spectra to Measure Stellar Radius, Composition, and Motion” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/17-4-using-spectra-to-measure-stellar-radius-composition-and-motion. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “The Future of the Orion Constellation,” 9 June 2017, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Gaia/The_future_of_the_Orion_constellation. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Two Million Stars on the Move,” 21 April 2017, https://www.esa.int/About_Us/ESAC/Two_million_stars_on_the_move. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “The Seasons and the Earth’s Orbit,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/seasons_orbit. [Open access]","NASA Science Editorial Team, “Milankovitch (Orbital) Cycles and Their Role in Earth’s Climate,” NASA Science, 27 February 2020, https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/earth-science/milankovitch-orbital-cycles-and-their-role-in-earths-climate/. [Open access]","A. Vallenari, A. G. A. Brown, T. Prusti, et al., for the Gaia Collaboration, “<i>Gaia</i> Data Release 3: Summary of the Content and Survey Properties,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 674 (2023): A1, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202243940. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did ancient astronomers call the stars fixed?","a":"Because over many generations the stars kept the same patterns among themselves, while the Sun, the Moon and the planets moved slowly among them from day to day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why will the Big Dipper change shape?","a":"Because its stars have proper motions of their own, so over tens of thousands of years they drift on the sky; in about 50,000 years its handle will be clearly more bent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are such future skies only projections?","a":"Because they extend today's measured velocities in straight lines, a reasonable first approximation over millions of years; longer spans need simulations that follow the gravity the stars actually feel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Fixed stars"},
    "astro-048": {"questions":["Almanacs time each principal <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the difference between the Moon's and the Sun's ecliptic longitudes, setting first quarter at 90° and full Moon at 180°.","A common mistake blames each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the Earth's shadow, but that shadow falls on the Moon only during a lunar eclipse, while the cycle of shapes repeats every 29.5 days."],"abstract":"A <b>lunar phase</b> is one of the recurring apparent forms of the Moon, which shines only by reflected sunlight and so shows more or less of its lit side as it orbits the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Like the Earth, the Moon is a sphere always half lit by the Sun, and the phase depends on how much of that lit half is turned towards us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eight phases are traditionally named, in order new Moon, waxing crescent, first quarter, waxing gibbous, full Moon, waning gibbous, last or third quarter and waning crescent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The principal phases are defined as the moments when the Moon's apparent ecliptic longitude exceeds the Sun's by 0°, 90°, 180° and 270°, which is how almanacs time them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The whole cycle repeats about every 29.5 days, and each complete cycle is called a lunation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> At new Moon the Moon lies in the same direction as the Sun with its dark side towards us, and it rises and sets with the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As it moves eastward by about 12° a day, a thin crescent usually becomes visible about a day after new Moon, its width set by the Moon's angular distance from the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> About a week after new Moon it reaches first quarter, rising around noon and setting around midnight, and at full Moon it stands opposite the Sun, rising at sunset and setting at sunrise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many people wrongly think the phases are caused by the Earth's shadow, but that shadow reaches the Moon only during a lunar eclipse, and it misses the Moon most months because the Moon's orbit is tilted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the Moon is a crescent its dark part often glows faintly with earthshine, sunlight reflected onto it from an Earth that looks nearly full from the Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §4.5, “Phases and Motions of the Moon” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-5-phases-and-motions-of-the-moon. [Open access]","Tracy Vogel, “Moon Phases,” NASA Science, updated 3 August 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moon-phases/. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Phases of the Moon and Percent of the Moon Illuminated,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/moon_phases. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Ernie Wright, “Moon Phase and Libration, 2025,” NASA Scientific Visualization Studio, 22 November 2024, https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/5415/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipses and the Moon,” https://science.nasa.gov/moon/eclipses/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Moonlight,” https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moonlight/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Moon Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 11 January 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/moonfact.html. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Crescent Moon Visibility,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/crescent. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a first-quarter Moon half lit rather than a quarter?","a":"Because the name counts the Moon's progress around its orbit: at first quarter it is a quarter of the way round from new, and we see half of its lit hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the full Moon rise at sunset?","a":"Because at full phase the Moon stands opposite the Sun in the sky, so it comes up as the Sun goes down and is highest around midnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the dark part of a crescent Moon sometimes be seen?","a":"Because sunlight reflected from the Earth, which looks nearly full from the Moon at that time, dimly lights the Moon's night side; this glow is called earthshine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lunar phase"},
    "astro-049": {"questions":["Although its mean length in 2000 was 29.530588 days, any single <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can last between about 29.26 and 29.80 days, because the Sun's gravity perturbs the Moon's eccentric orbit.","A calendar month sometimes holds two full Moons, on average every 2.7 years, because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from one new Moon to the next is shorter than most calendar months."],"abstract":"The <b>synodic month</b> is the period between successive new Moons as seen from the centre of the Earth, the time the Moon takes to run through its full cycle of phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its mean length is about 29.531 days, and the days counted from new Moon through one such cycle, or lunation, are called the Moon's age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The sidereal month is different: it is the Moon's period of revolution measured against the background stars, about 27.322 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The difference comes from the Earth's own motion around the Sun, which changes the direction of the sunlight during the month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To return to the same phase, the Moon must make more than a complete turn around the moving Earth, which takes a little over two days more than a sidereal month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Against the stars the Moon creeps visibly eastward, covering its own width in a little under an hour, and its rising is delayed by about 50 minutes a day on average.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figure of 29.53 days is an average: for the year 2000 the mean synodic month was 29.530588 days, or 29 days, 12 hours, 44 minutes and 3 seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Any one synodic month can be as short as about 29.26 days or as long as about 29.80 days, because the Sun's gravity perturbs the Moon's eccentric orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the cycle of phases is shorter than most calendar months, a phase seen at the start of a month usually recurs at its end, and a second full Moon in one month comes on average every 2.7 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some calendars still follow the synodic month directly: in the Islamic calendar a month begins with the first sighting of the thin crescent after new Moon, which usually comes about a day after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §4.5, “Phases and Motions of the Moon” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-5-phases-and-motions-of-the-moon. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Six Millennium Catalog of Phases of the Moon,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/phase/phasecat.html. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Moon Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 11 January 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/moonfact.html. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Phases of the Moon and Percent of the Moon Illuminated,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/moon_phases. [Open access]","Tracy Vogel, “Moon Phases,” NASA Science, updated 3 August 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moon-phases/. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Crescent Moon Visibility,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/crescent. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the synodic month longer than the sidereal month?","a":"Because while the Moon circles the Earth, the Earth moves along its orbit, so the Moon has to travel more than one full turn to line up with the Sun again in the same phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do individual synodic months differ in length?","a":"Because the Moon's orbit is eccentric and the Sun's gravity perturbs it, so a single month ranges from about 29.26 to about 29.80 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can one calendar month contain two full Moons?","a":"Because a cycle of phases lasts about 29.5 days, shorter than most calendar months, so a full Moon at a month's start can be followed by another before it ends, on average every 2.7 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-050": {"questions":["Because their orbits lie in nearly one plane, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are always found within a belt about 18° wide centred on the ecliptic, the band called the zodiac.","Uranus, faintly visible to the eye but first recognised as a planet with a telescope in 1781, is not counted among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that ancient astronomers watched wander."],"abstract":"The <b>classical planets</b> are the seven bodies that ancient observers saw moving among the stars: the Sun, the Moon, Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek astronomers of about 2,000 years ago distinguished these wandering stars from the fixed stars, and the word 'planet' comes from the Greek for ‘wanderer’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Today the Sun and Moon are not regarded as planets and the Solar System's eight planets include the Earth, but the ancients applied the term to all seven moving objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The seven also named the days of the week, from Sunday and Monday to Saturday, with the others named in English after Norse equivalents of the Roman gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Uranus is just visible to the naked eye, yet it was first recognised as a planet only with a telescope, in 1781, and so is not one of the seven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Over a single night the planets seem fixed among the stars, rising and setting with them, but from day to day they slowly change position against the background.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their usual drift is from west to east, the direction astronomers call direct motion, which results from the combined motions of the planet and the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the orbits of the planets, and of the Moon, lie in nearly the same plane, their paths stay close to the ecliptic, the Sun's yearly path around the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are therefore always found within a belt about 18° wide centred on the ecliptic, the zodiac, whose star patterns reminded the ancients of animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their speeds differ greatly: Mercury orbits the Sun in about 88 days, Mars in about 687 days and Saturn, the slowest of the naked-eye planets, in about 10,747 days, nearly 29.5 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §2.1, “The Sky Above” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i>, §4.5, “Phases and Motions of the Moon” (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-5-phases-and-motions-of-the-moon. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “The Equation of Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/eqtime. [Open access]","U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Astronomical Almanac Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Planetary Fact Sheet – Metric,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 18 March 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Uranus: Facts,” https://science.nasa.gov/uranus/facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “About the Planets,” https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were these seven bodies called wanderers?","a":"Because, unlike the fixed stars, which keep the same patterns for generations, they slowly changed position against the starry background from day to day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the planets stay near the ecliptic?","a":"Because their orbits, like the Moon's, lie in nearly the same plane as the Earth's, so seen from Earth they keep within a belt about 18° wide around the Sun's path, the zodiac.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Uranus not a classical planet?","a":"Because, though just visible to the naked eye, it was not recognised as a planet until it was found with a telescope in 1781.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Classical planet"},
    "astro-051": {"questions":["Ptolemy built his epicycles largely to reproduce the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the planets, the loops in which they briefly drift westward against the stars and grow brighter as they do so.","Centred on opposition, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of an outer planet traces a loop through the constellations, as Mars did through Gemini and Cancer between September 2024 and May 2025."],"abstract":"<b>Retrograde motion</b> is the temporary westward drift of a planet against the background stars, a reversal of the slow eastward progress it usually makes from night to night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the time Mars, for example, sits a little farther east among the constellations each night, but about every two years it spends a couple of months moving from east to west instead, before turning eastward again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The reversal is an illusion of viewpoint rather than a real change of course, much like the backward slip of a slower lorry as a car overtakes it on the road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Observers long before the telescope noticed a second clue, that a planet grows brighter while it retrogrades, and any geometric model of the heavens had to account for both effects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> This backward wandering puzzled early observers and helped give the planets their name, from the Greek word for ‘wanderer’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> For an outer planet, retrograde motion happens whenever the faster-moving Earth, on its smaller orbit, overtakes and laps the planet, while the inner planets retrograde as they in turn overtake Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Photographs stacked so that the stars coincide show Mars tracing a loop, the backward motion strongest at the top of the loop where Earth passed it, and in one such series Uranus is seen doing the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The backward stretch includes opposition, when the planet lies opposite the Sun and close to Earth, so it is then at its brightest and is seen almost all night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Mars traced its 2024–25 loop through Gemini and Cancer and was brightest near its opposition of 16 January 2025, when it was only about 96 million kilometres (60 million miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Ptolemy reproduced the loops with a small circle called an epicycle carried round a larger one, whereas a Sun-centred arrangement, in which the planets simply move at different speeds, explained them without that device.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.2 Ancient Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-2-ancient-astronomy. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.4 The Birth of Modern Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-4-the-birth-of-modern-astronomy. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Plato’s Homework Problem,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, chap. “History and Philosophy of Western Astronomy,” https://www.astronomynotes.com/history/s3.htm. [Open access]","Nick Strobel, “Renaissance,” in <i>Astronomy Notes</i>, chap. “History and Philosophy of Western Astronomy,” https://www.astronomynotes.com/history/s4.htm. [Open access]","R. Nave, “Retrograde Motion of Mars,” HyperPhysics, Department of Physics and Astronomy, Georgia State University, https://hyperphysics.gsu.edu/hbase/solar/retromars.html. [Open access]","Robert Nemiroff and Jerry Bonnell, eds., “Retrograde Mars,” Astronomy Picture of the Day, NASA, 16 December 2003, https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap031216.html. [Open access]","Robert Nemiroff and Jerry Bonnell, eds., “Mars in the Loop,” Astronomy Picture of the Day, NASA, 30 May 2025, https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap250530.html. [Open access]","National Astronomical Observatory of Japan, Ephemeris Computation Office, “Glossary on Celestial Phenomena,” Koyomi FAQ, https://eco.mtk.nao.ac.jp/koyomi/faq/phenomena.html.en. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a planet sometimes seem to go backwards?","a":"Because Earth is moving too. Earth travels on a smaller, faster orbit than Mars, Jupiter or Saturn, so it regularly catches up with and overtakes them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> While it does, the line of sight from Earth to the planet swings back across the distant stars, the same effect as a slower vehicle seeming to slip backwards as you pass it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The backward motion is an optical illusion, and the planet never reverses in its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do planets look brighter while they are retrograding?","a":"Because the backward stretch is centred on opposition, the time when Earth lies between the Sun and the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At opposition an outer planet is near its closest to Earth, so it shines at its brightest and stays up almost all night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Early observers saw the brightening without knowing its cause, and it became one of the clues any model of the planets had to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did retrograde motion matter in the argument over a Sun-centred system?","a":"Because the two systems explained it very differently. Ptolemy needed an epicycle, a small circle carried round a larger one, to make each planet loop backwards at the right time and speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a Sun-centred system the loops follow simply from the planets moving at different speeds, which made it a far more economical explanation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Retrograde and prograde motion"},
    "astro-052": {"questions":["Jupiter and Saturn come together in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about every 20 years, and in December 2020 they appeared only a tenth of a degree apart in the evening sky.","An outer planet in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the Sun shares the Sun's direction and is lost in its glare, the reverse of opposition, when it shines all night long."],"abstract":"A <b>conjunction</b> is the moment when two bodies in the sky line up as seen from Earth, sharing the same ecliptic longitude or, by another convention, the same right ascension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word is most often used of a planet and the Sun, and it then means that the geocentric longitude of the planet equals that of the Sun, so that the planet lies in the same direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Conjunctions between two planets, or between the Moon and a planet or bright star, are also common, though the moment of conjunction is not quite the moment of closest approach, which has its own name, appulse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The alignment is only one of direction, and planets in conjunction remain hundreds of millions of kilometres apart in space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Jupiter and Saturn line up about once every 20 years, and on 21 December 2020 they appeared just a tenth of a degree apart, closer than they had been for nearly 400 years, and its falling on the day of the winter solstice was mere coincidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Mercury and Venus have two kinds of conjunction with the Sun: inferior, when the planet passes between Earth and the Sun, and superior, when it lies on the far side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An outer planet can only reach superior conjunction, on the far side of the Sun, and at that time it is invisible in the Sun's glare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its counterpart is opposition, when the planet's longitude differs from the Sun's by 180° and it lies in exactly the opposite direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At opposition an outer planet is near its closest to Earth, at its brightest and visible almost all night, which makes it the best time to observe it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The interval from one opposition to the next is the planet's synodic period, about 2.135 years for Mars, and it is in a short stretch around each opposition that Mars moves backwards against the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["National Astronomical Observatory of Japan, Ephemeris Computation Office, “Glossary on Celestial Phenomena,” Koyomi FAQ, https://eco.mtk.nao.ac.jp/koyomi/faq/phenomena.html.en. [Open access]","Hong Kong Observatory, <i>Planetary Phenomena for 2022</i> (Hong Kong: Hong Kong Observatory, 2022), https://www.hko.gov.hk/en/gts/astron2022/files/2022planetary.pdf. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Astronomical Glossary,” AstroPixels, https://astropixels.com/main/glossary.html. [Open access]","Richard Fitzpatrick, “Determination of Conjunction and Greatest Elongation Dates,” in <i>A Modern Almagest: An Updated Version of Ptolemy’s Model of the Solar System</i> (Austin: University of Texas at Austin), https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/Books/Syntaxis/Almagest/node59.html. [Open access]","Bill Keeter, “The ‘Great’ Conjunction of Jupiter and Saturn,” NASA, 15 December 2020, https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/the-great-conjunction-of-jupiter-and-saturn/. [Open access]","R. Nave, “Retrograde Motion of Mars,” HyperPhysics, Department of Physics and Astronomy, Georgia State University, https://hyperphysics.gsu.edu/hbase/solar/retromars.html. [Open access]","Robert Nemiroff and Jerry Bonnell, eds., “Jupiter Meets Saturn: A Red Spotted Great Conjunction,” Astronomy Picture of the Day, NASA, 23 December 2020, https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap201223.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can an outer planet never have an inferior conjunction?","a":"Because its orbit lies outside Earth's, so it can never come between Earth and the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The only way it can line up with the Sun is by being on the far side, and that is a superior conjunction, usually just called conjunction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At that time the planet is lost in the Sun's glare and cannot be seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is a conjunction not the same as the closest approach?","a":"Because they are defined differently. A conjunction is the instant two bodies share the same ecliptic longitude or right ascension, while the closest approach, called an appulse, is the instant their separation is smallest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two moments are usually near each other but need not coincide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do astronomers look forward to an outer planet's opposition?","a":"Because it is the best time to see it. At opposition the planet lies opposite the Sun, so it rises at sunset and is visible for almost the whole night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also near its closest to Earth and at its brightest, which suits both telescopes and the naked eye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Conjunction (astronomy)"},
    "astro-053": {"questions":["At greatest eastern <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Venus stands farthest east of the Sun and shines in the western sky after sunset, the 'evening star' of popular speech.","The angle between a planet and the Sun as seen from Earth, its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, can reach 180° for Mars at opposition but never more than about 47° for Venus."],"abstract":"<b>Elongation</b> is the angle between a planet and the Sun as seen from Earth, and it changes continuously as Earth and the planet move round their orbits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An outer planet can stand opposite the Sun, 180° away, but Mercury and Venus, whose orbits lie inside Earth's, are always seen fairly close to the Sun's position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The moment an inner planet reaches its largest angle is called greatest elongation, eastern when it lies east of the Sun and western when it lies west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Greatest elongation ranges from about 18° to 28° for Mercury and from about 45° to 47° for Venus, varying because the orbits are ellipses rather than circles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Averaged over many cycles in a modern reworking of Ptolemy's model, the figure is about 22° for Mercury and about 46° for Venus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Around greatest eastern elongation an inner planet can be seen in the western sky after sunset, and around greatest western elongation in the eastern sky before sunrise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These are the best times to observe Mercury and Venus, and between them each planet passes through inferior conjunction, between Earth and the Sun, and superior conjunction, on the far side of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That alternation is why Venus, like Mercury, appears sometimes as an 'evening star' in the west after sunset and sometimes as a 'morning star' in the east before dawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Venus follows a nearly circular orbit about 0.72 AU from the Sun, which is why its greatest elongations are both larger and steadier than Mercury's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As its elongation changes, a telescope shows Venus going through a full range of phases like the Moon, an observation that Ptolemy's Earth-centred model could not reproduce and that showed Venus must orbit the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["National Astronomical Observatory of Japan, Ephemeris Computation Office, “Glossary on Celestial Phenomena,” Koyomi FAQ, https://eco.mtk.nao.ac.jp/koyomi/faq/phenomena.html.en. [Open access]","Hong Kong Observatory, <i>Planetary Phenomena for 2022</i> (Hong Kong: Hong Kong Observatory, 2022), https://www.hko.gov.hk/en/gts/astron2022/files/2022planetary.pdf. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Astronomical Glossary,” AstroPixels, https://astropixels.com/main/glossary.html. [Open access]","Richard Fitzpatrick, “Determination of Conjunction and Greatest Elongation Dates,” in <i>A Modern Almagest: An Updated Version of Ptolemy’s Model of the Solar System</i> (Austin: University of Texas at Austin), https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/Books/Syntaxis/Almagest/node59.html. [Open access]","Richard Fitzpatrick, “Mercury,” in <i>A Modern Almagest: An Updated Version of Ptolemy’s Model of the Solar System</i> (Austin: University of Texas at Austin), https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/Books/Syntaxis/Almagest/node60.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.1 The Nearest Planets: An Overview,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-1-the-nearest-planets-an-overview. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.4 The Birth of Modern Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-4-the-birth-of-modern-astronomy. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Mercury's greatest elongation vary so much?","a":"Because the orbits are ellipses rather than circles. Mercury's greatest elongation ranges from about 18° to 28°, depending on where the planet and Earth are in their orbits when it happens, while Venus, on a nearly circular orbit, varies only from about 45° to 47°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Averaged over many cycles Mercury's figure is about 22°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is greatest elongation the best time to look for Mercury or Venus?","a":"Because the planet is then as far from the Sun in the sky as it ever gets, which lifts it out of the glare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After greatest eastern elongation it can be seen in the western sky after sunset, and around greatest western elongation in the eastern sky before sunrise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Venus called both the evening star and the morning star?","a":"Because it swings from one side of the Sun to the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> East of the Sun it sets after the Sun and is seen in the evening; west of the Sun it rises before the Sun and is seen at dawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mercury does the same, in a much tighter swing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Elongation (astronomy)"},
    "astro-054": {"questions":["Expeditions sailed across the globe in 1761 and 1769 to time the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Venus, hoping to turn it into a measurement of the distance to the Sun.","After the pair of 2004 and 2012, the next <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Venus across the Sun's disc will not come until December 2117, more than a century later."],"abstract":"A <b>transit</b> is the passage of a planet across the bright disc of the Sun, which may be thought of as a special kind of eclipse, though transits are far rarer than eclipses of the Sun by the Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During a transit the planet shows as a small black dot crossing the Sun's face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As seen from Earth only Mercury and Venus can transit, because only their orbits lie inside Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A transit happens only when the planet reaches inferior conjunction, passing between Earth and the Sun, at the same time as it crosses the plane of Earth's orbit, the ecliptic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Mercury transits about 13 times a century, at present always within a few days of 8 May or 10 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The transit of Mercury on 11 November 2019 took about five and a half hours, Mercury looking like a small sunspot creeping across the disc, while the one before was in 2016 and the next falls in 2032.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Transits of Venus usually come in pairs eight years apart, with more than a century between pairs, as in 1761 and 1769, 1874 and 1882, and 2004 and 2012, and the first ever seen was in 1639.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their value was set out in 1716 by Edmond Halley, who saw that observers far apart on Earth could time the same transit and use their separation as a baseline to find the distance to the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Expeditions went out across the globe in 1761 and 1769, but the hundreds of reports fell short of the precision hoped for, the pair of 1874 and 1882 brought photography and telegraphy to the task, and radar now measures the distance far better.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same geometry now finds planets around other stars, which betray themselves by the slight, regular dimming of their star each time they cross in front of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Fred Espenak, “Planetary Transits Across the Sun,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/transit/transit.html. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Six Millennium Catalog of Venus Transits: 2000 BCE to 4000 CE,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/transit/catalog/VenusCatalog.html. [Open access]","NASA, “Mercury’s Solar Transit,” image article, 12 November 2019, https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/mercurys-solar-transit/. [Open access]","NOAA Space Weather Prediction Center, “Mercury Transits the Sun on Monday, November 11th, 2019,” 8 November 2019, https://www.spaceweather.gov/news/mercury-transits-sun-monday-november-11th-2019. [Open access]","History of Science Museum, University of Oxford, “Transits of Venus: Introduction,” https://transits.mhs.ox.ac.uk/intro/index.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “21.4 Planets beyond the Solar System: Search and Discovery,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-4-planets-beyond-the-solar-system-search-and-discovery. [Open access]","National Astronomical Observatory of Japan, Ephemeris Computation Office, “Glossary on Celestial Phenomena,” Koyomi FAQ, https://eco.mtk.nao.ac.jp/koyomi/faq/phenomena.html.en. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are transits of Venus so rare?","a":"Because two conditions must coincide. Venus has to be at inferior conjunction, passing between Earth and the Sun, at the same time as it crosses the plane of Earth's orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That happens in pairs eight years apart, and then not again for more than a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 2004 and 2012, the next are in December 2117 and December 2125.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did 18th-century astronomers care so much about transits of Venus?","a":"Because a transit could measure the size of the Solar System. Observers far apart on Earth would see Venus cross the Sun along slightly different lines, and with their separation as a baseline the distance to the Sun could be worked out, a method published in 1716.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Expeditions went out for 1761 and 1769, though the results were less precise than hoped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the idea of a transit help find planets around other stars?","a":"Because a planet crossing in front of its star blocks a little of the starlight, causing a small, regular dip in brightness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The depth of the dip depends on the planet's size compared with the star, so if the star's size is known, the transit gives the size of the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Astronomical transit"},
    "astro-055": {"questions":["In March 1977 a star blinked several times before and after passing behind Uranus, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that revealed at least five rings around the planet.","Observers spread across the narrow shadow track of an asteroid's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of a distant star can combine their timings into an outline of the asteroid's shape."],"abstract":"An <b>occultation</b> happens when a body in the Solar System passes in front of a more distant object, partly or wholly hiding it and briefly cutting off its light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strictly speaking a solar eclipse is an occultation, of the Sun by the Moon, and the Moon passing in front of a star is the most familiar kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each occultation can be seen only at the right time and from a limited part of the Earth, because the nearer body's shadow sweeps across the planet along a track.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A star covered by the Moon disappears almost instantly, whereas a planet, which shows a disc, fades gradually over a few seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When a star skims the Moon's edge it can vanish and reappear several times behind lunar mountains, a grazing occultation that teams of observers time to map the Moon's profile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Because the moment of disappearance can be timed precisely, a star that fades in two steps rather than one can be shown to be double, and several double stars have been found this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When observers spread across an asteroid's shadow track time a star's disappearance, their measurements together outline the asteroid's shape, information otherwise hard to get without visiting it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 10 March 1977 five brief dimmings of a star before and after it passed behind Uranus showed that at least five rings encircle the planet, a discovery made with a telescope carried aloft in an aircraft, and later occultations charted ten narrow rings in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 9 June 1988 the slow, gradual fading of a star behind Pluto showed that its light was being bent by an extended atmosphere around the dwarf planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 17 July 2017 mobile telescopes in Argentina caught the distant object later named Arrokoth briefly blocking a star, data that helped measure it before the New Horizons flyby of 1 January 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Occultation Timing Association, “What Is an Occultation?,” https://occultations.org/occultations/what-is-an-occultation/. [Open access]","Astronomical Society of Southern Africa, “Observing Occultations,” https://assa.saao.ac.za/how-to-observe/occultations/. [Open access]","J. L. Elliot, E. Dunham, and D. Mink, “The Rings of Uranus,” <i>Nature</i> 267, no. 5609 (1977): 328–30, https://doi.org/10.1038/267328a0. [Paywalled]","W. B. Hubbard, D. M. Hunten, S. W. Dieters, K. M. Hill, and R. D. Watson, “Occultation Evidence for an Atmosphere on Pluto,” <i>Nature</i> 336, no. 6198 (1988): 452–54, https://doi.org/10.1038/336452a0. [Paywalled]","NASA, Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory, and Southwest Research Institute, “PIA21865: Wink of a Star,” 19 July 2017, PDS Ring-Moon Systems Node, https://pds-rings.seti.org/press_releases/pages/PIA21xxx/PIA21865.html. [Open access]","Richard G. French, Colleen A. McGhee-French, and Mitchell K. Gordon, <i>Earth-Based Uranus Stellar Occultations User Guide</i>, version 1.0 (Planetary Data System Ring-Moon Systems Node, 2020), https://pds.nasa.gov/data/pds4/releases/rings/docs-20220124/earth-based-uranus-stellar-occultation-user-guide.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a given occultation be seen only from part of the Earth?","a":"Because the nearer body casts a kind of shadow in the starlight, and only observers inside it see the star hidden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For an asteroid that shadow is about the size of the asteroid itself and moves across the Earth along a narrow track, so where to stand matters a great deal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a star vanish at once behind the Moon while a planet fades?","a":"Because a star is so small in the sky that it is covered in an instant, while a planet shows a disc that the Moon's edge takes a few seconds to cross.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A star that fades in two steps instead of one gives itself away as a double star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a star's flickering near Uranus reveal rings?","a":"Because the star dimmed briefly five times before it passed behind Uranus and again afterwards, both sides of the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Something narrow and dark had to be crossing in front of the star on each side, and the simplest explanation was a set of rings encircling the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Occultation"},
    "astro-056": {"questions":["In the <i>Almagest</i> of around 150 CE, Ptolemy listed just over a thousand stars, each placed in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> such as Orion, the hunter of Greek myth.","To the International Astronomical Union a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is no longer a figure of stars but an area of sky enclosed by its boundary, so anything inside lies in it."],"abstract":"A <b>constellation</b> was originally a pattern of prominent stars in the night sky, linked by the people who named it to a figure from their myths or daily life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To find their way among the multitude of stars, the ancient Chinese, Egyptians and Greeks, among others, each found their own groupings, so different cultures have had different names and numbers of constellations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A study of 27 cultures found that some groupings, among them Orion, the Big Dipper, the Pleiades and the Southern Cross, recur around the world, and that simple rules of visual grouping account for many of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The stars of a pattern only look close together, since some may be near while others are very far away, at greatly varying distances from Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Many northern constellations go back to Babylonian and Greek patterns, and the Greek tradition was fixed in Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i> of around 150 CE, whose catalogue arranges just over a thousand stars into 48 constellations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The set of figures also changed: the stars of the Southern Cross were the hind legs of the Centaur in antiquity and were first used as a separate cross in the early 1500s, and new constellations were still being added in the 17th and 18th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Since the early 20th century astronomers have used the word in a stricter sense, for one of 88 internationally defined areas of the sky rather than for a picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The International Astronomical Union defines a constellation as an area surrounded by its boundary, and the boundary coordinates, not the figure, are what define it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When astronomers say an object is in a constellation, they mean only that it lies within those borders, so the modern Orion is a box on the sky holding the old hunter's stars and much else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many cultures keep their own star groupings in use, so the official scheme is a standard for astronomy rather than the only way to read the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","International Astronomical Union, “Constellations,” Astronomy FAQs, https://www.iau.org/IAU/Astronomy-FAQs/Constellations.aspx. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Astronomical Glossary,” AstroPixels, https://astropixels.com/main/glossary.html. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Ptolemy’s Almagest,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/almagest.html. [Open access]","Charles Kemp, Duane W. Hamacher, Daniel R. Little, and Simon J. Cropper, “Perceptual Grouping Explains Similarities in Constellations across Cultures,” <i>Psychological Science</i> 33, no. 3 (2022): 354–63, https://doi.org/10.1177/09567976211044157. [Paywalled]","Fred Espenak, “The Constellations,” AstroPixels, https://astropixels.com/constellations/constellationslist.html. [Open access]","NASA Space Place, “What Are Constellations?,” NASA Science, https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/constellations/en/. [Open access]","NSF NOIRLab, “Crux,” 88 Constellations, https://noirlab.edu/public/education/constellations/crux/. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Star Tales, Chapter 1, Part 4,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/startales1d.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do unrelated cultures often pick out the same star groups?","a":"Because human vision groups points of light in similar ways everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A comparison of 27 cultures found that patterns such as Orion, the Big Dipper, the Pleiades and the Southern Cross recur widely, and that a simple model of perceptual grouping explains many of the shared ones, while each culture also departs from that baseline in its own way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do constellations seldom look like the figures they are named after?","a":"Because most were never pictures in the first place. The ancients mostly picked out groupings that made a familiar geometric pattern, and only more rarely ones that resembled something they knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The names and stories were attached to patterns, not drawn from them, which is why readers are often puzzled by the mismatch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did astronomers turn constellations into areas of sky?","a":"Because science needs every point of the sky to have one address.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Defining each constellation by a boundary means any object, however faint, falls in exactly one of them, whereas a figure of stars leaves most of the sky unassigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Constellation"},
    "astro-057": {"questions":["Hydra is the largest and the Southern Cross the smallest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which fit together like the states of a country so that every point of sky lies in one.","Ptolemy's 48 Greek figures were joined by southern patterns charted in the 17th and 18th centuries before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were fixed as one official list covering the whole sky."],"abstract":"The <b>88 modern constellations</b> are the official list of constellations recognised by the International Astronomical Union, which between them cover the entire sky of both hemispheres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The list was agreed at the Union's first General Assembly, in Rome in 1922, with a three-letter abbreviation for each Latin name, such as UMa for Ursa Major.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The names appear to have been taken from the standard star catalogue of the day, the Revised Harvard Photometry published in 1908, whose own names came from earlier German atlases for the north and an Argentine survey for the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strictly, the 1922 list held 89 names, because it kept the old Greek ship Argo alongside the three parts it had been divided into, Carina, Puppis and Vela.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wherever possible, each modern constellation is named after the Latin translation of an ancient Greek star pattern lying within it, so the modern Orion contains the stars of the old hunter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Many of the northern constellations go back to Babylonian and Greek patterns, and Ptolemy catalogued 48 of them in the 2nd century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Large uncharted areas of the far southern sky were filled with new constellations by 17th- and 18th-century astronomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fourteen of these, named mostly after instruments of science and the arts, came from a survey of nearly 10,000 stars made at the Cape of Good Hope in 1751 and 1752, and seven faint northern ones date from a catalogue of 1687.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The constellations vary enormously in size, from Hydra, the largest at about 1,303 square degrees and stretching some 100° from east to west, to Crux, the smallest at about 68 square degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Like the states of a country, the 88 fit together without gaps or overlaps, so that every point in the sky falls in one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Astronomical Union, “Constellations,” Astronomy FAQs, https://www.iau.org/IAU/Astronomy-FAQs/Constellations.aspx. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “The IAU List of the 88 Constellations and Their Abbreviations,” http://www.ianridpath.com/iaulist1.html. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Star Tales, Chapter 1, Part 4,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/startales1d.html. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Astronomical Glossary,” AstroPixels, https://astropixels.com/main/glossary.html. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “The Constellations,” AstroPixels, https://astropixels.com/constellations/constellationslist.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","NSF NOIRLab, “Hydra,” 88 Constellations, https://noirlab.edu/public/education/constellations/hydra/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the 1922 list contain 89 names rather than 88?","a":"Because it included Argo, the obsolete Greek constellation of the ship, as well as Carina, Puppis and Vela, the three constellations it had been split into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Leaving Argo aside, that list is still the authoritative one, and the IAU recognises 88 constellations today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are so many southern constellations named after scientific instruments?","a":"Because they were invented in the 18th century to fill gaps in the southern sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fourteen came from a survey of nearly 10,000 stars made at the Cape of Good Hope in 1751 and 1752, and in the spirit of the Enlightenment they commemorated instruments of science and the arts rather than heroes of myth, with Mensa, the Table Mountain, as the exception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the constellations have Latin names?","a":"Because the modern scheme was built on the classical tradition. Wherever possible each constellation is named after the Latin translation of a Greek star pattern lying inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Latin names and their three-letter abbreviations were fixed by the International Astronomical Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"IAU designated constellations"},
    "astro-058": {"questions":["Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were fixed, a star could be shared between Taurus and Auriga, and faint stars fell in different constellations on different maps.","Because the Earth's axis precesses, a modern position must be converted back to the coordinates of 1875 to check it against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> published in 1930."],"abstract":"The <b>constellation boundaries</b> are the official borders that divide the sky among the 88 constellations, so that every point belongs to exactly one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They run along lines of right ascension and declination, the sky's equivalents of longitude and latitude, so every stretch of border runs either north–south or east–west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Before they existed, star charts showed borders snaking vaguely between the figures, and these varied from map to map, so a faint star could fall in different constellations on different charts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some stars were even shared, a habit going back to Ptolemy, who placed one star both on the northern horn of Taurus and on the foot of Auriga, and another both at the head of Andromeda and at the navel of Pegasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new scheme ended the practice, giving the first star to Taurus alone as Beta Tauri and leaving Andromeda her head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The 88 names agreed in 1922 still had no defined borders, and in 1925 a proposal from the Royal Observatory in Brussels was put to the International Astronomical Union for boundaries drawn on the coordinate grid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Approved at Leiden in 1928 and published in 1930, the borders were drawn so that every variable star with an established designation stayed in its constellation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were laid out on the coordinates of the year 1875, the date already used for an earlier set of southern boundaries published in 1877.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the Earth's axis slowly precesses, completing a circle in about 26,000 years, the coordinate grid drifts, and a position is converted back to 1875 to tell which constellation holds it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Serpens is the only constellation split in two by the borders, its head and tail lying on either side of Ophiuchus, yet it still counts as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["International Astronomical Union, “Constellations,” Astronomy FAQs, https://www.iau.org/IAU/Astronomy-FAQs/Constellations.aspx. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Constellation Boundaries: How the Modern Constellation Outlines Came to Be,” http://www.ianridpath.com/boundaries.html. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “The IAU List of the 88 Constellations and Their Abbreviations,” http://www.ianridpath.com/iaulist1.html. [Open access]","Nancy G. Roman, “Identification of a Constellation from a Position,” <i>Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific</i> 99 (1987): 695, https://doi.org/10.1086/132034. [Paywalled]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.2 Ancient Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-2-ancient-astronomy. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were official constellation borders needed?","a":"Because the old ones were vague and inconsistent. Charts showed borders snaking between the figures and differing from map to map, so a faint star could belong to different constellations on different charts, and some stars were shared between two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fixed borders give every point of the sky one constellation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the borders no longer follow modern lines of right ascension and declination?","a":"Because they were drawn on the coordinate grid of 1875, and the grid moves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Earth's axis precesses, completing a circle in about 26,000 years, which slowly shifts the celestial coordinates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> So a modern position is first converted back to its 1875 value, and tables exist for finding the constellation from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Serpens come in two pieces?","a":"Because the snake is held by Ophiuchus, and the two figures are entwined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the borders were drawn, the snake was divided into a head on one side of Ophiuchus and a tail on the other, though the two halves still count as a single constellation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-059": {"questions":["Called the Plough in Europe and the Big Dipper in America, the seven bright stars of Ursa Major form an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> within that much larger constellation.","Once a Greek word for any constellation, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now names a popular star pattern that is not one of the official 88, such as the Pleiades cluster in Taurus."],"abstract":"An <b>asterism</b> is a prominent pattern of stars, usually with a popular name, that is not one of the officially recognised constellations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some lie inside a single constellation and others span parts of several, so an asterism may be far smaller than a constellation or stretch across its borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word comes from the Greek 'asterismos', which simply meant ‘constellation’, and the two ideas were not distinguished until the International Astronomical Union defined constellations as areas of sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An asterism can be a handful of bright stars, a star cluster such as the Pleiades in Taurus, or even a single conspicuous star such as Capella, and like a constellation its stars may lie at very different distances from Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A comparison of 27 cultures around the world found that the same groupings, among them the Big Dipper and the Pleiades, recur widely and that basic rules of visual grouping explain many of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The best known is the group of seven bright stars in Ursa Major called the Plough in Europe, the Big Dipper in America, traditionally the Northern Dipper in China and the Wagon in ancient Mesopotamia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Chinese astronomers also knew a Southern Dipper, made of stars in Sagittarius, and saw the Northern Dipper as the emperor's chariot revolving around the pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Summer Triangle, prominent in the summer and autumn skies of the northern hemisphere, joins three bright stars from three different constellations, Altair, Deneb and Vega.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Asterisms have no official boundaries, only line patterns drawn alongside those of the constellations, so astronomers place an object by the constellation whose borders contain it, whatever pattern it seems to belong to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many cultures that never adopted the Greek figures keep their own asterisms in use, and the official scheme does not replace them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","International Astronomical Union, “Constellations,” Astronomy FAQs, https://www.iau.org/IAU/Astronomy-FAQs/Constellations.aspx. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Astronomical Glossary,” AstroPixels, https://astropixels.com/main/glossary.html. [Open access]","Charles Kemp, Duane W. Hamacher, Daniel R. Little, and Simon J. Cropper, “Perceptual Grouping Explains Similarities in Constellations across Cultures,” <i>Psychological Science</i> 33, no. 3 (2022): 354–63, https://doi.org/10.1177/09567976211044157. [Paywalled]","Ian Ridpath, “Ursa Major, Continued,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/ursamajor2.html. [Open access]","NSF NOIRLab, “Ursa Major,” 88 Constellations, https://noirlab.edu/public/education/constellations/ursa-major/. [Open access]","NASA Space Place, “What Are Constellations?,” NASA Science, https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/constellations/en/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the Big Dipper not counted as a constellation?","a":"Because it is only part of one. Its seven stars lie inside Ursa Major, the Great Bear, and make up a noticeable pattern within that larger constellation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Patterns like this are called asterisms and have no official status, however familiar they are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can an asterism cross constellation borders?","a":"Because it is defined by how the stars look, not by the official map. Some asterisms span parts of several constellations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Summer Triangle, for example, joins Altair, Deneb and Vega, each of which belongs to a different constellation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the same asterisms turn up in many cultures?","a":"Because people everywhere tend to group points of light in similar ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A study of 27 cultures found that groupings such as the Big Dipper and the Pleiades recur widely and that a simple model of perceptual grouping accounts for many of them, while each culture also adds patterns of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Asterism (astronomy)"},
    "astro-060": {"questions":["Homer's Bear 'which men also call the Wain', which never bathes in the ocean, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose pointer stars Dubhe and Merak lead the eye to the north celestial pole.","Romans saw its seven bright stars as plough oxen and Arab astronomers as a bier with mourners, but the constellation <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is named in Latin for the animal the Greeks saw."],"abstract":"<b>Ursa Major</b>, the Great Bear, is a large constellation of the northern sky, the third-largest of the 88 at about 1,280 square degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is one of the 48 Greek constellations listed by Ptolemy, and its Greek name in the <i>Almagest</i>, Arktos Megale, means the same as the Latin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its most familiar part is a pattern of seven stars, called the Plough in Europe, the Big Dipper in America and traditionally the Northern Dipper in China, where it was also seen as the emperor's chariot, and the Wagon in ancient Mesopotamia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That pattern is an asterism within the constellation, one recognised in many cultures, and its seven stars form the bear's rump and long tail while fainter stars make up the rest of the animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seen from mid-northern latitudes the bear never sets, and the Big Dipper keeps its shape through the night as it turns with the sky around the pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Homer already describes the Bear 'which men also call the Wain', which circles where it is, watches Orion and alone has no part in the baths of Ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Roman writers called it Septentrio, said to mean ‘seven plough oxen’, and the related word 'septentrional' was commonly used in Latin to mean ‘north’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name Plough is recorded early in the 1st century CE, when a Roman writer remarked that the shape of a plough comes closest to the real shape of the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two stars at the front of the bowl, Alpha and Beta Ursae Majoris, better known as Dubhe and Merak, are called the Pointers, because a line drawn through them points to the north celestial pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Arab astronomers saw the seven not as a bear but as a bier followed by three mourners, the daughters of the dead, and all seven stars still carry names derived from Arabic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Ian Ridpath, “Ursa Major,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/ursamajor.html. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Ursa Major, Continued,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/ursamajor2.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.1 The Sky Above,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","International Astronomical Union, “Constellations,” Astronomy FAQs, https://www.iau.org/IAU/Astronomy-FAQs/Constellations.aspx. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “The Constellations,” AstroPixels, https://astropixels.com/constellations/constellationslist.html. [Open access]","Homer, <i>The Odyssey</i> 5.270–275, trans. A. T. Murray, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1919), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0136%3Abook%3D5%3Acard%3D262. [Open access]","NSF NOIRLab, “Ursa Major,” 88 Constellations, https://noirlab.edu/public/education/constellations/ursa-major/. [Open access]","Charles Kemp, Duane W. Hamacher, Daniel R. Little, and Simon J. Cropper, “Perceptual Grouping Explains Similarities in Constellations across Cultures,” <i>Psychological Science</i> 33, no. 3 (2022): 354–63, https://doi.org/10.1177/09567976211044157. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the Big Dipper not one of the 88 constellations?","a":"Because it is an asterism, a noticeable pattern inside a constellation rather than a constellation itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its seven stars are only the rump and tail of the Great Bear, Ursa Major, whose fainter stars make up the rest of the animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Different cultures have given those seven stars their own names, the Plough, the Big Dipper and the Northern Dipper among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Great Bear so useful to ancient sailors?","a":"Because from mid-northern latitudes it never sets, circling the pole all night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ancient Greeks were said to steer their ships by it, and in the <i>Odyssey</i> the hero watches the Bear, which alone has no part in the baths of Ocean, as he steers his raft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the stars of the Plough have Arabic names?","a":"Because they come from the Arabic astronomical tradition, which saw the seven stars as a bier with three mourners following it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dubhe, for instance, comes from the Arabic for ‘the bear’ and Merak from a word meaning ‘the flank’; all seven names derive from Arabic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ursa Major"},
    "astro-061": {"questions":["Lying astride the celestial equator, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries the red supergiant Betelgeuse on one shoulder and the blue star Rigel at one foot, and a stellar nursery in its sword.","In the <i>Odyssey</i> the Bear circles in its place and keeps watch on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while Odysseus, steering by the stars, keeps the Bear on his left across the sea."],"abstract":"<b>Orion</b> is a constellation of the equatorial sky, one of the 48 Greek constellations listed by Ptolemy and one of the few star patterns that non-astronomers can still recognise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its figure is a hunter: the red star Betelgeuse marks a shoulder, the blue star Rigel a foot, and three stars close together in a short line form his belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The belt stars are Alnitak, Alnilam and Mintaka, whose names come from Arabic words for ‘belt’ and ‘string of pearls’, and the Greeks named neither Betelgeuse nor Rigel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The constellation lies astride the celestial equator, and in the modern scheme it is one of 88 boxes on the sky, holding the old hunter's stars among many others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also among the oldest constellations, known to the earliest Greek writers, and one account traces it to the Sumerians, who called it URU AN-NA, ‘light of heaven’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> For Greek farmers it was a calendar, and Hesiod's <i>Works and Days</i> tells the farmer to set his slaves to winnow the grain when strong Orion first appears, a moment its translator places in July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the <i>Odyssey</i>, Odysseus steers his raft by the Pleiades, Boötes and the Bear, which circles in its place, watches Orion and alone never bathes in Ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Below the belt hangs the sword, in front of a molecular cloud about 1,500 light-years away that holds gas estimated at 200,000 times the mass of the Sun and is still forming stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stars near the middle of the sword are only 300,000 to 1 million years old, against about 5 million years for the belt, and a radio parallax measured directly puts the Orion Nebula Cluster at 414 parsecs, give or take 7.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Betelgeuse, a red supergiant roughly 220 parsecs away, faded sharply between November 2019 and March 2020, and close-up images point to a clump of dust that formed after a cool patch appeared on its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, “The Sky Above,” https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 21.1, “Star Formation,” https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-1-star-formation. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Orion,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/orion.html. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Orion, Part 2,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/orion2.html. [Open access]","Homer, <i>The Odyssey</i> 5.270–277, trans. A. T. Murray, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press; London: William Heinemann, 1919), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0136%3Abook%3D5%3Acard%3D262. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Works and Days</i> 597–599, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press; London: William Heinemann, 1914), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Hes.%20WD%20597. [Open access]","M. Montargès et al., “A Dusty Veil Shading Betelgeuse during Its Great Dimming,” <i>Nature</i> 594, no. 7863 (2021): 365–68, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-03546-8. [Paywalled]","K. M. Menten et al., “The Distance to the Orion Nebula,” <i>Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 474, no. 2 (2007): 515–20, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20078247. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Orion appear in the poems of Homer and Hesiod?","a":"Because it is bright and old: its belt and its two brilliant stars make it easy to recognise, and it was among the few star groups known to the earliest Greek writers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hesiod uses its first appearance as the signal for winnowing grain, and Homer has the Bear keep watch on it while Odysseus steers by the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Orion's sword interesting to astronomers?","a":"Because it lies in front of a nearby molecular cloud where stars are still forming, with gas estimated at about 200,000 times the Sun's mass and stars in the middle of the sword less than a million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its distance has been measured directly by radio parallax, at about 414 parsecs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Betelgeuse dim in 2019 and 2020?","a":"Because, on the best current evidence, a clump of dust formed close to the star and hid part of its surface. High-resolution images showed its southern half ten times darker than usual in visible light, and the dust seems to have condensed after a cool patch appeared on the star's surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Orion (constellation)"},
    "astro-062": {"questions":["With its long axis pointing towards the south celestial pole, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the smallest of the 88 constellations, and its brightest star is Acrux.","For the Kamilaroi and Euahlayi the dark Coalsack inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the head of the Emu in the Sky, whose body stretches along the Milky Way."],"abstract":"The <b>Southern Cross</b>, known to astronomers as Crux, is the smallest of the 88 modern constellations, a compact group of four bright stars and a fainter fifth set in the Milky Way of the far southern sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its brightest star, Acrux, is the most southerly first-magnitude star, about 63 degrees south of the celestial equator, and within the cross lies the Coalsack, a dark cloud of dust seen in silhouette against the Milky Way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it lies so close to the south celestial pole, most observers in the United States never see it, for it sits in the zone of their sky that never rises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ptolemy catalogued its stars, but as the hind legs of Centaurus, and precession, the slow drift of the celestial pole against the stars, later carried them below the horizon of European observers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Seafarers venturing south in the 15th and 16th centuries found the stars again, and astronomers adopted the cross as a separate constellation by the end of the 16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Navigators valued it because its long axis points towards the south celestial pole, which no bright star marks, while the two bright Pointers, Alpha and Beta Centauri, lead the eye to the cross itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A Portuguese treatise on the compass of 1514 already gave the distances of its five stars from the pole as a guide to finding latitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hawaiian wayfinders call it Hanaiakamalama, ‘cared for by the moon’, and when it stands upright over the southern horizon its top and bottom stars point down to due south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Seen from Hawaiʻi at that moment, the gap from the horizon to its bottom star looks the same as the gap between its bottom and top stars, about 6 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aboriginal Australian readings of it differ from group to group, a river red gum to the Kamilaroi and an eaglehawk's foot in the Anmatyerr language, while for the Kamilaroi and Euahlayi the Coalsack is the head of the Emu in the Sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ian Ridpath, “Crux,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/crux.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, “The Sky Above,” https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Polynesian Voyaging Society, “Meridian Pointers to South,” Hōkūleʻa Learning Center, https://learningcenter.hokulea.com/?p=10924. [Open access]","Ray P. Norris, “Dawes Review 5: Australian Aboriginal Astronomy and Navigation,” <i>Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia</i> 33 (2016): e039, https://doi.org/10.1017/pasa.2016.25. [Open access]","Robert S. Fuller, Michael G. Anderson, Ray P. Norris, and Michelle Trudgett, “The Emu Sky Knowledge of the Kamilaroi and Euahlayi Peoples,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 17, no. 2 (2014): 171–79, https://doi.org/10.3724/SP.J.1440-2807.2014.02.04. [Open access]","David P. Stern, “Does the Southern Sky Have a Pole Star?,” <i>From Stargazers to Starships</i>, questions and answers no. 422, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://pwg.gsfc.nasa.gov/stargaze/StarFAQ23.htm. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Centaurus,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/centaurus.html. [Open access]","Polynesian Voyaging Society, “Star Compass,” Hawaiian voyaging traditions archive, https://archive.hokulea.com/navigate/stars.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Europeans lose sight of these stars for centuries?","a":"Because of precession, the slow drift of the celestial pole against the stars, which carried this part of the sky below the horizon of observers at Mediterranean latitudes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ptolemy had catalogued the stars as part of Centaurus, and seafarers found them again in the 15th and 16th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the cross useful to navigators?","a":"Because its long axis points towards the south celestial pole, which has no bright star of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By 1514 a Portuguese treatise gave the distances of its stars from the pole as a guide to latitude, and Hawaiian wayfinders read due south from it when it stands upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do Aboriginal stories of the cross vary so much?","a":"Because there was never a single Aboriginal sky: the Kamilaroi see a river red gum, Anmatyerr speakers an eaglehawk's foot, and other groups other things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One proposed explanation is that the cross was below the horizon while the ancestors of Aboriginal Australians lived farther north, though this remains a hypothesis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Crux"},
    "astro-063": {"questions":["In November 1572 a new star as bright as Venus blazed in the W of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and stayed visible to the naked eye for more than a year.","Aratus pictured the vain queen <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> plunging headlong into the sea like a diver, because from Greek latitudes her stars dipped low at the bottom of each circuit."],"abstract":"<b>Cassiopeia</b> is a constellation of the far northern sky, one of the 48 Greek constellations listed by Ptolemy, named after the queen of Ethiopia who boasted that she was more beautiful than the Nereids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its five brightest stars make a distinctive W, which Aratus likened to the key of a double door, and the figure is drawn as the queen seated on her throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several of the stars carry Arabic names, Schedar from a word for ‘breast’ and Caph from a phrase meaning ‘hand stained with henna’, while the central star of the W, an erratic variable, has no official name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For observers across the continental United States the W lies in the north circumpolar zone, the part of the sky within which stars never set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In November 1572 a brilliant new star appeared in it, reached about magnitude −4, comparable to Venus, and stayed visible to the naked eye for over a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Cassiopeia and the Big Dipper lie on opposite sides of the pole star, so as the sky turns one of them is high whenever the other is low near the horizon or hidden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That makes the W the second way to find Polaris: draw a line that splits the angle of its first V in half and follow it until it reaches the pole star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From Greek latitudes the queen dipped towards the sea at the lowest point of each circuit, and Aratus wrote that she plunged headlong like a diver, parted at the knees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Hawaiian wayfinders use its stars too, and their star lists give Caph the name Poloʻula and note that Segin, at the other end of the W, crosses the meridian about an hour and 45 minutes after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The star of 1572 is now known to have been a supernova, and an echo of its light, scattered off dust and caught more than four centuries later, shows it was a normal Type Ia explosion of a white dwarf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Ian Ridpath, “Cassiopeia,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/cassiopeia.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, “The Sky Above,” https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","David P. Stern, “Finding the Pole Star,” <i>From Stargazers to Starships</i>, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://pwg.gsfc.nasa.gov/stargaze/Spolaris.htm. [Open access]","Oliver Krause et al., “Tycho Brahe’s 1572 Supernova as a Standard Type Ia as Revealed by Its Light-Echo Spectrum,” <i>Nature</i> 456 (2008): 617–19, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature07608. [Paywalled]","Polynesian Voyaging Society, “Meridian Pointers to South,” Hōkūleʻa Learning Center, https://learningcenter.hokulea.com/?p=10924. [Open access]","Apollodorus, <i>The Library</i> 2.4.3, trans. James George Frazer, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press; London: William Heinemann, 1921), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0022%3Atext%3DLibrary%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D3. [Open access]","Aratus, <i>Phaenomena</i> 188–96, 634–58, trans. A. W. Mair and G. R. Mair, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1921), Theoi Classical Texts Library, https://www.theoi.com/Text/AratusPhaenomena.html. [Open access]","NASA, Chandra X-ray Center, “Tycho’s Supernova Remnant,” Chandra Photo Album, 2002, https://chandra.harvard.edu/photo/2002/0005/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Cassiopeia useful for finding the pole star?","a":"Because it lies on the opposite side of Polaris from the Big Dipper, so when the Dipper is low or hidden Cassiopeia is high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A line splitting the angle of the W's first V in half leads to the vicinity of Polaris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can people in most of the United States see it all year?","a":"Because it lies close enough to the north celestial pole to be inside the north circumpolar zone for those latitudes, the part of the sky that never sets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It circles the pole each night, sometimes upright and sometimes upside down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the star of 1572 still matter to astronomers?","a":"Because it was a supernova in our own Galaxy, close enough for its remnant to be studied in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A spectrum taken from its light echo more than four centuries later shows it belonged to the normal class of Type Ia supernovae, the exploding white dwarfs used as distance indicators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cassiopeia (constellation)"},
    "astro-064": {"questions":["Some 11 degrees from the pole in Ptolemy's day, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> owes its role as the north pole star to precession and will come closest around 2100 CE.","The nearest Cepheid variable, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a triple star whose close companion, first seen directly by the Hubble Space Telescope, circles it about every 30 years."],"abstract":"<b>Polaris</b>, or Alpha Ursae Minoris, is the star at the tip of the Little Bear's tail, the end of the Little Dipper's handle, and the nearest bright star to the north celestial pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because it sits so close to the pole, it moves less than any other star as the northern sky turns each night, while the rest wheel around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Contrary to common belief it is not especially bright: at magnitude 2.0 it is only on the fringe of the 50 brightest stars seen by the naked eye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a triple system, a supergiant with a close companion first seen directly by the Hubble Space Telescope in 2006 and a wider one visible in small telescopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The supergiant pulsates as a Cepheid variable, the nearest of its kind, and the roughly 30-year orbit of its close companion gives it a mass estimated at about 5.1 times the Sun's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its usefulness is a matter of geometry: the height of the north celestial pole above the horizon equals the observer's latitude, so the altitude of Polaris gives latitude almost directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hawaiian wayfinders call it Hōkūpaʻa and, when it can be seen, drop an imaginary line from it to the horizon to find north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its role is temporary, for in Ptolemy's day it lay some 11 degrees from the pole, and precession, the slow drift of the celestial pole against the stars, has carried the pole towards it ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The name 'stella polaris' first appears in print in an edition of the Alfonsine Tables of 1492, when the star was still about three and a half degrees from the pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It will come closest around 2100 CE, less than half a degree away, and its distance is still disputed, with parallaxes from different instruments disagreeing by more than their stated errors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, “The Sky Above,” https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Ursa Minor,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/ursaminor.html. [Open access]","Howard E. Bond et al., “Hubble Space Telescope Trigonometric Parallax of Polaris B, Companion of the Nearest Cepheid,” <i>The Astrophysical Journal</i> 853 (2018): 55, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/aaa3f9. [Open access]","Nancy Remage Evans et al., “The Orbit and Dynamical Mass of Polaris: Observations with the CHARA Array,” <i>The Astrophysical Journal</i> 971 (2024): 190, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ad5e7a. [Open access]","NASA, “Hubble Images Polaris’s Companion,” NASA Science, 9 January 2006, https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/hubble-images-polariss-companion. [Open access]","Polynesian Voyaging Society, “Holding a Course,” Hōkūleʻa Learning Center, https://learningcenter.hokulea.com/?p=10566. [Open access]","David P. Stern, “Finding the Pole Star,” <i>From Stargazers to Starships</i>, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://pwg.gsfc.nasa.gov/stargaze/Spolaris.htm. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Crux,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/crux.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Polaris not always the pole star?","a":"Because precession slowly shifts the celestial pole against the stars. In Ptolemy's day Polaris was some 11 degrees from the pole; it will be closest, under half a degree, around 2100 CE, and will then drift away again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Polaris tell a navigator the latitude?","a":"Because the north celestial pole stands as many degrees above the horizon as the observer's latitude, and Polaris is less than a degree from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From Honolulu, at 21 degrees north, it appears about 21 degrees up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do astronomers care about its mass and distance?","a":"Because it is the nearest Cepheid, a kind of pulsating star used to calibrate the scale of distances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its companion's orbit gives a mass of about 5.1 Suns, and together with the distance this shows the star to be more luminous than models predict for that mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Polaris"},
    "astro-065": {"questions":["The Greeks saw Asclepius, the god of medicine, in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a figure gripping the snake Serpens, whose two halves lie on either side of him.","The last supernova seen in our Galaxy, in 1604, flared near the ankle of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which stands on Scorpius with one foot as the ecliptic crosses its feet."],"abstract":"<b>Ophiuchus</b>, the serpent-bearer, is a large constellation crossed by the ecliptic between Scorpius and Sagittarius, one of the 48 Greek constellations listed by Ptolemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It shows a man grasping a huge snake, the separate constellation Serpens, which is unique among the 88 in being split into two halves on either side of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Greeks identified the figure with Asclepius, the god of medicine, and the snake became a symbol of healing because snakes shed their skin and seem to be reborn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its brightest star, Rasalhague, marks the head, its name coming from an Arabic phrase meaning ‘the head of the serpent holder’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ophiuchus was also the site of the last supernova seen in our Galaxy, in 1604, which was very likely a Type Ia explosion of a white dwarf, now a remnant about 13,000 light-years away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The ecliptic, the Sun's yearly path, crosses 13 of the modern constellations, and Ophiuchus is the one that the traditional twelve signs of the zodiac leave out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the modern boundaries the Sun is in Ophiuchus from about 29 November to 18 December, far longer than the week or so it spends in Scorpius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The signs are twelve equal sectors of 30 degrees named more than 2,000 years ago, whereas the constellations have unequal widths, and precession has since slid the stars about one sign's width along the ecliptic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The boundaries were adopted by the International Astronomical Union at Leiden in 1928, as hour circles and parallels of declination drawn up by the Belgian astronomer Eugène Delporte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They follow lines of right ascension and declination for the year 1875, and they zigzag so that every named variable star kept its old constellation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, “The Sky Above,” https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.3, “Astrology and Astronomy,” https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-3-astrology-and-astronomy. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Ophiuchus,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/ophiuchus.html. [Open access]","F. Schlesinger, “3. Commission des Notations, des Unités et de l’Économie des Publications,” <i>Transactions of the International Astronomical Union</i> 4 (1933): 19, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0251107X00016345. [Paywalled]","Jacco Vink, “Supernova 1604, Kepler’s Supernova, and Its Remnant,” in <i>Handbook of Supernovae</i> (Cham: Springer, 2017), 139–60, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-21846-5_49. [Paywalled]","Ian Ridpath, “Stars and Storytellers,” <i>Star Tales</i>, chap. 1, “Lacaille’s Southern Survey” and “The Final 88,” http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/startales1d.html. [Open access]","NASA, Chandra X-ray Center, “Kepler’s Supernova Remnant,” Chandra Photo Album, 6 October 2004, https://chandra.harvard.edu/photo/2004/kepler/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Ophiuchus not one of the twelve zodiac signs?","a":"Because the signs are twelve equal 30-degree sectors fixed more than 2,000 years ago, not the constellations themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sun's path does pass through Ophiuchus, but the traditional twelve leave it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Sun spend different times in each zodiacal constellation?","a":"Because the constellations are of unequal size, with boundaries drawn along lines of right ascension and declination rather than in equal slices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Sun crosses Ophiuchus in about three weeks but Scorpius in about six days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do sun signs no longer match the constellations?","a":"Because precession has slid the constellations westward along the ecliptic by about the width of one sign since the signs were named, while most astrology kept the old dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sign of Aries now occupies the constellation of Pisces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ophiuchus"},
    "astro-066": {"questions":["Dutch navigators in the 1590s and a French astronomer at the Cape in the 1750s filled the charts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with exotic animals and instruments of science.","From 38 degrees north, every star within 38 degrees of the south celestial pole never rises, hiding the Southern Cross and much of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from most of the United States."],"abstract":"The <b>southern celestial hemisphere</b> is the half of the sky that lies south of the celestial equator, centred on the south celestial pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How much of it can be seen depends on latitude: for an observer at 38 degrees north, every star within 38 degrees of the south celestial pole never rises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That hidden zone holds the Southern Cross for most observers in the United States, while the same observers see the north celestial pole stand 38 degrees above their horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unlike the north, the southern pole has no star close to it at present to mark its place, so it has to be found from other stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The southern Milky Way is rich in dark clouds of dust, and many Aboriginal Australian cultures named figures made from these dark spaces, an idea absent from European lore, perhaps because such clouds are less conspicuous from the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The far southern sky had been below the horizon for the ancient Greeks, so it lay outside the 48 constellations of Ptolemy and was charted by Europeans only in the age of exploration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twelve new constellations came from observations made by the Dutch navigator Pieter Dirkszoon Keyser off Madagascar on a trading voyage that left the Netherlands in 1595, and they appeared on a globe in 1598.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of those first southern figures were named after exotic animals, the toucan, the peacock and the chameleon among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The remaining gaps were filled by the French astronomer Nicolas Louis de Lacaille, who observed nearly 10,000 stars from the Cape of Good Hope in 1751 and 1752 and introduced 14 new constellations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of his names honour instruments of science and the arts, the exception being Mensa, after the Table Mountain under which he observed, and his split of the huge ship Argo into Carina, Puppis and Vela is still in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, “The Sky Above,” https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Stars and Storytellers,” <i>Star Tales</i>, chap. 1, “Scouting the Southern Sky,” http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/startales1c.html. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Stars and Storytellers,” <i>Star Tales</i>, chap. 1, “Lacaille’s Southern Survey” and “The Final 88,” http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/startales1d.html. [Open access]","David P. Stern, “Does the Southern Sky Have a Pole Star?,” <i>From Stargazers to Starships</i>, questions and answers no. 422, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://pwg.gsfc.nasa.gov/stargaze/StarFAQ23.htm. [Open access]","Ray P. Norris, “Dawes Review 5: Australian Aboriginal Astronomy and Navigation,” <i>Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia</i> 33 (2016): e039, https://doi.org/10.1017/pasa.2016.25. [Open access]","Polynesian Voyaging Society, “Meridian Pointers to South,” Hōkūleʻa Learning Center, https://learningcenter.hokulea.com/?p=10924. [Open access]","Robert S. Fuller, Michael G. Anderson, Ray P. Norris, and Michelle Trudgett, “The Emu Sky Knowledge of the Kamilaroi and Euahlayi Peoples,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 17, no. 2 (2014): 171–79, https://doi.org/10.3724/SP.J.1440-2807.2014.02.04. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the far southern constellations invented so late?","a":"Because those stars were below the horizon for the ancient Greeks, whose 48 constellations formed the basis of the European sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were charted only when European navigators sailed south, from the Dutch observations of the 1590s to Lacaille's survey at the Cape in 1751 and 1752.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is there no southern pole star?","a":"Because no star happens to lie close to the south celestial pole at present, unlike Polaris in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Observers must locate the pole from other stars instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Aboriginal astronomers see shapes in dark clouds?","a":"Because many Aboriginal cultures treated the dark spaces between the stars of the Milky Way as figures in their own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The idea is absent from European lore, perhaps because such clouds are less striking from the Northern Hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Southern celestial hemisphere"},
    "astro-067": {"questions":["Chinese court constellations, the Wiradjuri emu in the Milky Way's dust and Yolngu brothers in Orion's canoe are all material for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, sometimes called the anthropology of astronomy.","Asking why the Pleiades are sisters across much of Australia and the world, while the Southern Cross means something different to almost every group, is a question for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than for physics."],"abstract":"<b>Cultural astronomy</b> is the study of how different cultures have understood and used what they see in the sky, and of how that understanding is produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is usually divided into archaeoastronomy, which deals with past cultures, and ethnoastronomy, which deals with living ones, and it has been called the anthropology of astronomy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its starting point is that constellations are cultural: each civilisation found its own patterns among the roughly 3,000 stars visible to the naked eye, and the Chinese, Egyptians and Greeks all drew different figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 88 modern constellations grew from the Greek tradition, while the Chinese system, unknown in the West, had no influence on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Since 2016 a working group of the International Astronomical Union has catalogued star names, with a remit that includes searching cultural literature for traditional names and reaching beyond Greek, Roman and Arabic naming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Chinese astronomers divided the sky into 283 small constellations of 1,464 stars that showed not myths but the imperial court, society and rural life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many Aboriginal Australian cultures also made figures from the dark spaces between the stars, an idea absent from European lore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Wiradjuri see a celestial emu, Gugurmin, in the dust bands of the Milky Way, and a Dreaming of the creation ancestor Baiami places him in the stars of Orion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Orion and the Pleiades carry very similar stories across Australia and around the world, while the Southern Cross seems to have a different story in nearly every Aboriginal language group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same stars can mean quite different things, and for the Yolngu the three stars of Orion's belt are three brothers in a canoe, with Betelgeuse at the front and Rigel at the back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), section 2.1, “The Sky Above,” https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]","Robert S. Fuller, Michael G. Anderson, Ray P. Norris, and Michelle Trudgett, “The Emu Sky Knowledge of the Kamilaroi and Euahlayi Peoples,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 17, no. 2 (2014): 171–79, https://doi.org/10.3724/SP.J.1440-2807.2014.02.04. [Open access]","Ray P. Norris, “Dawes Review 5: Australian Aboriginal Astronomy and Navigation,” <i>Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia</i> 33 (2016): e039, https://doi.org/10.1017/pasa.2016.25. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Star Maps,” <i>Star Tales</i>, chap. 2, “The Chinese Sky – a Lost Tradition,” http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/startales2a.html. [Open access]","Susanne M. Hoffmann et al., “The IAU Working Group on Star Names (WGSN): A Review of Recent Activities,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 28 (2025): 280–86, https://doi.org/10.3724/SP.J.140-2807.2025.01.16. [Open access]","Trevor M. Leaman and Duane W. Hamacher, “<i>Baiami</i> and the Emu Chase: An Astronomical Interpretation of a Wiradjuri Dreaming Associated with the Burbung,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 22, no. 2 (2019): 225–37, https://doi.org/10.3724/SP.J.1440-2807.2019.02.03. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do different cultures see different constellations?","a":"Because constellations are patterns people impose on the roughly 3,000 stars visible to the eye, and each civilisation found its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese astronomers pictured the imperial court and rural life, while many Aboriginal cultures also drew figures from the dark spaces between stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the International Astronomical Union collect traditional star names?","a":"Because there had been no formal policy for naming stars, and its Working Group on Star Names, set up in 2016, was created to reduce confusion over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its remit includes searching cultural literature for traditional names and going beyond Greek, Roman and Arabic traditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are stories of Orion and the Pleiades so alike in many places?","a":"One hypothesis is that the stories are extremely old, older than the spread of people around the world, though it remains a hypothesis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By contrast the Southern Cross, which would have been below the horizon during that journey, has a different story in nearly every Aboriginal language group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cultural astronomy"},
    "astro-068": {"questions":["Two South Australian oral traditions recording the changing brightness of Betelgeuse, Aldebaran and Antares are unique evidence within <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of pulsating variable stars being observed.","Spread across some 250 language groups at the time of the British invasion, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is many traditions rather than one, and much of it remains unrecorded."],"abstract":"<b>Australian Aboriginal astronomy</b> is the body of sky knowledge held in the traditional cultures of Aboriginal Australians and passed on through oral tradition, ceremony and art, in which the sky was the upper part of one landscape with the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is not one tradition but many, since at the British invasion of 1788 Aboriginal Australia held about 250 distinct language groups, each with its own culture and language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The knowledge was practical, used to navigate and to build calendars, with the movements of the stars linked to the seasons of plants on Earth, and songlines, oral maps of the land, could carry routes in astronomical form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> There is also evidence of careful records of cyclical and unexpected events, such as eclipses and meteorite impacts, and of finding the cardinal points to within a few degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only a small fraction of the language groups has been studied, and much of the knowledge is held by ageing senior elders, so recording it is described as a race against time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One widely reported figure is the Emu in the Sky, a dark constellation of dust lanes in the Milky Way whose head is the Coalsack beside the Southern Cross.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the Kamilaroi and Euahlayi, the emu running across the evening sky in April and May signals that real emus are laying, and its change to a sitting bird in June and July marks the time of brooding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Wiradjuri call their celestial emu Gugurmin, and a Dreaming of the creation ancestor Baiami, seen in the stars of Orion, most likely describes the yearly movement of these figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two oral traditions from South Australia describe the changing brightness of Betelgeuse, Aldebaran and Antares, the only known Indigenous accounts anywhere of pulsating variable stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Claims about navigation need care, though, and a review of Western Australian accounts finds star maps used to teach routes on the ground but no evidence that they were used to navigate in real time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ray P. Norris, “Dawes Review 5: Australian Aboriginal Astronomy and Navigation,” <i>Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia</i> 33 (2016): e039, https://doi.org/10.1017/pasa.2016.25. [Open access]","Robert S. Fuller, Michael G. Anderson, Ray P. Norris, and Michelle Trudgett, “The Emu Sky Knowledge of the Kamilaroi and Euahlayi Peoples,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 17, no. 2 (2014): 171–79, https://doi.org/10.3724/SP.J.1440-2807.2014.02.04. [Open access]","Duane W. Hamacher, “Observations of Red-Giant Variable Stars by Aboriginal Australians,” <i>The Australian Journal of Anthropology</i> 29, no. 1 (2018): 89–107, https://doi.org/10.1111/taja.12257. [Open access]","Trevor M. Leaman and Duane W. Hamacher, “<i>Baiami</i> and the Emu Chase: An Astronomical Interpretation of a Wiradjuri Dreaming Associated with the Burbung,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 22, no. 2 (2019): 225–37, https://doi.org/10.3724/SP.J.1440-2807.2019.02.03. [Open access]","Patricia A. Forster, “Review of Aboriginal Astronomy and Navigation: A Western Australian Focus,” <i>Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia</i> 38 (2021): e066, https://doi.org/10.1017/pasa.2021.51. [Paywalled]","Philip A. Clarke, “The Aboriginal Australian Cosmic Landscape. Part 1: The Ethnobotany of the Skyworld,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 17, no. 3 (2014): 307–25, https://doi.org/10.3724/SP.J.1440-2807.2014.03.05. [Paywalled]","Philip A. Clarke, “The Aboriginal Australian Cosmic Landscape. Part 2: Plant Connections with the Skyworld,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 18, no. 1 (2015): 23–37, https://doi.org/10.3724/SP.J.1440-2807.2015.01.03. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do Aboriginal sky stories differ so much from place to place?","a":"Because Aboriginal Australia was never one culture: at the British invasion there were about 250 distinct language groups, each with its own culture and language, though many shared common threads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Southern Cross, for example, has a different story in nearly every group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Emu in the Sky change through the year?","a":"Because the turning of the sky shows different parts of the dark figure in the evening at different seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Kamilaroi and Euahlayi read the running emu of April and May as the laying season and the sitting emu of June and July as brooding, so the figure works as a calendar for gathering eggs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why should claims about star navigation be treated with care?","a":"Because the evidence is uneven: a review of Western Australian accounts found star maps used to teach routes on the ground but no evidence of their use in real-time navigation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Variable-star traditions, by contrast, were long missed because the ethnographic record had misidentified the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Australian Aboriginal astronomy"},
    "astro-069": {"questions":["Among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Purple Palace around the north pole was walled by 15 stars and was one of three enclosures, with Taiwei in Virgo and Tianshi in Ophiuchus.","Used in China until Jesuit missionaries brought Western star figures in the 17th century, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had also spread to Korea and Japan."],"abstract":"<b>Chinese constellations</b> are the star groups of the traditional Chinese sky, a system developed independently of the Greek one and fixed in its classic form by the late 3rd century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They were far smaller and more numerous than Western constellations, most containing only a handful of stars and some just one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court astronomer Chen Zhuo combined the star maps of three earlier schools, those of Gan De, Shi Shen and Wu Xian, into a standard sky of 283 asterisms holding 1,464 stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Among them were 28 ancient lunar mansions, unequal strips of sky from 2 to 33 degrees wide by which the Moon's monthly progress was followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system spread to Korea and Japan and stayed in use until Jesuit missionaries brought Western constellations to China in the early 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Chinese sky was a picture of the state, with the emperor, his family and officials, and even kitchens and stores of grain set among the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At its centre was the circumpolar region, the Purple Palace, which mirrored the central authority of the emperor and was enclosed by a wall of 15 stars in eastern and western sections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Purple Palace enclosure, Ziwei, was one of three, with Taiwei among the stars of Virgo and Tianshi among those of Ophiuchus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sima Qian's treatise on the heavenly offices places Taiyi, the Great One, in the brightest star of the Central Palace, with the Three Dukes beside him and twelve guarding stars as vassal ministers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same treatise calls the Northern Dipper the emperor's carriage, turning at the centre to oversee the four quarters and fix the four seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Ian Ridpath, “Charting the Chinese Sky,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/chinese.html. [Open access]","Ian Ridpath, “Ursa Minor,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/ursaminor.html. [Open access]","Jean-Marc Bonnet-Bidaud, Françoise Praderie, and Susan Whitfield, “The Dunhuang Chinese Sky: A Comprehensive Study of the Oldest Known Star Atlas,” <i>Journal of Astronomical History and Heritage</i> 12, no. 1 (2009): 39–59, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.3034. [Open access]","Sima Qian, <i>Shiji</i> (Records of the Grand Historian) 27, “Tianguan shu” 天官書, Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%8F%B2%E8%A8%98/%E5%8D%B7027. [in Chinese] [Open access]","Fang Xuanling et al., <i>Jin shu</i> (History of the Jin) 11, “Tianwen zhi shang” 天文志上, Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E6%99%89%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B7011. [in Chinese] [Open access]","Joseph Needham, <i>Science and Civilisation in China</i>, vol. 3, <i>Mathematics and the Sciences of the Heavens and the Earth</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1959), 265, https://archive.org/details/cftri.9621sciencecivilisat0000jose. [Open access]","Wei Zheng et al., <i>Sui shu</i> (History of the Sui) 19, “Tianwen zhi shang” 天文志上, Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E9%9A%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B719. [in Chinese] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Chinese sky look like a royal court?","a":"Because the heavens were read as a mirror of the state: the circumpolar Purple Palace stood for the emperor's central authority, and the Dunhuang charts place officials, grain and kitchens among the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sima Qian puts Taiyi in the brightest star of the Central Palace with ministers around him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What were the lunar mansions for?","a":"They served as markers for following the Moon's nightly progress through its monthly orbit, a kind of zodiac that gave the basis for a lunar calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their names are so old that the meaning of many is no longer understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are Chinese constellations hard to match with modern stars?","a":"Because they were symbolic rather than pictorial, so their member stars were rarely identified precisely and could change over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese charts also drew every star as a dot of the same size, with no attempt to show brightness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Chinese constellations"},
    "astro-070": {"questions":["Sailed to Tahiti in 1976 by a master navigator from Micronesia without instruments, the canoe Hōkūleʻa revived the ancient art of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across the open Pacific.","Navigators trained in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> read due south from pairs of stars crossing the meridian together and allow for rising points that shift with latitude."],"abstract":"<b>Polynesian navigation</b> is the traditional art of finding the way across the open Pacific without instruments, by reading the stars, the Sun, the swells and the wind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its frame is the star compass, a mental construct rather than a physical object, which divides the horizon into 32 houses 11.25 degrees apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A star that rises in one house sets in the house of the same name on the opposite side of the horizon, and wind and swell are placed on the same compass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system works well because most oceanic islands of the Pacific lie within the tropics, between 23.5 degrees north and south of the equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sailing trials with reconstructed double canoes showed that such craft can make good a course up to about 75 degrees off the wind, enough to control their movements on long voyages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Bearings are taken from stars low in the sky, since the higher a star climbs the harder it is to tell where it rose or will set, and an angled star is useful only to about 30 to 35 degrees up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The navigator must allow for latitude, because rising and setting points shift as the canoe moves north or south of the equator, and stars rise at an angle from the vertical equal to the observer's latitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pairs of stars that cross the meridian together point down to due north or south, and Hōkūpaʻa, the pole star, gives north directly when it can be seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1976 the canoe Hōkūleʻa sailed from Hawaiʻi to Tahiti navigated by Mau Piailug, a master navigator from Satawal in Micronesia, and a Hawaiian student of his later developed the 32-house compass now taught to new navigators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Voyages between Hawaiʻi, Tahiti and Aotearoa showed that canoes navigated without instruments could be sailed on purpose over long distances, so the voyaging traditions cannot be casually dismissed as myth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polynesian Voyaging Society, “The Star Compass,” Hōkūleʻa Learning Center, https://learningcenter.hokulea.com/education-at-sea/polynesian-navigation/the-star-compass/. [Open access]","Polynesian Voyaging Society, “Holding a Course,” Hōkūleʻa Learning Center, https://learningcenter.hokulea.com/?p=10566. [Open access]","Polynesian Voyaging Society, “Polynesian Wayfinding,” Hōkūleʻa, https://hokulea.com/polynesian-wayfin. [Open access]","Ben R. Finney, “Voyaging Canoes and the Settlement of Polynesia,” <i>Science</i> 196, no. 4296 (1977): 1277–85, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.196.4296.1277. [Paywalled]","Ben Finney, “Myth, Experiment, and the Reinvention of Polynesian Voyaging,” <i>American Anthropologist</i> 93, no. 2 (1991): 383–404, https://doi.org/10.1525/aa.1991.93.2.02a00060. [Paywalled]","Michelle Manes, <i>Mathematics for Elementary Teachers</i> (Honolulu: University of Hawaiʻi, n.d.), chap. 67, “Navigation,” https://pressbooks.oer.hawaii.edu/mathforelementaryteachers/chapter/navigation/. [Open access]","Kamehameha Schools, “Hawaiian Haʻawina: Star Compass,” family lesson plan, https://ksdigitalfiles.ksbe.edu/assets/ohana/hawaiianstarcompass_ohana.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the star compass work so well in the Pacific?","a":"Because most oceanic islands of the Pacific lie within the tropics, between 23.5 degrees north and south of the equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Away from the equator the navigator must allow for the shift of rising and setting points with latitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the experimental voyages important to historians?","a":"Because they tested the claim that canoes navigated without instruments could not have crossed such distances on purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Voyages between Hawaiʻi, Tahiti and Aotearoa showed that they could, so the voyaging traditions cannot be casually dismissed as myth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could Polynesians sail against the wind at all?","a":"Because their double canoes could make good a course up to about 75 degrees off the wind, as trials with reconstructions showed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Without that windward ability, the settlement of Polynesia might have had to wait for European navigators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Polynesian navigation"},
    "astro-071": {"questions":["First printed in a star atlas published at Augsburg in 1603, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> names a star by a Greek letter and the Latin genitive of its constellation.","When the 24 Greek letters ran out in a large constellation, a star's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> continued with Roman letters, and only Hercules reached the letter z."],"abstract":"A <b>Bayer designation</b> names a star by a Greek letter followed by the Latin genitive, or possessive form, of its constellation, so that Betelgeuse is Alpha Orionis, ‘the alpha of Orion’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The system first appeared in the <i>Uranometria</i>, a star atlas published at Augsburg in 1603 by the German lawyer and astronomer Johann Bayer, which is usually called the first modern star atlas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bayer took most of his star positions and magnitudes from Tycho Brahe's catalogue of 777 stars, and printed the lettered lists of stars on the back of each chart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In catalogues the letter is written as a symbol or a short code and the constellation is cut to its official three-letter abbreviation, so Betelgeuse appears as α Ori or alf Ori.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The scheme is still in everyday use, and the official catalogue of star names kept by the International Astronomical Union lists each named star against its Bayer letter wherever it has one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Bayer did not letter the stars strictly from brightest to faintest, and the magnitude estimates of his day were not good enough for that to have been possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instead he sorted the stars of each constellation into magnitude classes, from first to sixth, and lettered the members of each class as he saw fit, sometimes by their position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Plough of Ursa Major the seven bright stars run in order of right ascension, while in Gemini the three brightest are lettered from north to south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As a result there are 16 constellations in which the star labelled alpha is not the brightest, and Orion is one of them, since Rigel outshines its alpha star, Betelgeuse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Once the 24 Greek letters were used up Bayer went on with Roman letters, reaching z only in Hercules, and where he gave one letter to several stars, later astronomers added superscript numbers to tell them apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Ian Ridpath, “Bayer’s <i>Uranometria</i> and Bayer Letters,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/bayer.html. [Open access]","William B. Ashworth Jr., “Johann Bayer,” Scientist of the Day, Linda Hall Library, 7 March 2016, https://www.lindahall.org/about/news/scientist-of-the-day/johann-bayer/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “19.2 Surveying the Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-2-surveying-the-stars. [Open access]","SIMBAD Astronomical Database, “alf Ori — Betelgeuse,” Centre de Données astronomiques de Strasbourg, CfA mirror, accessed 7 November 2026, https://simbad.cfa.harvard.edu/simbad/sim-id?Ident=alf+Ori. [Open access]","IAU Working Group on Star Names, “IAU Catalog of Star Names (IAU-CSN),” last updated 4 April 2022, https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~emamajek/WGSN/IAU-CSN.txt. [Open access]","IAU Working Group on Star Names, <i>Bulletin of the IAU Working Group on Star Names</i>, no. 1 (July 2016), https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~emamajek/WGSN/WGSN_bulletin1.pdf. [Open access]","SIMBAD Astronomical Database, “bet Ori — Rigel,” Centre de Données astronomiques de Strasbourg, CfA mirror, accessed 7 November 2026, https://simbad.cfa.harvard.edu/simbad/sim-id?Ident=Rigel. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Betelgeuse Alpha Orionis when Rigel is usually brighter?","a":"Because Bayer did not letter the stars strictly by brightness, and the magnitude estimates of 1603 were too rough for that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He sorted each constellation's stars into magnitude classes and lettered them within each class as he chose, so in 16 constellations the alpha star is not the brightest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the constellation's name changed, Orionis rather than Orion?","a":"Because the designation uses the Latin genitive, the possessive form, so Alpha Orionis means ‘the alpha of Orion’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In catalogues the constellation is usually shortened to its three-letter IAU abbreviation, so the same star appears as α Ori or alf Ori.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some stars carry a letter with a small number attached?","a":"Because Bayer sometimes gave one letter to several stars, as he did with the six stars of the arc on Orion's arm, all lettered pi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later astronomers added superscript numbers to the letter to tell the individual stars apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bayer designation"},
    "astro-072": {"questions":["Though named after England's first Astronomer Royal, the numbering behind each star's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was actually added by Lalande in a French edition of the catalogue printed in 1783.","The star around which a giant planet with a 4.2-day orbit was announced in 1995 carries the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 51 Pegasi as well as the proper name Helvetios."],"abstract":"A <b>Flamsteed designation</b> names a star by a number followed by the Latin genitive of its constellation, as in 61 Cygni or 51 Pegasi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within each constellation the numbers run in order of right ascension, the east–west coordinate of the sky, so they follow a star's position rather than its brightness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is why Betelgeuse, already Alpha Orionis by its Greek letter, is also 58 Orionis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The numbers take their name from John Flamsteed, who became England's first Astronomer Royal in 1675 and spent 44 years observing at the new Greenwich Observatory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His catalogue was the first major star catalogue made with the aid of a telescope, and it was published in 1725, after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Flamsteed's own catalogue contained no such numbers: they were added by the French astronomer Joseph-Jérôme de Lalande, who numbered the stars of each constellation in order in an edition printed in 1783.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lalande said he took the idea from an unauthorised edition of 1712, printed from Flamsteed's preliminary data, though its numbers were arranged differently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern reference works still carry them, and the Bright Star Catalogue gives most of its stars a name built from a Flamsteed number and a Bayer letter wherever these exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A star without a Greek letter may be known by its number alone, and 61 Cygni, the 61st star of Cygnus in the sequence, was among the first three stars to have their parallaxes measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another, 51 Pegasi, a star of about magnitude 5.5, became famous in 1995 when a planet was announced around it, and the IAU's catalogue of star names lists it under its Flamsteed number with the proper name Helvetios, approved in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Ian Ridpath, “Flamsteed Numbers: Where They Really Came From,” <i>Star Tales</i>, http://www.ianridpath.com/startales/flamsteed.html. [Open access]","William B. Ashworth Jr., “John Flamsteed,” Scientist of the Day, Linda Hall Library, 19 August 2015, https://www.lindahall.org/about/news/scientist-of-the-day/john-flamsteed/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “19.2 Surveying the Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-2-surveying-the-stars. [Open access]","SIMBAD Astronomical Database, “alf Ori — Betelgeuse,” Centre de Données astronomiques de Strasbourg, CfA mirror, accessed 7 November 2026, https://simbad.cfa.harvard.edu/simbad/sim-id?Ident=alf+Ori. [Open access]","HEASARC, “BSC5P — Bright Star Catalog,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://heasarc.gsfc.nasa.gov/W3Browse/star-catalog/bsc5p.html. [Open access]","SIMBAD Astronomical Database, “51 Peg,” Centre de Données astronomiques de Strasbourg, CfA mirror, accessed 7 November 2026, https://simbad.cfa.harvard.edu/simbad/sim-id?Ident=51+Peg. [Open access]","NASA Science, “51 Pegasi b,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanet-catalog/51-pegasi-b/. [Open access]","IAU Working Group on Star Names, “IAU Catalog of Star Names (IAU-CSN),” last updated 4 April 2022, https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~emamajek/WGSN/IAU-CSN.txt. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do Flamsteed numbers not show which stars are brightest?","a":"Because within each constellation the stars are numbered in order of right ascension, their position in the sky, not by magnitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is why Betelgeuse, one of the brightest stars of all, carries the number 58 in Orion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are they called Flamsteed numbers if Flamsteed did not assign them?","a":"Because they number the stars of Flamsteed's catalogue, the first major star catalogue made with a telescope, published in 1725 after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The numbering itself was added by Lalande in a French edition of 1783, borrowing the idea from an unauthorised edition of 1712.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why must a Flamsteed designation always include a constellation?","a":"Because Lalande numbered the stars consecutively constellation by constellation, so the numbers start again in each one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A number alone identifies nothing, while 61 Cygni means the 61st star of Cygnus in that sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Flamsteed designation"},
    "astro-073": {"questions":["Under guidelines set by the IAU's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a new star name should preferably run to 4 to 16 characters and must never be commercial or honour a pet.","The Wardaman name Ginan, for a star of the Southern Cross, was among 86 names from many cultures that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> approved in 2017."],"abstract":"The <b>Working Group on Star Names</b> is the body of the International Astronomical Union that catalogues and standardises the proper names of stars for astronomers around the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Proper names are far older than any system of letters or numbers, and the brightest stars mostly carry names inherited from antiquity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A few are Greek, such as Sirius, ‘the scorched one’, a few are Latin, and many of the best known are Arabic, because much of Greek astronomy reached medieval Europe through Arabic translations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Betelgeuse, for example, means ‘Hand of al-Jawzā'’, the name of an old Arabian figure whose stars largely matched the Greek Orion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yet until 2016 the union had never officially recognised a popular name for any star, and astronomers identified stars by Bayer letters and catalogue numbers instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The group's terms of reference were approved by the union's Executive Committee on 6 May 2016, with a brief to search the world's astronomical history and cultural literature for candidate names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its guidelines strongly encourage names that preserve astronomical and cultural heritage, preferably 4 to 16 characters long and pronounceable, and they forbid commercial names, the names of pets and, for bright stars, the names of individuals except in rare historical cases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first names it adopted were mostly the familiar Arabic, Greek and Latin ones, but a later batch of 86 drew on Chinese, Coptic, Hindu, Mayan, Polynesian, South African and Aboriginal Australian traditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Among them was the Wardaman name Ginan, given to Epsilon Crucis in the Southern Cross and approved in November 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The catalogue the group keeps is free for anyone to use, and it held more than 600 names as of 2026, including names for stars that host exoplanets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["IAU Working Group on Star Names, “Terms of Reference,” https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~emamajek/WGSN/termsref.html. [Open access]","IAU Working Group on Star Names, <i>Bulletin of the IAU Working Group on Star Names</i>, no. 1 (July 2016), https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~emamajek/WGSN/WGSN_bulletin1.pdf. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “19.2 Surveying the Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-2-surveying-the-stars. [Open access]","IAU Working Group on Star Names, “IAU-Catalog of Star Names (CSN),” exopla.net, accessed 7 November 2026, https://exopla.net/star-names/modern-iau-star-names/. [Open access]","Duane Hamacher, “The Stories behind Aboriginal Star Names Now Recognised by the World’s Astronomical Body,” <i>The Conversation</i>, 14 January 2018, https://doi.org/10.64628/aa.tsfugsf53. [Open access]","IAU Working Group on Star Names, “IAU Catalog of Star Names (IAU-CSN),” last updated 4 April 2022, https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~emamajek/WGSN/IAU-CSN.txt. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the IAU need a group to standardise star names?","a":"Because until 2016 the union had never officially recognised a popular name for any star, and stars were identified by Bayer letters and catalogue numbers instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The group was asked to catalogue and standardise proper names for the international astronomical community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are so many familiar star names Arabic?","a":"Because much of Greek and Roman astronomy reached medieval Europe through Arabic translations, and many star names came with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Betelgeuse, for instance, means ‘Hand of al-Jawzā'’, an old Arabian figure whose stars largely matched Orion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What kinds of names do the group's guidelines prefer?","a":"Names that preserve astronomical, cultural and natural heritage are strongly encouraged, and common or cultural names are preferred over new inventions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since 2017 that has included names from Chinese, Polynesian, Mayan and Aboriginal Australian traditions, among others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"IAU Working Group on Star Names"},
    "astro-074": {"questions":["With its first extension, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives spectral types and rough positions for 272,150 stars, each identified by the letters HD and a number.","Far less precise than Hipparcos or Gaia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave its stars only rough positions for the equinox of 1900, yet astronomers relied on it for over half a century."],"abstract":"The <b>Henry Draper Catalogue</b> is a catalogue of stellar spectra compiled at Harvard College Observatory, and its numbers, written HD followed by an integer, are among the commonest catalogue designations for stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A catalogue designation is simply a star's entry in a particular list, and as specialised catalogues multiplied, astronomers took to calling stars by their catalogue numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same star therefore has a different number in every list that includes it, so Betelgeuse is HD 39801 in the Henry Draper Catalogue, HIP 27989 in the Hipparcos Catalogue and BD+07 1055 in the Bonner Durchmusterung.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some lists were enormous even before photography: the Bonner Durchmusterung, measured by eye at Bonn in the 1850s and 1860s, gave BD numbers to over 324,000 stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fuzzy objects have catalogues of their own, which is why the Andromeda Galaxy is M31, from the list Charles Messier made in 1781, and also NGC 224.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Henry Draper Catalogue was published between 1918 and 1924 in volumes 91 to 99 of the <i>Annals</i> of Harvard College Observatory, under the names of Annie Jump Cannon and Edward C. Pickering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Cannon simplified the older spectral classes and rearranged them in order of decreasing temperature into the sequence O, B, A, F, G, K, M that astronomers still use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> With its first extension of 1934, the catalogue gives spectral classifications and rough positions for 272,150 stars, numbered from HD 1 to HD 272150.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its positions are for the equinox of 1900 and are crude by modern standards, but it was widely used by astronomers for over half a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Precise positions now come from space: the Hipparcos Catalogue has 118,218 entries, and the third Gaia data release gives brightness measurements for about 1.8 billion sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["HEASARC, “HDEC — Henry Draper Extension Charts Catalog,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://heasarc.gsfc.nasa.gov/W3Browse/star-catalog/hdec.html. [Open access]","VizieR, “III/135A: Henry Draper Catalogue and Extension (Cannon+ 1918–1924; ADC 1989),” Centre de Données astronomiques de Strasbourg, CfA mirror, https://vizier.cfa.harvard.edu/viz-bin/VizieR?-source=III/135A. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “19.2 Surveying the Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-2-surveying-the-stars. [Open access]","SIMBAD Astronomical Database, “alf Ori — Betelgeuse,” Centre de Données astronomiques de Strasbourg, CfA mirror, accessed 7 November 2026, https://simbad.cfa.harvard.edu/simbad/sim-id?Ident=alf+Ori. [Open access]","SIMBAD Astronomical Database, “M 31 — Andromeda Galaxy,” Centre de Données astronomiques de Strasbourg, CfA mirror, accessed 7 November 2026, https://simbad.cfa.harvard.edu/simbad/sim-id?Ident=M+31. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “17.3 The Spectra of Stars (and Brown Dwarfs),” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/17-3-the-spectra-of-stars-and-brown-dwarfs. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Catalogue Summary,” Hipparcos, ESA Cosmos, https://www.cosmos.esa.int/web/hipparcos/catalogue-summary. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Gaia Data Release 3 (Gaia DR3),” Gaia, ESA Cosmos, https://www.cosmos.esa.int/web/gaia/dr3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does one star have so many different names?","a":"Because each catalogue numbers its own stars, and as catalogues multiplied, astronomers began calling stars by their catalogue numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So Betelgeuse is HD 39801 in the Henry Draper Catalogue and HIP 27989 in the Hipparcos Catalogue, among dozens of other designations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Henry Draper Catalogue so important?","a":"Because, with its first extension, it supplied spectral classifications for 272,150 stars and was widely used for over half a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its classes, rearranged by Cannon in order of decreasing temperature, became the sequence O, B, A, F, G, K, M.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do galaxies and nebulae carry different kinds of designation from stars?","a":"Because they come from separate lists of fuzzy-looking objects, such as the one Charles Messier made in 1781.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Andromeda Galaxy is therefore M31 in Messier's list and NGC 224 in another catalogue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Henry Draper Catalogue"},
    "astro-075": {"questions":["Only about 8.6 light-years away and some 25 times as luminous as the Sun, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> owes its brilliance to nearness as much as to power, unlike the far more distant Canopus.","A white dwarf with about the Sun's mass but less than Earth's diameter orbits <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> A, and it stayed unseen until 1862 because the two stars lie so close together."],"abstract":"<b>Sirius</b> is the brightest star in the night sky, a star of the constellation Canis Major with an apparent visual magnitude of about −1.46.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Bayer designation is Alpha Canis Majoris, and it is also HD 48915 in the Henry Draper Catalogue, HR 2491 in the Bright Star Catalogue and HIP 32349 in the Hipparcos Catalogue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Like most of the brightest stars it keeps a name inherited from antiquity, in its case a Greek one meaning ‘the scorched one’, and the IAU's Working Group on Star Names adopted Sirius as the star's official name in 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On the magnitude scale, where smaller numbers mean brighter objects, accurate measurement shows that Sirius sends us about ten times as much light as the average first-magnitude star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only the Sun and a few objects in the Solar System outshine it: Venus at its brightest reaches magnitude −4.4 and the Sun −26.8.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Sirius looks so bright largely because it is close, about 8.6 light-years away according to its measured parallax of about 379 milliarcseconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is also genuinely powerful, giving out about 25 times as much light as the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Canopus, the second brightest star at about magnitude −0.7, lies roughly 310 light-years away by its measured parallax, so it must be intrinsically far more luminous than Sirius to look nearly as bright, while Arcturus, at about magnitude zero, is about 37 light-years away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Sirius is a double star, and its companion, Sirius B, is a white dwarf about 10,000 times dimmer, with roughly the mass of the Sun packed into a body about 90 per cent of the Earth's diameter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sirius A is more than twice as massive as the Sun, and because the two stars are so close together in the sky, its faint partner escaped detection until 1862.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["SIMBAD Astronomical Database, “alf CMa — Sirius,” Centre de Données astronomiques de Strasbourg, CfA mirror, accessed 7 November 2026, https://simbad.cfa.harvard.edu/simbad/sim-id?Ident=Sirius. [Open access]","IAU Working Group on Star Names, “IAU Catalog of Star Names (IAU-CSN),” last updated 4 April 2022, https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~emamajek/WGSN/IAU-CSN.txt. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “19.2 Surveying the Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-2-surveying-the-stars. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “17.1 The Brightness of Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/17-1-the-brightness-of-stars. [Open access]","Chandra X-ray Center, “Sirius A and B: A Double Star System in the Constellation Canis Major,” Chandra X-ray Observatory, Harvard–Smithsonian Center for Astrophysics, 18 October 2000, https://chandra.harvard.edu/photo/2000/0065/. [Open access]","SIMBAD Astronomical Database, “alf Car — Canopus,” Centre de Données astronomiques de Strasbourg, CfA mirror, accessed 7 November 2026, https://simbad.cfa.harvard.edu/simbad/sim-id?Ident=Canopus. [Open access]","IAU Working Group on Star Names, <i>Bulletin of the IAU Working Group on Star Names</i>, no. 1 (July 2016), https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~emamajek/WGSN/WGSN_bulletin1.pdf. [Open access]","SIMBAD Astronomical Database, “alf Boo — Arcturus,” Centre de Données astronomiques de Strasbourg, CfA mirror, accessed 7 November 2026, https://simbad.cfa.harvard.edu/simbad/sim-id?Ident=Arcturus. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Sirius look brighter than stars that are far more powerful?","a":"Because how bright a star looks depends on its distance as well as its power, and Sirius is only about 8.6 light-years away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Canopus, the second brightest star, lies roughly 310 light-years away by its measured parallax, so it must give out far more light to look nearly as bright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the brightest star given a negative magnitude?","a":"Because the magnitude scale runs backwards, with brighter objects given smaller numbers, and Sirius proved about ten times brighter than an average first-magnitude star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the modern scale it is about −1.5, while Venus at its brightest reaches −4.4.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Sirius B so hard to find?","a":"Because it is about 10,000 times dimmer than Sirius A in visible light and the two stars lie very close together in the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It escaped detection until 1862 even though it has about the mass of the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sirius"},
    "astro-076": {"questions":["Because the Sun is about 400 times wider than the Moon and about 400 times farther away, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be total, with the Sun's bright disc completely hidden for a few minutes.","When the Moon is near the far point of its orbit, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leaves a bright ring of sunlight around the Moon's dark disc and is called annular."],"abstract":"A <b>solar eclipse</b> happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and the Earth and its shadow falls on part of the Earth's surface, hiding all or part of the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It can happen only at new moon, and only when the new moon falls near one of the two points where the Moon's tilted orbit crosses the plane of the Earth's orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> The Moon's shadow has a dark inner cone, the umbra, and a lighter, more diffuse outer region, the penumbra, and what an observer sees depends on which part passes overhead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Inside the umbra the Sun is completely hidden in a total eclipse, while the much wider penumbra sees only a partial eclipse, with part of the Sun covered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between 2000 BCE and 3000 CE the Earth will see 11,898 solar eclipses, and every calendar year has at least two and at most five, although each is visible from only a small part of the globe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> A total eclipse is possible because of a coincidence: the Sun is about 400 times wider than the Moon but also about 400 times farther away, so both look about half a degree across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both distances vary, and when the Moon is near the far point of its orbit it looks slightly smaller than the Sun and leaves a bright ring around its dark disc, an annular eclipse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Because the Earth's surface is curved, some eclipses shift between annular and total along their path across the globe, and these are called hybrid eclipses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Over the five millennia from 2000 BCE to 3000 CE, about 35 per cent of solar eclipses are partial, 33 per cent annular, 27 per cent total and 5 per cent hybrid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The uncovered part of the Sun is as dangerous to look at as ever, so except during the brief total phase an eclipse must be watched through eclipse glasses or by projection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.7 Eclipses of the Sun and Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-7-eclipses-of-the-sun-and-moon. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Types of Solar Eclipses,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/types/. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Five Millennium Catalog of Solar Eclipses: −1999 to +3000 (2000 BCE to 3000 CE),” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, 2014, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEcat5/SEcatalog.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Why Do Eclipses Happen?,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/geometry/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipse Viewing Safety,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/safety/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipses and the Moon,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/eclipses/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipse Glossary,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/glossary/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipses Frequently Asked Questions,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/faq/. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Periodicity of Solar Eclipses,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, 2012, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEsaros/SEperiodicity.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can the small Moon cover the much larger Sun?","a":"Because the Sun is about 400 times wider than the Moon but also about 400 times farther away, so both look about half a degree across in our sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the Moon is near enough, its dark umbral shadow reaches the ground and the Sun is completely hidden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do most people rarely see a total solar eclipse?","a":"Because the Moon's dark umbral shadow covers only a small area, sweeping along a thin band across the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A much wider region sees only a partial eclipse, so although every year has at least two solar eclipses, a total one rarely reaches any given place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is a partial eclipse dangerous to watch?","a":"Because the part of the Sun that is still uncovered is just as bright and damaging as ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> NASA advises eclipse glasses or a projection method such as a pinhole viewer for every phase except totality, when the Sun's bright face is completely hidden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Solar eclipse"},
    "astro-077": {"questions":["Aristotle argued that the Earth must be a sphere because the edge of its shadow, seen on the Moon during a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, is always curved.","In a penumbral <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Moon crosses only the faint outer part of the Earth's shadow and dims so slightly that a casual watcher may not notice."],"abstract":"A <b>lunar eclipse</b> happens when the Earth passes directly between the Sun and the Moon and the Earth's shadow falls on the Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It can occur only at full moon, near one of the two nodes where the Moon's tilted orbit crosses the ecliptic, and each time the Sun passes a node there is always one lunar eclipse and sometimes two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The eclipse is total if the Moon passes wholly into the dark inner shadow, the umbra, partial if only part of it does, and penumbral if it crosses only the faint outer shadow, the penumbra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A penumbral eclipse leaves the Moon still partly lit by direct sunlight and darkens it so slightly that it is easy to miss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between 2000 BCE and 3000 CE there are 12,064 lunar eclipses, about 36 per cent of them penumbral, 35 per cent partial and 29 per cent total, and every calendar year has from two to five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Even when totally eclipsed the Moon stays faintly visible, usually a dull coppery red, because sunlight is bent into the Earth's shadow by refraction as it passes through the atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The air scatters away the blue part of that light and lets the red through, so the Moon is lit as if by all the world's sunrises and sunsets, and the more dust or cloud there is, the redder it looks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> This is the popular 'blood moon', and some ancient texts that speak of the Moon turning to blood may be describing a lunar eclipse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Unlike a solar eclipse, a lunar eclipse can be seen from the whole night side of the Earth, so any one place sees lunar eclipses far more often, and when the Moon crosses the centre of the shadow, totality can last as long as 1 hour 40 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lunar eclipses also supplied one of the oldest arguments for a round Earth, for Aristotle noted that the Earth's shadow on the Moon is always curved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.7 Eclipses of the Sun and Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-7-eclipses-of-the-sun-and-moon. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipses and the Moon,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/eclipses/. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Index to Five Millennium Catalog of Lunar Eclipses: −1999 to +3000 (2000 BCE to 3000 CE),” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, 2014, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/LEcat5/LEcatalog.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.2 Ancient Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-2-ancient-astronomy. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipse Glossary,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/glossary/. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Periodicity of Lunar Eclipses,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, 2012, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/LEsaros/LEperiodicity.html. [Open access]","Royal Observatory Greenwich, “Lunar Eclipse Guide: When and Where to See in the UK,” Royal Museums Greenwich, https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/space-astronomy/lunar-eclipse-guide. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Types of Solar Eclipses,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/types/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “History of Eclipses,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/history/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the eclipsed Moon turn red instead of vanishing?","a":"Because some sunlight still reaches it after being bent through the Earth's atmosphere into the shadow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The air scatters the blue light away and lets the red through, so the Moon is lit by what amounts to all the world's sunrises and sunsets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are lunar eclipses seen more often than solar ones from any one place?","a":"Because a lunar eclipse is visible from the whole night side of the Earth, wherever the Moon is above the horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A total solar eclipse, by contrast, is seen only along the narrow band where the Moon's dark shadow falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a total lunar eclipse last so much longer than a total solar eclipse?","a":"Because the Earth is larger than the Moon, so its umbra is larger too and the Moon takes longer to cross it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Moon passes through the centre of the shadow, totality can last up to about 1 hour 40 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lunar eclipse"},
    "astro-078": {"questions":["Each ascending or descending <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> slides slowly westward along the ecliptic, completing a circuit in about 18.6 years, which is why eclipse seasons recur every 173 days rather than every half year.","The draconic month of about 27.21 days is the time the Moon takes to return to the same <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of two points where its tilted orbit meets the ecliptic plane."],"abstract":"A <b>lunar node</b> is either of the two points where the Moon's orbit crosses the plane of the Earth's orbit around the Sun, the plane whose path across the sky is the ecliptic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Moon's orbit is tilted to that plane at an average angle of about 5.1 degrees, so for most of each month the Moon lies north or south of the ecliptic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The node where the Moon crosses the ecliptic from south to north is the ascending node, and the one where it crosses from north to south is the descending node.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The nodes are not fixed but slide slowly westward along the ecliptic, completing a full circuit in about 18.6 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The time the Moon takes to return to the same node, the draconic month, averages about 27.21 days, a little shorter than its circuit measured against the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> If the Moon's path in the sky were the same as the Sun's, there would be an eclipse of the Sun and one of the Moon every month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because of the tilt, though, a new moon usually passes above or below the Sun and its shadow misses the Earth, and a full moon usually misses the Earth's shadow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A solar eclipse is possible only if the new moon falls within about 17 degrees of a node, a zone the Sun takes about 34.5 days to cross.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Since that crossing lasts longer than the 29.5-day cycle of phases, each one, called an eclipse season, brings at least one solar eclipse and sometimes two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Sun reaches a node about every 173.3 days, a little less than half a year because the nodes drift westward to meet it, so eclipse seasons come roughly twice a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Fred Espenak, “Eclipses and the Moon’s Orbit,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, 2012, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEhelp/moonorbit.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipse Glossary,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/glossary/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.7 Eclipses of the Sun and Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-7-eclipses-of-the-sun-and-moon. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Why Do Eclipses Happen?,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/geometry/. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Periodicity of Solar Eclipses,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, 2012, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEsaros/SEperiodicity.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipses Frequently Asked Questions,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/faq/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is there not an eclipse every month?","a":"Because the Moon's orbit is tilted about 5 degrees to the plane of the Earth's orbit, so at most new and full moons the Moon passes above or below the line to the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only when a new or full moon falls near a node do the Sun, Earth and Moon line up closely enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do eclipses come in seasons about twice a year?","a":"Because the Sun, moving along the ecliptic, passes each of the two nodes about once a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A solar eclipse needs the new moon within about 17 degrees of a node, and the Sun takes about 34.5 days to cross that zone, longer than a lunar month, so each crossing brings at least one eclipse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are eclipse seasons slightly less than six months apart?","a":"Because the nodes themselves drift westward along the ecliptic, by about 19.3 degrees a year, and so move to meet the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sun therefore reaches the next node after about 173.3 days rather than half a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lunar node"},
    "astro-079": {"questions":["Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs about 8 hours beyond a whole number of days, each solar eclipse in a series has its path about 120 degrees west of the one before.","Pliny the Elder knew that eclipses recur after 223 months, but the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came from an error in an 11th-century Byzantine lexicon and was first attached to the cycle in 1691."],"abstract":"The <b>saros</b> is a cycle of about 6,585.3 days, or 18 years 11 days 8 hours, after which eclipses of the Sun and Moon repeat with very similar geometry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It works because three lunar periods almost coincide: 223 months of phases, 239 returns to perigee and 242 returns to the same node all fall within a few hours of one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two eclipses one saros apart therefore happen at the same node, with the Moon at nearly the same distance from the Earth and at nearly the same time of year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A 223-month recurrence was known in antiquity, and Pliny the Elder reports that the eclipses complete their whole revolution in the space of 223 months, while the Greek Antikythera mechanism predicted eclipses from Babylonian cycles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The name itself is a later borrowing, adopted in 1691 from the 11th-century Byzantine lexicon of Suidas, which had wrongly linked a Babylonian term for 3,600 years to that eclipse period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because the saros is not a whole number of days, the Earth turns about 8 hours further each cycle, so each successive solar eclipse path lies about 120 degrees farther west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An eclipse therefore returns to roughly the same region only after three saroses, about 54 years and 34 days, a period called the exeligmos, which ancient astronomers also knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Each eclipse in a series also shifts slightly north or south, so a series begins with small partial eclipses near one pole, produces central eclipses for several centuries and dies away near the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A complete series lasts about 1,226 to 1,550 years and contains 69 to 87 eclipses, and around forty series are in progress at any one time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saros 145, for example, produced the total eclipse of 11 August 1999 and its successor in 2017, one saros later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Fred Espenak, “Eclipses and the Saros,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, 2012, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEsaros/SEsaros.html. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Periodicity of Solar Eclipses,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, 2012, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEsaros/SEperiodicity.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipses Frequently Asked Questions,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/faq/. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 2.10, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plin.+Nat.+2.10. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipse Glossary,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/glossary/. [Open access]","T. Freeth, Y. Bitsakis, X. Moussas, et al., “Decoding the Ancient Greek Astronomical Calculator Known as the Antikythera Mechanism,” <i>Nature</i> 444, no. 7119 (2006): 587–91, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature05357. [Paywalled]","R. H. van Gent, “Introduction,” in <i>A Catalogue of Eclipse Cycles</i>, Utrecht University, https://webspace.science.uu.nl/~gent0113/eclipse/eclipsecycles_introduction.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the saros make eclipses repeat so closely?","a":"Because 223 synodic months, 239 anomalistic months and 242 draconic months are equal to within a few hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After one saros the Moon is again new or full, at the same node and at nearly the same distance from the Earth, so the eclipse geometry is almost the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the next eclipse in a series happen somewhere else on Earth?","a":"Because the saros is about 6,585.3 days, roughly a third of a day more than a whole number, so the Earth has turned about 8 hours further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each solar eclipse path is shifted about 120 degrees west, and only after three saroses, the exeligmos, does a series return near the same region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a saros series eventually end?","a":"Because the three lunar months are not perfectly in step, so the Moon's position relative to the node shifts a little with each eclipse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eclipses creep from one pole to the other over 12 to 15 centuries, starting and ending with small partial eclipses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Saros (astronomy)"},
    "astro-080": {"questions":["The longest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> expected between 4000 BCE and 8000 CE will last 7 minutes 29 seconds, on 16 July 2186, along a track across Colombia, Venezuela and Guyana.","Seen only from a path at most about 270 kilometres (168 miles) wide, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> darkens the daytime sky enough for planets and bright stars to appear and chickens to roost."],"abstract":"<b>Totality</b> is the phase of a solar eclipse during which the Moon completely covers the bright disc of the Sun, and, in a lunar eclipse, the time the Moon spends wholly inside the Earth's shadow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a solar eclipse it is seen only from inside the Moon's darkest shadow, whose track across the Earth, the path of totality, is at most about 270 kilometres (168 miles) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Moon's motion sweeps that shadow across the Earth in four to five hours, so at any one place the total phase lasts only minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Totality lasts from about 10 seconds to about 7.5 minutes, and the longest between 4000 BCE and 8000 CE will last 7 minutes 29 seconds on 16 July 2186, crossing Colombia, Venezuela and Guyana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> As it begins, the sky darkens to an eerie twilight, planets and the brighter stars appear, some flowers close and chickens may go to roost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In the last seconds before totality, sunlight shines only through valleys on the Moon's edge as Baily's beads, and then through a single valley as one brilliant point, the diamond ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A thin crimson ring may then show around the edge of the Sun, the chromosphere, the lower layer of the Sun's atmosphere just above its visible surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once the bright disc is hidden, the pearly white corona, the Sun's thin outer atmosphere, comes into view, normally lost because its light is feeble beside that of the layers beneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The innermost corona can be studied from the ground only during totality, which helps scientists work out how heat and energy pass from the Sun into the solar wind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Totality is the only time an eclipse may be watched without eye protection, and eclipse glasses must go back on as soon as any part of the Sun's bright face reappears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Eclipse Glossary,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/glossary/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Why Do Eclipses Happen?,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/geometry/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipses Frequently Asked Questions,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/faq/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.7 Eclipses of the Sun and Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-7-eclipses-of-the-sun-and-moon. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Types of Solar Eclipses,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/types/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Eclipse Viewing Safety,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/safety/. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Five Millennium Catalog of Solar Eclipses: −1999 to +3000 (2000 BCE to 3000 CE),” NASA Eclipse Web Site, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, 2014, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEcat5/SEcatalog.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can the corona be seen only during totality?","a":"Because the corona's light is feeble compared with the light from the Sun's brilliant disc, which normally drowns it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only when the Moon blots out that disc completely does the pearly white corona become visible from the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does totality last only a few minutes at any one place?","a":"Because the Moon's motion sweeps its shadow across the Earth in just four to five hours, along a path of totality at most about 270 kilometres wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Any one spot is inside the darkest shadow for only a few minutes, never more than about 7.5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it safe to look at the Sun during totality but not just before?","a":"Because during totality the Moon completely covers the Sun's bright face, which is what damages the eyes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Before and after, even a thin sliver of uncovered Sun is dangerous, so eclipse glasses or a solar viewer must be used for every partial phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-081": {"questions":["Modern calculation places the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 28 May 585 BCE, a total eclipse in the reign of Alyattes of Lydia that Pliny dated to the 170th year of Rome.","Because Mesopotamian scribes could flag the months when an eclipse was possible but never where a solar one would be seen, the forecast behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remains unexplained."],"abstract":"The <b>eclipse of Thales</b> is the total eclipse of the Sun that, according to Herodotus, fell in the middle of a battle between the Lydians and the Medes in the sixth year of their war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Day suddenly turned to night, and both armies stopped fighting and became the more eager to make peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Lydian king was Alyattes and the Median king Cyaxares, and modern calculation places the event on 28 May 585 BCE, when the Moon's full shadow passed over the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A NASA table of historical eclipses lists that date for the story, while warning that such links between an eclipse and an ancient event are offered for comparison rather than as proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pliny the Elder dated the same eclipse to the fourth year of the 48th Olympiad, in the reign of Alyattes and the 170th year of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What made the event famous was the claim that Thales of Miletus had foretold it to the Ionians, fixing it within the year in which it happened, and later writers repeated the claim, Diogenes Laertius citing Eudemus for Thales as the first to predict eclipses of the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Ancient astronomers could know when an eclipse was possible at all, because the Moon's tilted path crosses the Sun's only twice a year, so eclipses cluster in two seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Assyrian and Babylonian scribes knew that solar eclipses come only at new moon and lunar ones only at full moon, and by about 650 BCE they could single out the months when an eclipse might occur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A lunar eclipse is seen from about half the globe but a solar eclipse only from a small area, and the 18-year cycle used for lunar eclipses moves each repeat of a solar eclipse about a third of the way round the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How Thales made his forecast, if he made it at all, is therefore unknown, and the eclipse cycles known in his day could not have told him where the shadow would fall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.74, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Hdt.%201.74. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 2.9, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plin.+Nat.+2.9. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Solar Eclipses of Historical Interest,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, Goddard Space Flight Center, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEhistory/SEhistory.html. [Open access]","Fred Espenak, “Eclipses and the Saros,” NASA Eclipse Web Site, Goddard Space Flight Center, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEsaros/SEsaros.html. [Open access]","Patricia O’Grady, “Thales of Miletus,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, https://iep.utm.edu/thales/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.7 Eclipses of the Sun and Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-7-eclipses-of-the-sun-and-moon. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 1.23, trans. R. D. Hicks, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0258:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the battle stop when the eclipse came?","a":"Because the sky went dark in the middle of the fighting. Herodotus says that when the Lydians and Medes saw the day turned to night they stopped fighting and both became more eager to make peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The armies seem to have taken the darkness as a warning against their war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a solar eclipse so much harder to forecast than a lunar one?","a":"Because a lunar eclipse can be seen from about half the globe, while a solar eclipse is seen only from a small area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Eclipses can happen only in the two seasons a year when the Moon's tilted path crosses the Sun's,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> so a scribe could flag a possible month, but not whether the Moon's shadow would reach his own country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the date of 585 BCE accepted?","a":"Because calculation shows a total eclipse of the Sun on 28 May 585 BCE whose shadow passed over the region, which fits Herodotus's story and the reign of Alyattes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pliny also placed the eclipse in Alyattes's reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> NASA's own table cautions, though, that linking an eclipse to an ancient story is a comparison, not a proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Eclipse of Thales"},
    "astro-082": {"questions":["The Sun is 27 million times more massive than the Moon, yet it raises <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only about half as large, because tidal force falls with the cube of distance.","Friction from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is slowly braking the Earth's spin, lengthening the day by about 0.002 seconds a century and pushing the Moon about 3.8 centimetres (1.5 inches) farther away each year."],"abstract":"<b>Tides</b> are the regular rise and fall of the sea, caused mainly by the Moon's gravity pulling unequally on different parts of the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The side of the Earth facing the Moon is pulled more strongly than the centre, and the centre more strongly than the far side, so the planet is stretched slightly along the line to the Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water flows over the surface towards the regions beneath and opposite the Moon and piles up in two bulges, which is why there is high water on the far side of the Earth as well as the near one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> As the Earth turns, a coast passes through both bulges, so most shores see two high and two low tides in every lunar day of 24 hours and 50 minutes, with high tides about 12 hours and 25 minutes apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Sun raises tides too, but although it is about 27 million times more massive than the Moon it is about 390 times farther away, and tidal force falls with the cube of distance, leaving it about half as effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Real tides are much messier than this picture, because continents block the bulges and each ocean basin develops its own pattern of rise and fall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most coasts have two roughly equal high tides a day, some have two unequal ones, and parts of the Gulf of Mexico have only one high and one low tide each day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The shape of a coastline plays a major role, and the greatest tides in the world are found in Canada's Bay of Fundy, between New Brunswick and Nova Scotia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the tidal bulges run slightly ahead of the Moon, tidal friction is slowly braking the Earth's spin and lengthening the day by about 0.002 seconds a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same exchange pushes the Moon outward, and laser ranging to reflectors left on its surface measures the retreat at about 3.8 centimetres (1.5 inches) a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.6 Ocean Tides and the Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-6-ocean-tides-and-the-moon. [Open access]","National Ocean Service, “What Causes Tides?,” in <i>Tides and Water Levels</i>, NOAA Ocean Service Education, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/education/tutorial_tides/tides02_cause.html. [Open access]","National Ocean Service, “Types and Causes of Tidal Cycles,” in <i>Tides and Water Levels</i>, NOAA Ocean Service Education, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/education/tutorial_tides/tides07_cycles.html. [Open access]","National Ocean Service, “Where Is the Highest Tide?,” Ocean Facts, NOAA, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/highesttide.html. [Open access]","Tracy Vogel, “Tides,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/tides/. [Open access]","T. W. Murphy Jr., “Lunar Laser Ranging: The Millimeter Challenge,” <i>Reports on Progress in Physics</i> 76, no. 7 (2013): 076901, https://doi.org/10.1088/0034-4885/76/7/076901. [Open access]","National Ocean Service, “What Are Tides?,” in <i>Tides and Water Levels</i>, NOAA Ocean Service Education, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/education/tutorial_tides/tides01_intro.html. [Open access]","National Ocean Service, “Frequency of Tides: The Lunar Day,” in <i>Tides and Water Levels</i>, NOAA Ocean Service Education, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/education/tutorial_tides/tides05_lunarday.html. [Open access]","National Ocean Service, “Gravity, Inertia, and the Two Bulges,” in <i>Tides and Water Levels</i>, NOAA Ocean Service Education, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/education/tutorial_tides/tides03_gravity.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is there a high tide on the side of the Earth facing away from the Moon?","a":"Because the Moon pulls hardest on the near side of the Earth, less on the centre and least on the far side, so the planet is stretched along the line to the Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water flows towards the regions beneath and opposite the Moon and piles up in two bulges, one on each side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Sun's tidal effect smaller than the Moon's?","a":"Because tide-raising force falls off with the cube of the distance to the body that raises it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sun is about 27 million times more massive than the Moon but about 390 times farther away, which leaves it a little less than half the Moon's tide-generating force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some places have far bigger tides than others?","a":"Because continents block the free movement of the tidal bulges, and each ocean basin develops its own pattern of tides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The shape and geometry of a coastline also play a major role, which is why the world's greatest tides are in Canada's Bay of Fundy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Tide"},
    "astro-083": {"questions":["Despite its name, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has nothing to do with the season, coming all year round whenever the Sun, Moon and Earth fall nearly into line.","A week after each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Moon at first or last quarter pulls at right angles to the Sun and the moderate neap tides follow."],"abstract":"A <b>spring tide</b> is a tide of larger range than usual, with higher high waters and lower low waters, that comes at new moon and full moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At those phases the Sun, Moon and Earth lie nearly in a line, so the pull of the Sun is added to that of the Moon and the oceans bulge a little more than usual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name has nothing to do with the season: it comes from the idea of the tide springing forth, and spring tides happen twice every lunar month all year round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About a week after each spring tide the Moon stands at first or last quarter, at right angles to the Sun, and the solar tide partly cancels the lunar one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The result is a neap tide, a period of moderate tides when high water is a little lower and low water a little higher than average.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The cycle follows from the solar tide being about half as large as the lunar tide, which makes it a variation on the lunar pattern rather than a separate set of tides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spring tides are about the same at new and full moon, because the tidal bulges form on both sides of the Earth whichever side the Sun is on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Distance changes the size of tides too, for the Moon's elliptical orbit brings it closest at perigee once a month, when tidal ranges are larger than average.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Six to eight times a year a new or full moon falls close to perigee, and the high waters of these perigean spring tides can be markedly higher than at other times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> King tide is a popular, non-scientific name for such exceptionally high tides, which typically come at a new or full moon with the Moon near perigee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["National Ocean Service, “What Are Spring and Neap Tides?,” Ocean Facts, NOAA, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/springtide.html. [Open access]","National Ocean Service, “Tidal Variations: The Influence of Position and Distance,” in <i>Tides and Water Levels</i>, NOAA Ocean Service Education, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/education/tutorial_tides/tides06_variations.html. [Open access]","National Ocean Service, “What Is a Perigean Spring Tide?,” Ocean Facts, NOAA, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/perigean-spring-tide.html. [Open access]","National Ocean Service, “What Is a King Tide?,” Ocean Facts, NOAA, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/kingtide.html. [Open access]","Tracy Vogel, “Tides,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/tides/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.6 Ocean Tides and the Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-6-ocean-tides-and-the-moon. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.5 Phases and Motions of the Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-5-phases-and-motions-of-the-moon. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are spring tides as strong at full moon as at new moon?","a":"Because the tidal bulges form on both sides of the Earth at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At new moon the Moon lies between the Earth and the Sun, and at full moon the Earth lies between them, but in both cases the three are nearly in line and the Sun's pull is added to the Moon's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do neap tides come a week after spring tides?","a":"Because about a week after new or full moon the Moon is a quarter of the way further round its orbit, at first or last quarter, and at right angles to the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Then the bulge raised by the Sun partly cancels the bulge raised by the Moon, giving moderate tides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are some spring tides much higher than others?","a":"Because distance matters as well as alignment. The Moon's elliptical orbit brings it closest at perigee once a month, when tide-generating forces are stronger than usual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When a new or full moon falls close to perigee, six to eight times a year, the perigean spring tide can be markedly higher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-084": {"questions":["Pluto and Charon show mutual <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each keeping the same face towards the other, so that Charon neither rises nor sets but hangs over one spot.","A tidal bulge dragged out of line by a fast-spinning moon flexes it and wastes its spin as heat until <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sets in and one rotation equals one orbit."],"abstract":"<b>Tidal locking</b> is the state in which a body takes exactly as long to spin once on its axis as it does to orbit its partner, so that it always keeps the same face towards it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Moon is the familiar case, for it rotates in the same period of about 27.3 days in which it circles the Earth, which is why the same hemisphere always faces us and nobody saw the far side until a Soviet spacecraft flew past in 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The state is common: all the Solar System's large moons are locked to their planets, and some binary stars are locked to each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pluto and its moon Charon are locked mutually, each keeping the same face towards the other, so Charon neither rises nor sets but hangs over one spot on Pluto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Evidence is also building that many planets around other stars are tidally locked to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Locking happens because a planet's gravity raises a bulge on its moon, and while the moon spins faster than it orbits the bulge is carried out of line and pulled back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That constant flexing turns energy of rotation into heat, slowing the spin until one rotation takes as long as one orbit and the bulge stops moving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Moon probably locked early, when it was hotter, less solid and closer to the Earth, and large moons are thought to lock within hundreds of thousands of orbits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The process still works on the Earth, whose ocean tides brake its spin while the Moon drifts outward by about 3.8 centimetres (1.5 inches) a year, a rate measured by laser ranging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only after tens of billions of years would the day and the month match, locking the Earth to the Moon as well, and the Sun is expected to die long before then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Tracy Vogel, “Tidal Locking,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/tidal-locking/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.5 Phases and Motions of the Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-5-phases-and-motions-of-the-moon. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.6 Ocean Tides and the Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-6-ocean-tides-and-the-moon. [Open access]","NASA, “Charon,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/pluto/moons/charon/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Moon Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/moonfact.html. [Open access]","T. W. Murphy Jr., “Lunar Laser Ranging: The Millimeter Challenge,” <i>Reports on Progress in Physics</i> 76, no. 7 (2013): 076901, https://doi.org/10.1088/0034-4885/76/7/076901. [Open access]","NASA, “Earth’s Moon: Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the Moon always show the same face?","a":"Because it rotates once in exactly the time it takes to orbit the Earth, a little over 27 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> If it did not rotate at all it would show us all its sides in turn; turning in step with its orbit keeps the same hemisphere towards us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a tidal bulge slow a moon's spin?","a":"Because the bulge is always carried slightly out of line by the spin and pulled back towards the planet by gravity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The flexing releases energy as heat, and the spin slows until rotation and orbit match, when the bulge no longer moves and the slowing stops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Moon moving away from the Earth?","a":"Because the Earth's ocean bulges are always a little out of step with the Moon, which slows the Earth's rotation and changes the Moon's orbital speed so that it drifts outward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Laser ranging to reflectors on the Moon measures the retreat at about 3.8 centimetres (1.5 inches) a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Tidal locking"},
    "astro-085": {"questions":["Clocks keep the mean <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of exactly 24 hours, while a sundial's version wanders through the year and can run as much as 16 minutes fast or slow.","Mercury spins once in 59 Earth days, yet its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one full cycle of day and night, lasts 176 Earth days, just over two of its years."],"abstract":"The <b>solar day</b> is the time the Earth takes to turn once relative to the Sun, from one noon to the next, and it is the day on which ordinary clocks are based.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Measured against the distant stars instead, one turn of the Earth takes a shorter sidereal day of about 23 hours, 56 minutes and 4 seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two differ because the Earth moves about one degree along its orbit each day, so it must turn about 1/365 of a rotation more before the Sun is back in the same place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That extra turn takes about four minutes, which is why a given star rises about four minutes earlier each night and the constellations shift with the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Astronomers plan their observations in sidereal time because in that system a star rises at the same time every day, whereas by the clock it rises earlier each night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The day shown by a sundial, apparent solar time, is not uniform, because the Earth's speed varies along its elliptical orbit and its axis is tilted to that orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Clocks therefore follow mean solar time, set by an imaginary mean sun moving at a steady rate, and a mean solar day contains exactly 24 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The difference between sundial time and clock time, called the equation of time, builds up to as much as 16 minutes at some seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Universal Time in its astronomical form, UT1, is a modern form of mean solar time on the Greenwich meridian, measured from the rotation of the Earth itself and so affected by its small irregularities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On other worlds the gap can be huge: Mercury spins once every 59 Earth days, but one full day-night cycle there lasts 176 Earth days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.3 Keeping Time,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-3-keeping-time. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “The Equation of Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/eqtime. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Universal Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/UT. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]","NASA, “Mercury: Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/mercury/facts/. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Computing Approximate Sidereal Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/GAST. [Open access]","National Ocean Service, “Frequency of Tides: The Lunar Day,” in <i>Tides and Water Levels</i>, NOAA Ocean Service Education, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/education/tutorial_tides/tides05_lunarday.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the solar day longer than the sidereal day?","a":"Because the Earth moves along its orbit while it turns, about one degree a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After one full turn against the stars the Sun is not yet back in the same place, and the extra 1/365 of a turn needed takes about four minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mean sidereal day is about 23 hours, 56 minutes and 4 seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do clocks not follow sundial time?","a":"Because the Sun's apparent motion across the sky is not uniform, so the length of an apparent solar day varies through the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Clocks run at a steady rate, so they follow an imaginary mean sun instead, and the gap between the two kinds of time can reach about 16 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the stars rise earlier each night?","a":"Because clocks are set to solar time, and the stars complete their circle of the sky about four minutes faster than the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four minutes a day adds up to about two hours a month, so a constellation that rises at sunset in winter rises with the Sun by summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Synodic day"},
    "astro-086": {"questions":["Measured from equinox to equinox, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is about 20 minutes shorter than the sidereal year measured against the stars, because precession slowly shifts the equinox.","The Gregorian calendar's average year of 365.2425 days matches the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to within about half a minute, leaving a day's error only after some 3,300 years."],"abstract":"The <b>tropical year</b> is the time the Sun takes to move once round the ecliptic measured from the equinox, which brings it through one complete cycle of the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its mean length is about 365 days, 5 hours, 48 minutes and 45 seconds, or about 365.2422 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is the year that a solar calendar tries to follow, because a calendar is meant to keep each season on roughly the same dates year after year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it is not a whole number of days, a calendar of 365 days would slip by about 24 days a century against the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Gregorian calendar's average of 365.2425 days matches it to within about half a minute, so the two drift a full day apart only after about 3,300 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The tropical year is about 20 minutes shorter than the sidereal year, the time the Earth takes to go round the Sun measured against the distant stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The difference arises because the equinox is not fixed: the Earth's axis wobbles like a spinning top in a cycle of about 26,000 years, a motion called precession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As the axis turns, the equinox shifts slowly along the ecliptic, so the Sun returns to it a little before the Earth has completed a full circuit against the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same wobble has moved the north celestial pole from near the star Thuban about 5,000 years ago to near Polaris today, and in about 14,000 years it will lie close to the bright star Vega.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For a time before 1967 the second itself was defined as a fraction of the tropical year, and the slight mismatch inherited from that definition is one reason leap seconds are needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.4 The Calendar,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-4-the-calendar. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “2.2 Ancient Astronomy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-2-ancient-astronomy. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Leap Years,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/leap_years. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]","National Institute of Standards and Technology, “Leap Seconds FAQs,” Time and Frequency Division, https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/leap-seconds-faqs. [Open access]","Michael A. Lombardi, “Fundamentals of Time and Frequency,” in <i>The Mechatronics Handbook</i>, ed. Robert H. Bishop (Boca Raton: CRC Press, 2002), chap. 17, https://tf.nist.gov/general/pdf/1498.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the tropical year shorter than the sidereal year?","a":"Because it is measured from the equinox, and the equinox is not fixed among the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Precession, a slow wobble of the Earth's axis over about 26,000 years, shifts it, so the Sun reaches the equinox again about 20 minutes before the Earth completes a full circuit against the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do calendars follow the tropical year rather than the sidereal year?","a":"Because a calendar is meant to keep the seasons on the same dates, and the tropical year is by definition one complete cycle of the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Gregorian calendar was built to match it, which is why its average year of 365.2425 days is so close to 365.2422.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a calendar never match the tropical year exactly?","a":"Because the tropical year is not a whole number of days, about 365.2422, and a calendar year must contain whole days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Leap days remove most of the error, and the Gregorian rule leaves only about one day of drift in 3,300 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Tropical year"},
    "astro-087": {"questions":["Because the first crescent after new moon cannot be predicted exactly, months in the Islamic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begin only when the thin Moon is actually seen after sunset.","Unlike lunisolar calendars such as the Hebrew one, which add a 13th month in seven years of every 19, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> follows only the Moon's phases through the year."],"abstract":"A <b>lunar calendar</b> is one whose months follow the cycle of the Moon's phases, each a full lunation or synodic month, without regard to the solar year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That cycle averages about 29.53 days, longer than the Moon's 27.3-day orbit against the stars, because the Earth's motion round the Sun means the Moon must go further to return to the same phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Lunar calendars usually start each month with a new moon or with the first visible crescent after it, and a tabular version alternates months of 29 and 30 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twelve lunar months are on average about 11 days shorter than the civil year, so a lunar year begins about 11 days earlier each year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A purely lunar calendar therefore drifts through the seasons, and the Islamic calendar, still primarily lunar, moves through all of them in about 33 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> For religious purposes Islamic months begin with the first sighting of the crescent Moon with the unaided eye after sunset, a day or so after new moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The time of new moon can be computed exactly, but the first sighting of the crescent cannot, since it depends on the sky, the observer's place and experience, and the Moon's angle from the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Naked-eye sightings as early as 15.5 hours after new moon have been reliably reported, but a sighting within one day is usually difficult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many cultures built lunisolar calendars instead, inserting an extra month every few years, helped by the fact that 235 lunar months come to almost exactly 19 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Hebrew calendar adds a 13th month in seven years of every 19, so that its years keep pace with the tropical year while its months follow the Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Introduction to Calendars,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/calendars. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “The Islamic Calendar,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/islamic. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Crescent Moon Visibility,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/crescent. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.5 Phases and Motions of the Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-5-phases-and-motions-of-the-moon. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.4 The Calendar,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-4-the-calendar. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Claus Tøndering, “The Hebrew Calendar,” in <i>Calendar FAQ</i>, https://www.tondering.dk/claus/cal/hebrew.php. [Open access]","NASA, “Earth’s Moon: Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a lunar calendar drift through the seasons?","a":"Because twelve lunar months are on average about 11 days shorter than the civil year, so each lunar year starts about 11 days earlier in the solar one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The twelve months of the Islamic calendar therefore move back through all the seasons in about 33 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the month of phases longer than the Moon's orbit?","a":"Because the Earth moves round the Sun while the Moon orbits the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Moon circles the Earth against the stars in about 27.3 days, but it must travel a little further to line up with the Sun again, so the phases repeat about every 29.53 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the start of an Islamic month not be fixed in advance?","a":"Because for religious purposes the month begins with an actual sighting of the young crescent with the unaided eye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> New moon can be computed exactly, but when the crescent first becomes visible depends on the sky, the observer and the Moon's angle from the Sun, so it cannot be predicted with certainty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lunar calendar"},
    "astro-088": {"questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, century years are leap years only when divisible by 400, which is why 1900 had no 29 February but the year 2000 did.","Britain and its colonies adopted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only in 1752, when an Act of Parliament made 14 September follow directly on 2 September."],"abstract":"The <b>Gregorian calendar</b> is the solar calendar now used for civil purposes around the world, a reform of the Julian calendar introduced by Pope Gregory XIII in 1582.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was decreed in February 1582 in the papal bull <i>Inter gravissimas</i>, named after its first two words, following instructions from the Council of Trent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its purpose was to keep Easter in place, for the church's rules fixed the vernal equinox on 21 March and reckoned the date of Easter from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Julian year of 365.25 days was about 11 minutes too long, and by 1582 the true equinox had drifted to about 11 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reform dropped ten days at once, so that Thursday 4 October 1582 was followed by Friday 15 October, with no break in the sequence of weekdays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its second step changed the leap-year rule: years divisible by 4 remain leap years, except century years, which are leap years only when divisible by 400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That gives 97 leap days every 400 years and an average year of 365.2425 days, within about half a minute of the tropical year of about 365.2422 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So 1700, 1800 and 1900 had no 29 February while 2000 did, and calendar and seasons will drift a full day apart only after about 3,300 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Catholic countries adopted the reform at once, but most Protestant and Orthodox countries came later, and in Britain and its colonies 2 September 1752 was followed by 14 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Russia changed only after the revolution of 1917, by which time 13 days had to be dropped, and some Eastern churches still reckon Easter by the older Julian method.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “4.4 The Calendar,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-4-the-calendar. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Introduction to Calendars,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/calendars. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Leap Years,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/leap_years. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “The Date of Easter,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/easter. [Open access]","Claus Tøndering, “The Gregorian Calendar,” in <i>Calendar FAQ</i>, https://www.tondering.dk/claus/cal/gregorian.php. [Open access]","<i>Calendar (New Style) Act 1750</i>, 24 Geo. 2, c. 23, legislation.gov.uk, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/apgb/Geo2/24/23. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Julian Date Converter,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/data/JulianDate. [Open access]","National Institute of Standards and Technology, “Leap Seconds FAQs,” Time and Frequency Division, https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/leap-seconds-faqs. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the church want the calendar reformed?","a":"Because Easter was reckoned from a vernal equinox fixed on 21 March, while the real equinox had drifted away from that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Julian year was about 11 minutes too long, and by 1582 the error had carried the start of spring back to about 11 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are most century years not leap years?","a":"Because a leap day every four years makes the average year 365.25 days, slightly longer than the tropical year of about 365.2422 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dropping three leap days in every four centuries gives 97 leap days in 400 years and an average of 365.2425 days, within about half a minute of the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain have to drop eleven days in 1752 rather than ten?","a":"Because 1700 had been a leap year in the Julian calendar but not in the Gregorian, so the gap between them had grown by one more day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Countries that changed in the 1500s and 1600s dropped ten days and those in the 1700s eleven, and the Act of 1750 made 2 September 1752 followed by 14 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Gregorian calendar"},
    "astro-089": {"questions":["Since 1972, every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> added to atomic time has been positive, and 27 have been inserted, the latest at the end of December 2016.","Because each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> risks breaking satellite navigation and telecommunications, the world's metrologists decided in 2022 to widen the permitted gap between UT1 and UTC by 2035."],"abstract":"A <b>leap second</b> is a one-second adjustment to Coordinated Universal Time, UTC, made to keep the world's atomic clock time in step with the rotation of the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> UTC is built from atomic clocks, whose second is defined by 9,192,631,770 cycles of radiation from the caesium-133 atom, while the astronomical time scale UT1 follows the Earth's actual, irregular spin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By international agreement UTC is not allowed to differ from UT1 by more than 0.9 seconds, and when the gap approaches that limit a leap second is added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is inserted as an extra second, 23:59:60, at the end of June or December, and a negative leap second removing one is allowed but has never been used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The International Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service, IERS, measures the Earth's rotation and publishes the difference between UT1 and UTC on which the decision rests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The system began in 1972, when UTC was set ten seconds behind International Atomic Time, and 27 leap seconds have been added since, the latest at the end of 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are needed partly because the atomic second was matched to the older ephemeris second, slightly shorter than a mean solar second, and partly because the Earth's spin is not constant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unlike leap days, which follow a fixed rule, leap seconds are added only when measurements of the Earth's rotation call for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each one is a break in time that risks malfunctions in satellite navigation, telecommunications and energy networks, and recent rotation data suggest a first negative leap second may be needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 2022 the General Conference on Weights and Measures decided that the permitted gap between UT1 and UTC will be increased in or before 2035, so that UTC can run unbroken for at least a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["National Institute of Standards and Technology, “Leap Seconds FAQs,” Time and Frequency Division, https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/leap-seconds-faqs. [Open access]","International Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service, “Leap_Second.dat: Value of TAI−UTC,” IERS Earth Orientation Centre, Paris Observatory, updated through Bulletin C 72 (July 2026), https://hpiers.obspm.fr/iers/bul/bulc/Leap_Second.dat. [Open access]","General Conference on Weights and Measures, “Resolution 4 of the 27th CGPM (2022): On the Use and Future Development of UTC,” Bureau International des Poids et Mesures, https://www.bipm.org/en/cgpm-2022/resolution-4. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Universal Time,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/UT. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","Michael A. Lombardi, “Fundamentals of Time and Frequency,” in <i>The Mechatronics Handbook</i>, ed. Robert H. Bishop (Boca Raton: CRC Press, 2002), chap. 17, https://tf.nist.gov/general/pdf/1498.pdf. [Open access]","Bureau International des Poids et Mesures, “Second,” SI Base Units, https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/second. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does atomic time drift away from the Earth's rotation?","a":"Because an atomic clock keeps a fixed rate, while the Earth's rotation is irregular and sometimes speeds up or slows down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The atomic second was also calibrated against the ephemeris second, which was slightly shorter than the mean solar second, so the two scales were mismatched from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why not simply let the two kinds of time drift apart?","a":"Because astronomers and navigators have long relied on UTC staying within a second of UT1, so that it can stand in for astronomical time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cost is that each leap second jolts digital systems, and the 2022 decision to widen the allowed gap by 2035 chose an unbroken UTC instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can leap seconds not be scheduled years ahead like leap days?","a":"Because they depend on measurement rather than on a rule: one is added only when the observed difference between UT1 and UTC calls for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The IERS publishes that difference, and its table records every change in the offset between atomic time and UTC since 1972.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Leap second"},
    "astro-090": {"questions":["The ten days dropped in October 1582 leave no gap in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which runs from JD 2,299,159.5 straight on to 2,299,160.5 across the reform.","The starting point of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was chosen because three calendar cycles of 15, 19 and 28 years all stood at 1 in 4713 BCE."],"abstract":"A <b>Julian date</b> is a continuous count of days and fractions of a day since noon Universal Time on 1 January 4713 BCE in the Julian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost 2.5 million days have passed since that starting point, and the integral part of the count is called the Julian day number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each Julian day begins at noon, so midnight falls at a fraction of .5, and noon on 1 January 2000 in Terrestrial Time is JD 2,451,545.0, the reference epoch J2000.0.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the count ignores months, years and leap rules, the gap between two events is a simple subtraction, and the day of the week is the remainder after dividing by 7.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ten days dropped in 1582 leave no gap in it: 4 October 1582 in the Julian calendar begins at JD 2,299,159.5 and 15 October in the Gregorian at JD 2,299,160.5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The starting year comes from the Julian Period, which the French scholar Joseph Justus Scaliger devised to give every year a positive number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He chose 4713 BCE because three old calendar cycles of 15, 19 and 28 years all stood at 1 in that year, and they will not coincide again until 7,980 years later, in 3268 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For modern data astronomers often use the modified Julian date, which subtracts 2,400,000.5, so that the count starts at midnight on 17 November 1858.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Julian dates are widely used as the time variable in astronomical software, and in precise work the time scale behind them, such as Terrestrial Time or Universal Time, must be stated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Exoplanet researchers time transits in barycentric Julian dates, and the unstated time standards used by different groups have differed by as much as a minute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Julian Date Converter,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/data/JulianDate. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Converting Between Julian Dates and Gregorian Calendar Dates,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/JD_formula. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Glossary,” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary. [Open access]","US Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, “Terrestrial Time (TT),” https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/TT. [Open access]","Claus Tøndering, “The Julian Period,” in <i>Calendar FAQ</i>, https://www.tondering.dk/claus/cal/julperiod.php. [Open access]","Jason Eastman, Robert Siverd, and B. Scott Gaudi, “Achieving Better Than 1 Minute Accuracy in the Heliocentric and Barycentric Julian Dates,” <i>Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific</i> 122, no. 894 (2010): 935–46, https://doi.org/10.1086/655938. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do astronomers count days instead of using calendar dates?","a":"Because calendar dates are awkward: different calendars have been used at different times and places, and the Julian and Gregorian calendars differ in their leap years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A continuous count turns the interval between two observations into a subtraction, and a double-precision number can hold a Julian date to about 20 microseconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the count begin in 4713 BCE?","a":"Because that is the start of the Julian Period, 7,980 years long, when three old cycles of 15, 19 and 28 years all stood at 1 together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Starting so early gives every year since then a positive number, with no need to count backwards across the start of the era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why must the time scale of a Julian date be stated?","a":"Because a Julian date can be expressed in several time standards, such as Terrestrial Time or Universal Time, which differ by about a minute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In exoplanet work such unstated differences could be mistaken for real variations in the timing of transits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Julian day"},
    "astro-091": {"questions":["Four small rocky worlds close in, four giants of gas and ice beyond them, and a shell of icy debris far outside make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held together by the Sun's gravity.","Because its planets all circle the Sun in one direction and in nearly one plane, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is thought to have formed from a single spinning cloud of gas and dust."],"abstract":"The <b>Solar System</b> is the Sun together with everything its gravity holds in orbit: eight planets, five officially recognised dwarf planets, hundreds of moons and thousands of asteroids and comets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Sun is a star and dominates it completely, holding about 99.8 per cent of all the mass, while Jupiter, more massive than all the other planets combined, holds most of the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The four planets nearest the Sun, Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars, are small worlds of rock and metal with solid surfaces, while Jupiter and Saturn are gas giants and Uranus and Neptune ice giants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between the two families lies the asteroid belt, whose total mass is tiny, and beyond Neptune lies the Kuiper Belt, a ring of icy bodies that includes the dwarf planet Pluto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Farther out still, the Oort Cloud is thought to be a vast shell of icy debris reaching from about 5,000 to 100,000 astronomical units, though it is too distant to have been imaged directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its motions are strikingly orderly, since the planets all orbit the Sun in the same direction and in nearly the same plane, close to the plane of the Sun's own spin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most planets also rotate in that direction, and the exceptions, such as Venus turning slowly backwards and Uranus tipped on its side, are details that any account of its origin must also explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That order, and the hydrogen-rich make-up that Jupiter and Saturn share with the Sun, is read as the mark of a common birth about 4.5 billion years ago from one rotating cloud of gas and dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Discs of gas and dust tens to hundreds of astronomical units across surround almost all young low-mass stars today, which is why such a birth is thought to be normal for a star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Its present layout is still debated, because models suggest the giant planets once lay closer together and spread out later, and Jupiter-like planets on wide, nearly circular orbits seem uncommon around other stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.1 Overview of Our Planetary System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-1-overview-of-our-planetary-system. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Solar System: Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated September 25, 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/solar-system-facts/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","NASA Science, “About the Planets,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated October 28, 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Oort Cloud,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated April 22, 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/oort-cloud/. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]","Alessandro Morbidelli and Sean N. Raymond, “Challenges in Planet Formation,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 121, no. 10 (2016): 1962–80, https://doi.org/10.1002/2016JE005088, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07202. [Open access]","David Nesvorný, “Dynamical Evolution of the Early Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 56 (2018): 137–74, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-081817-052028, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06647. [Open access]","Jonathan P. Williams and Lucas A. Cieza, “Protoplanetary Disks and Their Evolution,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 49 (2011): 67–117, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-081710-102548, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.0556. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the Sun control the motion of everything else in it?","a":"Because it holds about 99.8 per cent of the system's mass, so its gravity outweighs everything else put together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The planets, dwarf planets, asteroids and comets all orbit it, and the Oort Cloud marks roughly where the Sun's gravitational hold finally gives out, at up to about 100,000 astronomical units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the inner planets small and the outer ones giants?","a":"Because the disc they formed in was hot near the young Sun and cooler farther out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Close in, only metals and rock could condense, so the planets there are small rocky worlds; farther out ices were also available, the protoplanets grew much larger, and the biggest pulled in great envelopes of hydrogen and helium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might the Solar System be an unusual planetary system?","a":"Because surveys of other stars suggest its combination is rare: only about one Sun-like star in ten has a gas giant within a few to ten astronomical units, and only about one giant exoplanet in ten has a wide, nearly circular orbit like Jupiter's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Put together, those figures make the Sun and Jupiter something like a one-in-a-thousand case, though the authors present it only as an order of magnitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Solar System"},
    "astro-092": {"questions":["The flat, spinning discs seen around almost all young stars support the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which accounts for why the planets orbit the Sun in one plane and in one direction.","In the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a collapsing cloud spins faster as it shrinks, like a skater pulling in her arms, and flattens into a disc around the newborn star at its centre."],"abstract":"The <b>nebular hypothesis</b> is the explanation that the Sun and its planets formed together from a rotating cloud of gas and dust, now usually called the solar nebula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the cloud collapsed under its own gravity it grew hotter and spun faster, as a skater spins faster when she pulls in her arms, because its angular momentum was conserved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Material near the axis of spin fell straight to the centre, where the Sun formed, while faster-moving material settled into a flat disc turning around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The planets grew within that disc, so the hypothesis explains at a stroke why they all orbit in nearly one plane and in one direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same disc accounts for their chemistry, because it was hot near the young Sun and cooler farther out, so ices could condense only in its outer parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The idea is older than modern astrophysics, for in 1755 Immanuel Kant described the planetary system forming by gravity from matter once scattered through space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1796 Pierre-Simon Laplace proposed that the Sun's atmosphere, once spread by great heat beyond all the planetary orbits, shrank and left behind zones in the plane of its equator that condensed into planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Laplace offered it with the caution due to anything not drawn from observation or calculation, and modern versions keep its collapsing cloud while giving turbulence and magnetic fields the job of draining the disc's spin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The strongest support came from looking outwards, since discs of dust and gas tens to hundreds of astronomical units across are now found around almost all young low-mass stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 2014 the ALMA telescope imaged the disc of HL Tauri, a star no more than a million years old, and showed bright rings separated by gaps, structures that young planets are expected to carve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Immanuel Kant, <i>Allgemeine Naturgeschichte und Theorie des Himmels</i>, part 2, chap. 1 (Königsberg and Leipzig: Johann Friederich Petersen, 1755), https://archive.org/details/11427758bsb. [in German] [Open access]","Pierre-Simon Laplace, <i>Exposition du système du monde</i>, vol. 2, book 5, chap. 6 (Paris: Imprimerie du Cercle-Social, 1796), 298–303, https://archive.org/details/bnf-bpt6k1050382f. [in French] [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.4 Origin of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-4-origin-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Jonathan P. Williams and Lucas A. Cieza, “Protoplanetary Disks and Their Evolution,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 49 (2011): 67–117, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-081710-102548, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.0556. [Open access]","European Southern Observatory, “Revolutionary ALMA Image Reveals Planetary Genesis,” press release eso1436, November 6, 2014, https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso1436/. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a collapsing cloud flatten into a disc?","a":"Because its angular momentum is conserved, so it spins faster as it shrinks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Near the poles of the spin, material falls more or less straight in, but material near the equator is moving too fast to do so and collapses into a flat, rotating disc around the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Discs around young stars are understood today as a consequence of exactly this conservation during star formation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Laplace present his version so cautiously?","a":"Because it was a conjecture rather than a result. He wrote that he offered it with the distrust owed to anything that is not a product of observation or calculation, while arguing that five regularities of the planets and moons followed naturally from it and that the rings of Saturn made it more likely still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do discs around other young stars matter to the hypothesis?","a":"Because nobody can watch the Solar System form, but many young stars can be seen at that stage now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Discs of cool dust and gas are found around almost all low-mass stars shortly after birth and last for several million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rings and gaps in the disc of HL Tauri suggest that planets are already forming there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nebular hypothesis"},
    "astro-093": {"questions":["Because the gas of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> disperses within a few million years, a growing giant planet must gather its thick envelope of hydrogen before the supply runs out.","Bright rings and dark gaps imaged in 2014 around HL Tauri, a star no more than a million years old, were seen in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that may already hold forming planets."],"abstract":"A <b>protoplanetary disc</b> is the flattened, rotating disc of cool gas and dust that surrounds a young star and supplies the raw material from which planets form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such discs are found around almost all low-mass stars shortly after their birth, and they extend for tens to hundreds of astronomical units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their masses are estimated at roughly 0.2 to 20 per cent of the central star's mass, often more than all the planets of the Solar System put together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of that mass is hydrogen gas, which is hard to observe directly, so estimates usually rest on the glow of the dust and on an assumption that dust is about 1 per cent of the total.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dust is warmed by the young star and shines in the infrared, which is how such discs were first recognised before images showed some as dark silhouettes against the Orion Nebula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A disc does not last long, because the share of young stars with an inner disc falls away on a timescale of about 3 million years and most inner discs are gone by about 10 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That short life is a deadline, since any planet that is to capture a thick envelope of gas must be built while the gas is still there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Inside the disc, grains collide and stick, and observations at millimetre wavelengths show that in most discs around young stars the dust has already grown into pebble-sized clumps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 2014 an image of the disc of HL Tauri, a star no more than a million years old, revealed concentric bright rings separated by dark gaps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A later survey of 20 nearby bright discs found such rings and gaps in all of them, and in one young disc the ring where carbon monoxide freezes onto grains has been mapped at about 30 astronomical units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Jonathan P. Williams and Lucas A. Cieza, “Protoplanetary Disks and Their Evolution,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 49 (2011): 67–117, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-081710-102548, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.0556. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “21.3 Evidence That Planets Form around Other Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-3-evidence-that-planets-form-around-other-stars. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","European Southern Observatory, “Revolutionary ALMA Image Reveals Planetary Genesis,” press release eso1436, November 6, 2014, https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso1436/. [Open access]","Sean M. Andrews et al., “The Disk Substructures at High Angular Resolution Project (DSHARP). I. Motivation, Sample, Calibration, and Overview,” <i>Astrophysical Journal Letters</i> 869, no. 2 (2018): L41, https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/aaf741, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.04040. [Open access]","Chunhua Qi et al., “Imaging of the CO Snow Line in a Solar Nebula Analog,” <i>Science</i> 341, no. 6146 (2013): 630–32, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1239560. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the masses of these discs so uncertain?","a":"Because most of a disc's mass is hydrogen gas, which is very hard to detect, so its mass is usually worked out from the glow of the dust instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That calculation assumes the dust is about 1 per cent of the total, a figure that is highly uncertain in discs and probably changes with time and place, and it also depends on the size and make-up of the grains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why must giant planets form so quickly?","a":"Because the gas does not wait for them. The share of young stars that still have an inner disc falls off on a timescale of about 3 million years, and the dense inner parts of most discs are gone by about 10 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A planet with a thick gaseous envelope therefore has to be assembled within a few million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once the young Sun's wind cleared the remaining gas, the Solar System's giants stopped growing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were these discs first found in the infrared?","a":"Because every grain of dust in a disc is heated by the young star and gives off infrared radiation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before the dust is gathered into planets it presents an enormous radiating surface, so the infrared glow is strongest at exactly the stage when planets have not yet formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The discs were first inferred from this excess emission at infrared and submillimetre wavelengths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Protoplanetary disk"},
    "astro-094": {"questions":["Because metre-sized bodies would spiral inwards through the gas within centuries, how the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> formed is unsettled, though a collapsing cloud of pebbles may have made one in a single step.","Flown past by the New Horizons spacecraft in the Kuiper Belt, Arrokoth is a two-lobed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whose gentle merger preserves a record of how the solid building blocks of planets came together."],"abstract":"A <b>planetesimal</b> is one of the solid bodies, from about 1 kilometre to several hundred kilometres (0.6 to a few hundred miles) across, that formed in a young planetary disc and served as the building blocks of planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Planetesimals were the seeds of the rocky planets and of the solid cores of the giants, and the leftovers survive today as asteroids, comets and trans-Neptunian objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Once they existed, the largest could pull in their neighbours by gravity, and big bodies grew faster than small ones because their gravity drew in material from a wider path.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the inner Solar System several dozen centres of accretion seem to have grown this way into protoplanets with masses like those of Mercury or Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each incoming planetesimal struck hard enough to melt part of the growing body, so the protoplanets heated up and separated into a metal core and a rocky mantle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How planetesimals themselves formed is one of the least settled steps in planet formation, because dust grains sticking together run into several barriers on the way up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Once clumps reach about 10 centimetres (4 inches), drag from the slightly slower gas robs them of orbital motion, and in the asteroid belt metre-sized bodies would fall towards the Sun within a few hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One proposed escape is that pebbles crowd together in the turbulent gas until dense clumps collapse under their own gravity, forming planetesimals of about 100 to 1,000 kilometres (60 to 600 miles) in one step.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The asteroid belt's shortage of bodies smaller than about 100 kilometres (60 miles) fits that idea, suggesting that many asteroids were born large and the small ones are mostly fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The New Horizons flyby of Arrokoth in the Kuiper Belt showed a planetesimal of two flattened lobes, whose shape points to a gentle, low-speed merger inside a collapsing cloud of solid particles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “21.3 Evidence That Planets Form around Other Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-3-evidence-that-planets-form-around-other-stars. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]","Anders Johansen et al., “The Multifaceted Planetesimal Formation Process,” in <i>Protostars and Planets VI</i> (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2014), https://doi.org/10.2458/azu_uapress_9780816531240-ch024, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.1344. [Open access]","W. B. McKinnon et al., “The Solar Nebula Origin of (486958) Arrokoth, a Primordial Contact Binary in the Kuiper Belt,” <i>Science</i> 367, no. 6481 (2020): eaay6620, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aay6620, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.05576. [Open access]","Alessandro Morbidelli and Sean N. Raymond, “Challenges in Planet Formation,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 121, no. 10 (2016): 1962–80, https://doi.org/10.1002/2016JE005088, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07202. [Open access]","Alessandro Morbidelli et al., “Building Terrestrial Planets,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 40 (2012): 251–75, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-042711-105319, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.4694. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the first planetesimals have to grow so fast?","a":"Because the gas in the disc orbits slightly more slowly than solid bodies would, and the resulting drag makes clumps of about 10 centimetres (4 inches) and larger lose orbital motion and spiral inwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the asteroid belt metre-sized bodies would fall towards the Sun in a few hundred years, so growth had to outrun the drift, reaching about a kilometre quickly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are many asteroids thought to have been born large?","a":"Because there are fewer asteroids smaller than about 100 kilometres (60 miles) than a simple extrapolation from the largest ones predicts, which does not fit planetesimals built up step by step from kilometre-sized bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The smaller asteroids seen today may instead be mostly fragments from collisions, and a similar shortage of small Kuiper Belt objects has been read the same way, though an alternative view exists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Arrokoth so useful for studying planetesimals?","a":"Because it has hardly changed since it formed. Its two flattened lobes suggest a slow pile-up of many smaller bodies inside a collapsing cloud of solid particles, followed by a gentle merger of the pair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It survived in that state because the cold classical Kuiper Belt is a quiet place, with few violent collisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Planetesimal"},
    "astro-095": {"questions":["Earth's elements follow how readily each condenses rather than how heavy it is, the mark of a planet assembled from building blocks poor in water and other <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that escape easily.","Beyond the distance where water freezes, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> locked up as ice roughly quadrupled the solid material in the disc, giving the outer planets far more to build with than the inner ones."],"abstract":"In planetary science, <b>volatiles</b> are substances with low condensation temperatures, such as water, methane, ammonia and the lightest gases, which stay gaseous where metals and rock-forming silicates have already turned solid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In a cooling disc of gas around a young star, metals and silicates are the first materials to form solid grains, and the volatiles follow only as the temperature falls much further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water ice condenses where it is cold enough, and farther out, beyond about the orbit of Saturn, carbon and nitrogen combine with hydrogen into methane and ammonia ices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where ices can form they add greatly to the solids, and in a standard model of the solar nebula the solid material increases about fourfold once water ice freezes out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is why the outer Solar System is rich in ice, and why the three largest moons of Jupiter and Saturn are about half frozen water and half rock and metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The inner planets are rocky because the inner disc never cooled enough for ices or carbon-rich organic compounds to condense, so their building blocks were mostly metal and silicate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About one third of the mass of Earth, Venus and Mars is iron combined with nickel or sulphur and two thirds is silicate rock, and Mercury holds a greater share of metal still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Earth's chemistry bears this out, since the abundance of its elements follows how readily each condenses rather than how heavy it is, the pattern expected of a world built from volatile-poor material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The gas of the nebula probably dispersed before the inner disc cooled enough for volatiles to condense there, which leaves the question of where Earth's water came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The hydrogen isotopes of Earth's water and nitrogen point to material like the carbonaceous chondrite meteorites, fragments of water-bearing asteroids, rather than to comets, whose water has a different signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.2 Composition and Structure of Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-2-composition-and-structure-of-planets. [Open access]","Alessandro Morbidelli et al., “Building Terrestrial Planets,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 40 (2012): 251–75, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-042711-105319, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.4694. [Open access]","C. M. O’D. Alexander et al., “The Provenances of Asteroids, and Their Contributions to the Volatile Inventories of the Terrestrial Planets,” <i>Science</i> 337, no. 6095 (2012): 721–23, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1223474. [Paywalled]","M. Lecar et al., “On the Location of the Snow Line in a Protoplanetary Disk,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 640, no. 2 (2006): 1115–18, https://doi.org/10.1086/500287, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0602217. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Earth have water if it formed from volatile-poor material?","a":"Because water was added from material that formed farther out. The isotopes of hydrogen and nitrogen in Earth suggest that carbonaceous chondrites, fragments of water-bearing asteroids, were the main source, and they argue against comets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Models of planet formation have this water arriving while Earth was still growing, faster towards the end, rather than as a coating added afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why couldn't Earth simply have lost its volatiles after it formed?","a":"Because the pattern of what is missing is wrong for that. If Earth had gathered volatiles in the Sun's proportions and lost them to space later, the losses would follow how heavy each molecule is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Instead the abundance of Earth's elements follows their condensation temperature, which points to building blocks that formed where the disc was too hot for volatiles to condense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the largest moons of Jupiter and Saturn so icy?","a":"Because they formed in the cold outer Solar System, where water could condense as ice and become part of the solid building material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ganymede and Callisto at Jupiter and Titan at Saturn are each about half frozen water and half rock and metal, with icy outer layers over rocky cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a planet-forming disc, water freezes at about 150 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-096": {"questions":["The make-up of the asteroid belt suggests that the Solar System's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once lay near 2.7 astronomical units, between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter, dividing drier rock from ice.","Ice-rich solids outside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are thought to have let the cores of the giant planets grow big quickly, while only small rocky embryos formed in the warmer disc nearer the Sun."],"abstract":"The <b>frost line</b>, also called the snow line, is the distance from a young star beyond which the surrounding disc is cold enough for water to freeze into solid ice grains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the conditions in such a disc, water freezes at about 150 K, so the frost line lies where the disc's temperature falls to roughly that value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other substances have frost lines of their own much farther out, and carbon monoxide, for example, freezes only at about 20 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Outside the water frost line, ice adds so much to the solid material that its density in the disc rises about fourfold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its position is hard to predict, since one model of a low-mass disc places it at 1.6 to 1.8 astronomical units, just past Mars, and moving it farther out needs a disc with more mass, opacity or inflow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the Solar System, the make-up of the asteroid belt suggests that the frost line once lay near 2.7 astronomical units, between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Planetesimals inside it appear to have been dry and poor in volatiles, while the ice-rich solids beyond it are thought to have let giant-planet cores grow large before the gas dispersed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pebbles drifting inwards across the line would also lose their ice, about half their solid mass, which is one proposed reason why bodies on either side of it grew at such different rates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isotopes in iron meteorites show two separate reservoirs of material coexisting in the early disc, a division most plausibly kept open by a gap that the young Jupiter opened in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Frost lines can now be traced around other stars, and in the disc of the young star TW Hydrae the carbon monoxide snow line has been mapped at about 30 astronomical units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["M. Lecar et al., “On the Location of the Snow Line in a Protoplanetary Disk,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 640, no. 2 (2006): 1115–18, https://doi.org/10.1086/500287, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0602217. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]","Chunhua Qi et al., “Imaging of the CO Snow Line in a Solar Nebula Analog,” <i>Science</i> 341, no. 6146 (2013): 630–32, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1239560. [Paywalled]","Alessandro Morbidelli and Sean N. Raymond, “Challenges in Planet Formation,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 121, no. 10 (2016): 1962–80, https://doi.org/10.1002/2016JE005088, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07202. [Open access]","Thomas S. Kruijer et al., “Age of Jupiter Inferred from the Distinct Genetics and Formation Times of Meteorites,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 26 (2017): 6712–16, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1704461114, open copy at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5495263/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Alessandro Morbidelli et al., “Building Terrestrial Planets,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 40 (2012): 251–75, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-042711-105319, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.4694. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the frost line matter for giant planets?","a":"Because outside it water ice adds to the dust, raising the density of solids about fourfold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mass a growing embryo can sweep up rises steeply with that density, and ice-rich material has long been thought necessary to build gas-giant cores before the disc's gas disperses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Solar System's frost line hard to place?","a":"Because theory and evidence disagree. A standard low-mass disc model puts it at 1.6 to 1.8 astronomical units, just past the orbit of Mars, while the asteroid belt suggests it lay near 2.7.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pushing it out that far requires a disc with considerably more opacity, mass or inflow onto the young Sun than the model assumes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do astronomers look for carbon monoxide snow lines?","a":"Because how efficiently planets form, and what they are made of, is closely tied to where the snow lines of common volatiles lie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the disc around TW Hydrae, a ring of a molecule that survives only where carbon monoxide is frozen out marks that snow line at about 30 astronomical units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Snow lines may also release complex organic molecules into the gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Frost line (astrophysics)"},
    "astro-097": {"questions":["In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Uranus and Neptune stayed smaller than Jupiter and Saturn because they had gathered only a few Earth masses of gas when the disc's gas dispersed.","Iron meteorites suggest that Jupiter's core grew to about 20 Earth masses within a million years, early enough to fit <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the standard model of how giant planets form."],"abstract":"<b>Core accretion</b> is the standard model of how giant planets form, in which a solid core is built first from colliding planetesimals and then gathers a massive envelope of gas from the disc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its first stage is the same as the growth of a rocky planet, with an embryo sweeping up the smaller solid bodies around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once the core's gravity is strong enough to hold onto the gas around it, solids and gas build up together, slowly at first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the mass of the envelope approaches that of the core, gas can pour in very rapidly and build the planet towards the mass of Jupiter, until the disc disperses or the planet clears a gap in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A core of about 10 Earth masses is commonly given as the threshold for that runaway, though the exact critical mass depends on conditions in the disc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the Solar System, the model explains why the giant planets lie beyond the rocky ones, where ices added to the raw material and protoplanets grew to about ten times the mass of Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Jupiter and Saturn captured the most gas and have nearly the Sun's composition, while Uranus and Neptune had gathered only a few Earth masses of it when the gas was gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The classical version has a timing problem, because building cores from thinly spread planetesimals is slow, and one proposed remedy is that cores grew far faster by sweeping up small pebbles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isotopes in iron meteorites suggest that Jupiter's core reached about 20 Earth masses within a million years of the Solar System's start, early enough to fit the model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The main alternative, disc instability, has a giant planet form directly when part of a massive disc fragments under its own gravity, and the two models are still tested against planets found around other stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “21.3 Evidence That Planets Form around Other Stars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-3-evidence-that-planets-form-around-other-stars. [Open access]","Ravit Helled et al., “Giant Planet Formation, Evolution, and Internal Structure,” in <i>Protostars and Planets VI</i> (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2014), https://doi.org/10.2458/azu_uapress_9780816531240-ch028, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.1142. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]","Thomas S. Kruijer et al., “Age of Jupiter Inferred from the Distinct Genetics and Formation Times of Meteorites,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 26 (2017): 6712–16, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1704461114, open copy at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5495263/. [Open access]","Alessandro Morbidelli and Sean N. Raymond, “Challenges in Planet Formation,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 121, no. 10 (2016): 1962–80, https://doi.org/10.1002/2016JE005088, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07202. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Jupiter's core have to form so early?","a":"Because a core can only gather gas while the disc still has some, and the solar nebula probably dispersed within less than 10 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Disc lifetimes measured around other young stars point the same way, with inner discs fading on a timescale of about 3 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Meteorite isotopes suggest that Jupiter's core reached about 20 Earth masses in under a million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do Uranus and Neptune have so much less gas than Jupiter?","a":"Because they captured far less of it before it ran out. Their compositions are dominated by the icy and rocky material of their large cores rather than by hydrogen and helium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In core accretion, the ice giants manage to attract only a few Earth masses of gas before the disc's gas disperses after a few million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is disc instability still taken seriously?","a":"Because it can form giant planets quickly in places where core accretion struggles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Core accretion often fails around small red dwarf stars because it takes too long there, and disc instability offers a way round that, though there is no consensus yet on when discs actually fragment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Giant planets on very wide orbits may need either very fast pebble accretion or instability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-098": {"questions":["In the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Jupiter and Saturn crossing an orbital resonance destabilised the giant planets and flung Uranus and Neptune outwards to roughly where they orbit today.","Later versions of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begin with a third ice giant, eventually thrown out of the Solar System by Jupiter, to make the planets' simulated orbits come out right."],"abstract":"The <b>Nice model</b> is a computer-simulated account of how the giant planets moved from a compact arrangement to their present, widely spaced orbits, a model judged by how well it reproduces the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is named after the city in southern France where it was conceived, and its original form was published in 2005.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In it the giant planets leave the gas disc closer together, all within about 15 astronomical units of the Sun, surrounded by a disc of leftover icy planetesimals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By scattering those planetesimals, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune drift outwards while Jupiter, massive enough to fling them out of the Solar System, moves slightly inwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Jupiter and Saturn cross the point where Saturn takes exactly twice as long to orbit, the system turns unstable, throwing Uranus and Neptune outwards and scattering the planetesimal disc violently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The original model reproduced the giant planets' final distances, the modest stretching of their orbits and their small tilts, starting from orbits that were almost circular and flat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its scattering of planetesimals was also offered as the cause of a spike in impacts on the Moon and as a framework for the structure of the Kuiper Belt and the capture of Jupiter's Trojan asteroids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later versions changed it substantially, and the planets' orbits come out right most easily if there was once a third ice giant that Jupiter threw out of the Solar System during the instability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The timing of the instability cannot be fixed by modelling alone, and an early instability is argued to have several advantages over the late one first proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A separate model, the Grand Tack, adds an earlier migration in which Jupiter moved in to about 1.5 astronomical units and back out while the gas disc remained, which could explain why Mars is so small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["K. Tsiganis et al., “Origin of the Orbital Architecture of the Giant Planets of the Solar System,” <i>Nature</i> 435, no. 7041 (2005): 459–61, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature03539. [Paywalled]","David Nesvorný, “Dynamical Evolution of the Early Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 56 (2018): 137–74, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-081817-052028, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06647. [Open access]","Kevin J. Walsh et al., “A Low Mass for Mars from Jupiter’s Early Gas-Driven Migration,” <i>Nature</i> 475, no. 7355 (2011): 206–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature10201, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.5177. [Open access]","Alessandro Morbidelli and Sean N. Raymond, “Challenges in Planet Formation,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 121, no. 10 (2016): 1962–80, https://doi.org/10.1002/2016JE005088, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07202. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “21.6 New Perspectives on Planet Formation,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-6-new-perspectives-on-planet-formation. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the giant planets thought to have moved at all?","a":"Because their present orbits are hard to get any other way. Theory suggests they formed on circular, flat orbits, yet Jupiter, Saturn and Uranus have eccentricities of 6 to 9 per cent and the orbital planes of the outer three are tilted by up to about 2 degrees from Jupiter's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The disc beyond Saturn was also too thin to build Uranus and Neptune in time at their present distances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do later versions add a third ice giant?","a":"Because a smoother migration would have disturbed the inner Solar System too much. Encounters with an ice giant let Jupiter jump inwards and Saturn outwards in one step, sparing the terrestrial planets and the asteroid belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That jump happens most easily if there was once a third ice giant between Saturn and Uranus, which Jupiter then ejected into interstellar space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Nice model described as a model rather than a finding?","a":"Because it is a family of simulations whose results change with their starting conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The instability is chaotic, so small changes produce different outcomes and many runs must be compared statistically, and its timing cannot be fixed from the modelling alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reviews of planet formation stress that even its most successful models rest on uncertain foundations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nice model"},
    "astro-099": {"questions":["Argon ages in lunar samples can produce apparent peaks even when impacts were steadily declining, which is why some doubt the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ever happened as a sharp spike.","Simulations of the giant planets' migration offered a trigger for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a sudden flood of asteroids and comets thought to have battered the Moon about 3.9 billion years ago."],"abstract":"The <b>Late Heavy Bombardment</b> is a proposed episode, centred about 3.9 billion years ago, in which the Moon and the inner planets were struck by an unusually intense burst of impacts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The idea grew out of the Apollo missions, whose rock samples dated the Moon's great impact basins, among them Imbrium, Serenitatis, Nectaris and Orientale, to a short interval about 4.0 to 3.7 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since that came hundreds of millions of years after the planets formed, it seemed to call for a late rise in the number of small bodies crossing the inner Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Planet formation cannot easily produce so late a spike, and in 2005 simulations proposed that a sudden migration of the giant planets destabilised the outer planetesimal disc and the asteroid belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Whether such a spike happened at all remains one of the live disagreements of planetary science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> On a cataclysm reading, and under the usual assumption about metals delivered to the lunar mantle, only a surge in impacts starting about 3.95 billion years ago fits both the craters and the chemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On an accretion-tail reading, the same record is the end of a bombardment declining since planet formation, which fits if those metals entered the lunar mantle only after its magma ocean had crystallised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of the evidence for a spike comes from histograms of argon ages in Apollo samples, and modelling shows that such histograms tend to produce peaks even when the real impact rate was falling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One review of the lunar record finds most evidence supporting a prolonged bombardment from about 4.2 to 3.4 billion years ago, while others favour a modest rise in impacts layered on a declining flux.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The answer matters beyond the Moon, because it bears on when the giant planets became unstable and on the conditions that early life on Earth may have faced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]","R. Gomes et al., “Origin of the Cataclysmic Late Heavy Bombardment Period of the Terrestrial Planets,” <i>Nature</i> 435, no. 7041 (2005): 466–69, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature03676. [Paywalled]","Patrick Boehnke and T. Mark Harrison, “Illusory Late Heavy Bombardments,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 39 (2016): 10802–6, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1611535113, open copy at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5047187/. [Open access]","Nicolle E. B. Zellner, “Cataclysm No More: New Views on the Timing and Delivery of Lunar Impactors,” <i>Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres</i> 47, no. 3 (2017): 261–80, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11084-017-9536-3. [Open access]","A. Morbidelli et al., “The Timeline of the Lunar Bombardment: Revisited,” <i>Icarus</i> 305 (2018): 262–76, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2017.12.046, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.03756. [Open access]","David Nesvorný, “Dynamical Evolution of the Early Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 56 (2018): 137–74, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-081817-052028, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06647. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why might the Apollo ages be misleading?","a":"Because nearly every Apollo-era sample shows signs that its argon clock was disturbed by later heating and impacts, which can make ages look younger than they are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Modelling shows that histograms of these ages tend to produce peaks even when the true impact rate was declining smoothly, and choosing which part of a sample's data to trust involves a good deal of judgement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the giant planets linked to the bombardment?","a":"Because theories of planet formation cannot naturally produce an intense bombardment so late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A sudden migration of the giant planets after a long quiet period would destabilise the disc of planetesimals beyond them and stir up the asteroid belt, sending a flood of bodies inwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Changes in the orbits of Jupiter and Saturn could have scattered asteroids into the inner Solar System in this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the question matter for the early Earth?","a":"Because the Moon keeps a record that Earth has largely lost, so its impact history is used to infer what Earth experienced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether impacts came as a sharp spike around 3.9 billion years ago or a long, declining barrage changes the picture of the conditions in which life arose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also helps date the giant planets' instability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Late Heavy Bombardment"},
    "astro-100": {"questions":["Modelling of the Sun's internal vibrations gives about 4.57 billion years, in close agreement with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> measured from the oldest mineral grains found in meteorites.","Earth's oldest known mineral grain, at about 4.4 billion years, falls short of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because the planet took millions of years to form and its surface has been remade since."],"abstract":"The <b>age of the Solar System</b> is estimated at about 4.567 billion years, counted from the formation of the first solid grains in the disc of gas and dust around the young Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a definition of time zero rather than the age of any one planet, since the planets and their crusts formed over the following few tens of millions of years at most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest solids known are the calcium–aluminium-rich inclusions, mineral grains found inside primitive meteorites and regarded as the first material to condense in the solar nebula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lead isotopes in such inclusions give ages between 4,567 and 4,568 million years, with one study finding 4,567.3 million and another 4,568.2 million, figures within about a million years of each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best ages are now so precise that the inclusions appear to have formed within a brief interval, while the grains called chondrules went on forming for about 3 million years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Other clocks agree with the meteorites, for modelling of the Sun's internal vibrations gives the Sun an age of 4.57 billion years, uncertain by about 0.11 billion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Earth cannot give the figure directly, because it finished growing some 50 to 150 million years after time zero, its surface has been remade many times, and its oldest known mineral, a zircon grain, is about 4.4 billion years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Most summaries round the figure to 4.5 or 4.6 billion years, which is right at that level of precision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The small differences between the inclusion ages come partly from method, since one study corrected its lead ages for the uranium isotope ratio and another checked its lead age against an aluminium-based clock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the inclusions mark time zero, the formation of meteorites, asteroids and planets is commonly timed in millions of years after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["James N. Connelly et al., “The Absolute Chronology and Thermal Processing of Solids in the Solar Protoplanetary Disk,” <i>Science</i> 338, no. 6107 (2012): 651–55, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1226919. [Paywalled]","Audrey Bouvier and Meenakshi Wadhwa, “The Age of the Solar System Redefined by the Oldest Pb–Pb Age of a Meteoritic Inclusion,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 3, no. 9 (2010): 637–41, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo941. [Paywalled]","Alessandro Morbidelli and Sean N. Raymond, “Challenges in Planet Formation,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 121, no. 10 (2016): 1962–80, https://doi.org/10.1002/2016JE005088, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07202. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","A. Bonanno, H. Schlattl, and L. Paternò, “The Age of the Sun and the Relativistic Corrections in the EOS,” <i>Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 390, no. 3 (2002): 1115–18, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20020749, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0204331. [Open access]","John W. Valley et al., “Hadean Age for a Post-Magma-Ocean Zircon Confirmed by Atom-Probe Tomography,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 7, no. 3 (2014): 219–23, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo2075. [Paywalled]","NASA Science, “Solar System: Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated September 25, 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/solar-system-facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is time zero set by grains in meteorites?","a":"Because the age of the Solar System is defined as the moment the first solid grains formed in the disc around the young Sun, and calcium–aluminium-rich inclusions in meteorites are the earliest solids known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their formation starts the clocks used to time later events, which makes them an anchor point for the whole early chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The primitive meteorites that carry them are unaltered building blocks of the planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can't Earth's own rocks give the age of the Solar System?","a":"Because Earth took tens of millions of years to form, ending with the giant impact thought to have made the Moon, and has kept reworking its surface ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its oldest rocks are at least 3.8 billion years old and its oldest known mineral, a zircon grain, about 4.4 billion, both younger than the first solids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Only material that has not been melted or remade since the beginning keeps the original date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do careful studies give slightly different ages?","a":"Because they measure different grains with refined methods. One study dated an inclusion at 4,568.2 million years, between 0.3 and 1.9 million years older than earlier estimates, and concluded that the Sun and its disc formed earlier than had been thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Another, correcting its lead ages for the uranium isotope ratio, found 4,567.3 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A review puts the range of such inclusion ages at 4,567 to 4,568 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Formation and evolution of the Solar System"},
    "astro-101": {"questions":["Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mark the first solids to form around the young Sun, their lead–lead age is used as time zero for dating every later meteorite.","White specks visible in broken pieces of the Allende meteorite are <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which refractory elements are about 20 times more concentrated than in CI chondrites."],"abstract":"<b>Calcium–aluminium-rich inclusions</b>, usually shortened to CAIs, are rare pale inclusions inside chondrites, the stony meteorites that preserve unmelted material from the earliest Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are built largely of aluminium-rich minerals, essentially the most refractory minerals that would condense from a cooling gas with the Sun's composition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In one inclusion from the Allende meteorite, 29 refractory elements, those that turn solid at the highest temperatures, are about 20 times more concentrated than in the CI chondrites used as a chemical standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Allende meteorite, which fell in Mexico in 1969, is a carbonaceous meteorite whose broken surfaces show such white inclusions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inclusions formed at the very start then had to travel through the disc around the Sun for more than 3 million years before being gathered into the parent bodies of carbonaceous chondrites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> CAIs are the oldest dated solids of the Solar System, and lead isotope dating puts their formation at about 4,567 to 4,568 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One inclusion from the meteorite Northwest Africa 2364 gave a lead–lead age of 4,568.2 million years, the oldest age then obtained for any Solar System object.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measurements of uranium and lead isotopes in a collection of inclusions and chondrules showed that chondrules began forming at the same time as the inclusions, 4.567 billion years ago, and went on forming for about 3 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the inclusions mark the first solids, their formation is used as time zero, the moment from which the ages of other meteorites and of planetary growth are counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Measured from that zero, the parent bodies of chondrites gathered about 2 to 4 million years later, while Earth took at least 38 million years to grow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.2 Meteorites: Stones from Heaven,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-2-meteorites-stones-from-heaven. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]","James N. Connelly et al., “The Absolute Chronology and Thermal Processing of Solids in the Solar Protoplanetary Disk,” <i>Science</i> 338, no. 6107 (2012): 651–55, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1226919. [Paywalled]","Audrey Bouvier and Meenakshi Wadhwa, “The Age of the Solar System Redefined by the Oldest Pb–Pb Age of a Meteoritic Inclusion,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 3, no. 9 (2010): 637–41, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo941. [Paywalled]","S. J. Desch et al., “Short-Lived Radionuclides in Meteorites and the Sun’s Birth Environment,” in <i>Protostars and Planets VII</i>, Astronomical Society of the Pacific Conference Series 534 (2023): 759–97, https://doi.org/10.26624/sxjy1095, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.11169. [Open access]","Katharina Lodders et al., “Condensation and the Volatility Trend of the Earth,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 221, no. 5 (2025): 54, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-025-01182-6, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.01362. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are these inclusions thought to be the first solids?","a":"Because their chemistry and their age agree: their minerals are essentially the most refractory that would condense from a cooling gas of the Sun's composition, so they are what a hot disc would make first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their lead isotope ages, about 4,567 to 4,568 million years, are older than those of any other material dated from the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do different studies give slightly different ages for them?","a":"Because each study dates particular inclusions: one that measured uranium and lead isotopes across a collection of inclusions and chondrules found an age of 4.567 billion years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A single inclusion from the meteorite Northwest Africa 2364 gave 4,568.2 million years, between 0.3 and 1.9 million years older than earlier estimates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do chondrules matter for the inclusions' story?","a":"Because chondrules are the other main ingredient of chondrites, and whether they formed later than the inclusions was long contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Earlier results suggested a gap of 1 to 2 million years, but uranium and lead measurements showed that chondrules began forming at the same time, 4.567 billion years ago, and kept forming for about 3 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Calcium–aluminium-rich inclusion"},
    "astro-102": {"questions":["Turbulence and magnetic fields that drained spin from the disc, and winds that slowed the young Sun, are the usual answers to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> posed by the planets' orbits.","Planets with under 1 per cent of the Solar System's mass hold over 99 per cent of its spin, the imbalance known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in theories of how the Sun formed."],"abstract":"The <b>angular momentum problem</b> is the puzzle of why the Sun, which holds almost all the mass of the Solar System, carries so little of its spin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Angular momentum measures the rotation of a body about a centre, combining its mass, its speed and its distance from that centre, and it is conserved, so a body that moves closer to the centre speeds up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A shrinking cloud therefore spins faster as it contracts, like a skater pulling in her arms, and the cloud that formed the Solar System flattened into a rotating disc as it shrank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sun holds about 99.8 per cent of the Solar System's mass, while Jupiter, the largest planet, holds about 0.1 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Yet the Sun has less than 0.6 per cent of the total angular momentum, and the planets, with under 1 per cent of the mass, have more than 99 per cent of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The usual reading is that the young Sun lost most of its angular momentum, and that only a small part of what it shed went into the planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside the disc, turbulence and magnetic fields can drain angular momentum from the gas, robbing it of some of its spin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As angular momentum is moved through such a disc, gas accretes onto the star while the disc spreads outwards to larger distances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Observations of young stars show that they must lose angular momentum at nearly every stage, and stars still feeding from discs seem to be held back from spinning up as they contract, a process called disc locking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After their discs are gone, Sun-like stars go on slowing because magnetised stellar winds remove angular momentum far more efficiently than they remove mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “3.2 Newton’s Great Synthesis,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/3-2-newtons-great-synthesis. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Jian Li, Zhihong Jeff Xia, and Liyong Zhou, “Calibration of the Angular Momenta of the Minor Planets in the Solar System,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 630 (2019): A68, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201834196, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.11293. [Open access]","Joshua N. Winn and Daniel C. Fabrycky, “The Occurrence and Architecture of Exoplanetary Systems,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 53 (2015): 409–47, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-082214-122246, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.4199. [Open access]","J. Bouvier et al., “Angular Momentum Evolution of Young Low-Mass Stars and Brown Dwarfs: Observations and Theory,” in <i>Protostars and Planets VI</i> (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2014), https://doi.org/10.2458/azu_uapress_9780816531240-ch019, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.7851. [Open access]","Philip J. Armitage, “Dynamics of Protoplanetary Disks,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 49 (2011): 195–236, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-081710-102521, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.1496. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.1 Overview of Our Planetary System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-1-overview-of-our-planetary-system. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why should a collapsing cloud spin faster as it shrinks?","a":"Because angular momentum, which combines mass, speed and distance from the centre, is conserved, so material that moves inwards must speed up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A skater who pulls in her arms spins faster for the same reason, and the cloud that made the Solar System spun up and flattened into a disc as it contracted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a disc feed a star without handing it all its spin?","a":"Because turbulence and magnetic fields inside the disc drain angular momentum from the gas, robbing it of some of its spin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When angular momentum is moved through a disc in this way, gas accretes onto the star while the disc spreads outwards to larger distances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Sun spin slowly today?","a":"Because Sun-like stars lose angular momentum at nearly every stage of their lives, and the torques needed while they are very young are about a million times larger than the torque acting on the present Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Once their discs are gone, magnetised winds keep slowing them, removing angular momentum far more efficiently than mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Angular momentum problem"},
    "astro-103": {"questions":["The orbits of the eight planets are tilted by only 1.9° on average to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the Sun's own equator lies 6° from it.","A warp in the distant Kuiper belt, measured against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for objects 80 to 400 au out, has been suggested as a sign of an unseen planet."],"abstract":"The <b>invariable plane</b> of the Solar System is the flat plane at right angles to the system's total angular momentum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Angular momentum measures the rotation of a body about a fixed point, combining its mass, speed and distance, and it is a conserved quantity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The plane is tilted by 1.58° to the ecliptic, the plane of Earth's orbit, in the standard reference frame of the year 2000, with its ascending node at a longitude of 107.58°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its direction is set almost entirely by the planets, and Jupiter's angular momentum points close to the direction of the total for the Sun and planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small bodies matter too, since adding the dwarf planets Eris, Haumea and Makemake changes the calculated tilt of the plane by about 1.25 seconds of arc, far more than Ceres, Vesta and Pallas do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Measured against the invariable plane, the planets' orbits are nearly aligned, with a root-mean-square tilt of 1.9° and individual tilts from 0.33° to 6.3°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sun's own equator is tilted by 6.0° to the plane, a small angle that fits the idea that the Sun and the planets took their spin from the same rotating disc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The planets all revolve about the Sun in the same direction and roughly in the plane of the Sun's rotation, the pattern a rotating disc was invoked to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Far from the planets the plane that small bodies settle around approaches the invariable plane, so the mean plane of the distant Kuiper belt should match it unless some unseen massive body is tugging on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One 2025 analysis found a possible warp relative to the plane for objects 80 to 400 au from the Sun, which, if it is not spurious, might point to an unseen planet between Mercury and Earth in mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Ian Matheson and Renu Malhotra, “A Measurement of the Kuiper Belt’s Mean Plane from Objects Classified by Machine Learning,” <i>Astronomical Journal</i> 165, no. 6 (2023): 241, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/accffd, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.14312. [Open access]","Joshua N. Winn and Daniel C. Fabrycky, “The Occurrence and Architecture of Exoplanetary Systems,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 53 (2015): 409–47, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-082214-122246, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.4199. [Open access]","Jian Li, Zhihong Jeff Xia, and Liyong Zhou, “Calibration of the Angular Momenta of the Minor Planets in the Solar System,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 630 (2019): A68, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201834196, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.11293. [Open access]","Amir Siraj, Christopher F. Chyba, and Scott Tremaine, “Measuring the Mean Plane of the Distant Kuiper Belt,” <i>Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society: Letters</i> 543, no. 1 (2025): L27–33, https://doi.org/10.1093/mnrasl/slaf091, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.14156. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “3.2 Newton’s Great Synthesis,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/3-2-newtons-great-synthesis. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Jupiter matter so much to where the plane lies?","a":"Because the planets carry more than 99 per cent of the Solar System's angular momentum, and the plane is defined by that total.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jupiter's own angular momentum points close to the direction of the total for the Sun and planets, so the plane lies near Jupiter's orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the plane matter for the search for unseen planets?","a":"Because, without any unseen planetary-mass bodies, the mean plane of the distant Kuiper belt should be the same as the invariable plane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A warp found for objects 80 to 400 au from the Sun could be explained by a planet between Mercury and Earth in mass, though the signal may yet prove spurious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Sun's small tilt to the plane significant?","a":"Because the Sun's equator lies only 6° from the plane, a low angle that fits the long-held idea that the Sun and planets inherited their angular momentum from one rotating disc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Around other stars this alignment is not guaranteed, and stars hosting close-in giant planets can be grossly misaligned with their planets' orbits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Invariable plane"},
    "astro-104": {"questions":["Venus, turning slowly backwards, and Triton, circling against Neptune's rotation, are rare exceptions to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shared by nearly every planet and large moon.","Giant exoplanets found circling at right angles to their star's spin showed that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of every planet in the Solar System is not guaranteed elsewhere."],"abstract":"<b>Prograde motion</b> is orbiting or spinning in the same direction as the main rotation of a system, while motion in the opposite direction is called retrograde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All the planets revolve around the Sun in the same direction, approximately in the plane of the Sun's own rotation, and the Sun spins the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of the planets also rotate in the same direction as they revolve, and most moons move in anticlockwise orbits when seen from the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The exceptions include Venus, which rotates backwards very slowly, and Uranus and Pluto, which spin about axes tipped nearly on their sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neptune's moon Triton is the only large moon in the Solar System that orbits opposite to its planet's rotation, and it is thought to be a Kuiper belt object captured by Neptune's gravity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The shared direction is explained by the planets forming from a single rotating disc of gas and dust around the young Sun, so that they all orbit the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cloud that became that disc spun faster as it contracted, through the conservation of angular momentum, and its rotation flattened it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The low tilts between the planets' orbits tell the same story, because the laws of motion allow far larger tilts and the present Solar System has no way of damping them, so they must date from its birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The exceptions are probably the work of enormous collisions between growing planets and planetesimals long before life began on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around other stars the rule can fail, since some giant exoplanets orbit at right angles to the spin of their star or even in the opposite sense, a startling discovery when it was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.1 Overview of Our Planetary System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-1-overview-of-our-planetary-system. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.4 Origin of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-4-origin-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Joshua N. Winn and Daniel C. Fabrycky, “The Occurrence and Architecture of Exoplanetary Systems,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 53 (2015): 409–47, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-082214-122246, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.4199. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “21.6 New Perspectives on Planet Formation,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-6-new-perspectives-on-planet-formation. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Triton,” https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/moons/triton/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “3.2 Newton’s Great Synthesis,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/3-2-newtons-great-synthesis. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the planets all orbit in the same direction?","a":"Because they formed together from one disc of gas and dust that was already rotating around the young Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cloud spun faster as it shrank, through the conservation of angular momentum, flattened into a disc, and the planets that grew in it all orbit the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do Venus and Uranus break the pattern?","a":"Because the building of the planets was violent and partly random, with planetesimals crashing into each other and into the growing planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Enormous collisions of that kind are the likely reason why Venus spins slowly backwards and why Uranus and Pluto spin on their sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Triton's backward orbit taken as a clue to its origin?","a":"Because most moons travel in the same direction as their planets, anticlockwise when seen from the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Triton is the only large moon in the Solar System that orbits opposite to its planet's rotation, and it is thought instead to be a Kuiper belt object captured by Neptune's gravity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Retrograde and prograde motion"},
    "astro-105": {"questions":["Samples brought home from the asteroids Ryugu and Bennu proved very close in composition to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the rare meteorites that best preserve the Solar System's average make-up.","Lithium is about 190 times scarcer in the Sun's surface than in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because the Sun destroys it, while for most other non-volatile elements the two agree closely."],"abstract":"<b>CI chondrites</b> are a rare group of carbonaceous meteorites, named after Ivuna in Tanzania, whose chemistry matches the Sun's more closely than that of any other group of meteorites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chondrites are undifferentiated meteorites that were never fully melted and represent an aggregation of primary Solar System material, so their fairly uniform composition should record the average make-up of the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Carbonaceous meteorites are dark stones that contain carbon, complex organic molecules and, in some cases, chemically bound water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> CI chondrites have been altered by water and heat, which changed their minerals, but they are not thought to have been noticeably sorted chemically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Samples returned from the asteroid Ryugu by Hayabusa2 and from Bennu by OSIRIS-REx turned out to be very close to CI chondrites in composition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Sun holds about 99.8 per cent of the Solar System's mass and shares its hydrogen-dominated composition with Jupiter and Saturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By mass the Sun's surface layers are about 74 per cent hydrogen, 25 per cent helium and 1.3 per cent heavier elements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Measured in the laboratory, CI chondrites agree very well with the Sun's surface for nearly all elements that do not escape easily, over abundances that span 12 orders of magnitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The clear exceptions are the most volatile elements, such as hydrogen, carbon, nitrogen, oxygen and the noble gases, which the meteorites did not keep, and lithium, about 190 times scarcer at the Sun's surface because the Sun destroys it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Combining the two, the Sun for the volatile elements and CI chondrites for the rest, gives the standard table of Solar System abundances, and the compositions of Earth and of other meteorites are routinely expressed relative to CI values.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.1 Overview of Our Planetary System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-1-overview-of-our-planetary-system. [Open access]","Martin Asplund et al., “The Chemical Composition of the Sun,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 47 (2009): 481–522, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.astro.46.060407.145222, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.0948. [Open access]","Maria Bergemann, Katharina Lodders, and Herbert Palme, “The Chemical Composition of the Sun,” preprint of a chapter for the <i>Encyclopedia of Astrophysics</i> (Elsevier, 2025), https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05402. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.2 Meteorites: Stones from Heaven,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-2-meteorites-stones-from-heaven. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.4 Origin of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-4-origin-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Katharina Lodders et al., “Condensation and the Volatility Trend of the Earth,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 221, no. 5 (2025): 54, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-025-01182-6, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.01362. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why trust a meteorite to tell us what the Sun is made of?","a":"Because laboratory analysis of meteorites can measure almost every element and isotope with a precision that studies of sunlight cannot match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the two can be compared, CI chondrites agree with the Sun's surface over abundances spanning 12 orders of magnitude, so they are used for the elements that cannot be measured in the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are hydrogen and carbon left out of the comparison?","a":"Because even the most pristine meteorites have lost volatile elements to varying degrees, including the six most abundant elements, hydrogen, helium, carbon, nitrogen, oxygen and neon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those elements have to be measured in the Sun itself, which is why meteoritic abundances are counted relative to silicon rather than hydrogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the Ryugu and Bennu samples matter?","a":"Because they are material collected directly from asteroids by the Hayabusa2 and OSIRIS-REx missions, and they turned out to be very close in composition to CI chondrites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That may help to improve the meteorite-based part of the Solar System's standard composition in future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"CI chondrite"},
    "astro-106": {"questions":["In one Allende meteorite inclusion, 29 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are about 20 times more concentrated than in CI chondrites, while elements that condense at lower temperatures are depleted.","Metals such as tungsten, osmium and iridium count among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that condense at the highest temperatures, forming metal nuggets rather than oxide or silicate minerals."],"abstract":"In planetary science, <b>refractory elements</b> are the chemical elements that turn solid at the highest temperatures as a gas of the Sun's composition cools, above about 1,300 to 1,360 K at a pressure of a ten-thousandth of a bar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They condense before the major rock-forming elements magnesium, silicon and iron, while moderately volatile elements condense between about 1,300 K and 660 K and highly volatile elements below 660 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most refractory elements, among them calcium, aluminium, titanium and most of the rare earths, end up in oxide and silicate minerals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A few refractory metals, including tungsten, osmium, iridium and platinum, condense instead into refractory metal nuggets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In meteorites the refractory elements behave roughly as a group, rising or falling together to within about 10 to 20 per cent of their mean enrichment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> As the disc around the young Sun cooled, the first materials to form solid grains were metals and rock-forming silicates, joined later by sulphur compounds and silicates rich in carbon and water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Close to the Sun the temperature never fell low enough for ice or carbon-rich compounds to condense, so Earth and its neighbours are made mostly of heavier elements such as iron and silicon and are poor in light gases and ices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Condensation calculations have been made for more than 70 years, and their main conclusion is that condensation between gas and solid was the chief factor setting the abundances of elements in Solar System bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The calcium–aluminium-rich inclusions in meteorites are made of essentially the most refractory minerals that a gas of solar composition would condense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In one inclusion from the Allende meteorite, 29 refractory elements are about 20 times more concentrated than in CI chondrites, the most pristine meteorites, while 13 moderately volatile elements are depleted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.3 Formation of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-3-formation-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Katharina Lodders et al., “Condensation and the Volatility Trend of the Earth,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 221, no. 5 (2025): 54, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-025-01182-6, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.01362. [Open access]","S. J. Desch et al., “Short-Lived Radionuclides in Meteorites and the Sun’s Birth Environment,” in <i>Protostars and Planets VII</i>, Astronomical Society of the Pacific Conference Series 534 (2023): 759–97, https://doi.org/10.26624/sxjy1095, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.11169. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.4 Origin of the Solar System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-4-origin-of-the-solar-system. [Open access]","Maria Bergemann, Katharina Lodders, and Herbert Palme, “The Chemical Composition of the Sun,” preprint of a chapter for the <i>Encyclopedia of Astrophysics</i> (Elsevier, 2025), https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05402. [Open access]","Martin Asplund et al., “The Chemical Composition of the Sun,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 47 (2009): 481–522, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.astro.46.060407.145222, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.0948. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the elements sorted by condensation temperature?","a":"Because in a cooling gas of solar composition each element turns solid at its own temperature, which divides them into refractory, moderately volatile and highly volatile groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than 70 years of calculations have concluded that this condensation between gas and solid was the chief factor setting the abundances of elements in Solar System bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the inner planets poor in water and ice?","a":"Because in the inner parts of the disc the temperature never dropped low enough for ice or carbon-rich compounds to condense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The planets there were built mostly from heavier elements such as iron and silicon, leaving them deficient in the light gases and ices that are common elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are calcium–aluminium-rich inclusions so rich in refractory elements?","a":"Because they are made of essentially the most refractory minerals that a gas of solar composition would condense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In one inclusion from the Allende meteorite, 29 refractory elements are about 20 times more concentrated than in CI chondrites, while 13 moderately volatile elements are depleted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-107": {"questions":["Once read as proof of a nearby supernova, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found in meteorites now seems to have been inherited from the Sun's molecular cloud, enriched by Wolf-Rayet winds.","Gamma rays at 1,809 keV mapped by the INTEGRAL observatory trace <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across the Galaxy, concentrated roughly where new stars are forming."],"abstract":"<b>Aluminium-26</b> is a radioactive isotope of aluminium with a half-life, the time in which half of a sample decays, of about 0.72 million years, made in stars and now extinct in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its decay gives off gamma rays at 1,809 keV, and maps of that emission made by the INTEGRAL observatory show aluminium-26 spread unevenly across the Galaxy, roughly following star-forming regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meteorites show it was alive in the early Solar System, since when most calcium–aluminium-rich inclusions formed there were about 5.23 atoms of aluminium-26 for every 100,000 atoms of ordinary aluminium-27.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radioactive decay releases heat, and aluminium-26 decayed so fast that it is thought to have melted and differentiated the first asteroids, a heating confined to the first 5 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was also spread evenly enough through the disc to serve as a clock, letting the formation of meteorites and their components be dated to within tens of thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Because aluminium-26 decays so quickly, it must have been made in stars shortly before the Sun formed, which makes it a clue to the Sun's birthplace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For a long time its presence was read as demanding that the Sun formed very close to a supernova, which injected fresh material into the young disc or its parent cloud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A suitable supernova needs a large birth cluster, and one estimate holds that a group of at least 1,000 stars would give a reasonable chance of producing a star of about 25 solar masses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A newer view is that the Sun inherited aluminium-26 from its molecular cloud, enriched over millions of years by winds from massive Wolf-Rayet stars and by supernovae in a spiral arm of the Galaxy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Another model has it delivered by the wind of a single massive star into a dense shell of gas in which the Sun then formed, and the Solar System's starting ratio is similar to that found in star-forming regions today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["S. J. Desch et al., “Short-Lived Radionuclides in Meteorites and the Sun’s Birth Environment,” in <i>Protostars and Planets VII</i>, Astronomical Society of the Pacific Conference Series 534 (2023): 759–97, https://doi.org/10.26624/sxjy1095, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.11169. [Open access]","Steve Desch and Núria Miret-Roig, “The Sun’s Birth Environment: Context for Meteoritics,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 220, no. 7 (2024): 76, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-024-01113-x, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.10638. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]","M. Gounelle and G. Meynet, “Solar System Genealogy Revealed by Extinct Short-Lived Radionuclides in Meteorites,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 545 (2012): A4, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201219031, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.5879. [Open access]","Fred C. Adams, “The Birth Environment of the Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 48 (2010): 47–85, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-081309-130830, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.5444. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.3 Dating Planetary Surfaces,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-3-dating-planetary-surfaces. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does such a short-lived isotope point to stars near the Sun's birth?","a":"Because with a half-life of about 0.72 million years, aluminium-26 must have been made in stars shortly before the Sun formed to still be present in the solar nebula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its abundance therefore ties the Sun's birth to recent activity by massive stars, whether a single nearby supernova or a molecular cloud enriched by many such stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the first asteroids melt?","a":"Because they grew large while they still contained live aluminium-26, whose decay released heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With a half-life of about 0.7 million years, that heating was confined to the first 5 million years, so the parent bodies of chondrites, which formed later, escaped melting and kept their pristine structure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why has the nearby-supernova explanation lost favour?","a":"Because the short-lived isotopes in meteorites were spread evenly through the solar nebula, which points to inheritance from the Sun's molecular cloud rather than a late injection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their abundances fit a cloud in a spiral arm enriched mainly by Wolf-Rayet winds, which massive stars blow within 4 million years while still near their birth clouds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Aluminium-26"},
    "astro-108": {"questions":["Of 30 candidate stars examined in detail, only HD 162826 in Hercules matched the Sun in both composition and past orbit, making it a likely member of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> scattered through the Galaxy.","Sedna's distant orbit, probably shaped by a star passing near the young Sun, is evidence for a dense birth cluster whose other members, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, later drifted apart."],"abstract":"The <b>solar siblings</b> are the stars that were born in the same cluster as the Sun and have since dispersed through the Galaxy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most stars are not born alone but in groups and clusters, and the great majority of these disperse within about 10 million years, with only about 10 per cent staying bound much longer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Sun's birth cluster dissolved its members kept similar orbits around the centre of the Galaxy, and one model predicts that 10 to 60 of them still lie within about 100 parsecs of the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The old open cluster M 67 has stars with a composition similar to the Sun's, but its present orbit around the Galaxy is very different from the Sun's, which counts against it being the lost birth cluster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Of 30 candidates examined in detail, only two had the Sun's chemical composition, and only one, HD 162826, a star about 110 light-years away in Hercules, also passed the test of its past orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The strongest evidence that the Sun was born in a cluster comes from the outer Solar System, where about 200 objects scattered by Neptune have orbits that must have been disturbed afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sedna, with an average distance of about 506 au and a closest approach of 76 au, is a more extreme case, and the one simulation that matched these orbits used a star passing about 175 au from the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Such an encounter would be improbable unless the Sun stayed in a dense, gravitationally bound cluster for up to about 100 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Short-lived radioactive isotopes and the size of the early disc point the same way, and one review settled on a birth group of about 1,000 to 10,000 stars as a working scenario.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Measurements of distance and motion from the Gaia mission are expected to narrow the search, though predictions of where siblings now lie depend strongly on the assumed gravity of the Galaxy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Fred C. Adams, “The Birth Environment of the Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 48 (2010): 47–85, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-081309-130830, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.5444. [Open access]","Steve Desch and Núria Miret-Roig, “The Sun’s Birth Environment: Context for Meteoritics,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 220, no. 7 (2024): 76, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-024-01113-x, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.10638. [Open access]","Simon F. Portegies Zwart, “The Lost Siblings of the Sun,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 696, no. 1 (2009): L13–16, https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-637X/696/1/L13, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0903.0237. [Open access]","I. Ramírez et al., “Elemental Abundances of Solar Sibling Candidates,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 787, no. 2 (2014): 154, https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-637X/787/2/154, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.1723. [Open access]","C. A. Martínez-Barbosa et al., “The Evolution of the Sun’s Birth Cluster and the Search for the Solar Siblings with <i>Gaia</i>,” <i>Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society</i> 457, no. 1 (2016): 1062–75, https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stw006, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.00447. [Open access]","S. Pfalzner et al., “The Formation of the Solar System,” <i>Physica Scripta</i> 90, no. 6 (2015): 068001, https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-8949/90/6/068001, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03101. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would the Sun's siblings be hard to find?","a":"Because the birth cluster dissolved long ago and its stars now hide among unrelated field stars, spread through the Galaxy on orbits similar to the Sun's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of 30 candidates chosen from their motions and other survey data, only two proved to share the Sun's chemical composition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do distant Kuiper belt objects suggest a crowded birthplace?","a":"Because about 200 objects scattered by Neptune, and Sedna in a more extreme way, have orbits that must have been disturbed after they were scattered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The one simulation that matched them used a star passing about 175 au from the Sun, an event that is improbable unless the Sun stayed in a dense cluster for tens of millions of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does finding even a few siblings matter?","a":"Because finding even a few would strongly constrain the size of the parent cluster and the place in the Galaxy where the Sun was born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Simulations show that where the siblings are now depends on the cluster's starting conditions and on the Galaxy's bar and spiral arms, so real siblings would test those models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Solar sibling"},
    "astro-109": {"questions":["With about 0.1 hydrogen atoms per cubic centimetre and temperatures of thousands of kelvin, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one of about fifteen clouds within 15 parsecs of the Sun.","Possibly swept up along with its neighbours by a supernova about 1.2 million years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now presses on the heliosphere that Voyager 1 left in 2012."],"abstract":"The <b>Local Interstellar Cloud</b> is the warm, thin, partly ionised cloud of interstellar gas through which the Sun is currently travelling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is one of about fifteen such clouds within roughly 15 parsecs of the Sun, known together as the Cluster of Local Interstellar Clouds, and the Sun may be passing through it together with a neighbouring cloud called the G cloud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> These clouds are warm, at about 3,000 to 12,000 K, and extremely thin, with about 0.1 hydrogen atoms in each cubic centimetre of the Local Interstellar Cloud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within about 4 parsecs of the Sun such clouds fill space completely, and the temperature inside the Local Interstellar Cloud varies on scales smaller than about 5,100 au, a distance the Sun crosses in about 1,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The clouds sit inside the Local Bubble, a cavity of thin, ionised gas that extends about 100 to 200 parsecs from the Sun in every direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On the scale of the Galaxy, the Sun orbits the centre at a distance of about 26,000 light-years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It moves at about 200 kilometres (124 miles) per second and takes approximately 225 million years to complete one circuit of the Galaxy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sun lies near the inner edge of the Orion–Cygnus Arm, a short spur about 10,000 light-years long that also contains the Orion Nebula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surrounding interstellar gas presses on the heliosphere, the bubble that the solar wind carves out around the Sun and planets, and Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 crossed its boundary into interstellar space in 2012 and 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The clouds' shared motion may come from a supernova in the nearby Upper Centaurus Lupus group about 1.2 million years ago, which one model says swept them up over the past million years as its remnant expanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “25.2 Spiral Structure,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/25-2-spiral-structure. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “25.3 The Mass of the Galaxy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/25-3-the-mass-of-the-galaxy. [Open access]","Catherine Zucker et al., “The Origin of the Cluster of Local Interstellar Clouds,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 986, no. 1 (2025): 58, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/adc920, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00093. [Open access]","Jeffrey L. Linsky et al., “Inhomogeneity within Local Interstellar Clouds,” <i>Astronomical Journal</i> 164, no. 3 (2022): 106, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ac816b, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.02428. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Voyager: Interstellar Mission,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/interstellar-mission/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “IBEX: Interstellar Boundary Explorer,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/ibex/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the cloud around the Sun matter to Earth?","a":"Because the pressure of the surrounding interstellar medium, balanced against the outer heliosphere, can greatly alter the heliosphere's size and shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cloud the Sun is travelling through is known to have a substantial effect on the heliosphere and on how many Galactic cosmic rays reach Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could conditions around the Sun change fairly quickly?","a":"Because the gas of the Local Interstellar Cloud is far from uniform, and its temperature varies on scales smaller than about 5,100 au.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Sun crosses that distance in about 1,000 years, so the conditions pressing on the heliosphere can change within that time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the nearby clouds thought to share an origin?","a":"Because although each cloud has its own velocity, the whole complex shows a coherent bulk motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One model traces this to the most recent supernova in the nearby Upper Centaurus Lupus group, about 1.2 million years ago, whose expanding remnant would have swept the clouds up over the past million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Local Interstellar Cloud"},
    "astro-110": {"questions":["Surveys counting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around other stars find giant planets most common at 1 to 10 au and more frequent around massive, metal-rich stars.","Whether the Solar System is typical depends partly on how often <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> occur, since each of the Sun's traits is common on its own even if all of them together are rarer."],"abstract":"<b>Jupiter analogues</b> are giant planets that orbit Sun-like stars at roughly Jupiter's distance, having stayed near where they formed beyond the ice line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their frequency is a key number for theories of planet formation and for judging how common or rare systems like the Solar System are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a sample of 202 Sun-like stars, about 6 per cent turned out to have a giant planet orbiting 3 to 7 au from their star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Counted another way, there are about 14 giant planets per 100 stars between 2 and 8 au, and giant planets are most common at 1 to 10 au, near the water-ice line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Giant planets of this kind are more common around stars that are more massive and richer in heavy elements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> As of November 2026 more than 6,400 planets had been confirmed around other stars, enough to ask how typical the Sun's family is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The most common planets turn out to be between one and three times Earth's radius, sizes that are completely absent from the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Sun-like star has about a 10 per cent chance of hosting a giant planet with a period shorter than a few years and about a 50 per cent chance of a compact system of smaller planets with periods under a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Taken one at a time, the Sun's traits are common, since it is a single star, rich in heavy elements, and among the roughly 20 per cent of Sun-like stars that have made giant planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Combined, the chance of a system having all of them is under 0.2 per cent, yet with about 100 billion stars in the Galaxy that is no reason to call the Solar System unusual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Robert A. Wittenmyer et al., “The Anglo-Australian Planet Search XXIV: The Frequency of Jupiter Analogs,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 819, no. 1 (2016): 28, https://doi.org/10.3847/0004-637X/819/1/28, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.05465. [Open access]","Benjamin J. Fulton et al., “California Legacy Survey. II. Occurrence of Giant Planets beyond the Ice Line,” <i>Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series</i> 255, no. 1 (2021): 14, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4365/abfcc1, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.11584. [Open access]","Joshua N. Winn and Daniel C. Fabrycky, “The Occurrence and Architecture of Exoplanetary Systems,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 53 (2015): 409–47, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-082214-122246, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.4199. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “21.5 Exoplanets Everywhere: What We Are Learning,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-5-exoplanets-everywhere-what-we-are-learning. [Open access]","Fred C. Adams, “The Birth Environment of the Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 48 (2010): 47–85, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-081309-130830, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.5444. [Open access]","NASA Exoplanet Science Institute, “NASA Exoplanet Archive,” accessed 12 November 2026, https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the frequency of Jupiter-like planets matter?","a":"Because a giant planet that has stayed near where it formed beyond the ice line is a critical datum for testing the details of planet formation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also needed to understand how common or rare planetary systems like the Solar System are in the Galaxy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were astronomers surprised by the planets they found?","a":"Because before exoplanets were discovered most astronomers expected other systems to look like the Solar System, with roughly circular orbits and the most massive planets several au from their star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Instead the most common planets turned out to be sizes the Solar System lacks, between one and three times Earth's radius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Solar System not considered unusual despite its rare combination?","a":"Because each of the Sun's properties, such as being a single star rich in heavy elements with giant planets, is relatively common on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The chance of having all of them together is under 0.2 per cent, but with about 100 billion stars in the Galaxy the probability would have to be much lower for the Solar System to count as unusual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Jupiter analogue"},
    "astro-111": {"questions":["Around other stars, NASA counts a planet as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> if its radius lies between about half and twice Earth's and its bulk is dominated by rock or iron.","A gap in the sizes of small Kepler planets near 1.5 to 2 Earth radii suggests where a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ends and a larger world wrapped in gas begins."],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.1 Overview of Our Planetary System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-1-overview-of-our-planetary-system. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.2 Composition and Structure of Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-2-composition-and-structure-of-planets. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Planetary Fact Sheet – Metric,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 18 March 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Terrestrial,” Exoplanets, https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/terrestrial/. [Open access]","S. Seager et al., “Mass-Radius Relationships for Solid Exoplanets,” <i>The Astrophysical Journal</i> 669, no. 2 (2007): 1279–97, https://doi.org/10.1086/521346, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.2895. [Open access]","Leslie A. Rogers, “Most 1.6 Earth-Radius Planets Are Not Rocky,” <i>The Astrophysical Journal</i> 801, no. 1 (2015): 41, https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-637X/801/1/41, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.4457. [Open access]","Benjamin J. Fulton et al., “The California-Kepler Survey. III. A Gap in the Radius Distribution of Small Planets,” <i>The Astronomical Journal</i> 154, no. 3 (2017): 109, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/aa80eb, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.10375. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Earth: Facts,” https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/. [Open access]"],"abstract":"A <b>terrestrial planet</b> is a relatively small planet made mainly of rock and metal, with a solid surface, and in the Solar System the name covers the four inner planets, Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars, of which Earth is the largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Moon is often discussed with them, because chemically and structurally it is built like them, which brings the number of terrestrial worlds near the Sun to five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their most abundant rocks are silicates, compounds of silicon and oxygen, and their most common metal is iron, elements rarer in the universe as a whole than the hydrogen and helium of the giant planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each is layered, with the densest metals in a central core and lighter silicates nearer the surface, which shows that all of them were once hot enough to melt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Earth, Venus and Mars have roughly similar bulk compositions, about one third iron alloys and two thirds silicates by mass, while Mercury has the greatest proportion of metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their densities show it plainly: Earth averages about 5.5 times the density of water and Mars about 3.9, against about 1.3 for Jupiter and 0.7 for Saturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around other stars, NASA counts a planet as terrestrial if its radius lies between about half and twice Earth's, with a bulk dominated by rock or iron and a solid or liquid surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Size alone is an imperfect guide, since models of solid planets show that a measured mass and radius can tell iron, silicate and water-ice worlds apart only when both are known to about 5 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Measured masses suggest that most close-in planets of 1.6 Earth radii are too low in density to be made of iron and silicates alone, so the rocky class probably ends below that size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A survey of 2,025 Kepler planets found a scarcity of planets between 1.5 and 2 Earth radii, a gap that splits small planets into rocky cores of up to about 1.5 Earth radii and larger worlds wrapped in gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why are the terrestrial planets layered inside?","a":"Because they were once hot enough to melt, and in a molten planet gravity separates the materials by density.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The heavier metals sink to form a core while the lightest minerals float up to form a crust, and the layering is preserved when the planet cools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For a rocky planet to separate this way it must be heated to the melting point of rock, typically more than 1,300 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a planet of Earth's size still fail to be rocky?","a":"Because radius alone does not reveal what a planet is made of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Planets with a thick envelope of gas or a large share of water can be as big as rocky ones but far less dense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Measured masses show that most close-in planets of 1.6 Earth radii are too light for iron and silicates alone, so they must carry lighter material such as hydrogen, helium or ices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the gap in planet sizes matter?","a":"Because it seems to mark a boundary between two kinds of small planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Among 2,025 Kepler planets on orbits shorter than 100 days, planets between 1.5 and 2 Earth radii were at least two times rarer than their neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The survey read this as evidence that such planets have rocky cores of up to about 1.5 Earth radii, with varying amounts of low-density gas deciding their total size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Terrestrial planet"},
    "astro-112": {"questions":["Although its noon surface reaches about 430 °C (806 °F), water ice survives in permanently shadowed polar craters on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the innermost planet, which MESSENGER was first to orbit.","An iron-rich core reaching about 85 per cent of the way to the surface makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the second densest planet after Earth, though it is less than half Earth's width."],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.5 Mercury,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-5-mercury. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Mercury: Facts,” https://science.nasa.gov/mercury/facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Mercury Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 11 January 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/mercuryfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “MESSENGER,” Missions, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/messenger/. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “BepiColombo,” ESA Science & Exploration, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/BepiColombo. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.2 Composition and Structure of Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-2-composition-and-structure-of-planets. [Open access]","Alexandre C. M. Correia and Jacques Laskar, “Mercury’s Capture into the 3/2 Spin-Orbit Resonance as a Result of Its Chaotic Dynamics,” <i>Nature</i> 429, no. 6994 (2004): 848–50, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature02609. [Paywalled]","Paul K. Byrne et al., “Mercury’s Global Contraction Much Greater Than Earlier Estimates,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 7, no. 4 (2014): 301–7, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo2097. [Paywalled]","NASA Science Editorial Team, “Mercury’s Strange Hollows,” NASA Science, 17 October 2023, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/mercury/mercurys-strange-hollows/. [Open access]"],"abstract":"<b>Mercury</b> is the smallest planet in the Solar System and the nearest to the Sun, which it circles every 88 days at an average 0.39 au, about 58 million kilometres (36 million miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its orbit has a high eccentricity of 0.206, which carries it from 46 million kilometres (29 million miles) from the Sun at its closest to about 70 million kilometres (43 million miles) at its farthest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is 4,878 kilometres (3,031 miles) across, less than half the width of Earth, yet its mean density is about 5.4 times that of water, second only to Earth's among the planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That density means it is largely metal, the greatest proportion of any terrestrial planet, with an iron-rich core reaching about 85 per cent of the way to the surface and a rocky outer shell only about 400 kilometres (250 miles) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has no true atmosphere, only a thin exosphere of atoms knocked off the surface by the solar wind and by meteoroids, and its weak magnetic field is about 1 per cent as strong as Earth's at the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> With no air to hold the heat, its surface climbs to about 430 °C (806 °F) at noon and falls to about −170 °C (−274 °F) before dawn, the greatest range of temperature on any planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so, water ice lies in polar craters that never see sunlight, which is possible because the planet's axis is barely tilted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It turns three times on its axis for every two orbits, and its cratered face is crossed by long cliffs raised as the cooling planet shrank, by as much as 7 kilometres (4 miles) in radius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> MESSENGER became the first spacecraft to orbit it in 2011 and found bright hollows that look younger than the craters around them, and the European and Japanese BepiColombo, launched in 2018, is the second orbiter sent there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Why it holds so much metal is still unsettled: giant impacts early in its history may have torn away much of its rocky mantle, or heat from the young Sun may have driven off lighter material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why does Mercury get so cold at night when it is so close to the Sun?","a":"Because it has no appreciable atmosphere to hold heat once the Sun has set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its days and nights are also very long, so the ground has many Earth days in which to radiate its warmth away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surface reaches about 430 °C (806 °F) at noon and drops to about −170 °C (−274 °F) before dawn, and craters near the poles that never receive sunlight are colder still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is such a small planet so dense?","a":"Because most of it is metal rather than rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its iron-rich core has a radius of about 2,074 kilometres (1,289 miles), about 85 per cent of the planet's radius, leaving a rocky outer shell only about 400 kilometres (250 miles) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among the terrestrial planets Mercury has the greatest proportion of metals and the Moon the lowest, which their densities reveal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can ice exist on the planet nearest the Sun?","a":"Because some places on Mercury never receive any sunlight at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its rotational axis is tilted by almost nothing, so the floors of deep craters near the poles stay in permanent shadow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Water ice was first detected there by radar and confirmed by MESSENGER, which also found organic, carbon-rich compounds mixed with the ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mercury (planet)"},
    "astro-113": {"questions":["The Caloris basin, about 1,550 kilometres (960 miles) across, is the largest impact structure in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a cratered record that at first sight resembles the Moon's.","Bright shallow hollows that look younger than the craters they sit in suggest that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is still changing, perhaps as volatile minerals vaporise in the fierce sunlight."],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.5 Mercury,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-5-mercury. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Mercury: Facts,” https://science.nasa.gov/mercury/facts/. [Open access]","Paul K. Byrne et al., “Mercury’s Global Contraction Much Greater Than Earlier Estimates,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 7, no. 4 (2014): 301–7, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo2097. [Paywalled]","NASA Science Editorial Team, “Mercury’s Strange Hollows,” NASA Science, 17 October 2023, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/mercury/mercurys-strange-hollows/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “MESSENGER,” Missions, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/messenger/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.2 Composition and Structure of Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-2-composition-and-structure-of-planets. [Open access]"],"abstract":"The <b>geology of Mercury</b> is the record kept in the planet's surface, which at first sight looks much like the Moon's, covered with thousands of craters and larger impact basins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The largest basin, Caloris, is about 1,550 kilometres (960 miles) across, and the smooth plains inside it cover almost two million square kilometres (770,000 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bright rays of crushed rock splash out from younger craters, and they darken with time as dust impacts and particles of the solar wind weather the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between the craters lie wide smooth plains, and the surface preserves evidence of past volcanic activity that spread lava across parts of the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That volcanism probably ended long ago, at about the same time as the Moon's, because a small world loses the heat of its formation quickly, much as a small potato cools faster than a large one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The planet's most distinctive landforms are long curved cliffs called scarps, some more than a kilometre high and hundreds of kilometres long, which wind across the plains and the cratered ground alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because they cut across craters, the scarps must be younger than them, and they are read as wrinkles in a crust that shrank as the interior cooled and contracted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There is no sign of plate tectonics, since Mercury's outer shell forms a single plate, so its ridges and scarps record the whole planet growing smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Orbital mapping found far more of these structures than earlier images had shown, and together they imply that Mercury's radius has shrunk by as much as 7 kilometres (4 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> MESSENGER also found hollows, bright shallow pits up to about 1.6 kilometres (1 mile) across that look younger than the craters they lie in, perhaps formed as sulphur-bearing minerals vaporised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why must Mercury's scarps be younger than its craters?","a":"Because the scarps can be seen cutting across craters, so the craters were already there when the cliffs formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Discovery Scarp, nearly a kilometre high and more than 100 kilometres (62 miles) long, slices through several of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If the standard cratering timeline applies to Mercury, the shrinking that raised the scarps happened within the last 4 billion years, after the early period of heavy bombardment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the whole planet shrink?","a":"Because its interior has been cooling since it formed, and cooling material contracts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mercury's crust is a single plate rather than many, so the shrinking could not be taken up at plate boundaries and instead folded and faulted the crust into belts of ridges and scarps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mapping from orbit showed many more such structures than earlier pictures had, raising the estimated shrinkage of the radius to as much as 7 kilometres (4 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the hollows suggest that Mercury is still changing?","a":"Because they appear to be younger than the craters in which they are found, while those craters date back as far as 4 billion years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many lie on the mountains raised inside large craters, material thrown up from deep underground by the impact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One idea is that heat, the solar wind and tiny meteorites vaporise sulphur-bearing minerals in that exposed rock, weakening it until it crumbles into pits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Geology of Mercury"},
    "astro-114": {"questions":["Radar in the mid-1960s showed that Mercury does not keep one face to the Sun but is held in a 3:2 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a rotation period of about 59 days.","Solar tides slowed Mercury's early spin until it was captured in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an outcome that its chaotic and at times highly eccentric orbit made much more likely."],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.5 Mercury,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-5-mercury. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Mercury Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 11 January 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/mercuryfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Mercury: Facts,” https://science.nasa.gov/mercury/facts/. [Open access]","G. Colombo, “Rotational Period of the Planet Mercury,” <i>Nature</i> 208, no. 5010 (1965): 575, https://doi.org/10.1038/208575a0. [Paywalled]","Alexandre C. M. Correia and Jacques Laskar, “Mercury’s Capture into the 3/2 Spin-Orbit Resonance as a Result of Its Chaotic Dynamics,” <i>Nature</i> 429, no. 6994 (2004): 848–50, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature02609. [Paywalled]","Alexandre C. M. Correia and Jacques Laskar, “Mercury’s Capture into the 3/2 Spin–Orbit Resonance Including the Effect of Core–Mantle Friction,” <i>Icarus</i> 201, no. 1 (2009): 1–11, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2008.12.034, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.1843. [Open access]"],"abstract":"A <b>spin–orbit resonance</b> is a state in which a body's rotation period and its orbital period stand in a ratio of small whole numbers, so that the same alignment with the body it orbits repeats exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Mercury is the only planet in such a state, turning three times on its axis for every two trips round the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its rotation period measured against the stars is about 58.6 days, two thirds of its 88-day year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One result is a very long solar day, since the Sun takes 176 Earth days, two Mercury years, to return to the same place in the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the orbit is eccentric, at Caloris the Sun hangs almost motionless overhead at noon, which makes such hot longitudes the hottest places on Mercury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For many years the planet was thought to keep one face to the Sun, turning once in each 88-day orbit, until radar echoes in the mid-1960s showed that it spins faster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radar gave a period of about 59 days, and in 1965 it was pointed out that a spin of exactly two thirds of the orbital period could be stable, with the long axis of the planet's slightly uneven body lined up with the Sun at each closest approach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tides raised by the Sun slowed an originally faster spin until it was caught in the resonance, a capture regarded as well understood once the other planets' pull is included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Why it stopped at three to two rather than another ratio is less settled, since simple tidal models give that outcome only about a 7 per cent chance, while simulations of its chaotic orbit made capture into it the most likely result, 55.4 per cent of the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Friction at the boundary of a molten core makes capture into other ratios, such as two to one, more likely, and later simulations ended in some resonance 99.8 per cent of the time, with three to two only one of several common results.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why does a day on Mercury last two of its years?","a":"Because its spin and its orbit are so close in speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mercury turns once against the stars in about 58.6 days while it travels round the Sun in 88, so after each turn it has moved a long way along its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sun therefore takes 176 Earth days, exactly two Mercury years, to return to the same place in its sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the three-to-two state stay stable?","a":"Because the Sun's pull on Mercury is strongest at its closest approach, and the resonance lines the planet up the same way every time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With a spin of two thirds of the orbital period, the axis of the planet's slightly uneven body points nearly at the Sun around each perihelion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The torque on that uneven shape can balance the tidal torque, giving a stable rotation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the capture hard to explain?","a":"Because simple models made it unlikely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With a realistic tidal model, the computed chance of capture into the three-to-two state was only about 7 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Simulations of 1,000 possible histories over 4 billion years showed that Mercury's chaotic orbit can push its eccentricity above 0.325, which made capture the most likely outcome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A molten core complicates the picture, because friction at its boundary favours other resonances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-115": {"questions":["Spinning backwards once every 243 Earth days, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a day longer than its 225-day year and comes closer to Earth than any other planet does.","An atmosphere holding about a hundred times the expected share of deuterium is a sign that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the second planet from the Sun, once had far more water than it has today."],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.1 The Nearest Planets: An Overview,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-1-the-nearest-planets-an-overview. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.2 The Geology of Venus,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-2-the-geology-of-venus. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Venus: Facts,” https://science.nasa.gov/venus/facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Venus Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 11 January 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/venusfact.html. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Climate and Evolution,” Venus Express, 29 November 2007, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Venus_Express/Climate_and_evolution. [Open access]","T. M. Donahue et al., “Venus Was Wet: A Measurement of the Ratio of Deuterium to Hydrogen,” <i>Science</i> 216, no. 4546 (1982): 630–33, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.216.4546.630. [Paywalled]","Jean-Luc Margot et al., “Spin State and Moment of Inertia of Venus,” <i>Nature Astronomy</i> 5, no. 7 (2021): 676–83, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41550-021-01339-7, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.01504. [Open access]","Robert R. Herrick and Scott Hensley, “Surface Changes Observed on a Venusian Volcano during the Magellan Mission,” <i>Science</i> 379, no. 6638 (2023): 1205–8, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abm7735. [Paywalled]","M. J. Way et al., “Was Venus the First Habitable World of Our Solar System?,” <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 43, no. 16 (2016): 8376–83, https://doi.org/10.1002/2016GL069790, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.00706. [Open access]"],"abstract":"<b>Venus</b> is the second planet from the Sun, orbiting at an average of 0.72 au, about 108 million kilometres (67 million miles), on a path that is very nearly circular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In bulk it is almost Earth's twin, with 95 per cent of Earth's radius and 82 per cent of its mass, and it comes closer to Earth than any other planet, to about 38 million kilometres (24 million miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Sun and the Moon it is the brightest object in the sky, seen as a morning or evening star, and a small telescope shows it going through phases like the Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has no moon and no rings, and it spins very slowly in the opposite direction to most planets, taking 243 Earth days to turn once against a year of 225.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Permanent clouds hide its surface, so its landscape has been mapped by radar from orbit, most fully by the Magellan spacecraft at a resolution of about 100 metres (330 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Beneath the clouds the surface averages about 464 °C (867 °F), hotter than Mercury's, under an atmosphere that presses down with about 92 times the pressure at sea level on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That atmosphere is 96.5 per cent carbon dioxide, and the greenhouse effect it drives, not the planet's nearness to the Sun, is what makes Venus the hottest planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its size and composition make it the planet most like Earth, yet it shows no plate tectonics of Earth's kind, it is almost entirely dry, and a volcanic vent seen to change shape in radar images suggests it is still active.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Venus may once have been far more like Earth, with large amounts of water since lost to space, and climate models suggest it could have kept a shallow ocean until at least 715 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Its hydrogen holds about a hundred times the expected share of deuterium, the heavy form of hydrogen, which means that at least a small fraction of an ocean's worth of water was once released on Venus, and possibly much more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why is Venus hotter than Mercury, though farther from the Sun?","a":"Because of its atmosphere rather than its distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Venus has a dense atmosphere that is 96.5 per cent carbon dioxide and presses down with about 92 bars, and the greenhouse effect it drives keeps the surface at about 464 °C (867 °F) with almost no difference between day and night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mercury has no atmosphere to hold heat, so its night side cools sharply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Venus have to be mapped by radar?","a":"Because its permanent clouds hide the surface from ordinary telescopes and cameras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Radar waves pass through the clouds and reflect from the ground, as an air traffic controller's radar finds planes in thick cloud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Magellan spacecraft mapped Venus with imaging radar at a resolution of about 100 metres (330 feet), so features larger than a football field became visible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do scientists think Venus once had water?","a":"Because its remaining hydrogen is unusually rich in deuterium, the heavy form of hydrogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A probe measured about a hundred times the expected proportion, which means that at least a small fraction of a terrestrial ocean was once released on Venus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Venus Express found that hydrogen and oxygen still escape from the top of the atmosphere, the last stage of a long loss of water to space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Venus"},
    "astro-116": {"questions":["At the cloud tops, winds of up to 360 kilometres per hour (224 miles per hour) race round the planet in the super-rotating <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, far faster than the solid surface turns.","Condensed, all the water vapour in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would make a layer only about 3 centimetres (1.2 inches) deep, a measure of how much water that world has lost."],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.3 The Massive Atmosphere of Venus,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-3-the-massive-atmosphere-of-venus. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Venus Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 11 January 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/venusfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Venus: Facts,” https://science.nasa.gov/venus/facts/. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Climate and Evolution,” Venus Express, 29 November 2007, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Venus_Express/Climate_and_evolution. [Open access]","Y. J. Lee et al., “Brightness Modulations of Our Nearest Terrestrial Planet Venus Reveal Atmospheric Super-Rotation Rather Than Surface Features,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 11 (2020): 5720, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-19385-6. [Open access]","T. M. Donahue et al., “Venus Was Wet: A Measurement of the Ratio of Deuterium to Hydrogen,” <i>Science</i> 216, no. 4546 (1982): 630–33, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.216.4546.630. [Paywalled]","Jean-Luc Margot et al., “Spin State and Moment of Inertia of Venus,” <i>Nature Astronomy</i> 5, no. 7 (2021): 676–83, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41550-021-01339-7, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.01504. [Open access]"],"abstract":"The <b>atmosphere of Venus</b> is a massive envelope of gas that presses on the planet's surface with about 92 bars, roughly ninety times the pressure at sea level on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is 96.5 per cent carbon dioxide and 3.5 per cent nitrogen by volume, with small traces of sulphur dioxide, argon and water vapour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its lowest layer, the troposphere, reaches at least 50 kilometres (31 miles) up, and within it gas heated from below rises slowly near the equator and sinks over the poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between about 30 and 60 kilometres (19 and 37 miles) up, a thick deck of cloud made mainly of droplets of sulphuric acid covers the whole planet, while below 30 kilometres (19 miles) the air is clear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the bottom the surface averages about 464 °C (867 °F), with almost no difference between day and night and only very gentle winds of less than a few kilometres per hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Higher up the picture changes, for at the cloud tops winds of up to 360 kilometres per hour (224 miles per hour) whip around the planet, and shifts in the air's motion alter the solid planet's day by about 20 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> These super-rotating winds carry planetary-scale waves that make Venus brighten and dim with periods of about 3.7 and 4.6 days, rhythms unrelated to the slow turning of the solid planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 50 kilometres (31 miles) above the surface, inside the clouds, the temperature falls to between 30 and 70 °C (86 and 158 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The atmosphere is very dry, and condensed, all its water vapour would form a layer only about 3 centimetres (1.2 inches) deep, against about 3 kilometres (1.9 miles) for Earth's oceans and air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its hydrogen holds about a hundred times the expected share of deuterium, which points to a larger store of water in the past, broken up by sunlight while the light hydrogen escaped to space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why is there almost no difference between day and night at the surface?","a":"Because the atmosphere is so massive that it evens out the temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Being at its base is like being a kilometre or more below the ocean surface on Earth, where the mass of water above keeps conditions uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surface stays at about 464 °C (867 °F) and its diurnal temperature range is close to zero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can clouds of sulphuric acid last on Venus?","a":"Because the atmosphere is so dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sulphuric acid forms when sulphur dioxide combines with water, and on Earth sulphur dioxide from volcanoes is quickly diluted and washed out by rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the dry air of Venus there is no rain to remove it, so the acid droplets persist in a thick layer between about 30 and 60 kilometres (19 and 37 miles) above the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the deuterium suggest lost water?","a":"Because an excess of deuterium that large implies far more water in the past than the trace left today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ultraviolet sunlight breaks up water vapour and the light hydrogen escapes from the atmosphere, so once the water is gone it cannot be restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A probe found about a hundred times the expected proportion of deuterium, which means at least a small fraction of an ocean's worth of water was once released on Venus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Venus Express showed that hydrogen and oxygen are still escaping from the top of the atmosphere today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Atmosphere of Venus"},
    "astro-117": {"questions":["In a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, air thick with water vapour limits the heat a planet can radiate, so warming goes on until the surface nears about 1,400 K and the oceans boil away.","Earth is expected to undergo a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in around 2 billion years as the Sun brightens, though adding carbon dioxide alone probably cannot trigger one now."],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.3 The Massive Atmosphere of Venus,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-3-the-massive-atmosphere-of-venus. [Open access]","Colin Goldblatt and Andrew J. Watson, “The Runaway Greenhouse: Implications for Future Climate Change, Geoengineering and Planetary Atmospheres,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 370, no. 1974 (2012): 4197–216, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2012.0004, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.1593. [Open access]","Jérémy Leconte et al., “Increased Insolation Threshold for Runaway Greenhouse Processes on Earth-Like Planets,” <i>Nature</i> 504, no. 7479 (2013): 268–71, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12827, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.3337. [Open access]","M. J. Way et al., “Was Venus the First Habitable World of Our Solar System?,” <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 43, no. 16 (2016): 8376–83, https://doi.org/10.1002/2016GL069790, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.00706. [Open access]","M. J. Way and Anthony D. Del Genio, “Venusian Habitable Climate Scenarios: Modeling Venus Through Time and Applications to Slowly Rotating Venus-Like Exoplanets,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 125, no. 5 (2020): e2019JE006276, https://doi.org/10.1029/2019JE006276, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.05704. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.6 Divergent Planetary Evolution,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-6-divergent-planetary-evolution. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Climate and Evolution,” Venus Express, 29 November 2007, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Venus_Express/Climate_and_evolution. [Open access]"],"abstract":"A <b>runaway greenhouse effect</b> is a climate feedback in which warming evaporates more water and releases more greenhouse gas, which traps more heat and drives further warming until the oceans are gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is not simply a large greenhouse effect but an evolutionary process, in which a planet passes from mild greenhouse warming to a new and far hotter balance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The trap works because a hot atmosphere rich in water vapour limits the heat a planet can radiate to space, and warming stops only when the surface reaches about 1,400 K and radiates at wavelengths where water absorbs poorly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Such heating would evaporate an entire ocean, and Earth is expected to suffer a runaway in around 2 billion years as the Sun grows brighter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most evidence suggests that adding carbon dioxide cannot trigger a full runaway on present-day Earth, and a three-dimensional climate model put the threshold higher than simpler models had, at about 375 watts per square metre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Venus is the classic case, for it may once have had moderate temperatures and oceans, and a small increase in heating, perhaps from the slowly brightening Sun, could have set the feedback going.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As its oceans evaporated, Venus lost the water that on Earth holds carbon dioxide dissolved in the sea or bound into rocks, so the gas built up in the atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sunlight then broke up the water vapour, the light hydrogen escaped to space and the oxygen combined with surface rock, so the loss of water could not be reversed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Climate models using Magellan's map of the surface suggest that a slowly rotating early Venus with a shallow ocean could have stayed habitable until at least 715 million years ago, and one proposal is that vast volcanic outpourings ended that mild period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Why Venus took this path while Earth did not is still not understood, and the planet stands as evidence that a world cannot keep warming without a major change in its oceans and atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why does water vapour make the warming run away?","a":"Because water vapour is itself a greenhouse gas, so a warmer climate that evaporates more water traps still more heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A hot atmosphere rich in water vapour limits how much thermal radiation can escape to space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The warming stops only when the surface reaches about 1,400 K and emits in the near-infrared, where water absorbs poorly, and by then the whole ocean has evaporated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can Venus not return to a milder climate?","a":"Because its water is gone for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On Earth most carbon dioxide is held in rocks or dissolved in the oceans, but Venus lost that safety valve when its oceans evaporated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ultraviolet sunlight split the water vapour, the hydrogen escaped to space and the oxygen combined with surface rock, and Venus Express found hydrogen and oxygen still escaping from the top of the atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might the rotation of early Venus have mattered?","a":"Because simulations show that rotation and topography play a vital part in a planet's climate history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Climate simulations found that early Venus could have had moderate temperatures if it rotated more slowly than once in about 16 Earth days, despite receiving 46 to 70 per cent more sunlight than Earth does today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At its present 243-day rotation, it could have stayed habitable until at least 715 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Runaway greenhouse effect"},
    "astro-118": {"questions":["Craters in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are so few and so fresh that they give an average surface age of only 300 to 600 million years, the mark of a planet-wide outpouring of lava.","A volcanic vent that changed shape between radar images taken eight months apart is among the evidence that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may still be shaped by active eruptions today."],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.2 The Geology of Venus,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-2-the-geology-of-venus. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Venus: Facts,” https://science.nasa.gov/venus/facts/. [Open access]","Suzanne E. Smrekar et al., “Recent Hotspot Volcanism on Venus from VIRTIS Emissivity Data,” <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5978 (2010): 605–8, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1186785. [Paywalled]","European Space Agency, “Hot Lava Flows Discovered on Venus,” Venus Express, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Venus_Express/Hot_lava_flows_discovered_on_Venus. [Open access]","Robert R. Herrick and Scott Hensley, “Surface Changes Observed on a Venusian Volcano during the Magellan Mission,” <i>Science</i> 379, no. 6638 (2023): 1205–8, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abm7735. [Paywalled]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.2 Composition and Structure of Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-2-composition-and-structure-of-planets. [Open access]"],"abstract":"The <b>geology of Venus</b> is known almost entirely from radar, which sees through the planet's permanent clouds, and above all from the Magellan orbiter's images, which show features down to about 100 metres (330 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With no water or ice and only weak surface winds, almost nothing erodes or buries what volcanoes, impacts and movements of the crust produce, so the surface is laid bare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 75 per cent of it is lowland lava plains, and above them rise two highland continents, Aphrodite, about the size of Africa, and Ishtar, about the size of Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ishtar holds the Maxwell Mountains, the highest region on the planet at 11 kilometres (7 miles) above the lowlands, raised like the Himalayas by compression of the crust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thousands of volcanoes dot the surface, from Sif Mons, about 500 kilometres (310 miles) across, to small domes, alongside coronae, broad circular features raised where hot material welled up beneath the crust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Venus shows no subduction zones and so lacks plate tectonics of Earth's kind, though some geologists see possible evidence of past plates in the Aphrodite region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its craters are few and fresh, and their numbers give an average surface age of only about 300 to 600 million years, which suggests a planet-wide outpouring of lava within that time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As one of the two largest terrestrial planets, Venus is expected to keep a molten interior today, so whether its volcanoes are still active is a central question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lava flows at three hotspots give off more infrared heat than their surroundings, suggesting they are younger than 2.5 million years, and short-lived bright spots along the Ganiki Chasma rift have been read as fresh lava.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Radar images taken eight months apart in the early 1990s show a volcanic vent of about 2.2 square kilometres (0.85 square miles) that changed shape, which has been interpreted as evidence of ongoing eruptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why do the craters of Venus look so fresh?","a":"Because very little erodes or fills them once they form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no water or ice on Venus and the surface winds are weak, so almost all the large craters show little damage from lava or windblown dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is one way scientists know that erosion and the laying down of sediment are very slow, and that little has happened since the plains were last resurfaced by volcanic activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are small craters rare on Venus?","a":"Because the thick atmosphere stops small projectiles before they reach the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crater counts show very few craters under 10 kilometres (6 miles) across, so bodies smaller than about 1 kilometre were halted in the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Craters of 10 to 30 kilometres (6 to 19 miles) are often distorted or multiple, because the incoming body broke apart in the atmosphere before striking the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do scientists expect Venus to be volcanically active now?","a":"Because larger planets keep their internal heat longer, and Venus is almost the size of Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The larger a world, the more slowly it cools, and Earth and Venus are thought to still have molten interiors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That heat has to escape somehow, and one way is through volcanic eruptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lava flows at three hotspots look younger than 2.5 million years, because they are less altered by the corrosive atmosphere than their surroundings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Geology of Venus"},
    "astro-119": {"questions":["Fact sheets record Venus's spin period as a negative number and its axial tilt as about 177°, the formal way of describing the planet's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the direction of its orbit.","Solar tides in the rock and in the thick air are the most likely cause of the slow <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Venus, though giant collisions during its formation have also been proposed."],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.1 The Nearest Planets: An Overview,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-1-the-nearest-planets-an-overview. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.1 Overview of Our Planetary System,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-1-overview-of-our-planetary-system. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Venus: Facts,” https://science.nasa.gov/venus/facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Venus Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 11 January 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/venusfact.html. [Open access]","Jean-Luc Margot et al., “Spin State and Moment of Inertia of Venus,” <i>Nature Astronomy</i> 5, no. 7 (2021): 676–83, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41550-021-01339-7, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.01504. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Could Venus Be Shifting Gear?,” Venus Express, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Venus_Express/Could_Venus_be_shifting_gear. [Open access]","Alexandre C. M. Correia and Jacques Laskar, “The Four Final Rotation States of Venus,” <i>Nature</i> 411, no. 6839 (2001): 767–70, https://doi.org/10.1038/35081000. [Paywalled]","P. Auclair-Desrotour et al., “The Rotation of Planets Hosting Atmospheric Tides: From Venus to Habitable Super-Earths,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 603 (2017): A108, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201628701, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.05678. [Open access]"],"abstract":"<b>Retrograde rotation</b> is a body's spin in the opposite direction to its revolution about the Sun, while most planets turn in the same direction as they orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Venus is the clearest planetary case, spinning backwards from east to west, so that on its surface the Sun would rise in the west and set in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its spin is also extremely slow, one turn against the stars every 243 days, longer than its 225-day year, and the Sun takes about 117 Earth days to return to the same place in its sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fact sheets write its rotation period as a negative number, −5,832.6 hours, and its obliquity as 177.36°, a tilt of nearly 180° that records the backward spin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because no surface can be seen through the clouds, the rotation could be measured only by bouncing radar signals off the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The spin is not perfectly steady, and radar from 2006 to 2020 gave an average day of 243.0226 Earth days that varies by about 20 minutes as the atmosphere trades angular momentum with the solid planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Features seen by Venus Express could be lined up with those Magellan had mapped some 16 years earlier only if the day were on average 6.5 minutes longer than Magellan had measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The most likely cause of the slow backward spin is tides raised by the Sun in both the solid planet and its thick atmosphere, though one or more giant collisions during its formation have also been suggested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Models show that a planet with a dense atmosphere can end in only one of four rotation states, and Venus could reach its present one either by its axis flipping over or by starting prograde and reversing as its tilt fell towards zero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The final spin depends on a contest between tides in the rock, which dominate close to a star, and thermal tides in the air, which dominate farther out, and between the two both prograde and retrograde balances appear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why is a day on Venus shorter than one turn of the planet?","a":"Because its spin runs opposite to its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Venus takes 243 Earth days to turn once against the stars and 225 to orbit the Sun, and the two motions add together rather than cancel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As a result the Sun returns to the same place in its sky after about 117 Earth days, and it rises in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the thick atmosphere matter to the spin?","a":"Because the Sun raises tides in the air as well as in the rock, and the two pull the spin in different ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Close to a star the tide in the solid planet dominates, while farther out the thermal tide in the atmosphere drives the rotation, and between them both prograde and retrograde equilibrium states appear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The day also varies by about 20 minutes as the atmosphere exchanges angular momentum with the solid planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might Venus not have flipped over at all?","a":"Because models show more than one route to its present state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The long-favoured view was that its spin axis was turned through 180° by core–mantle friction and atmospheric tides, which requires a high starting tilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Simulations found that a planet with a dense atmosphere can instead begin with prograde rotation and develop retrograde rotation while its obliquity falls towards zero, with no flip of the axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Retrograde and prograde motion"},
    "astro-120": {"questions":["Photographed by Voyager 1 in 1990 from about 6 billion kilometres (3.7 billion miles) away, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appears in that famous image as a single point of light, the Pale Blue Dot.","When the Galileo spacecraft flew past in December 1990, it found oxygen, methane and a red-absorbing surface pigment on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a control test for the search for life elsewhere."],"sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “8.1 The Global Perspective,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/8-1-the-global-perspective. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Earth: Facts,” https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 15 November 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Planetary Fact Sheet – Metric,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 18 March 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Voyager 1’s Pale Blue Dot,” Voyager, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1s-pale-blue-dot/. [Open access]","Carl Sagan et al., “A Search for Life on Earth from the Galileo Spacecraft,” <i>Nature</i> 365, no. 6448 (1993): 715–21, https://doi.org/10.1038/365715a0. [Paywalled]","Lisa Kaltenegger, “How to Characterize Habitable Worlds and Signs of Life,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 55 (2017): 433–85, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-astro-082214-122238, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05597. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “8.4 Life, Chemical Evolution, and Climate Change,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/8-4-life-chemical-evolution-and-climate-change. [Open access]","Colin Goldblatt and Andrew J. Watson, “The Runaway Greenhouse: Implications for Future Climate Change, Geoengineering and Planetary Atmospheres,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 370, no. 1974 (2012): 4197–216, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2012.0004, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.1593. [Open access]"],"abstract":"<b>Earth</b> is the third planet from the Sun and the largest of the four rocky inner planets, with an equatorial diameter of 12,756 kilometres (7,926 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It orbits at an average of about 150 million kilometres (93 million miles), the distance that defines the astronomical unit, once every 365.25 days, and it spins once every 23.9 hours on an axis tilted by 23.4°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the densest planet, its mean density about 5.5 times that of water, because like the other inner planets it is built mainly of heavy elements such as iron, silicon and oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It formed about 4.5 billion years ago and has one natural satellite, the Moon, the fifth largest moon in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its English name is the only planetary name not taken from Greek or Roman mythology, coming instead from Old English and Germanic words that mean ‘the ground’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What sets Earth apart as a planet is liquid water at its surface, for its global ocean covers about 71 per cent of the surface and holds 97 per cent of its water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the only place known to be inhabited, and life has changed the planet itself, for oxygen released by photosynthesis began to build up in its air about 2 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> From deep space it is tiny, and on 14 February 1990 Voyager 1, about 6 billion kilometres (3.7 billion miles) from the Sun, photographed it as a single point of light, the Pale Blue Dot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the Galileo spacecraft flew past in December 1990, it found abundant oxygen, a surface pigment absorbing sharply in the red and methane far out of chemical balance, together strongly suggestive of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Earth now serves as the reference for judging rocky planets around other stars, and its habitable spell is finite, since it is expected to suffer a runaway greenhouse in around 2 billion years as the Sun brightens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why is Earth the only planet with liquid water on its surface?","a":"Because its temperature lets water stay liquid for long periods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Earth is the only planet in the Solar System that is neither too hot nor too cold for liquid water at the surface, and its orbit is nearly circular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its global ocean covers about 71 per cent of the surface, has an average depth of about 3.6 kilometres (2.3 miles) and holds 97 per cent of Earth's water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a spacecraft look for life on Earth?","a":"Because Earth is the one planet known to have life, so it could test the methods meant for other worlds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> During its December 1990 flyby, Galileo found abundant oxygen, a widespread surface pigment with a sharp absorption edge in the red, and methane far out of chemical balance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It also picked up narrow-band, pulsed radio transmissions that seem attributable only to intelligence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Earth matter to the study of other planets?","a":"Because it is the only example of a living planet that scientists can study in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its wide range of life is used as a guide to how habitability and signs of life could be detected on planets orbiting other stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Earth's own basic properties, from its density of about 5.5 times that of water to its 23.4° tilt, are the standard against which rocky worlds are compared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Earth"},
    "astro-121": {"questions":["A Soviet borehole on the Kola Peninsula reached only about 12 kilometres (7.5 miles) down, so almost everything known about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from earthquake waves and from comparing the planet's density with its rocks.","Earth is about 5.5 times as dense as water while its surface rocks are only about three times, a gap explained by the iron core at the heart of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beneath the mantle."],"abstract":"<b>Earth's interior</b> is the layered body of rock and metal beneath the planet's thin outer skin, made up of a crust, a mantle and a core that is liquid outside and solid at its centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Almost none of it can be reached directly, for a Soviet borehole on the Kola Peninsula went down only about 12 kilometres (7.5 miles), and drilling costs rise enormously with depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is known comes mostly from earthquake waves, which bend and change speed as they cross layers of different material, so that a worldwide network of seismometers can map the layers they have passed through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The crust is the thinnest layer, about 5 to 6 kilometres (3 to 4 miles) thick under the oceans and 20 to 70 kilometres (12 to 43 miles) under the continents, and it holds only about 0.3 per cent of Earth's mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Below it the mantle reaches down to about 2,900 kilometres (1,800 miles), solid rock that is nevertheless hot enough to deform and flow very slowly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Earth as a whole is about 5.5 times as dense as water, while its surface rocks are only about three times as dense, so much heavier material must lie deep inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That material is the core, which has a radius of about 3,500 kilometres (2,200 miles) and is made mostly of iron, with some nickel and lighter elements whose exact mix is still uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The outer core is thought to be liquid because earthquake shear waves, which cannot travel through a fluid, do not pass through it, while the way other waves behave shows the inner core to be solid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The solid inner core is about 2,400 kilometres (1,500 miles) across, and the liquid outer core around it is roughly 2,200 kilometres (1,400 miles) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The layering records a time when the young Earth was hot enough inside for heavy metal to sink to the centre, and the stirring of the liquid outer core today generates Earth's magnetic field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “8.1 The Global Perspective,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/8-1-the-global-perspective. [Open access]","U.S. Geological Survey, <i>The Interior of the Earth</i>, USGS General Interest Publication, https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/interior/. [Open access]","W. Jacquelyne Kious and Robert I. Tilling, “Inside the Earth,” in <i>This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics</i> (Washington, DC: U.S. Geological Survey, 1996), https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/inside.html. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 15 November 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]","British Geological Survey, “The Earth’s Magnetic Field: An Overview,” BGS Geomagnetism, https://geomag.bgs.ac.uk/education/earthmag.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Earth Facts,” NASA, https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.2 Composition and Structure of Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-2-composition-and-structure-of-planets. [Open access]","Kei Hirose, Stéphane Labrosse, and John Hernlund, “Composition and State of the Core,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 41 (2013): 657–91, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-050212-124007. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the outer core thought to be liquid?","a":"Because of how earthquake waves behave when they reach it. Shear waves, which cannot travel through a fluid, do not pass through the outer core, and compressional waves that enter it slow down sharply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The waves also bend as they cross layers of different material, leaving some seismometers in a shadow, and a network of instruments can turn that pattern into a model with liquid and solid layers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the densest material sit at the centre?","a":"Because Earth was once warm enough inside to melt, which let the heavier metals sink to the centre and form the core while lighter rock stayed above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This sorting by density is called differentiation, and the gap between Earth's bulk density and that of its surface rocks is the evidence for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The core is mostly iron, alloyed with about 10 per cent of oxygen, sulphur or nickel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the liquid outer core matter at the surface?","a":"Because that is where Earth's magnetic field is made. Electrical currents flowing in the slowly moving molten iron sustain the field by a self-exciting dynamo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When many charged particles move together in this way they produce a magnetic field, and Earth's extends far out into the surrounding space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Internal structure of Earth"},
    "astro-122": {"questions":["Magnetic stripes in the rock of the ocean floor, laid down as lava cooled at the mid-ocean ridges, gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> key evidence by recording how the sea floor spreads.","The Himalayas rose when India collided with Asia, a mountain-building event that geologists explain through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the movement of Earth's outer shell driven by slow convection in the mantle."],"abstract":"<b>Plate tectonics</b> is the theory that Earth's rigid outer shell, the crust together with the top of the mantle, is broken into a dozen or more large and small plates that move against one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plates ride on hotter, more mobile rock beneath, and slow convection in the mantle, with warm material rising and cooler material sinking, supplies the power that moves them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They travel about 4 to 5 centimetres (1.5 to 2 inches) a year, slower than fingernails grow, yet over millions of years that motion opens oceans and moves continents across the globe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> At divergent boundaries such as the Mid-Atlantic Ridge plates pull apart and new crust wells up from below, while at convergent boundaries one plate is usually forced down beneath another, a process called subduction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At transform boundaries such as the San Andreas Fault plates grind past each other sideways, and the stress that builds up there is released in sudden slips that cause earthquakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because new crust is made at the ridges about as fast as old crust is destroyed in subduction zones, the ocean floor is renewed in a little more than 100 million years, which makes the ocean basins among the youngest features on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where two continents meet neither sinks, and the crust buckles upwards instead, which is how the collision of India with Asia raised the Himalayas and the Tibetan Plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ocean floor also holds key evidence for the theory, since rock forming at the ridges takes on the direction of Earth's magnetic field as it cools, leaving stripes that record the field's reversals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The older idea of continental drift, set out in 1912 by the German meteorologist Alfred Wegener, was long rejected because no one could say what moved the continents, and plate tectonics supplied that mechanism in the 1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Plate tectonics also works as a cooling system that carries heat from the interior out to space, and when it began on the young Earth is still debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “8.2 Earth’s Crust,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/8-2-earths-crust. [Open access]","W. Jacquelyne Kious and Robert I. Tilling, “Historical Perspective,” in <i>This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics</i> (Washington, DC: U.S. Geological Survey, 1996), https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/historical.html. [Open access]","W. Jacquelyne Kious and Robert I. Tilling, “Understanding Plate Motions,” in <i>This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics</i> (Washington, DC: U.S. Geological Survey, 1996), https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/understanding.html. [Open access]","U.S. Geological Survey, <i>The Interior of the Earth</i>, USGS General Interest Publication, https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/interior/. [Open access]","Jun Korenaga, “Initiation and Evolution of Plate Tectonics on Earth: Theories and Observations,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 41 (2013): 117–51, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-050212-124208. [Paywalled]","National Centers for Environmental Information, “Geomagnetism Frequently Asked Questions,” National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/geomagnetism-frequently-asked-questions. [Open access]","British Geological Survey, “The Earth’s Magnetic Field: An Overview,” BGS Geomagnetism, https://geomag.bgs.ac.uk/education/earthmag.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Earth Facts,” NASA, https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the ocean basins so young?","a":"Because the sea floor is constantly made and destroyed. New crust wells up along about 60,000 kilometres (37,000 miles) of active rifts, and about as much sinks back into the mantle at subduction zones, so the whole oceanic crust is renewed in a little more than 100 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deep ocean trenches mark the places where old sea floor goes down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was continental drift rejected at first?","a":"Because it had evidence but no mechanism. Its supporters pointed to the fit of the coasts of South America and Africa and to matching fossils on both, but no one could explain how solid continents could drift so far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Once decades of work in geology, oceanography and geophysics supplied the mechanism, the idea became the centrepiece of the modern view of a dynamic Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why don't continents sink when plates collide?","a":"Because continental rock is too light. When two continents meet head-on neither is subducted, and the crust buckles and is pushed upwards or sideways instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thinner oceanic plates, by contrast, are readily thrust down into the upper mantle, where they eventually melt several hundred kilometres below the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Plate tectonics"},
    "astro-123": {"questions":["Oxygen released by photosynthesising microbes was soaked up by the crust for ages before it began to build up in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> roughly two and a half to two billion years ago.","Clouds and weather are confined almost entirely to the troposphere, the lowest of the five layers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, below the stratosphere where ozone absorbs the Sun's ultraviolet light."],"abstract":"<b>Earth's atmosphere</b> is the envelope of gas held to the planet by gravity, pressing on the surface at sea level with a pressure that scientists define as 1 bar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dry air is about 78 per cent nitrogen and 21 per cent oxygen, with nearly 1 per cent argon and small amounts of carbon dioxide, while water vapour varies widely and typically makes up about 1 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> For all its importance the atmosphere is slight, since its total mass is only about a millionth of the mass of Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the air lies in the troposphere, the lowest layer, about 12 kilometres (7.5 miles) deep on average, where air warmed by the ground rises and cools and almost all weather and clouds form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Above it the stratosphere, reaching about 50 kilometres (31 miles), holds the ozone layer, which absorbs much of the Sun's ultraviolet light, and above that lie the mesosphere, the thermosphere and the exosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Today's air is not the planet's first atmosphere, which contained abundant carbon dioxide and some methane but no free oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Oxygen made by photosynthesising microbes was at first removed by chemical reactions with the crust as quickly as it formed, and it began to build up in the air only around two and a half to two billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Before that change, in the Archean eon, oxygen near the ground was less than a millionth of today's level, while carbon dioxide and methane were far more plentiful and kept the planet warm under a fainter young Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The oxygen that accumulated made possible the ozone layer, which shielded the surface from ultraviolet light and allowed life to colonise the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The atmosphere still leaks slowly at its top, where light atoms such as hydrogen and helium escape into space, and without the greenhouse warming of its gases the average surface temperature would be well below freezing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “8.3 Earth’s Atmosphere,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/8-3-earths-atmosphere. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Earth’s Atmosphere: A Multi-layered Cake,” NASA, last updated 22 October 2024, https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-atmosphere/earths-atmosphere-a-multi-layered-cake/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 15 November 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “8.4 Life, Chemical Evolution, and Climate Change,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/8-4-life-chemical-evolution-and-climate-change. [Open access]","Timothy W. Lyons, Christopher T. Reinhard, and Noah J. Planavsky, “The Rise of Oxygen in Earth’s Early Ocean and Atmosphere,” <i>Nature</i> 506, no. 7488 (2014): 307–15, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13068. [Paywalled]","David C. Catling and Kevin J. Zahnle, “The Archean Atmosphere,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 9 (2020): eaax1420, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aax1420. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Earth Facts,” NASA, https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/. [Open access]","UCAR Center for Science Education, “Layers of Earth’s Atmosphere,” University Corporation for Atmospheric Research, https://scied.ucar.edu/learning-zone/atmosphere/layers-earths-atmosphere. [Open access]","Heinrich D. Holland, “The Oxygenation of the Atmosphere and Oceans,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 361, no. 1470 (2006): 903–15, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2006.1838. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the stratosphere get warmer with height?","a":"Because of ozone, a form of oxygen with three atoms per molecule, which is concentrated near the top of the stratosphere. Ozone absorbs much of the Sun's ultraviolet light, and the breakup of ozone adds heat to the layer, reversing the fall in temperature seen in the troposphere below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ultraviolet radiation is the reason that the higher you go in the stratosphere, the warmer it becomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did oxygen take so long to build up in the air?","a":"Because the crust consumed it. Plants and microbes were releasing oxygen by photosynthesis long before it accumulated, but chemical reactions with rocks removed the gas as fast as it formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first lasting rise came about two and a half billion years ago, and oxygen reached near-modern levels in the sea and air only about two billion years after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can't Earth hold on to hydrogen and helium?","a":"Because the lightest atoms move fastest. Above about 100 kilometres (62 miles) the air is so thin that individual atoms can occasionally escape Earth's gravity altogether, and light atoms such as hydrogen and helium leak away into space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Archean eon, hydrogen escaping rapidly to space may even have helped to oxidise the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Atmosphere of Earth"},
    "astro-124": {"questions":["Lava cooling at the mid-ocean ridges locks in the direction of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that record shows its north and south poles swapping places many times at irregular intervals.","Shaped by the solar wind into a long tail on the night side, the magnetosphere is the region of space where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dominates the weak magnetic field carried outward from the Sun."],"abstract":"<b>Earth's magnetic field</b> surrounds the planet and makes it behave in many ways as though a giant bar magnet lay inside it, lined up roughly with the axis of rotation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> More than 90 per cent of the field measured at the surface is generated within the liquid outer core, where electrical currents in slowly moving molten iron sustain it by a self-exciting dynamo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At the surface its strength ranges from about 22,000 to 67,000 nanotesla, and at the magnetic poles a dip needle points straight down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The field's axis is tilted by roughly 10 degrees from Earth's spin axis, so the magnetic poles lie away from the geographic ones, and the north magnetic pole has been drifting at about 55 kilometres (34 miles) a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beyond the atmosphere the field forms the magnetosphere, which reaches about ten Earth radii towards the Sun and is drawn out into a long tail on the far side by the solar wind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The field is not fixed, and rocks that record it as they form show that its polarity has reversed many times, slowly and irregularly, with the north and south magnetic poles changing places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Over the past 100 million years reversals have come anywhere from about 5,000 years to 50 million years apart, and the last one happened about 750,000 to 780,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dipole has recently been weakening by about 6 per cent a century, but it is still stronger than it has been for most of the last 50,000 years, and no one can say whether or when the next reversal will come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The three satellites of the European Space Agency's Swarm mission, launched in 2013, have tracked a weak region over the South Atlantic that has grown by an area nearly half the size of continental Europe since 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The field is very old, since tiny magnetic inclusions in zircon crystals from Western Australia appear to record a field between 3.3 and 4.2 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “8.1 The Global Perspective,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/8-1-the-global-perspective. [Open access]","National Centers for Environmental Information, “Geomagnetism Frequently Asked Questions,” National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/geomagnetism-frequently-asked-questions. [Open access]","British Geological Survey, “The Earth’s Magnetic Field: An Overview,” BGS Geomagnetism, https://geomag.bgs.ac.uk/education/earthmag.html. [Open access]","John A. Tarduno et al., “A Hadean to Paleoarchean Geodynamo Recorded by Single Zircon Crystals,” <i>Science</i> 349, no. 6247 (2015): 521–24, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaa9114. [Paywalled]","European Space Agency, “Swarm: ESA’s Magnetic Field Mission,” ESA, https://www.esa.int/Applications/Observing_the_Earth/FutureEO/Swarm. [Open access]","W. Jacquelyne Kious and Robert I. Tilling, “Inside the Earth,” in <i>This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics</i> (Washington, DC: U.S. Geological Survey, 1996), https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/inside.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Earth Facts,” NASA, https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Earth Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 15 November 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why doesn't magnetic north match true north?","a":"Because the field's axis is tilted from Earth's spin axis, by about 9 to 11 degrees, so the magnetic poles sit away from the geographic ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The magnetic poles also wander, and the north magnetic pole has been drifting north-northwest at about 55 kilometres (34 miles) a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The angle between magnetic north and true north at any place is called declination, and it has been recorded since the 16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why would a reversal not leave Earth without a field?","a":"Because a reversal is slow. A full one would take several thousand years, and Earth would probably keep a field throughout, weaker than normal and with several magnetic poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dipole is weakening at present, by about 6 per cent a century, but whether that signals a coming reversal is difficult to say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the magnetosphere trap charged particles?","a":"Because a charged particle that meets a magnetic field in space is caught by it. Above the atmosphere Earth's field traps small quantities of electrons and other atomic particles, many of them carried out from the Sun by the solar wind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The magnetosphere deflects most of the solar wind around Earth, and the field lines guide the motion of the particles inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Earth's magnetic field"},
    "astro-125": {"questions":["With only about one-sixth of Earth's surface gravity, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is too small to keep a true atmosphere, and its sunlit ground can reach about 127 °C (260 °F).","Apollo astronauts brought back about 382 kilograms (842 pounds) of rock and soil from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, samples showing that its surface rocks are older than most of those on Earth."],"abstract":"The <b>Moon</b> is Earth's only natural satellite, a rocky world about 3,476 kilometres (2,160 miles) across, roughly half the width of Earth's core, that orbits at an average distance of about 384,400 kilometres (238,900 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It has about one-eightieth of Earth's mass and one-sixth of its surface gravity, too little to hold a real atmosphere, and deep inside lies an iron-rich core, partly liquid, only a few hundred kilometres in radius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its average density, about 3.3 times that of water, shows that it is made almost entirely of silicate rock poor in metals, under a crust that averages about 34 to 43 kilometres (21 to 27 miles) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Moon takes about 27.3 days to orbit Earth and turns once on its axis in the same time, so the same hemisphere always faces the planet, an effect called tidal locking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As its sunlit half is seen from changing angles the Moon goes through phases, and the full cycle from one new moon to the next takes about 29.5 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The far side, which never faces Earth, was not seen by anyone until a Soviet spacecraft photographed it in 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Moon is the only world beyond Earth that people have visited, and the Apollo missions brought back about 382 kilograms (842 pounds) of rock and soil that are still being studied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those samples show that lunar rocks solidified between about 4.4 and 3.3 billion years ago, far older than most rocks on Earth, on a world with little internal activity and no air or water to rework its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Without air to hold heat, the surface swings from about 127 °C (260 °F) in full sunlight to about −173 °C (−280 °F) in darkness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Moon is also slowly drawing away from Earth, by about 3.8 centimetres (1.5 inches) a year, driven mainly by the tides it raises in Earth's oceans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.1 General Properties of the Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-1-general-properties-of-the-moon. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Moon Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 11 January 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/moonfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Moon Facts,” NASA, last updated 12 February 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Tidal Locking,” NASA, last updated 23 July 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/tidal-locking/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.2 The Lunar Surface,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-2-the-lunar-surface. [Open access]","U.S. Geological Survey, <i>The Interior of the Earth</i>, USGS General Interest Publication, https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/interior/. [Open access]","T. W. Murphy, “Lunar Laser Ranging: The Millimeter Challenge,” <i>Reports on Progress in Physics</i> 76, no. 7 (2013): 076901, https://doi.org/10.1088/0034-4885/76/7/076901, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.6294. [Open access]","Mark A. Wieczorek et al., “The Crust of the Moon as Seen by GRAIL,” <i>Science</i> 339, no. 6120 (2013): 671–75, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1231530. [Paywalled]","NASA Astromaterials Acquisition and Curation Office, “Lunar Rocks and Soils from Apollo Missions,” NASA Johnson Space Center, https://curator.jsc.nasa.gov/lunar/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do we always see the same side of the Moon?","a":"Because the Moon spins on its axis in the same time it takes to go once around Earth, about 27.3 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> This synchronous rotation, or tidal locking, keeps one hemisphere turned towards Earth, and it is common among moons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The far side is not permanently dark, since as the Moon orbits, every part of it passes through day and night in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Moon have almost no atmosphere?","a":"Because its gravity is too weak. Gas molecules can escape a world much as a rocket does, and the lower the gravity, the easier it is for gas to leak away into space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gas brought by impacting comets is soon lost, either by freezing onto the surface or by escaping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What remains is a very thin exosphere, which is not breathable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are lunar rocks older than most rocks on Earth?","a":"Because little has happened to the Moon's surface since they formed. Radioactive dating of the Apollo samples gave solidification ages of about 3.3 to 4.4 billion years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With little internal activity and no air or water, the Moon has not reworked its surface the way Earth does, so most of its geological history is unlike anything on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Moon"},
    "astro-126": {"questions":["Earth and the Moon share almost identical oxygen isotopes, a puzzle for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because early simulations built most of the Moon from the colliding body rather than from Earth.","Replacing the older fission, capture and sister theories, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has the Moon gather from a ring of debris that a glancing collision flung around Earth about 4.5 billion years ago."],"abstract":"The <b>giant-impact hypothesis</b> holds that the Moon formed from debris thrown into orbit when a body about the size of Mars struck the young Earth a glancing blow about 4.5 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the usual version the impactor had about a tenth of Earth's mass, close to the largest blow the planet could take without being shattered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Material amounting to several per cent of Earth's mass would have been thrown out, most of it from the rocky mantles of the two bodies rather than their metal cores, and it formed a ring around Earth that gathered into the Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The impactor is sometimes called Theia, after the mother of Selene, the Greek goddess of the Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The older ideas fared worse, since no known way exists for Earth to have captured so large a moon, and calculations show that Earth could not simply have split in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The hypothesis explains why the Moon is so poor in iron, since its raw material came mainly from mantle rock, and why it lacks volatile elements, which would have been lost in the heat after the collision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The lunar core holds only about 1.6 to 1.8 per cent of the Moon's mass, against about 30 per cent for Earth's, and the Apollo samples showed that the young Moon was covered by an ocean of magma, as an energetic impact would produce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The difficulty is that Earth and the Moon have almost identical oxygen isotopes, yet early simulations made most of the Moon from the impactor, whose composition ought to have differed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Later simulations proposed answers, such as a larger impactor or a fast-spinning young Earth, that make a Moon-forming disc mostly from Earth's own mantle, with the extra spin lost afterwards through a resonance with the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The combined spin of the Earth and Moon, nearly unchanged for 4.5 billion years, limits the size and angle of the blow, and suitable impacts near the very end of Earth's growth can match it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.4 The Origin of the Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-4-the-origin-of-the-moon. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Moon Formation,” NASA, last updated 23 July 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/formation/. [Open access]","Robin M. Canup and Erik Asphaug, “Origin of the Moon in a Giant Impact near the End of the Earth’s Formation,” <i>Nature</i> 412, no. 6848 (2001): 708–12, https://doi.org/10.1038/35089010. [Paywalled]","Robin M. Canup, “Forming a Moon with an Earth-like Composition via a Giant Impact,” <i>Science</i> 338, no. 6110 (2012): 1052–55, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1226073, author manuscript at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6476314/. [Open access]","Matija Ćuk and Sarah T. Stewart, “Making the Moon from a Fast-Spinning Earth: A Giant Impact Followed by Resonant Despinning,” <i>Science</i> 338, no. 6110 (2012): 1047–52, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1225542. [Paywalled]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.1 General Properties of the Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-1-general-properties-of-the-moon. [Open access]","Erik Asphaug, “Impact Origin of the Moon?,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 42 (2014): 551–78, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-050212-124057. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the capture theory rejected?","a":"Because no one knows how the young Earth could have captured so large a moon. A body approaching another cannot settle into orbit without losing a great deal of energy, and a captured object would follow a very eccentric orbit rather than the nearly circular one the Moon has.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The close match between the two worlds' oxygen isotopes also argues against a wholly separate origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Moon have so little iron?","a":"Because in the impact scenario its raw material came mostly from the stony mantles of Earth and the impactor, not from their metal cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lunar core is only about 1.6 to 1.8 per cent of the Moon's mass, while Earth's iron-rich core makes up around 30 per cent of the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Simulations have found impacts that yield an iron-poor Moon together with the present masses and spin of the Earth–Moon system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the hypothesis still not considered proven?","a":"Because there is no current way of showing directly that it is the right model, although it offers answers to most of the major problems raised by the Moon's chemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several versions compete, from a single Mars-sized body to a series of impacts, and scientists hope that new samples from the far side and the poles will help to narrow them down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Giant-impact hypothesis"},
    "astro-127": {"questions":["Astronauts' boots sank several centimetres into the porous <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose fine, dark grains were kicked up by every step and worked their way into all of their equipment.","Glassy particles called agglutinates, welded together by micrometeoroid impacts, grow more common the longer the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies exposed, so their share shows how mature a sample of lunar soil is."],"abstract":"The <b>lunar regolith</b> is the layer of loose, broken rock and dust that covers almost the entire Moon, burying the bedrock everywhere except perhaps on some very steep crater walls and lava channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is made by impacts, from asteroids tens of kilometres across down to specks of cosmic dust, each of which shatters, melts and scatters the rock it strikes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fresh rock exposed on the Moon begins to be broken down at once, and over billions of years the repeated blows have reduced the surface to a rubble of boulders, pebbles and fine powder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of the regolith is made of particles smaller than 1 centimetre (0.4 inches), a fine, grey, somewhat cohesive soil whose grains average about 60 to 80 micrometres across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is generally about 4 to 5 metres (13 to 16 feet) thick on the maria and 10 to 15 metres (33 to 49 feet) on the older highlands, with a zone of fractured bedrock called the megaregolith beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Because there is no oxygen, wind, water or life on the Moon, its regolith forms by processes unlike those that make soil on Earth, chiefly impacts and the steady arrival of charged particles from the Sun and the stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Tiny micrometeoroid impacts melt small patches of soil into glass that welds grains together into particles called agglutinates, and their share grows the longer a soil lies exposed, so it serves as a measure of the soil's maturity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agglutinates always contain gases such as hydrogen and helium that the solar wind has implanted in the soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Apollo astronauts found the top layer porous and loosely packed, sinking several centimetres into it, and the dark dust worked its way into all of their equipment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its grains are highly angular and can pick up an electric charge in sunlight and the solar wind, even lifting off the ground, and the fine dust is a hazard to machines and health that future expeditions must manage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["David S. McKay et al., “The Lunar Regolith,” in <i>Lunar Sourcebook: A User’s Guide to the Moon</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1991), 285–356, https://www.lpi.usra.edu/publications/books/lunar_sourcebook/pdf/Chapter07.pdf. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.2 The Lunar Surface,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-2-the-lunar-surface. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Moon Facts,” NASA, last updated 12 February 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Moon Composition,” NASA, last updated 23 July 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/composition/. [Open access]","J. E. Colwell et al., “Lunar Surface: Dust Dynamics and Regolith Mechanics,” <i>Reviews of Geophysics</i> 45, no. 2 (2007), https://doi.org/10.1029/2005RG000184. [Paywalled]","NASA Astromaterials Acquisition and Curation Office, “Lunar Rocks and Soils from Apollo Missions,” NASA Johnson Space Center, https://curator.jsc.nasa.gov/lunar/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the regolith thicker on the highlands than on the maria?","a":"Because the highlands are older and have been bombarded for longer. Impacts begin breaking up fresh rock the moment it is exposed, and the layer deepens as craters pile on top of one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regolith is generally about 4 to 5 metres (13 to 16 feet) thick on the maria but may average 10 to 15 metres (33 to 49 feet) on the highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In some young maria it is thin enough for bedrock to be exposed in rare cases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the regolith grow more slowly as it thickens?","a":"Because once it is a metre or more deep, only the larger and much rarer impacts reach through it to bring up fresh bedrock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The smaller and more numerous impacts merely stir and mix the layer already there, a process called gardening, so the regolith rarely becomes more than 10 to 20 metres (33 to 66 feet) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is lunar dust a problem for explorers?","a":"Because its particles are sharp and easily charged. The fine part of the regolith poses operational and health hazards, and grains charged by sunlight and the solar wind can levitate and drift across the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the Apollo missions the dark basaltic dust of the maria was kicked up by every footstep and eventually worked its way into all of the astronauts' equipment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lunar regolith"},
    "astro-128": {"questions":["Early observers took the dark patches of the 'man in the Moon' for seas, a belief preserved in the name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which are in fact frozen floods of volcanic basalt.","Carrying about a tenth as many craters per area as the bright anorthosite highlands, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are younger surfaces laid down by lava after the heaviest bombardment had passed."],"abstract":"The <b>lunar maria</b> are the dark plains of the Moon, sheets of basalt lava that flooded its great impact basins and that early observers took for seas, the meaning of the Latin word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Together they cover only about 17 per cent of the surface, mostly on the side that faces Earth, while the bright, heavily cratered highlands cover the remaining 83 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The highlands are made largely of anorthosite, a pale, low-density rock that solidified early and floated to the top of the cooling Moon between about 4.4 and 4.1 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dark maria are richer in iron and titanium, while the highlands are rich in calcium and aluminium, so the two differ in composition as well as in age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the highlands are older and took a much heavier rain of impacts before about 3.8 billion years ago, they carry about ten times as many craters as an equal area of mare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The mare lavas rose from hundreds of kilometres down and, being very fluid, spread in thin flows over the low ground of the basins, much like flood basalts on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The major mare volcanism ended about 3.3 billion years ago, but its full span is uncertain, with crater counts in nearside basins suggesting eruptions from about 3.9 to 2.0 billion years ago and NASA giving 4.2 to 1.2 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mare deposits are generally less than about 2 kilometres (1.2 miles) thick, though they may reach 6 to 8 kilometres (4 to 5 miles) at the centres of some basins, and they carry a regolith only about 4 to 5 metres (13 to 16 feet) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The far side has few maria even in its deepest basins, probably because its crust is much thicker, about 150 kilometres (93 miles) against 70 kilometres (43 miles) on the near side, so lava rarely reached the surface there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rock collected from the maria by the Apollo crews has the gas-bubble holes typical of lava and is very similar to the basalt of Earth's ocean floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.2 The Lunar Surface,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-2-the-lunar-surface. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.3 Impact Craters,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-3-impact-craters. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Moon Composition,” NASA, last updated 23 July 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/composition/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Moon Formation,” NASA, last updated 23 July 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/formation/. [Open access]","James W. Head, “Lunar Volcanism in Space and Time,” <i>Reviews of Geophysics</i> 14, no. 2 (1976): 265–300, https://doi.org/10.1029/RG014i002p00265. [Paywalled]","Harald Hiesinger et al., “Ages of Mare Basalts on the Lunar Nearside,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 105, no. E12 (2000): 29239–75, https://doi.org/10.1029/2000JE001244. [Paywalled]","David S. McKay et al., “The Lunar Regolith,” in <i>Lunar Sourcebook: A User’s Guide to the Moon</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1991), 285–356, https://www.lpi.usra.edu/publications/books/lunar_sourcebook/pdf/Chapter07.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the maria mostly on the near side?","a":"Probably because the crust is thinner there. The lunar crust is about 70 kilometres (43 miles) thick on the near side but about 150 kilometres (93 miles) on the far side, and the near side has had a much richer history of volcanism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lava rising from below filled the low ground of the basins, and a thicker crust may explain why even the deep far-side basins hold only patchy maria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the highlands have so many more craters than the maria?","a":"Because they caught the heavy early bombardment. Highland samples are typically about 4.2 billion years old and mare samples about 3.8, yet the highlands have about ten times as many craters on a similar area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> If the impact rate had been constant, the highlands would have had to be impossibly old, so the rate must have been much higher before 3.8 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the maria dark?","a":"Because they are made mostly of dark-coloured basalt, volcanic lava laid down in eruptions billions of years ago that partly filled huge basins made by early impacts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Compared with the bright highlands, which are rich in calcium and aluminium, the mare rocks hold more iron and titanium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lunar mare"},
    "astro-129": {"questions":["Shatter cones and quartz deformed by shock waves allow geologists to recognise an eroded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on Earth even where every fragment of the meteorite that made it has vanished.","A small <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keeps a simple bowl shape, but above about 15 kilometres (9 miles) across on the Moon its walls collapse into terraces around a flat floor and central peak."],"abstract":"An <b>impact crater</b> is a roughly circular depression blasted into the surface of a planet, moon or asteroid when a smaller body strikes it at high speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Impacts typically happen at tens of kilometres a second, faster than sound travels through rock, so the projectile's energy becomes a shock wave and heat that vaporise most of it and some of the target.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The result is an explosion that digs out material evenly, which is why craters are almost always circular, and the hole is usually 10 to 15 times wider than the object that made it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Small craters keep a simple bowl shape, while larger ones collapse within minutes into complex craters with flat floors, terraced walls and central peaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The change from simple to complex comes at about 15 kilometres (9 miles) across on the Moon but at only about 2 to 4 kilometres (1.2 to 2.5 miles) on Earth, because stronger gravity makes the walls give way sooner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Around the rim lies a blanket of ejected debris, and faster ejecta dig secondary craters and leave the bright rays seen around young craters such as Tycho.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the Moon, which has had little erosion or internal activity, the number of craters on a surface can be used to estimate its age, and crater counts on the maria agree with radioactive dates of 3.3 to 3.8 billion years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Earth has been struck just as often, but plate tectonics and erosion have erased most of its craters, so few survive, among them the Chicxulub structure in Mexico, whose impact about 65.5 million years ago triggered a mass extinction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Meteor Crater in Arizona, about 1.2 kilometres (0.75 miles) across, was made about 50,000 years ago by a lump of iron about 40 metres (130 feet) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the roughly 150 impact structures known on Earth by 1998 were identified not from meteorite fragments but from shock effects such as shatter cones and deformed quartz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.3 Impact Craters,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-3-impact-craters. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “8.5 Cosmic Influences on the Evolution of Earth,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/8-5-cosmic-influences-on-the-evolution-of-earth. [Open access]","Bevan M. French, <i>Traces of Catastrophe: A Handbook of Shock-Metamorphic Effects in Terrestrial Meteorite Impact Structures</i>, LPI Contribution 954 (Houston: Lunar and Planetary Institute, 1998), https://www.lpi.usra.edu/publications/books/CB-954/CB-954.pdf. [Open access]","H. J. Melosh and B. A. Ivanov, “Impact Crater Collapse,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 27 (1999): 385–415, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.earth.27.1.385. [Paywalled]","NASA Science, “Moon Facts,” NASA, last updated 12 February 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/. [Open access]","Peter Schulte et al., “The Chicxulub Asteroid Impact and Mass Extinction at the Cretaceous-Paleogene Boundary,” <i>Science</i> 327, no. 5970 (2010): 1214–18, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1177265. [Paywalled]","Harald Hiesinger et al., “Ages of Mare Basalts on the Lunar Nearside,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 105, no. E12 (2000): 29239–75, https://doi.org/10.1029/2000JE001244. [Paywalled]","Impact Earth, “Impact Cratering Process,” Department of Earth Sciences, University of Western Ontario, https://impact.uwo.ca/impact-craters/products-of-impact-crater/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are impact craters round?","a":"Because the impactor explodes rather than ploughing a furrow. It hits at no less than the escape velocity of the body it strikes, plus its own speed of typically 10 kilometres (6 miles) a second or more, and the energy excavates material symmetrically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the instant of impact, intense shock waves radiate outward through the target rock at a few kilometres a second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Earth have so few craters compared with the Moon?","a":"Because Earth's active geology destroys them. As plate tectonics constantly renews the crust, the evidence of past impacts is slowly erased, and older craters may be so eroded that only a trained eye can see them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the Moon, by contrast, most of the impact history is preserved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do large craters have central peaks?","a":"Because the bowl first dug out, the transient crater, is unstable and collapses. In large craters the walls slump and the floor rises, giving central peaks, terraces and, in still larger ones, internal rings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Field and theoretical studies suggest that such central uplifts form in only a few minutes, even in the largest structures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Impact crater"},
    "astro-130": {"questions":["With a day of about 24.6 hours and an axis tilted by about 25 degrees, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has seasons much like Earth's, though each one lasts roughly twice as long.","Seismic waves recorded by the InSight lander revealed the large liquid core of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a planet with no global magnetic field today but with strongly magnetised patches of ancient crust."],"abstract":"<b>Mars</b> is the fourth planet from the Sun, a cold, dusty desert world about 6,790 kilometres (4,220 miles) across that orbits at an average distance of 1.52 astronomical units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has about half Earth's diameter but only about 11 per cent of its mass, and its surface gravity is about 38 per cent of Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A day on Mars lasts about 24.6 hours and its year 687 Earth days, and because its axis is tilted by about 25 degrees it has seasons like Earth's, each lasting about twice as long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its red colour comes from iron minerals in its rocks, dust and soil that have oxidised, or rusted, and the Romans named it after their god of war because the colour reminded them of blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its atmosphere is mostly carbon dioxide, with a surface pressure less than 1 per cent of Earth's, and it has two small, irregular moons, Phobos and Deimos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Like the other rocky planets, Mars has a metal core of iron, nickel and sulphur, a rocky mantle and a crust whose average thickness lies between about 24 and 72 kilometres (15 and 45 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Seismic waves reflected from the core and recorded by NASA's InSight lander, one of more than 50 spacecraft sent towards Mars since the 1960s, gave it a radius of about 1,830 kilometres (1,140 miles) and a density that requires a large share of lighter elements mixed with the metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More recent results suggest the core may be smaller, about 1,650 to 1,675 kilometres (1,025 to 1,040 miles) in radius, so its size is still being refined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Mars has no global magnetic field today, but parts of its ancient southern crust are strongly magnetised, which shows that it once had one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Large impact basins such as Hellas and Argyre lack that magnetism, which implies that the planet's internal dynamo had stopped by about 4 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.1 The Nearest Planets: An Overview,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-1-the-nearest-planets-an-overview. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Mars Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 19 May 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/marsfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Mars Facts,” NASA, last updated 10 July 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/. [Open access]","Simon C. Stähler et al., “Seismic Detection of the Martian Core,” <i>Science</i> 373, no. 6553 (2021): 443–48, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abi7730. [Paywalled]","M. H. Acuña et al., “Global Distribution of Crustal Magnetization Discovered by the Mars Global Surveyor MAG/ER Experiment,” <i>Science</i> 284, no. 5415 (1999): 790–93, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.284.5415.790. [Paywalled]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.4 The Geology of Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-4-the-geology-of-mars. [Open access]","Henri Samuel et al., “Geophysical Evidence for an Enriched Molten Silicate Layer above Mars’s Core,” <i>Nature</i> 622, no. 7984 (2023): 712–17, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06601-8. [Open access]","A. Khan et al., “Evidence for a Liquid Silicate Layer atop the Martian Core,” <i>Nature</i> 622, no. 7984 (2023): 718–23, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06586-4. [Open access]","Brigitte Knapmeyer-Endrun et al., “Thickness and Structure of the Martian Crust from InSight Seismic Data,” <i>Science</i> 373, no. 6553 (2021): 438–43, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abf8966. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Mars have seasons like Earth's?","a":"Because its axis is tilted by about 25 degrees, close to Earth's 23.4 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each season lasts about six of our months, since the Martian year is almost two Earth years long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seasons also differ from one another in length, from about 142 to 194 Martian days, because the planet's orbit is noticeably elliptical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do scientists think Mars once had a magnetic field?","a":"Because parts of its crust are strongly magnetised, which only a field could have done. The magnetised rocks lie mostly in the ancient cratered terrain of the southern highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their absence near the great basins Hellas and Argyre suggests that the internal dynamo had stopped by about 4 billion years ago, before those basins formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the size of the Martian core matter?","a":"Because it tells what the planet is made of. A liquid core about 1,830 kilometres (1,140 miles) in radius implies a mantle like Earth's upper mantle but without the deep layer that dominates Earth's lower mantle, and a core holding a substantial share of light elements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later estimates of a smaller core show that these figures are still being refined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mars"},
    "astro-131": {"questions":["Rusted iron in the rocks, regolith and fine dust gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its red colour, while soil dug at the Phoenix site in the far north proved salty and rich in perchlorate.","Landers on the northern plains photographed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a flat, windswept expanse littered with angular volcanic rocks, from tiny pebbles up to boulders a metre across."],"abstract":"The <b>Martian surface</b> is a cold, dry landscape of volcanic rock, impact craters and wind-blown dust, with an area nearly equal to all the dry land on Earth although Mars is only about half Earth's diameter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its red colour comes from iron in the rocks, in the loose soil called regolith and in the dust, which has oxidised, or rusted, and the fine dust coats almost everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The first landers touched down on flat northern plains strewn with angular rocks, where they found soils of clays and iron oxides and rocks that all appeared to be volcanic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The soil is chemically unusual as well, for at the Phoenix site in the far north it held dissolved salts including perchlorate, at 0.4 to 0.6 per cent by mass, and was moderately alkaline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> With no oceans to moderate them temperatures swing widely, and at the Viking 1 site the summer maximum was about 240 K, falling to about 190 K just before dawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The largest pattern on the surface is a split between the hemispheres, with heavily cratered highlands mostly in the south and younger, lightly cratered volcanic plains mostly in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern plains lie on average about 5 kilometres (3 miles) lower than the highlands, and the whole range of height from the summit of Olympus Mons to the floor of the Hellas basin is about 30 kilometres (19 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The boundary between the two provinces marks a change in the thickness of the crust, and craters buried beneath the young northern surface suggest that the division is among the planet's oldest features.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its cause is still debated between a giant impact and movements in the mantle, and one study of gravity and topography found that the boundary fits an ellipse about 10,600 by 8,500 kilometres (6,600 by 5,300 miles), most simply explained by an impact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Across the boundary sits the Tharsis rise, a volcanic region whose enormous load of lava bent the outer shell of the planet and shaped much of its global form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.4 The Geology of Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-4-the-geology-of-mars. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Mars: Facts,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/. [Open access]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Mars Fact Sheet,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 19 May 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/marsfact.html. [Open access]","Jeffrey C. Andrews-Hanna, Maria T. Zuber, and W. Bruce Banerdt, “The Borealis Basin and the Origin of the Martian Crustal Dichotomy,” <i>Nature</i> 453, no. 7199 (2008): 1212–15, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature07011. [Paywalled]","Roger J. Phillips et al., “Ancient Geodynamics and Global-Scale Hydrology on Mars,” <i>Science</i> 291, no. 5513 (2001): 2587–91, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1058701. [Paywalled]","M. H. Hecht et al., “Detection of Perchlorate and the Soluble Chemistry of Martian Soil at the Phoenix Lander Site,” <i>Science</i> 325, no. 5936 (2009): 64–67, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1172466. [Paywalled]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.5 Water and Life on Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-5-water-and-life-on-mars. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Mars look red?","a":"Because iron in its rocks, regolith and dust has oxidised, which is to say rusted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Close up the ground shows browns, golds and tans, but winds lift the fine reddish dust into the atmosphere and the dust coats almost all of the surface, so from a distance the planet looks mostly red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The soils analysed by the first landers contained iron oxides, as the colour had long suggested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the northern plains less cratered than the southern highlands?","a":"Because they are younger surfaces. The north is mostly lightly cratered volcanic plains, while the south is heavily cratered and older highland terrain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plains have about the same density of craters as the lunar maria and probably formed between 3 and 4 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Older craters lie buried beneath the young lowland surface, so the division between the two halves is itself far older still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What could have split Mars into two such different halves?","a":"The two main suggestions are a giant impact and a pattern of movement in the mantle, and for a long time little evidence favoured either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tracing the boundary beneath the Tharsis volcanoes with gravity and topography, one study found that the whole boundary follows an ellipse about 10,600 by 8,500 kilometres (6,600 by 5,300 miles), which it argued is most simply explained by an enormous impact, though the question remains open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Martian surface"},
    "astro-132": {"questions":["Vapour rich in heavy hydrogen suggests that the ancient supply of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could once have covered the whole planet to a depth of at least 137 metres (449 feet).","Radar echoes beneath the south polar ice and dark seasonal streaks on crater walls keep open the question of whether liquid <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still exists in small amounts today."],"abstract":"<b>Water on Mars</b> exists today almost entirely as ice and as traces of vapour, because the air is too thin and cold for liquid water to last long on the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below a pressure of about 0.006 bar water changes straight from ice to vapour, and the average surface pressure on Mars is only about 0.007 bar, so pure liquid water is unstable except at the lowest elevations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The largest known stores are the polar caps, and the northern permanent cap is water ice about 3 kilometres (2 miles) thick, with a volume of roughly 10 million cubic kilometres (2.4 million cubic miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ice also lies just below the ground, for an orbiter detected large amounts of buried hydrogen and the Phoenix lander found an ice table only about 5 centimetres (2 inches) below the soil near the north polar cap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The landscape records a wetter past, with ancient river valleys, deltas, lake beds and minerals that form only in liquid water, and some features suggest huge floods about 3.5 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> How much water early Mars held can be estimated from the ratio of deuterium, a heavy form of hydrogen, to ordinary hydrogen, which rises as water is lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Maps of the water vapour in the atmosphere show this ratio to be about seven times that of Earth's oceans, and imply that the polar ice is enriched at least eight times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On that evidence early Mars, about 4.5 billion years ago, had enough water to cover the whole planet to a depth of at least 137 metres (449 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether liquid water exists today is more contested, and radar on the Mars Express orbiter found a bright reflection 20 kilometres (12 miles) wide beneath the south polar ice that its team interpreted as a body of liquid water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dark seasonal streaks on slopes, called recurring slope lineae, carry hydrated salts that suggested flowing brine, but their slopes match those of dry sand flows, so the liquid involved may be small or none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.5 Water and Life on Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-5-water-and-life-on-mars. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Mars: Facts,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/. [Open access]","G. L. Villanueva et al., “Strong Water Isotopic Anomalies in the Martian Atmosphere: Probing Current and Ancient Reservoirs,” <i>Science</i> 348, no. 6231 (2015): 218–21, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaa3630. [Paywalled]","R. Orosei et al., “Radar Evidence of Subglacial Liquid Water on Mars,” <i>Science</i> 361, no. 6401 (2018): 490–93, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aar7268, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.04587. [Open access]","Lujendra Ojha et al., “Spectral Evidence for Hydrated Salts in Recurring Slope Lineae on Mars,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 8, no. 11 (2015): 829–32, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo2546. [Paywalled]","Colin M. Dundas et al., “Granular Flows at Recurring Slope Lineae on Mars Indicate a Limited Role for Liquid Water,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 10, no. 12 (2017): 903–7, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-017-0012-5. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.4 The Geology of Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-4-the-geology-of-mars. [Open access]","M. H. Hecht et al., “Detection of Perchlorate and the Soluble Chemistry of Martian Soil at the Phoenix Lander Site,” <i>Science</i> 325, no. 5936 (2009): 64–67, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1172466. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can't liquid water last on the Martian surface?","a":"Because the pressure is too low as well as the temperature. The average surface pressure is about 0.007 bar, and below about 0.006 bar water passes straight from solid to vapour without becoming liquid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dissolved salts lower the freezing point, so salty water can sometimes stay liquid for a while under the right conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> NASA describes the atmosphere as too thin for liquid water to exist for long on the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can heavy hydrogen show how much water Mars has lost?","a":"Because water left behind after great losses is enriched in deuterium. Maps of water vapour across Mars show a deuterium-to-hydrogen ratio about seven times that of Earth's oceans, and polar ice enriched at least eight times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Working back from that enrichment gives an early global layer of water at least 137 metres (449 feet) deep, about 4.5 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The valleys, deltas and lake beds of the landscape tell the same story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the slope streaks no longer taken as proof of flowing water?","a":"Because their shape fits dry sand better. The streaks grow downhill in warm seasons, and spectra showed hydrated salts such as magnesium perchlorate where they were active, which was taken as strong support for flowing water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A later study found that they stop on slopes at the same angle as flows of loose sand on active dunes, and suggested that the liquid involved may be small or zero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Water on Mars"},
    "astro-133": {"questions":["Curiosity's seasonal methane readings in Gale crater and an orbiter's failure to find any methane at all are still being reconciled in studies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its chemistry.","High hazes of frozen carbon dioxide, a kind of cloud unknown on Earth, form in the cold upper reaches of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alongside clouds of dust and of water ice."],"abstract":"The <b>atmosphere of Mars</b> is a thin envelope of carbon dioxide whose average surface pressure is about 6 millibars, less than one per cent of the pressure at sea level on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By volume it is about 95 per cent carbon dioxide, 2.6 per cent nitrogen and 1.9 per cent argon, with small amounts of oxygen, carbon monoxide and water vapour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The air is so thin that heat escapes easily, and the average temperature is about 214 K, while at the Viking 1 site the temperature swung each day from about 184 K to 242 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Winds can be fast but push far less hard than winds of the same speed on Earth, though they lift the fine dust that makes the sky look hazy and red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its clouds are of three kinds, made of dust, of water ice like clouds on Earth, and of frozen carbon dioxide in high hazes that have no counterpart on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The atmosphere changes with the seasons, because in winter carbon dioxide freezes out of the air onto the polar caps, and the surface pressure varies from about 4 to 8.7 millibars through the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trace gases have drawn particular attention, above all methane, whose presence has been read as a sign that geological or biological activity may continue on Mars today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over five years the Curiosity rover measured a background level of about 0.41 parts per billion in Gale crater that rose and fell with the seasons, with occasional spikes near 7 parts per billion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An orbiter looking down from above found no methane at all, setting an upper limit of about 0.05 parts per billion, 10 to 100 times lower than earlier reports, and the two results have yet to be reconciled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Today's thin air is probably a remnant, since loss of gas to space over the planet's history may have removed as much as 0.8 bar of carbon dioxide from an atmosphere once much thicker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Mars Fact Sheet,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 19 May 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/marsfact.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.5 Water and Life on Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-5-water-and-life-on-mars. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Mars: Facts,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/. [Open access]","Christopher R. Webster et al., “Background Levels of Methane in Mars’ Atmosphere Show Strong Seasonal Variations,” <i>Science</i> 360, no. 6393 (2018): 1093–96, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaq0131. [Paywalled]","Oleg Korablev et al., “No Detection of Methane on Mars from Early ExoMars Trace Gas Orbiter Observations,” <i>Nature</i> 568, no. 7753 (2019): 517–20, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1096-4. [Paywalled]","B. M. Jakosky et al., “Loss of the Martian Atmosphere to Space: Present-Day Loss Rates Determined from MAVEN Observations and Integrated Loss through Time,” <i>Icarus</i> 315 (2018): 146–57, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2018.05.030. [Paywalled]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.5 Planetary Evolution,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-5-planetary-evolution. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the air pressure on Mars change through the year?","a":"Because part of the atmosphere freezes. In winter, when the surface drops below about 150 K, carbon dioxide condenses straight out of the air as frost, building seasonal polar caps of dry ice that reach down to about 50 degrees of latitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As a result the surface pressure at the planet's mean radius varies from about 4 to 8.7 millibars, depending on the season.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does methane on Mars matter so much?","a":"Because its presence has been read as a sign that geological or biological activity could continue on Mars today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Curiosity rover found a small background level that changes with the seasons more than expected from known non-living sources such as the breakdown of delivered organic material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An orbiter, however, found none, and reconciling the two seems to need some unknown process that removes methane quickly near the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Martian atmosphere so thin?","a":"Mostly because much of it has gone to space. Mars is small, and its low gravity let gases escape more easily than from Earth or Venus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Measurements by the MAVEN spacecraft suggest that loss to space over the planet's history could have removed as much as 0.8 bar of carbon dioxide, and that this loss was the main cause of the change from an early, warmer climate to today's cold, dry one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Atmosphere of Mars"},
    "astro-134": {"questions":["A cliff more than 7,000 metres (23,000 feet) high rings the foot of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, beyond which giant landslide ridges and blocks spread some 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) from the summit.","Very few craters scar the young lava of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the dating of its summit caldera suggests eruptions only a few million years ago on a volcano that may still be active."],"abstract":"<b>Olympus Mons</b> is a shield volcano in the Tharsis region of Mars and the largest volcano known in the Solar System, tall enough that its summit showed above the global dust storm that hid the planet from Mariner 9 in 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It is about 500 kilometres (310 miles) across at its base and rises more than 20 kilometres (12 miles) above the surrounding plains, nearly three times the height of the tallest mountain on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its volume is nearly 100 times that of Mauna Loa in Hawaii, and its summit holds a complex caldera, a collapsed crater, about 3 kilometres (2 miles) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its caldera formed as the ground sank into emptied magma chambers, and later collapses partly overprint the older ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around its foot runs an escarpment more than 7,000 metres (23,000 feet) high, and beyond it lie giant ridges and blocks reaching some 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) from the summit, most often explained as landslides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The volcano stands in the Tharsis region, where an uplifted volcanic area the size of North America rises about 10 kilometres (6 miles) and is crowned by four great volcanoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The roughly 300 million cubic kilometres (72 million cubic miles) of magma erupted at Tharsis were heavy enough to bend the outer shell of the planet and shape much of its global form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Olympus Mons grew so tall partly because Mars has no moving plates, so its crust stayed over the same hot source for hundreds of millions of years instead of carrying each new volcano away as happens at Hawaii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gravity matters too, because a mountain must be strong enough to bear its own weight, and with about one third of Earth's surface gravity Mars can support far greater heights than the limit of about 10 kilometres (6 miles) on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its surface is young, with very few craters, and images of its caldera and western foot show repeated activity, some only a few million years old, so the volcano may still be intermittently active.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.4 The Geology of Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-4-the-geology-of-mars. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.5 Planetary Evolution,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-5-planetary-evolution. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Mars: Facts,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Olympus Mons – the Caldera in Close-Up,” Mars Express, 11 February 2004, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Mars_Express/Olympus_Mons_-_the_caldera_in_close-up. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Escarpment and Landslides of Olympus Mons,” Mars Express, 5 August 2004, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Mars_Express/Escarpment_and_landslides_of_Olympus_Mons. [Open access]","G. Neukum et al., “Recent and Episodic Volcanic and Glacial Activity on Mars Revealed by the High Resolution Stereo Camera,” <i>Nature</i> 432, no. 7020 (2004): 971–79, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature03231. [Paywalled]","Roger J. Phillips et al., “Ancient Geodynamics and Global-Scale Hydrology on Mars,” <i>Science</i> 291, no. 5513 (2001): 2587–91, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1058701. [Paywalled]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Mariner 9,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, accessed November 2026, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1971-051A. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the volcanoes of Mars so much taller than Earth's?","a":"Because two limits that hold on Earth are weaker there. On Earth moving plates carry a volcano away from its hot spot, building a row of islands like Hawaii, while on Mars the crust stays put and one volcano can keep growing for hundreds of millions of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mars also has about one third of Earth's surface gravity, so its rock can support mountains far higher than the limit of about 10 kilometres (6 miles) on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Olympus Mons thought possibly still active?","a":"Because its surface is so young. It has very few impact craters, so its present surface cannot be more than about 100 million years old and may be much younger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> High-resolution images show that the calderas of the major Martian volcanoes were repeatedly reactivated and resurfaced, with phases as young as about two million years, and that glacial deposits at the foot of Olympus Mons were active about four million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What is the great cliff around Olympus Mons?","a":"It is an escarpment that rises from the surrounding surface to more than 7,000 metres (23,000 feet) on the volcano's western flank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Beyond it lie the aureole deposits, regions of huge ridges and blocks extending some 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) from the summit; their origin has puzzled scientists for decades, and the most persistent explanation is giant landslides, possibly helped by glacial activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Olympus Mons"},
    "astro-135": {"questions":["No river cut <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose canyons have no outlets, because they began as cracks in the crust opened by the same stretching that raised the Tharsis bulge.","Springs undercutting its cliffs set off landslides that widened the cracks of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into valleys whose walls tower up to 10 kilometres (6 miles) above the floor."],"abstract":"<b>Valles Marineris</b> is a vast system of connected canyons on Mars and the largest canyon system known in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It stretches about 4,000 kilometres (2,500 miles), nearly a quarter of the way around the planet, and is commonly given as about 200 kilometres (120 miles) wide and up to 7 kilometres (4 miles) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> NASA gives its greatest width as about 600 kilometres (370 miles) and its greatest depth, from rim to floor, as about 9.3 kilometres (5.8 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On Earth it would reach from Los Angeles to Washington, and it is roughly ten times longer, 20 times wider and five times deeper than the Grand Canyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its name means ‘Mariner Valleys’, after Mariner 9, the first spacecraft to orbit another planet, which arrived in 1971 and returned the first detailed views of the canyons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Unlike the Grand Canyon it was not cut by a river, and its canyons have no outlets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most explanations tie it to the neighbouring Tharsis bulge, which swelled with magma in the planet's first billion years, bending the planet's outer shell and stretching the surrounding crust until it tore and collapsed into troughs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mechanism is not settled, and other suggestions are that water or melting ice removed rock from below and let the surface collapse, or that several such processes worked together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Water also played a later part, seeping from deep springs and undercutting the cliffs, so that landslides widened the original cracks into great valleys whose walls rise up to 10 kilometres (6 miles) above piles of debris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Minerals altered by water hundreds of millions of years ago occur in the region, and today wind is probably the main force wearing the canyons down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.4 The Geology of Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-4-the-geology-of-mars. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Mars: Facts,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Mars Express Peers into Mars’ ‘Grand Canyon’,” <i>Science in School</i> 60 (2 November 2022), https://scienceinschool.org/article/2022/peer-into-mars-grand-canyon/. [Open access]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Mariner 9,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, accessed November 2026, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1971-051A. [Open access]","Roger J. Phillips et al., “Ancient Geodynamics and Global-Scale Hydrology on Mars,” <i>Science</i> 291, no. 5513 (2001): 2587–91, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1058701. [Paywalled]","European Space Agency, “The Solar System’s Grandest Canyon,” 22 October 2012, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/The_Solar_System_s_grandest_canyon. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Coprates Chasma and Coprates Catena,” Mars Express, 10 June 2005, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Mars_Express/Coprates_Chasma_and_Coprates_Catena. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Valles Marineris not a canyon in the usual sense?","a":"Because it was not carved by running water. A canyon on Earth such as the Grand Canyon is cut by a river, but the Martian canyons have no outlets and are basically tectonic cracks, made by the same tensions in the crust that raised the Tharsis bulge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stretching associated with the rise of Tharsis and its volcanoes opened a crack in the crust, which erosion later widened, though other mechanisms involving water or melting ice below the surface have also been proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did water shape the canyons if it did not cut them?","a":"By seeping out of deep springs and undercutting the cliffs, which led to landslides that gradually widened the original cracks into the valleys seen today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Enormous landslide debris lies piled beneath walls that tower up to 10 kilometres (6 miles) above the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water-bearing sulphate minerals in Tithonium Chasma may mark liquid that once filled part of the system, though that idea is still hotly debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the canyons get their name?","a":"From the spacecraft that first showed them in detail. Mariner 9 reached Mars in November 1971 during a global dust storm, and once the dust cleared it mapped the planet and returned the first detailed views of its volcanoes and of Valles Marineris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name means the Mariner Valleys, after that mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Valles Marineris"},
    "astro-136": {"questions":["Perhaps captured asteroids or perhaps gathered from the debris of a giant impact, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were first seen in August 1877 and their origin is still unexplained.","The inner of the two <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is creeping towards the planet by about 1.8 metres (6 feet) a century and may break up into a ring within about 50 million years."],"abstract":"The <b>moons of Mars</b> are two small, dark, irregular bodies, Phobos and Deimos, both discovered in August 1877.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phobos, the larger and the inner of the two, measures about 27 by 22 by 18 kilometres (17 by 14 by 11 miles), while Deimos measures about 15 by 12 by 11 kilometres (9 by 7 by 6.8 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They have too little mass for gravity to make them round, and their names, meaning ‘fear’ and ‘dread’, are those of the sons of Ares, the Greek counterpart of the Roman god Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both have dark surfaces like carbon-rich asteroids, and Phobos is scarred by Stickney, a crater about 9 kilometres (6 miles) across, while Deimos looks smoother because loose debris partly fills its craters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Phobos circles Mars three times a day at a mean distance of 9,378 kilometres (5,827 miles) from the planet's centre, and Deimos takes about 30 hours at 23,459 kilometres (14,577 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Where the two moons came from is unresolved, and NASA describes them as possibly captured asteroids or debris from early in the formation of the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another explanation holds that they gathered from the outer part of a disc of debris thrown up by a giant impact on Mars, and one set of simulations found that this could explain why Mars has two small moons instead of one large one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Phobos is spiralling slowly inwards, closing on Mars by about 1.8 metres (6 feet) every hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At that rate it will either crash into Mars or break apart into a ring in about 50 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> One analysis of its strength suggests that much of Phobos is weak, heavily damaged material that tides will pull apart in 20 to 40 million years, forming a ring that could last for a million to a hundred million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Mars Moons,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/moons/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Phobos,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/moons/phobos/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Deimos,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/moons/deimos/. [Open access]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Mars Fact Sheet,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 19 May 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/marsfact.html. [Open access]","Benjamin A. Black and Tushar Mittal, “The Demise of Phobos and Development of a Martian Ring System,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 8, no. 12 (2015): 913–17, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo2583. [Paywalled]","Pascal Rosenblatt et al., “Accretion of Phobos and Deimos in an Extended Debris Disc Stirred by Transient Moons,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 9, no. 8 (2016): 581–83, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo2742. [Paywalled]","NASA Science, “Mars: Facts,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are Phobos and Deimos not round?","a":"Because they have too little mass. A body needs enough gravity to pull itself into a sphere, and the two moons, the larger only about 27 kilometres (17 miles) long, are too small, so they remain potato-shaped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They have also been battered by impacts, and Phobos was nearly shattered by the one that made Stickney, its largest crater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Where did the moons of Mars come from?","a":"No one is sure. NASA describes them as possibly captured asteroids or debris from early in the formation of the Solar System, and their dark surfaces resemble those of carbon-rich asteroids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Capture and a giant impact have both struggled to explain the system as it is, and simulations suggest the two may have gathered at the outer edge of a disc of impact debris, which would make them a mixture of Mars and the impactor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What will happen to Phobos?","a":"Its orbit is slowly shrinking, by about 1.8 metres (6 feet) every hundred years, and in roughly 50 million years it will crash into Mars or break apart into a ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One study of its strength suggests that much of it is weak, damaged rock that tides will scatter in 20 to 40 million years, while any fragment strong enough to survive would strike Mars at a low angle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Moons of Mars"},
    "astro-137": {"questions":["When Mariner 9 arrived in 1971, one of the largest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ever observed left only the summits of Olympus Mons and the three Tharsis volcanoes visible from orbit.","The weak push of wind in thin air makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> little threat to topple machines, but the darkness they bring ended the solar-powered Opportunity rover's mission in 2018."],"abstract":"<b>Dust storms on Mars</b> are winds that lift the planet's fine surface dust into the air, ranging from small whirlwinds to storms that wrap the whole planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every Martian year some moderately large storms cover areas the size of continents and last for weeks, and on average once every three Martian years, about five and a half Earth years, ordinary storms grow into a global dust storm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are driven by sunlight warming the ground and the air just above it, so that warm air rises and carries dust with it, and the larger storms arise mostly in southern summer, when Mars is closest to the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wind in the strongest storms reaches about 100 kilometres per hour (60 miles per hour), but the air is only about one per cent as dense as Earth's, so it pushes far less hard than wind on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smaller dust devils form on sunny afternoons as heated air turns turbulent, and they lift dust and leave dark tracks where they strip away the light surface layer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A global storm can hide almost the whole surface, as when Mariner 9 reached Mars in November 1971 and found only the summits of Olympus Mons and the three Tharsis volcanoes showing through the dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> During the global storm of 2018 the Curiosity rover measured a 97 per cent drop in the ultraviolet sunlight reaching the ground and a narrowing of the daily range of air temperature by 30 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The darkness is the real danger to machines, since dust settles on solar panels and a dimmed sky cuts the light that reaches them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The solar-powered Opportunity rover stopped communicating when that storm blanketed its site in June 2018, and after more than a thousand commands failed to revive it the mission was declared over in February 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The storms also limit themselves, because once dust blocks sunlight from the ground the heating that drives them weakens, and the gaps of years between global storms are still not well understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.5 Water and Life on Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-5-water-and-life-on-mars. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.4 The Geology of Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-4-the-geology-of-mars. [Open access]","Karl B. Hille, “The Fact and Fiction of Martian Dust Storms,” NASA, 18 September 2015, https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/the-fact-and-fiction-of-martian-dust-storms/. [Open access]","NASA, “NASA’s Record-Setting Opportunity Rover Mission on Mars Comes to End,” news release 19-004, 13 February 2019, https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasas-record-setting-opportunity-rover-mission-on-mars-comes-to-end/. [Open access]","Scott D. Guzewich et al., “Mars Science Laboratory Observations of the 2018/Mars Year 34 Global Dust Storm,” <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 46, no. 1 (2019): 71–79, https://doi.org/10.1029/2018GL080839. [Open access]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Mariner 9,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, accessed November 2026, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1971-051A. [Open access]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Mars Fact Sheet,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 19 May 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/marsfact.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Could a Martian dust storm blow over a spacecraft?","a":"Probably not. The strongest Martian winds top out at about 100 kilometres per hour (60 miles per hour), and the atmosphere is only about one per cent as dense as Earth's, so they push far less hard than winds of the same speed on Earth and likely could not tip or tear apart major equipment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Viking landers recorded winds of 17 to 30 metres per second (38 to 67 miles per hour) during a dust storm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the big storms come mostly in southern summer?","a":"Because that is when Mars is closest to the Sun. Its orbit is less circular than Earth's, and the warmer part of its year falls in southern summer, when the sunlight heating the ground, which drives the rising air that lifts dust, is strongest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Global storms come on average once every three Martian years, though why years pass between them is still unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the 2018 storm end the Opportunity mission?","a":"By cutting off its power. Opportunity ran on solar panels, and the severe storm that blanketed its site in June 2018 blocked the sunlight; its final message was received on 10 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Instruments on the Curiosity rover elsewhere showed how dark it became, recording a 97 per cent cut in ultraviolet sunlight at the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Engineers sent more than a thousand commands before the mission was declared over in February 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-138": {"questions":["Loss rates of hydrogen and oxygen measured by the MAVEN spacecraft, adjusted for a more active young Sun, suggest that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> removed as much as 0.8 bar of carbon dioxide from Mars.","Because charged particles knock lighter argon atoms off more readily, the argon left high in the Martian air records how much gas <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has carried away over the planet's history."],"abstract":"<b>Atmospheric escape</b> is the loss of a planet's gas to space, and for Mars it is thought to be the main reason a once warmer, wetter world became the cold, dry planet seen today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gas escapes most easily from a small planet, and the escape velocity of Mars is only about 5 kilometres per second (3 miles per second), less than half of Earth's 11.2 kilometres per second (7 miles per second).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Light atoms leave first, and early in the history of the inner Solar System sunlight split hydrogen-bearing molecules so that much of the light hydrogen escaped, leaving atmospheres dominated by oxygen compounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some loss happens when atoms high in the atmosphere are knocked away by charged particles, a process called sputtering, which removes the lighter forms of an element more readily than the heavier ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mars has no global magnetic field today, though strongly magnetised rocks in its southern crust show that it had one about 4 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The MAVEN spacecraft measured escape from the upper atmosphere for a full Martian year and found hydrogen and oxygen being lost at about 2 to 3 kilograms (4 to 7 pounds) every second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Escape would have been faster early on, when the young Sun was more active, and allowing for that suggests that Mars may have lost as much as 0.8 bar of carbon dioxide and a global layer of water 23 metres (75 feet) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The isotopes of argon high in the atmosphere point the same way, requiring that about two thirds of the planet's atmospheric argon has been lost to space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Water tells a similar story, for Martian water vapour is about seven times richer in deuterium, a heavy form of hydrogen, than Earth's oceans, an enrichment that indicates great loss of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As the gas escaped the surface cooled, water froze out and the atmosphere held still less heat, a runaway cooling that probably ran its course within a billion years of the planet's formation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “10.5 Water and Life on Mars,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/10-5-water-and-life-on-mars. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.5 Planetary Evolution,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-5-planetary-evolution. [Open access]","B. M. Jakosky et al., “Loss of the Martian Atmosphere to Space: Present-Day Loss Rates Determined from MAVEN Observations and Integrated Loss through Time,” <i>Icarus</i> 315 (2018): 146–57, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2018.05.030. [Paywalled]","B. M. Jakosky et al., “Mars’ Atmospheric History Derived from Upper-Atmosphere Measurements of 38Ar/36Ar,” <i>Science</i> 355, no. 6332 (2017): 1408–10, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aai7721. [Paywalled]","G. L. Villanueva et al., “Strong Water Isotopic Anomalies in the Martian Atmosphere: Probing Current and Ancient Reservoirs,” <i>Science</i> 348, no. 6231 (2015): 218–21, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaa3630. [Paywalled]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Mars Fact Sheet,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 19 May 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/marsfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Mars: Facts,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Mars lose more of its air than Earth or Venus?","a":"Mainly because it is smaller. Its lower gravity let gases escape more easily, and its escape velocity is about 5 kilometres per second (3 miles per second) against Earth's 11.2 kilometres per second (7 miles per second).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> As the atmosphere thinned the surface cooled, most of the water froze out, and the planet held even less heat, a kind of runaway refrigerator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can argon show how much atmosphere was lost?","a":"Because argon is lost to space by sputtering, which removes the lighter atom more readily, so the gas left behind grows richer in the heavier form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measurements of argon's two isotopes in the upper atmosphere require that about two thirds of the argon has escaped, which means that a large fraction of the whole atmosphere has gone too, helping to turn an early warm, wet climate into today's cold, dry one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Could the early atmosphere have gone somewhere other than space?","a":"In principle it could have been locked into rocks or ice, but no surface or underground stores of carbon dioxide large enough to hold the remains of an early, thick atmosphere are known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Combined with the measured loss rates, that suggests escape to space was the main process that changed the climate of Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its water vapour is also strongly enriched in deuterium, as expected if much water was lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Atmospheric escape"},
    "astro-139": {"questions":["In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a planet's composition, its mass and its distance from the Sun are the three factors weighed to explain why neighbouring worlds evolved along such different paths.","Using a baked potato to explain why large planets stay geologically active longer, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sets worlds side by side to learn which processes shaped each one."],"abstract":"<b>Comparative planetology</b> is the study of planets side by side, using the differences between them to work out which processes control how each one evolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The inner planets make a natural test, because Earth, Venus and Mars all formed in the same zone from a similar mix, about one third iron with nickel or sulphur and two thirds silicate rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet they ended very differently, with Venus hot under a carbon dioxide atmosphere more than 90 times Earth's surface pressure, Mars cold under air at about one hundredth of Earth's surface pressure, and only Earth keeping liquid water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three factors largely decide each world's course, namely its composition, its mass and its distance from the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Distance sets the starting temperature, since sunlight weakens with the square of the distance and a planet's surface temperature falls roughly with its square root.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Size matters because a larger planet keeps its internal heat longer, just as a large baked potato stays hot longer than a small one, so bigger worlds stay geologically active.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Moon, the smallest, ended its major volcanism about 3.3 billion years ago, Mars is an intermediate case, and Earth and Venus are still active, with most of Earth's surface less than 200 million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Water fared differently with size and distance, for Mars lost the carbon dioxide needed for a strong greenhouse effect and froze, while Venus suffered a runaway greenhouse effect that removed its water permanently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On Mars, loss of gas to space appears to have been the main driver of the change from an early, warmer climate to the cold, dry one of today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Setting the planets side by side also frames open questions, such as whether small size is fatal to habitability and how a magnetic field affects the evolution of an atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.2 Composition and Structure of Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-2-composition-and-structure-of-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “14.5 Planetary Evolution,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/14-5-planetary-evolution. [Open access]","B. L. Ehlmann et al., “The Sustainability of Habitability on Terrestrial Planets: Insights, Questions, and Needed Measurements from Mars for Understanding the Evolution of Earth-like Worlds,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 121, no. 10 (2016): 1927–61, https://doi.org/10.1002/2016JE005134. [Open access]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Planetary Fact Sheet – Metric,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 18 March 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Venus: Facts,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/venus/facts/. [Open access]","B. M. Jakosky et al., “Loss of the Martian Atmosphere to Space: Present-Day Loss Rates Determined from MAVEN Observations and Integrated Loss through Time,” <i>Icarus</i> 315 (2018): 146–57, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2018.05.030. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do bigger rocky planets stay active longer?","a":"Because they lose their internal heat more slowly. Geological activity is driven by heat escaping from a planet's interior, and a large body, like a large baked potato, cools more slowly than a small one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So the Moon went quiet about 3.3 billion years ago, Mars stayed active for longer, and Earth and Venus, the largest of the rocky planets, are active today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did Venus and Mars both end up dry?","a":"By opposite routes. Early Mars apparently had a thick atmosphere and liquid water, but it lost the carbon dioxide needed for a strong greenhouse effect, cooled, and its remaining water froze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On Venus a runaway greenhouse effect led to the permanent loss of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both now have atmospheres of about 96 per cent carbon dioxide, and the surface of Venus is hot enough to melt lead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does studying Mars help with planets around other stars?","a":"Because Mars keeps the longest record in the Solar System of the processes behind habitability on an accessible rocky planet with an atmosphere and water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Questions it can test include whether small size is fatal, how magnetic fields affect an atmosphere and how far the starting composition dictates what follows, and the answers bear on Earth, Venus and thousands of rocky planets yet to be found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Comparative planetary science"},
    "astro-140": {"questions":["Gravity data from the Dawn spacecraft, revealing an iron core about 110 kilometres (68 miles) in radius, showed that Vesta had undergone <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> like the far larger rocky planets.","Clocks based on hafnium decaying into tungsten show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built the cores of the terrestrial planets within about the first 30 million years of the Solar System."],"abstract":"<b>Planetary differentiation</b> is the process by which a planet's interior separates into layers of different composition and density, with heavy metals sinking to form a core and lighter minerals rising to form a crust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It requires the body to be hot enough to melt, and for a rocky planet that means heating to the melting point of rock, typically more than 1,300 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The layers survive after the planet cools, which is why the solid terrestrial planets, with dense metal cores beneath silicate mantles and crusts, are taken to have once been molten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Earth shows the result plainly, since its bulk density of about 5.5 grams per cubic centimetre is far above the 3 grams of its surface rocks, and a dense metallic core begins 2,900 kilometres (1,800 miles) down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not every body went through it, for most asteroids, comets and the smallest moons were probably never heated to melting, while some of the largest asteroids, such as Vesta, did differentiate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Dawn spacecraft confirmed this for Vesta, whose mass, volume and gravity fit an iron core with an average radius of about 107 to 113 kilometres (66 to 70 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mars is layered too, with a core of iron, nickel and sulphur, a rocky mantle, and a crust 10 to 50 kilometres (6 to 30 miles) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Seismic waves recorded by the InSight lander and reflected from the boundary between mantle and core put the radius of the liquid metal core of Mars at about 1,830 kilometres (1,140 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its density of 5.7 to 6.3 grams per cubic centimetre is too low for pure metal, so the core must contain a large share of lighter elements dissolved in the iron and nickel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The decay of a radioactive form of hafnium into tungsten dates the separation, and on that clock the cores of the terrestrial planets formed within about the first 30 million years of the Solar System, faster in smaller bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “7.2 Composition and Structure of Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-2-composition-and-structure-of-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “8.1 The Global Perspective,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/8-1-the-global-perspective. [Open access]","T. Kleine et al., “Rapid Accretion and Early Core Formation on Asteroids and the Terrestrial Planets from Hf–W Chronometry,” <i>Nature</i> 418, no. 6901 (2002): 952–55, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature00982. [Paywalled]","C. T. Russell et al., “Dawn at Vesta: Testing the Protoplanetary Paradigm,” <i>Science</i> 336, no. 6082 (2012): 684–86, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1219381. [Paywalled]","Simon C. Stähler et al., “Seismic Detection of the Martian Core,” <i>Science</i> 373, no. 6553 (2021): 443–48, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abi7730. [Paywalled]","NASA Science, “Mars: Facts,” NASA, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/. [Open access]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Mars Fact Sheet,” NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, last updated 19 May 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/marsfact.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How do we know Earth has a dense core if no one has seen it?","a":"Because the numbers do not add up otherwise. Earth's average density is about 5.5 grams per cubic centimetre, while the rocks at its surface are about 3, so much denser material must lie inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Seismic waves passing through the planet reveal its layers directly, including a liquid outer core and an inner core that is probably solid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why didn't most small asteroids differentiate?","a":"Because they were never hot enough. Separating metal from rock needs the interior heated to the melting point of rock, typically more than 1,300 K, and most asteroids, comets and the smallest moons probably never reached it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some large asteroids did, and Dawn found that the crust of Vesta formed by melting and that the asteroid has an iron core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the core of Mars thought to contain light elements?","a":"Because it is not dense enough to be pure metal. Seismic reflections recorded by InSight constrained the radius of the liquid core to about 1,830 kilometres (1,140 miles), and for a core that size the average density must be 5.7 to 6.3 grams per cubic centimetre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That requires a substantial amount of light elements dissolved in the iron and nickel, and NASA lists sulphur among the core's ingredients.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Planetary differentiation"},
    "astro-141": {"questions":["Hot Jupiters, which can orbit their stars in as little as 18 hours, are a kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found around other stars and unknown in our own Solar System.","With no solid surface beneath its clouds, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a deep atmosphere that grows hotter and denser downwards until its hydrogen is squeezed into a liquid, then a metallic fluid."],"abstract":"A <b>gas giant</b> is a giant planet made mostly of hydrogen and helium, the two most abundant elements in the universe, and in the Solar System the name belongs to Jupiter and Saturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word describes what such a planet was built from rather than what it is now, because under the immense pressure inside it hydrogen gas is compressed into a liquid and then into a metallic fluid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A gas giant has no true surface, and what can be seen from outside is the top of a deep atmosphere that gives way, far below, to swirling liquids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Analyses of their gravity show that Jupiter and Saturn each have a core of heavier material, but in each it makes up only a few per cent of the planet's mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> How heavy elements are spread through such a planet depends on how hydrogen behaves at extreme pressure, and for Jupiter that uncertainty alone amounts to some 18 Earth masses of core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In the outer Solar System the raw material for planets was about 75 per cent hydrogen and 24 per cent helium by mass, with ices and rocks making up only about one per cent between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jupiter alone outweighs all the other planets put together, and it has the same main ingredients as a star but never grew massive enough to ignite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Uranus and Neptune are giants too, but because their interiors hold far more of the icy component they are often called ice giants instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gas giants are also common around other stars, and those that orbit very close in, called hot Jupiters, can circle their stars in as little as 18 hours and reach temperatures of thousands of degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Such planets are thought to form within the first 10 million years of a Sun-like star's life or not at all, and whether hot Jupiters formed close in or migrated inwards later is still an open question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.1 Exploring the Outer Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-1-exploring-the-outer-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Gas Giant,” last updated 22 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/gas-giant/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Jupiter Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]","Jonathan J. Fortney and Nadine Nettelmann, “The Interior Structure, Composition, and Evolution of Giant Planets,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 152 (2010): 423–47, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-009-9582-x, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.0533. [Open access]","David J. Stevenson, “Jupiter's Interior as Revealed by Juno,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 48 (2020): 465–89, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-081619-052855. [Paywalled]","NASA Science, “Saturn: Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/facts/. [Open access]","Tristan Guillot, “The Interiors of Giant Planets: Models and Outstanding Questions,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 33 (2005): 493–530, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.earth.32.101802.120325, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0502068. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a planet that is mostly liquid inside called a gas giant?","a":"Because the name describes what it was made from, not the state of that material now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Jupiter and Saturn were built largely from hydrogen and helium gas, but a few thousand kilometres below the visible clouds the pressure turns hydrogen into a liquid, and deeper still the liquid begins to behave like a metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On Jupiter the greater part of the interior is liquid metallic hydrogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do Jupiter and Saturn have nearly the Sun's composition?","a":"Because they were built from the same raw material and kept most of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the outer Solar System the available material was about 75 per cent hydrogen and 24 per cent helium by mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jupiter took most of the mass left over after the Sun formed and has the same ingredients as a star, though it never grew massive enough to ignite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Calculations show that only these two light gases can build planets with the observed masses and densities of Jupiter and Saturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were hot Jupiters among the first exoplanets found?","a":"Because they tug hard on their stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A hot Jupiter is a gas giant so close to its star that it can orbit in as little as 18 hours, and its pull makes the star wobble enough to shift the spectrum of its light measurably.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That made such planets easier to detect in the early days of the radial velocity method, and 51 Peg b, found in 1995, was the first planet discovered around a Sun-like star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Gas giant"},
    "astro-142": {"questions":["Although the name of an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> suggests frozen material, its water, methane and ammonia are thought to exist inside as a hot, dense fluid above a small rocky core.","Visited only by single flybys of Voyager 2, each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Solar System has a steeply tilted, many-poled magnetic field that implies a deep conducting fluid layer."],"abstract":"An <b>ice giant</b> is a giant planet made mostly of elements heavier than hydrogen and helium, and the Solar System has two, Uranus and Neptune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ices of the name are compounds of oxygen, carbon and nitrogen with hydrogen, chiefly water, methane and ammonia, and the word describes their chemistry rather than whether they are frozen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside the planets these materials are thought to form a hot, dense fluid making up 80 per cent or more of the mass, above a small rocky core and beneath an atmosphere mostly of hydrogen and helium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Uranus and Neptune have masses of about 14.5 and 17 Earths and are about four times Earth's size, far smaller than Jupiter and Saturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their average densities are much higher than Saturn's, one sign that their make-up differs fundamentally from that of the gas giants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Most of their mass lies in their cores, which shows that they were unable to gather large amounts of hydrogen and helium while forming, though why they stopped short of becoming gas giants is still hard to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their magnetic fields are tilted by about 60 and 55 degrees and have several poles, which implies a convecting, electrically conducting layer beneath the outer envelope, often suggested to be ionic water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether the planets are truly icy is debated, because the same measurements can in principle be matched by interiors far richer in rock, and only single flybys by Voyager 2 have ever visited them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neptune still gives off heat from inside while Uranus is close to balance with the sunlight it receives, a difference that may mean layers inside Uranus hinder convection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Planets of about their size, which NASA calls Neptunian, turn out to be common around other stars, and an ice giant was found 25,000 light-years away in 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.1 Exploring the Outer Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-1-exploring-the-outer-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","Ravit Helled, Nadine Nettelmann, and Tristan Guillot, “Uranus and Neptune: Origin, Evolution and Internal Structure,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 216, no. 3 (2020): 38, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-020-00660-3, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.04891. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Uranus: Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/uranus/facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Neptune: Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/neptune-facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “What Are Neptunian Planets?,” last updated 22 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/neptune-like/. [Open access]","Jonathan J. Fortney and Nadine Nettelmann, “The Interior Structure, Composition, and Evolution of Giant Planets,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 152 (2010): 423–47, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-009-9582-x, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.0533. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are Uranus and Neptune called ice giants when their interiors are not frozen?","a":"Because ice here is a chemical label, not a description of a solid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It means compounds built from oxygen, carbon and nitrogen with hydrogen, such as water, methane and ammonia, which are typically found frozen in the cold outer Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Inside the planets these materials are warm enough not to be frozen and are thought to form a hot, dense fluid making up most of each planet's mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might the ice giants not really be icy?","a":"Because the evidence does not settle what is inside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The arguments for water-rich interiors are that water-rich solids should have been plentiful where they formed and that their magnetic fields need a conducting fluid, possibly ionic water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet the observed properties can in principle be matched by interiors that are mostly rock, and compressed silicates mixed with hydrogen might also conduct well enough to make a magnetic field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do astronomers want a spacecraft to orbit an ice giant?","a":"Because each planet has been seen up close only once, during the Voyager 2 flybys of 1986 and 1989, and no orbiter has yet been sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Better measurements of their gravity, magnetic fields, rotation and deep atmospheres are needed to pin down their interiors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That matters beyond the Solar System, because planets of about their size are common around other stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ice giant"},
    "astro-143": {"questions":["First seen through a telescope in 1610, the four large moons of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, lead a family of more than 110 recognised moons.","Since 2016 the Juno orbiter has looped over the poles of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, measuring a magnetic field that in places is more than 30 times as powerful as Earth's."],"abstract":"<b>Jupiter</b> is the fifth planet from the Sun and the largest in the Solar System, a gas giant whose mass of about 318 Earths exceeds that of all the other planets combined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It orbits at about 5.2 astronomical units and takes just under 12 years to circle the Sun, while it spins once in about 9 hours 56 minutes, the shortest day of any planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its radius of about 69,900 kilometres (43,400 miles) makes it 11 times wider than Earth, and its fast spin flattens it, so that its equatorial radius is some 4,600 kilometres (2,900 miles) greater than its polar radius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Like the Sun it is made mostly of hydrogen and helium, and it has the same main ingredients as a star but never grew massive enough to ignite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its average density is only about a quarter of Earth's, because its material is spread through a volume that could hold about 1,300 Earths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Seen through a telescope it shows dark belts and light zones of cloud, made of ammonia and other compounds and drawn out by jet streams that blow east and west in opposite directions and reach thousands of kilometres below the clouds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Its four large moons, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, were first observed through a telescope in 1610, and more than 110 moons were officially recognised as of October 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since July 2016 the Juno orbiter has circled it on long orbits that pass over the poles, measuring its gravity and magnetic field to probe the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That magnetic field, generated by currents in a deep layer of liquid metallic hydrogen, is in some places more than 30 times as powerful as Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jupiter took shape about 4.6 billion years ago, taking most of the material left over after the Sun formed, and isotopes in iron meteorites suggest that its core reached about 20 Earth masses within the first million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["David R. Williams, “Jupiter Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 2 October 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/jupiterfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Jupiter Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.1 Exploring the Outer Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-1-exploring-the-outer-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Juno,” last updated 3 November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/juno/. [Open access]","David J. Stevenson, “Jupiter's Interior as Revealed by Juno,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 48 (2020): 465–89, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-081619-052855. [Paywalled]","Thomas S. Kruijer et al., “Age of Jupiter Inferred from the Distinct Genetics and Formation Times of Meteorites,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 26 (2017): 6712–16, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1704461114. [Open access]","Tristan Guillot, “The Interiors of Giant Planets: Models and Outstanding Questions,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 33 (2005): 493–530, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.earth.32.101802.120325, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0502068. [Open access]","Y. Kaspi et al., “Jupiter's Atmospheric Jet Streams Extend Thousands of Kilometres Deep,” <i>Nature</i> 555 (2018): 223–26, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature25793. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Jupiter not become a star?","a":"Because it never grew massive enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jupiter is made mostly of hydrogen and helium, like the Sun, and it took most of the material left over after the Sun formed, more than twice that of all the other bodies of the Solar System together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so, it has the ingredients of a star without the mass needed to ignite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is still warm inside, giving off about as much energy from within as it absorbs from the Sun, most of it heat left over from its formation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Jupiter's day measured from radio waves rather than clouds?","a":"Because its visible clouds may turn at a rate that has little to do with the spin of the planet beneath them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Astronomers instead time the regular variations in radio waves from Jupiter, which are controlled by its magnetic field; since the field is generated deep inside, it shares the rotation of the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured this way, Jupiter spins once in 9 hours 56 minutes, the shortest day of any planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Juno fly over Jupiter's poles?","a":"Because its job is to study the planet's interior, atmosphere and magnetosphere, and its polar orbits made it the first mission to circle an outer planet from pole to pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Juno arrived in July 2016 on a very elongated orbit that carries it over the poles and as close as about 4,000 kilometres (2,500 miles) above the cloud tops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its gravity data showed that Jupiter's banded jet streams reach about 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) below the clouds and that its core is large and partly dissolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Jupiter"},
    "astro-144": {"questions":["Juno's gravity measurements showed that the jet streams of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reach about 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) down, so that this moving layer holds roughly one per cent of the planet's mass.","Falling into a dry downdraft in 1995, the Galileo probe found far less water in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than expected, missing the deeper clouds of water ice and droplets that models predicted."],"abstract":"The <b>atmosphere of Jupiter</b> is the deep envelope of gas, mostly hydrogen and helium, that forms the whole visible face of the planet, with no solid surface beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By volume it is about 90 per cent molecular hydrogen and 10 per cent helium, with traces of methane, ammonia, water and other compounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The clouds that can be seen are crystals of frozen ammonia, and they mark the top of the troposphere, where the temperature is about 140 K, with a clear, colder stratosphere above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath them a layer of ammonium hydrosulfide crystals is thought to lie, and deeper still clouds of water ice and vapour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The colours of the clouds, from white to orange and brown, come from impurities made by chemical reactions, possibly from sulfur- and phosphorus-bearing gases rising from the warmer interior, and remain something of a puzzle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Jupiter's rapid spin draws the circulation into bands parallel to the equator: light zones where air rises, capped by white ammonia clouds, and darker belts where it sinks and the view reaches deeper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bands are separated by east–west jet streams, with winds of up to about 540 kilometres per hour (335 miles per hour) at the equator, and the pattern has barely changed over many decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no solid surface for the winds to rub against, and heat from the interior adds about as much energy as sunlight does, so deep convection keeps the circulation going.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Galileo probe that parachuted in on 7 December 1995 found far less water than expected, because it happened to fall into an unusually dry downdraft, and Juno later showed water to be more abundant near the equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gravity measurements by Juno show that the jet streams extend roughly 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) below the clouds, and that this moving outer layer holds about one per cent of Jupiter's mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.3 Atmospheres of the Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-3-atmospheres-of-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.1 Exploring the Outer Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-1-exploring-the-outer-planets. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Jupiter Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 2 October 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/jupiterfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Jupiter Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Juno,” last updated 3 November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/juno/. [Open access]","Y. Kaspi et al., “Jupiter's Atmospheric Jet Streams Extend Thousands of Kilometres Deep,” <i>Nature</i> 555 (2018): 223–26, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature25793. [Paywalled]","Yohai Kaspi et al., “Comparison of the Deep Atmospheric Dynamics of Jupiter and Saturn in Light of the Juno and Cassini Gravity Measurements,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 216, no. 5 (2020): 84, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-020-00705-7, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.09613. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Jupiter's atmosphere look banded?","a":"Because the planet's fast spin smears its circulation into east–west patterns parallel to the equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The light zones are regions of rising air topped by white ammonia clouds, while the darker belts are where cooler air sinks, leaving fewer clouds so that the view reaches deeper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strong jet streams blowing in opposite directions separate the bands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Galileo probe find so little water?","a":"Because it happened to enter an unusually dry, cloud-free patch of the atmosphere, a giant downdraft of cool, dry gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The probe had been expected to pass through clouds of water ice and droplets, but it saw no other cloud layers before its instruments stopped working at a pressure of 22 bars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Juno later showed that water is much more abundant nearer Jupiter's equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it matter how deep the jet streams go?","a":"Because their depth had been a long-standing question that pictures of the cloud tops could not answer, and resolving it was a primary goal for Juno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Flows inside the planet leave a north–south imbalance in its gravity field, and Juno's measurements of that imbalance showed that the jets reach roughly 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That depth is about where the gas begins to conduct electricity, which suggests that the magnetic field helps to brake the flow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Atmosphere of Jupiter"},
    "astro-145": {"questions":["About 25,000 kilometres (16,000 miles) long in 1979 but only 20,000 kilometres (12,000 miles) by 2000, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been shrinking and growing rounder for as long as it has been measured.","A 2024 study argued that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first seen in 1831, not in the 17th century, and that it grew from a disturbance between two opposed jet streams."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Red Spot</b> is a vast, reddish oval storm in Jupiter's southern hemisphere, the largest and longest-lived vortex known on any planet in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a high-pressure system, an anticyclone, which sets it apart from Earth's great storms, since on Earth such storms are regions of low pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It turns counterclockwise once in about six days, its fast-moving collar around a quieter interior, and a simple calculation from that period puts the wind at its outer edge at about 436 kilometres per hour (271 miles per hour).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It was about 25,000 kilometres (16,000 miles) long in 1979 and had shrunk to 20,000 kilometres (12,000 miles) by 2000, growing more nearly circular as it went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its roots reach some 350 to 500 kilometres (220 to 310 miles) below the cloud tops, beyond the level where water condenses, and spectra show an opaque water cloud at a pressure of about 5 bars beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Measurements of the spot span more than 150 years, and they show it shrinking steadily in length and width while its westward drift has speeded up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is often said to have been seen for more than 300 years, since soon after the invention of the telescope, but that depends on identifying it with a dark Permanent Spot observed from 1665 to 1713.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A 2024 study of the historical records judged that the Permanent Spot was unlikely to be the same storm, and that the present spot was first observed in 1831.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its simulations suggest that the spot formed not from merging vortices or a superstorm but from a disturbance in the flow between the two opposed jet streams that flank it, a westward one to the north and an eastward one to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Storms on Jupiter can last so long because there is no solid surface to slow them by friction, and a vortex as large as the spot should survive for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.3 Atmospheres of the Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-3-atmospheres-of-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Jupiter Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Juno,” last updated 3 November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/juno/. [Open access]","Amy A. Simon et al., “Historical and Contemporary Trends in the Size, Drift, and Color of Jupiter's Great Red Spot,” <i>The Astronomical Journal</i> 155, no. 4 (2018): 151, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/aaae01. [Open access]","Agustín Sánchez-Lavega et al., “The Origin of Jupiter's Great Red Spot,” <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 51, no. 12 (2024): e2024GL108993, https://doi.org/10.1029/2024GL108993, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13222. [Open access]","Eli Galanti et al., “Determining the Depth of Jupiter's Great Red Spot with Juno: A Slepian Approach,” <i>The Astrophysical Journal Letters</i> 874, no. 2 (2019): L24, https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/ab1086, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.09956. [Open access]","G. L. Bjoraker et al., “The Gas Composition and Deep Cloud Structure of Jupiter's Great Red Spot,” <i>The Astronomical Journal</i> 156, no. 3 (2018): 101, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/aad186, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.01402. [Open access]","Sushil Shetty, Xylar S. Asay-Davis, and Philip S. Marcus, “On the Interaction of Jupiter's Great Red Spot and Zonal Jet Streams,” <i>Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences</i> 64, no. 12 (2007): 4432–44, https://doi.org/10.1175/2007JAS2097.1, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.4193. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can the Great Red Spot last so long?","a":"Because nothing beneath it slows it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A hurricane on Earth usually lasts a few weeks, and dies sooner when it moves over land and meets friction, but Jupiter has no solid surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The storm's sheer size also lends it stability, and calculations suggest that a vortex as large as the spot should last for centuries, roughly what has been observed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the spot's age disputed?","a":"Because it rests on whether two storms seen more than a century apart were the same one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A dark oval at the same latitude, the Permanent Spot, was observed from 1665 until 1713, and the present spot was first observed in 1831.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Comparing how the sizes and motions of the two evolved, a 2024 study judged it unlikely that they were the same storm, which would make the present spot younger than the 300 years or more often quoted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did scientists expect the spot to be deep?","a":"Because a shallow storm should not survive so long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its appearance as an anticyclonic vortex suggested a feature of the shallow weather layer, but its very long lifetime implied deep roots, since otherwise Jupiter's turbulent atmosphere would have sheared it apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Juno's measurements later put its base some 350 to 500 kilometres (220 to 310 miles) below the cloud tops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Great Red Spot"},
    "astro-146": {"questions":["Juno's gravity data suggest that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds no small solid core but a dilute one, with heavy elements mixed through perhaps 63 per cent of the planet's radius.","Hydrogen and helium make up most of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose heavy-element core is estimated at anywhere from about 7 to 25 Earth masses, depending on how hydrogen behaves under pressure."],"abstract":"<b>Jupiter's interior</b> is hidden beneath its clouds, and what is known of it comes from the planet's mass, size and gravity field, compared with models of how matter behaves at extreme pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Calculations show that only hydrogen and helium can build a planet with Jupiter's mass and low density, so these two light elements make up most of the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A few thousand kilometres below the clouds the pressure turns hydrogen gas into a liquid, and deeper still the liquid begins to conduct electricity like a metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Liquid metallic hydrogen fills the greater part of the interior, and currents driven in it by the fast rotation are thought to generate the planet's magnetic field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best models put the pressure at the centre above 100 million bars, some 25 times that at the centre of Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The older picture was of a fairly uniform mix of hydrogen and helium wrapped around a small, solid core of ice, rock and metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Juno's precise measurements of the gravity field were hard to reconcile with that picture, because some of the gravity harmonics came out unexpectedly small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Models that fit them instead spread the heavy elements through a dilute core, mixed with hydrogen and helium and reaching perhaps 63 per cent of the way out from the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Estimates of how much heavy material the core holds range from about 7 to 25 Earth masses, largely because the behaviour of hydrogen at such pressures is still uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within such a core the heavy elements may make up only 18 per cent of the mass, and how the planet came to have this structure is still hard to square with theories of its formation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Jupiter Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Juno,” last updated 3 November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/juno/. [Open access]","S. M. Wahl et al., “Comparing Jupiter Interior Structure Models to Juno Gravity Measurements and the Role of a Dilute Core,” <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 44, no. 10 (2017): 4649–59, https://doi.org/10.1002/2017GL073160, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.01997. [Open access]","Burkhard Militzer et al., “Juno Spacecraft Measurements of Jupiter's Gravity Imply a Dilute Core,” <i>The Planetary Science Journal</i> 3 (2022): 185, https://doi.org/10.3847/PSJ/ac7ec8. [Open access]","David J. Stevenson, “Jupiter's Interior as Revealed by Juno,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 48 (2020): 465–89, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-081619-052855. [Paywalled]","Tristan Guillot, “The Interiors of Giant Planets: Models and Outstanding Questions,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 33 (2005): 493–530, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.earth.32.101802.120325, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0502068. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does hydrogen become a metal inside Jupiter?","a":"Because the pressure is so great that electrons are squeezed off the hydrogen atoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a metal, electrons are not firmly attached to their nuclei and can wander, which is why metals conduct electricity so well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At depths perhaps halfway to the centre, compressed liquid hydrogen behaves in this way, and Jupiter's fast rotation is thought to drive currents in it that generate the magnetic field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can anyone know what is inside Jupiter?","a":"Mostly by measuring its gravity very precisely from orbit, as Juno has done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How mass is arranged inside a spinning planet shapes its gravity field, and Juno measured that field's harmonics with unprecedented precision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Comparing the measurements with models built from computer simulations of hydrogen–helium mixtures shows which interiors fit, although the answer depends strongly on the equation of state chosen for hydrogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a dilute core unexpected?","a":"Because Jupiter had long been pictured as a fairly uniform ball of hydrogen and helium around a small, solid core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Juno's gravity data were difficult to match with conventional models that assume a large, distinct core of rock and ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Instead the heavy elements appear to be partly dissolved, with no clear boundary between the core and the metallic hydrogen around it, which is why the core is described as dilute or fuzzy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-147": {"questions":["Ballooning up to 3 million kilometres (2 million miles) towards the Sun, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> trails a tail more than a billion kilometres (600 million miles) long, reaching as far as Saturn's orbit.","Radio waves that strengthen at longer wavelengths, picked up from Jupiter in the late 1950s, gave the first hint of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where fast electrons spiral along magnetic field lines."],"abstract":"The <b>magnetosphere of Jupiter</b> is the region of space in which the planet's own magnetic field, rather than the general interplanetary field, controls the motion of charged particles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the planet's largest feature, ballooning 1 to 3 million kilometres (0.6 to 2 million miles) towards the Sun and trailing a tail more than 1 billion kilometres (600 million miles) long, as far as Saturn's orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The field is generated by electric currents in the rapidly spinning interior and is 16 to 54 times as powerful as Earth's, with its axis tilted about 10 degrees from the axis of rotation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first hint of it came in the late 1950s, when Jupiter was found to emit radio waves that grow stronger at longer wavelengths, the signature of fast electrons spiralling in a magnetic field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Within it, charged particles are trapped and accelerated to very high energies, creating intense radiation that bombards the innermost moons and can damage spacecraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What makes the magnetosphere unusual is that the main source of its plasma lies deep inside it, in the volcanically active moon Io.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Material lost from Io's thin atmosphere forms neutral clouds, a ring of plasma along its orbit called the Io plasma torus, and finally a sheet of plasma spread through the magnetosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Keeping the torus supplied takes roughly 1,000 kilograms (2,200 pounds) of material every second, and occasionally the torus changes markedly over several weeks for reasons still not understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Because the field rotates with the planet and its plasma source lies so deep within it, the magnetosphere is driven chiefly by rotation, and that combination is the main cause of Jupiter's main auroral ovals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its aurorae are among the most spectacular in the Solar System, and Juno found the particles that cause them arriving with energies of up to 400,000 electron volts, against a few thousand in Earth's strongest aurorae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.1 Exploring the Outer Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-1-exploring-the-outer-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Jupiter Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Jupiter Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 2 October 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/jupiterfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Juno,” last updated 3 November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/juno/. [Open access]","Lorenz Roth et al., “Mass Supply from Io to Jupiter's Magnetosphere,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 221, no. 1 (2025): 13, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-025-01137-x, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.13970. [Open access]","Joachim Saur, Emmanuel Chané, and Oliver Hartkorn, “Modeling Magnetospheric Fields in the Jupiter System,” in <i>Planetary Magnetism</i>, Astrophysics and Space Science Library (Cham: Springer, 2018), 153–82, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-64292-5_6, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.10884. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Io matter so much to Jupiter's magnetosphere?","a":"Because Io is the main source of its plasma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Material escapes from Io's upper atmosphere, driven mostly by its interaction with the surrounding plasma rather than blown straight out by its volcanoes, and forms clouds of gas, the Io plasma torus and eventually the plasma sheet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Keeping the torus supplied takes roughly 1,000 kilograms (2,200 pounds) of material every second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the first spacecraft to Jupiter nearly crippled?","a":"Because the magnetosphere traps charged particles and accelerates them to very high energies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Measuring that radiation hazard was one of the objectives of Pioneers 10 and 11, and the energetic particles in Jupiter's magnetic field nearly wiped out their electronics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they learned was used to design later missions to survive there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What causes Jupiter's main aurorae?","a":"Mainly the planet's own rotation and the plasma inside its magnetosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The magnetosphere is strongly driven by rotation and has large sources of plasma deep within it, and the combination of the two is the primary reason for Jupiter's main auroral ovals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The field rotates with the planet and sweeps up charged particles, and it causes some of the Solar System's most spectacular aurorae at Jupiter's poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Magnetosphere of Jupiter"},
    "astro-148": {"questions":["Helium droplets sinking through liquid hydrogen help explain why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> radiates almost twice as much energy per kilogram as Jupiter, despite having far less heat left from its formation.","Diving between <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its rings in 2017, Cassini measured a gravity field implying winds at least 9,000 kilometres (5,600 miles) deep before plunging into the planet."],"abstract":"<b>Saturn</b> is the sixth planet from the Sun and the second largest, a gas giant made mostly of hydrogen and helium and best known for its broad, bright rings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It orbits at about 9.5 astronomical units, taking about 29.5 years to circle the Sun, and spins once in about 10 hours 40 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its mass is about 95 times Earth's, spread across an equatorial diameter of about 120,500 kilometres (74,900 miles), which gives it the lowest average density of any planet, less than that of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its fast spin flattens it markedly, so that its polar radius is about 5,900 kilometres (3,700 miles) shorter than its equatorial one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its axis is tilted by about 27 degrees, so it has seasons, which change slowly over its long year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its butterscotch clouds of ammonia ice lie deeper than Jupiter's, which makes its banding more subdued, while its equatorial winds reach about 1,800 kilometres per hour (1,100 miles per hour).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Inside, the pressure turns hydrogen into a liquid, but because Saturn is less massive than Jupiter it holds only a small volume of metallic hydrogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It gives off almost twice as much energy per kilogram as Jupiter, and most of it is thought to come from droplets of helium sinking through the liquid hydrogen and releasing gravitational energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Gravity measured as Cassini dived between the planet and its rings in 2017 showed winds reaching at least 9,000 kilometres (5,600 miles) deep, and waves in the rings point to a diffuse core extending about 60 per cent of the way out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> As of March 2025 Saturn had more than 270 confirmed moons, more than any other planet, among them Titan and the geyser-spouting Enceladus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["David R. Williams, “Saturn Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 18 March 2025, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/saturnfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Saturn: Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/facts/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.3 Atmospheres of the Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-3-atmospheres-of-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Cassini-Huygens,” last updated 17 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/cassini/. [Open access]","L. Iess et al., “Measurement and Implications of Saturn's Gravity Field and Ring Mass,” <i>Science</i> 364, no. 6445 (2019): eaat2965, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aat2965. [Paywalled]","Christopher R. Mankovich and Jim Fuller, “A Diffuse Core in Saturn Revealed by Ring Seismology,” <i>Nature Astronomy</i> 5 (2021): 1103–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41550-021-01448-3, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13385. [Open access]","Yohai Kaspi et al., “Comparison of the Deep Atmospheric Dynamics of Jupiter and Saturn in Light of the Juno and Cassini Gravity Measurements,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 216, no. 5 (2020): 84, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-020-00705-7, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.09613. [Open access]","J. J. Fortney et al., “The Interior of Saturn,” in <i>Saturn in the 21st Century</i>, ed. Kevin H. Baines et al. (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2018), 44–68, https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316227220.003, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.06324. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Saturn float in water?","a":"Because its average density is lower than water's, the only planet in the Solar System of which that is true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its mass is 95 times Earth's, but that mass is spread through an enormous volume of mostly hydrogen and helium, giving it an average density about one eighth of Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Being less massive than Jupiter, it holds only a small volume of metallic hydrogen, though most of its interior is liquid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Saturn give off more heat than expected?","a":"Because something besides heat left over from its formation is at work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Saturn's internal energy source is about half as large as Jupiter's, so with about a quarter of Jupiter's mass it produces twice as much energy per kilogram.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The likely source is helium separating from hydrogen inside the planet: in the liquid hydrogen mantle the heavier helium forms droplets that sink, releasing gravitational energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Cassini end its mission by plunging into Saturn?","a":"Because it had almost run out of propellant, and operators wanted to be sure it could never crash into the moons, especially ocean-bearing Enceladus and Titan with its pre-biotic chemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Before that, its final orbits took it between the planet and its innermost ring at 2,600 to 3,900 kilometres (1,600 to 2,400 miles) above the cloud tops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Radio tracking on those passes measured Saturn's gravity field and the mass of its rings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Saturn"},
    "astro-149": {"questions":["Weighed by Cassini at less than half the mass of the moon Mimas, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are made almost entirely of water ice, and their cleanness has been read as a sign of youth.","Geysers on the moon Enceladus feed the faint E ring, the outermost of the lettered bands of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that stretch outwards from the D ring nearest the planet."],"abstract":"The <b>rings of Saturn</b> are the largest and most massive ring system in the Solar System, a vast, flat disc of countless icy particles orbiting above the planet's equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The three main rings are named A, B and C from the outside in, with the bright, densely packed B ring holding most of the mass and the 4,700-kilometre (2,900-mile) Cassini Division separating it from the A ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fainter rings lie inside and beyond them, from the D ring nearest the planet out to the tenuous E ring, which is fed by ice from geysers on the moon Enceladus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The particles are mostly water ice and range from grains like sand to boulders the size of houses, each following its own orbit, so that inner particles travel faster than outer ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main rings are about 70,000 kilometres (43,000 miles) wide but on average only about 20 metres (66 feet) thick, and fine structure is carved in them by the gravity of moons, both outside the rings and embedded within them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Their age is one of the great open questions about them, and it bears on how they formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Cassini's final orbits, passing inside the rings, weighed them at about 0.41 times the mass of the small moon Mimas, a figure that suggested they may have formed only 10 to 100 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rings are also strikingly clean, mostly water ice although dust from beyond Saturn falls on them all the time, and Cassini's measurements of that dust put their exposure at no more than about 100 to 400 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A 2025 study argued that the rings may resist such pollution, because much of the impacting dust is vaporised and removed rather than stuck to the ice, so that clean rings need not be young.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rings even carry a record of the planet beneath them, since some of their waves are driven by vibrations inside Saturn that point to a diffuse core reaching about 60 per cent of the way out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.5 Planetary Rings and Enceladus,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-5-planetary-rings-and-enceladus. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Saturn: Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/facts/. [Open access]","L. Iess et al., “Measurement and Implications of Saturn's Gravity Field and Ring Mass,” <i>Science</i> 364, no. 6445 (2019): eaat2965, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aat2965. [Paywalled]","Sascha Kempf et al., “Micrometeoroid Infall onto Saturn's Rings Constrains Their Age to No More Than a Few Hundred Million Years,” <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 19 (2023): eadf8537, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adf8537. [Open access]","Ryuki Hyodo, Hidenori Genda, and Gustavo Madeira, “Pollution Resistance of Saturn's Ring Particles during Micrometeoroid Impact,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 18 (2025): 44–49, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-024-01598-9. [Open access]","Christopher R. Mankovich and Jim Fuller, “A Diffuse Core in Saturn Revealed by Ring Seismology,” <i>Nature Astronomy</i> 5 (2021): 1103–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41550-021-01448-3, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13385. [Open access]","Matthew S. Tiscareno, “Planetary Rings,” in <i>Planets, Stars and Stellar Systems</i>, vol. 3, ed. Paul Kalas and Linda French (Dordrecht: Springer, 2013), 309–75, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-007-5606-9_7, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.3305. [Open access]","S. Charnoz, R. M. Canup, A. Crida, and L. Dones, “The Origin of Planetary Ring Systems,” in <i>Planetary Ring Systems</i>, ed. Matthew S. Tiscareno and Carl D. Murray (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2018), 517–38, https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316286791.018, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.09741. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do Saturn's rings have gaps?","a":"Mostly because of resonances with Saturn's moons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A resonance occurs when two orbital periods form an exact ratio such as 1:2, and a particle at the inner edge of the Cassini Division would circle Saturn exactly twice for each orbit of the moon Mimas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Repeated tugs from Mimas, always in the same direction, push such particles into new orbits, so over long periods the gap has been emptied of ring material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the cleanness of the rings matter for their age?","a":"Because dust from outside the Saturn system falls on them continually and should darken them over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rings are mostly water ice with only about 0.1 to 2 per cent of other material, and Cassini's measurements of the incoming dust imply an exposure time of no more than about 100 to 400 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That argument assumes the dust sticks, and simulations suggest instead that much of it is vaporised and lost, so the rings could be older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did Cassini weigh the rings?","a":"By flying between them and the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the last phase of the mission the spacecraft dived through the gap at altitudes of 2,600 to 3,900 kilometres (1,600 to 2,400 miles) above the cloud tops, while a radio link with Earth tracked how the gravity of planet and rings tugged on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The total came to about 0.41 times the mass of the moon Mimas, which pointed to rings formed perhaps 10 to 100 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Rings of Saturn"},
    "astro-150": {"questions":["Each particle in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> follows its own orbit like a tiny moon, so the inner particles overtake the outer ones and the whole never turns as a solid body.","Found around all four giant planets, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has also been detected around the distant dwarf planet Quaoar, whose two such bands lie beyond the zone where tides should stop a moon forming."],"abstract":"A <b>planetary ring</b> is a flat band of countless small particles of ice, rock or dust, each following its own orbit around a planet or smaller body like a tiny moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All four giant planets have rings, but the systems differ greatly, with broad, bright, icy rings at Saturn, narrow, dark ones at Uranus and Neptune, and a faint band of dust at Jupiter, perhaps kicked up as meteoroids strike its small inner moons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Rings are not confined to planets, for they have also been found around the dwarf planets Haumea and Quaoar and around at least one asteroid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because each particle obeys Kepler's laws, inner particles travel faster than outer ones, so a ring does not turn as a solid body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most rings lie close to their planet, within the distance at which tides can tear bodies apart or stop loose particles from gathering into a moon, although the rings of Quaoar lie farther out than that limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A ring is flat because the planet's spin gives it an equatorial bulge, which sets a preferred plane, and collisions between particles then damp out any motion across that plane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those collisions lose energy but keep the total angular momentum, so a swarm of debris settles into a flat, circular disc, the lowest-energy state open to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dense rings are therefore exceptionally cold and thin, with a thickness as little as a ten-millionth of their width, and left alone a thin disc of small particles gradually spreads and dissipates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moons sculpt rings, clearing gaps through resonances, confining narrow rings between shepherding moons and raising waves like the wake of a ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How rings form is still unsettled, and they may come from several processes, including giant collisions, the tidal break-up of moons or comets, and material left over from planet formation itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.5 Planetary Rings and Enceladus,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-5-planetary-rings-and-enceladus. [Open access]","Matthew S. Tiscareno, “Planetary Rings,” in <i>Planets, Stars and Stellar Systems</i>, vol. 3, ed. Paul Kalas and Linda French (Dordrecht: Springer, 2013), 309–75, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-007-5606-9_7, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.3305. [Open access]","H. N. Latter, G. I. Ogilvie, and H. Rein, “Planetary Rings and Other Astrophysical Disks,” in <i>Planetary Ring Systems</i>, ed. Matthew S. Tiscareno and Carl D. Murray (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2018), 549–76, https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316286791.020, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.04312. [Open access]","S. Charnoz, R. M. Canup, A. Crida, and L. Dones, “The Origin of Planetary Ring Systems,” in <i>Planetary Ring Systems</i>, ed. Matthew S. Tiscareno and Carl D. Murray (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2018), 517–38, https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316286791.018, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.09741. [Open access]","C. L. Pereira et al., “The Two Rings of (50000) Quaoar,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 673 (2023): L4, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202346365, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.09237. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Jupiter Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Saturn: Facts,” last updated 15 October 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are planetary rings so flat?","a":"Because collisions remove motion in every direction except around the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A cloud of debris in orbit keeps its total angular momentum but loses energy in inelastic collisions, so it contracts into a flat, circular disc, its lowest-energy state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For rings the preferred plane is set by the planet's equatorial bulge, and once it is established, collisions damp out motions perpendicular to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do rings lie close to their planets?","a":"Because close in, the planet's tides tear bodies apart or keep loose particles from coming together into a moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only really small moons survive inside that stability limit, so material there tends to stay as a ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rule has exceptions, though, for both of the rings found around the distant dwarf planet Quaoar lie outside its classical limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the origin of rings still uncertain?","a":"Because the known rings are too varied for one explanation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some may be the remains of a shattered moon, broken by a collision and spread out by tides, while others may be material that never managed to form a moon in the first place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Proposed processes range from giant collisions and the tidal stripping of comets or moons to planet formation itself, and no single theory explains every ring system in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ring system"},
    "astro-151": {"questions":["Saturn's main rings lie inside the planet's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where tides stop loose particles from gathering into moons, while farther out the same material clumps into small satellites.","Torn into more than 20 pieces by Jupiter's gravity in July 1992, a passing comet had strayed inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before its fragments struck the planet two years later."],"abstract":"The <b>Roche limit</b> is the distance from a planet within which the planet's tides can pull apart a smaller body that is held together only by its own gravity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tide arises because the planet pulls harder on the near side of a moon than on its far side, and inside the limit that difference outstrips the moon's own self-gravity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The limit has no single value, since it depends on the density and internal strength of the body, and a denser object can venture closer to the planet without breaking up than a less dense one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Small solid objects can survive inside it, because they are held together by the strength of their material rather than by gravity, and only really small moons orbit within this zone around each giant planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The limit also works in reverse, since close to a planet tides stop loose particles from gathering into a moon at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That is why the rings of the giant planets lie close to their planets, and why two origins are offered for them: a moon shattered and spread into a disc, or material that was never able to form a moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The limit was seen at work in July 1992, when a comet passed so close to Jupiter that tidal forces tore it into more than 20 pieces, which struck the planet two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One model of Saturn's rings, which are more than 90 per cent water ice, has a large moon spiralling inwards while tides strip away its icy outer layers and leave a ring of almost pure ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Material that spreads outwards past the limit can clump into moons, and simulations reproduce Saturn's small, young moons just outside its main rings in this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The same fate may await Phobos, the larger moon of Mars, whose orbit is shrinking, and one study predicts that its weakest material will be torn into a ring in 20 to 40 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.5 Planetary Rings (and Enceladus),” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-5-planetary-rings-and-enceladus. [Open access]","Matthew S. Tiscareno, “Planetary Rings,” in <i>Planets, Stars and Stellar Systems</i> (Dordrecht: Springer, 2013), 309–75, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-007-5606-9_7, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.3305. [Open access]","“P/Shoemaker-Levy 9,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/p-shoemaker-levy-9/. [Open access]","Benjamin A. Black and Tushar Mittal, “The Demise of Phobos and Development of a Martian Ring System,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 8, no. 12 (2015): 913–17, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo2583. [Paywalled]","Robin M. Canup, “Origin of Saturn’s Rings and Inner Moons by Mass Removal from a Lost Titan-Sized Satellite,” <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7326 (2010): 943–46, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature09661. [Paywalled]","A. Crida and S. Charnoz, “Formation of Regular Satellites from Ancient Massive Rings in the Solar System,” <i>Science</i> 338, no. 6111 (2012): 1196–99, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1226477, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.3808. [Open access]","Sébastien Charnoz, Julien Salmon, and Aurélien Crida, “The Recent Formation of Saturn’s Moonlets from Viscous Spreading of the Main Rings,” <i>Nature</i> 465, no. 7299 (2010): 752–54, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature09096. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a small rock survive inside the Roche limit when a large moon cannot?","a":"Because the limit is set by a balance between the planet's tide and the body's own gravity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A small object can instead be held together by the strength of its material, which the simple calculation leaves out, so it can orbit inside the limit unharmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is why only really small moons are found in the zone close to each giant planet where tides break larger bodies apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do moons form just outside a ring rather than inside it?","a":"Because inside the Roche limit tides keep ring particles from gathering together, while beyond it they are free to clump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When a ring slowly spreads outwards past the limit, the material that crosses it can build moons, and simulations reproduce Saturn's small, young moons just outside its main rings this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Models of the same kind suggest that many regular satellites of the giant planets grew from ancient massive rings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might Mars one day have a ring?","a":"Because its moon Phobos is spiralling slowly inwards and feeling ever stronger tidal stresses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of Phobos seems to be weak, heavily damaged material, and one analysis predicts that tides will disperse this material into a ring in 20 to 40 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Any fragment strong enough to survive would eventually strike Mars in a low, glancing impact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Roche limit"},
    "astro-152": {"questions":["At Uranus, Cordelia and Ophelia circle about 2,000 kilometres (1,200 miles) inside and outside the Epsilon ring as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that stop its dark particles from spreading out.","Narrow planetary rings ought to spread and fade over time, so their sharp edges are explained by small <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whose gravitational nudges push stray particles back into line."],"abstract":"<b>Shepherd moons</b> are small moons whose gravity confines the particles of a planetary ring, keeping a narrow ring from spreading out or holding the edge of a gap in a broad one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Left to themselves, thin discs of small particles gradually spread and dissipate, so narrow rings with sharp edges need some mechanism to counteract that natural spreading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A ring particle that passes a nearby moon receives a small gravitational kick that pushes its orbit away from the moon's, so a moon on each side of a ringlet can hem it in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The idea was proposed for the rings of Uranus before any shepherd had been seen, with a pair of small satellites roughly 500 kilometres (300 miles) on either side of the Epsilon ring to hold it in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A single moon can also keep a gap open inside a broad ring, as Pan does within the Encke Gap of Saturn's A ring, raising waves and stripes called wakes in the ring material on either side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The clearest pairs are Prometheus and Pandora on either side of Saturn's narrow F ring, and Cordelia and Ophelia, which orbit about 2,000 kilometres (1,200 miles) inside and outside the Epsilon ring of Uranus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Prometheus, a potato-shaped and probably porous icy body, constrains the inner edge of the F ring and circles Saturn every 14.7 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Each close approach of Prometheus draws out a streamer of ring material and leaves behind a channel, which orbital motion then shears into a slanting dark lane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has never been shown conclusively how the two moons hold the F ring in place, and they appear to stir it up at least as much as they confine it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The other narrow rings of Uranus and Neptune should need shepherds too, but none has been found, and the failed searches suggest that shepherding may not be the main way those rings are confined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.5 Planetary Rings (and Enceladus),” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-5-planetary-rings-and-enceladus. [Open access]","Matthew S. Tiscareno, “Planetary Rings,” in <i>Planets, Stars and Stellar Systems</i> (Dordrecht: Springer, 2013), 309–75, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-007-5606-9_7, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.3305. [Open access]","Peter Goldreich and Scott Tremaine, “Towards a Theory for the Uranian Rings,” <i>Nature</i> 277, no. 5692 (1979): 97–99, https://doi.org/10.1038/277097a0. [Paywalled]","Carl D. Murray et al., “How Prometheus Creates Structure in Saturn’s F Ring,” <i>Nature</i> 437, no. 7063 (2005): 1326–29, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04212. [Paywalled]","“Prometheus,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/prometheus/. [Open access]","“Cordelia,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/uranus/moons/cordelia/. [Open access]","“Pan,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/pan/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do narrow rings need shepherds at all?","a":"Because a thin ring of colliding particles does not stay thin by itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Left alone, a disc of small particles gradually spreads and dissipates, so a narrow ring with sharp edges needs something to counteract that spreading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Moons on either side can do this by giving nearby particles small gravitational kicks that push their orbits away from the moons' own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Saturn's F ring so twisted and lumpy?","a":"Because its shepherds disturb it as well as guard it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each time Prometheus makes its closest approach it draws out a streamer of particles, and one orbit later the disturbed region has been sheared into a channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Prometheus passes the ring every 14.7 hours, so the pattern is renewed again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some scientists doubt that shepherds explain every narrow ring?","a":"Because most of the moons the theory needs have never been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shepherds for the other narrow rings of Uranus and Neptune would be about 10 kilometres (6 miles) across, at the limit of what the Voyager cameras could detect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The failed searches have become significant enough that shepherding may not be the main way those rings are confined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Shepherd moon"},
    "astro-153": {"questions":["Because the six corners of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have kept a steady rotation period of about 10 hours 39 minutes for years, the feature may reveal how fast the planet's deep interior turns.","First imaged by the Voyager probes in 1980 and 1981, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> encloses a vast polar storm with an eye about 50 times larger than a typical hurricane eye on Earth."],"abstract":"<b>Saturn's hexagon</b> is a six-sided jet stream that circles the planet's north pole, a wave pattern in the clouds whose sides are each longer than the diameter of Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Clouds whip along it like cars on a racetrack, and the whole pattern measures nearly 25,000 kilometres (15,000 miles) across, wide enough to hold almost four Earths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Voyager spacecraft first imaged it in 1980 and 1981, and its reappearance in Cassini images a quarter of a century later showed that it is a long-lived feature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It reaches some 100 kilometres (60 miles) below the cloud tops, deeper into the atmosphere than had been expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At its centre spins a huge storm with an eye about 50 times larger than a typical hurricane eye on Earth, and the jet acts as a barrier, with different haze particles inside it and outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its six corners turn with a steady period of 10 hours 39 minutes 23 seconds, a rate that held unchanged through the seasonal swings in sunlight between 2008 and 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pattern is interpreted as a Rossby wave, a planet-scale meander of the kind also seen in Earth's polar jet stream, trapped on a narrow eastward jet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Because it has survived strong seasonal changes unaltered, the hexagon is thought to be rooted deep in the atmosphere, and its period may reveal the true rotation of Saturn's interior, which is still uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its influence reaches upwards too, since the warm vortex that forms high in the stratosphere as northern summer approaches has a hexagonal edge some 300 kilometres (190 miles) above the clouds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Saturn's south pole had no such shape for decades, but a ten-sided wave has been seen encircling it in observations made since 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.3 Atmospheres of the Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-3-atmospheres-of-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","“Cassini Images Bizarre Hexagon on Saturn,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/missions/cassini/cassini-images-bizarre-hexagon-on-saturn/. [Open access]","“In Full View: Saturn’s Streaming Hexagon,” NASA Photojournal, 4 December 2013, https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/in-full-view-saturns-streaming-hexagon/. [Open access]","A. Sánchez-Lavega et al., “The Long-Term Steady Motion of Saturn’s Hexagon and the Stability of Its Enclosed Jet Stream under Seasonal Changes,” <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 41, no. 5 (2014): 1425–31, https://doi.org/10.1002/2013GL059078, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.06371. [Open access]","L. N. Fletcher et al., “A Hexagon in Saturn’s Northern Stratosphere Surrounding the Emerging Summertime Polar Vortex,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 9 (2018): 3564, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-06017-3. [Open access]","“Saturn: Facts,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/facts/. [Open access]","“Saturn’s Famous Hexagon May Tower Above the Clouds,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/saturn/saturns-famous-hexagon-may-tower-above-the-clouds/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why might the hexagon reveal how fast Saturn really spins?","a":"Because the rotation of Saturn's interior is still uncertain, and the hexagon appears to be anchored deep in the atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its corners have kept a steady period of 10 hours 39 minutes 23 seconds, unchanged through the long polar night and the return of sunlight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A feature that survives such seasonal swings is likely to be deep-rooted, so its period could reflect the true rotation of the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a jet stream take a six-sided shape?","a":"Because the jet carries a wave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pattern is interpreted as a Rossby wave, a large meander of the kind also seen in Earth's polar jet stream, trapped on Saturn's narrow eastward polar jet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Why it settles into exactly six sides is still not settled, and the feature remains one of the planet's open puzzles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the air inside the hexagon differ from the air outside?","a":"Because the jet stream acts as a barrier that limits mixing across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cassini images taken through several filters showed fewer large haze particles and more small ones inside the hexagon, and the reverse outside, a contrast NASA likens to the ozone hole over Antarctica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A storm with an eye about 50 times larger than a typical hurricane eye on Earth sits at the pole itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Saturn's hexagon"},
    "astro-154": {"questions":["A blue-green colour comes from methane absorbing red sunlight, and beneath that haze <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is mostly a hot, dense fluid of water, methane and ammonia over a small rocky core.","With an atmosphere that dips to 49 K, colder in places than Neptune's, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives off almost no measurable heat from its interior, unlike the other giant planets."],"abstract":"<b>Uranus</b> is the seventh planet from the Sun, an ice giant about four times wider than Earth that orbits at about 19 astronomical units and takes about 84 years to circle the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has about 14.5 times the mass of Earth and a mean density of 1.27 grams per cubic centimetre, the second lowest of any planet after Saturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of its mass, 80 per cent or more, is thought to be a hot, dense fluid of water, methane and ammonia over a small rocky core, though its exact make-up is still uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its atmosphere is mostly hydrogen and helium, its methane absorbs red light and gives the planet its blue-green colour, and it can fall to 49 K, colder in places than Neptune's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has 13 faint rings and more than two dozen known moons as of November 2026, named after characters from the works of Shakespeare and Alexander Pope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Uranus was the first planet found with a telescope, on 13 March 1781, when an observer studying small stars near the constellation Gemini noticed one that looked larger than the rest and suspected a comet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its disc, visible even through a fairly low-powered telescope, set it apart from the stars, and within about two years it was accepted as a planet and named after the Greek god of the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unlike Jupiter, Saturn and Neptune, it gives off no measurable heat from its interior, which leaves its atmosphere very stable and almost featureless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One proposed cause is a giant impact early in its history, which simulations suggest could have left a hot shell of material deep inside that holds the heat in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Voyager 2 flew past in 1986, when the planet's axis, tilted by about 98 degrees, had its south pole pointing almost straight at the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["“Uranus: Facts,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/uranus/facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Uranus Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 2 October 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/uranusfact.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.3 Atmospheres of the Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-3-atmospheres-of-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","William Herschel, “Account of a Comet,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London</i> 71 (1781): 492–501, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstl.1781.0056. [Open access]","Ravit Helled, Nadine Nettelmann, and Tristan Guillot, “Uranus and Neptune: Origin, Evolution and Internal Structure,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 216, no. 3 (2020): 38, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-020-00660-3, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.04891. [Open access]","J. J. O’Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Mathematical Discovery of Planets,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, September 1996, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Neptune_and_Pluto/. [Open access]","E. C. Stone and E. D. Miner, “The Voyager 2 Encounter with the Uranian System,” <i>Science</i> 233, no. 4759 (1986): 39–43, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.233.4759.39. [Paywalled]","J. A. Kegerreis et al., “Consequences of Giant Impacts on Early Uranus for Rotation, Internal Structure, Debris, and Atmospheric Erosion,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 861, no. 1 (2018): 52, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/aac725, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.07083. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the discoverer of Uranus first think it was a comet?","a":"Because it did not look like an ordinary star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Searching among the small stars near the constellation Gemini on 13 March 1781, the observer saw one that appeared visibly larger than the rest and suspected a comet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its disc showed even in a fairly low-powered telescope, and after about two years it was accepted as a new planet, the first found with a telescope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Uranus look so bland compared with Jupiter?","a":"Because it has almost no internal heat to stir its atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Without heat rising from inside, there is little up-and-down movement, and the atmosphere stays very stable with little visible structure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The methane in its atmosphere absorbs red sunlight, which leaves the planet a nearly uniform blue-green.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Uranus called an ice giant?","a":"Because most of it is thought to be made of the materials astronomers call ices, such as water, methane and ammonia, rather than of hydrogen and helium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These form a hot, dense fluid over a small rocky core and make up 80 per cent or more of the planet's mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The label is not certain, since the gravity data can also be matched by interiors much richer in rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Uranus"},
    "astro-155": {"questions":["A collision with a massive body early in the Solar System's history is one proposed explanation for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which leaves the planet rolling around the Sun on its side.","When Voyager 2 arrived in 1986 the south pole pointed straight at the Sun, a consequence of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which keeps one hemisphere sunlit for about 21 years at a time."],"abstract":"The <b>tilt of Uranus</b> is the angle of about 98 degrees between the planet's rotation axis and the perpendicular to its orbit, so that Uranus effectively orbits the Sun lying on its side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its equator is nearly at a right angle to its orbit, so the planet seems to roll around the Sun like a ball, and its rings and moons follow along, orbiting around its equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Uranus is also one of only two planets, with Venus, that rotate in the opposite direction to most of the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its year lasts about 84 Earth years, and for nearly a quarter of each orbit the Sun shines almost directly over one pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The other giant planets are far less tilted, since Jupiter's axis leans by only 3 degrees, Saturn's by 27 and Neptune's by 29.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The result is the most extreme seasons in the Solar System, with about 21 years of sunlit summer in one hemisphere while the other is dark, and some 42 years of light followed by 42 of night at a pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Voyager 2 arrived in 1986 the south pole was facing straight at the Sun, and in the following 21-year season the Sun shone on the equator, giving both hemispheres day and night as the planet turned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The massive, heat-storing atmosphere smooths out these swings, so its winds still circle parallel to the equator, and the dark winter side was even measured a few degrees warmer than the sunlit one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cause of the tilt is unknown, but a giant collision early in the planet's history is often proposed, and simulations show that one can tip the axis and throw enough material into orbit to form a disc of debris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Any such impact must also explain why the main moons orbit in the planet's equator in the same direction as it spins, which one model achieves if Uranus was already moderately tilted before the final blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.3 Atmospheres of the Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-3-atmospheres-of-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","“Uranus: Facts,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/uranus/facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Uranus Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 2 October 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/uranusfact.html. [Open access]","J. A. Kegerreis et al., “Consequences of Giant Impacts on Early Uranus for Rotation, Internal Structure, Debris, and Atmospheric Erosion,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 861, no. 1 (2018): 52, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/aac725, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.07083. [Open access]","A. Morbidelli et al., “Explaining Why the Uranian Satellites Have Equatorial Prograde Orbits despite the Large Planetary Obliquity,” <i>Icarus</i> 219, no. 2 (2012): 737–40, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2012.03.025, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.4685. [Open access]","Ravit Helled, Nadine Nettelmann, and Tristan Guillot, “Uranus and Neptune: Origin, Evolution and Internal Structure,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 216, no. 3 (2020): 38, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-020-00660-3, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.04891. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the sunlit summer side of Uranus not much warmer than its winter side?","a":"Because the atmosphere is so massive that it stores a great deal of heat and evens out the swings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The alternating 42-year periods of sunlight and darkness at the poles have little effect on its circulation, which still runs parallel to the equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Voyager measurements even found the dark winter side a few degrees warmer than the hemisphere facing the Sun, a result still not fully understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the moons of Uranus orbit around its tilted equator?","a":"Because, in one model, they formed after the planet had been tipped over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A disc of material around Uranus at the time of the impact would have precessed around the new equator and collapsed into a thin equatorial disc, from which the moons grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Simulations of giant impacts show that a collision can also place enough debris in orbit to make such a disc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the seasons on Uranus each about 21 years long?","a":"Because its year lasts about 84 Earth years and its axis lies almost in the plane of its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For roughly a quarter of the orbit one pole points towards the Sun, then the Sun shines on the equator for another quarter, and then the other pole takes its turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At a pole this means about 42 years of light followed by 42 years of darkness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-156": {"questions":["Although slightly smaller across than Uranus, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is denser and more massive, and unlike its neighbour it still gives off a measurable amount of heat from its interior.","Flying past in August 1989, Voyager 2 found that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a blue atmosphere with dark storms, a highly tilted magnetic field and faint rings around it."],"abstract":"<b>Neptune</b> is the eighth and most distant planet from the Sun, an ice giant about four times wider than Earth that orbits at about 30 astronomical units and takes about 165 years to complete one orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the only planet in the Solar System not visible to the naked eye, and sunlight there is roughly 900 times fainter than on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Slightly smaller than Uranus but about 17 times as massive as Earth, it is the densest of the giant planets, about 30 per cent denser than Uranus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Most of its mass is a hot, dense fluid of water, methane and ammonia above a small rocky core, and an ocean of very hot water may lie beneath its clouds, kept from boiling away by enormous pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its atmosphere is mostly hydrogen and helium with a little methane, which gives the planet its blue colour, and it has more than a dozen known moons as of November 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Neptune was located in 1846 by calculating where an unseen planet must be to explain the irregular motion of Uranus, and it was found on the first night of searching at the Berlin Observatory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> It had already been recorded, without being recognised, as a fixed star in 1612 and 1613 and several more times in the centuries that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unlike Uranus, Neptune gives off heat from its interior, which drives rising currents of gas and forms bright clouds about 75 kilometres (47 miles) above the main methane cloud layer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its winds exceed 2,000 kilometres per hour (1,200 miles per hour), the strongest of any planet, yet they are confined to a weather layer no more than about 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Voyager 2 flew past in August 1989 and found a highly tilted magnetic field, faint rings and six new moons, all orbiting inside the backwards orbit of Triton, a large moon that Neptune probably captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“Neptune: Facts,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Neptune Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 3 October 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/neptunefact.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.3 Atmospheres of the Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-3-atmospheres-of-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","E. C. Stone and E. D. Miner, “The Voyager 2 Encounter with the Neptunian System,” <i>Science</i> 246, no. 4936 (1989): 1417–21, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.246.4936.1417. [Paywalled]","Ravit Helled, Nadine Nettelmann, and Tristan Guillot, “Uranus and Neptune: Origin, Evolution and Internal Structure,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 216, no. 3 (2020): 38, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-020-00660-3, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.04891. [Open access]","Yohai Kaspi et al., “Atmospheric Confinement of Jet Streams on Uranus and Neptune,” <i>Nature</i> 497, no. 7449 (2013): 344–47, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12131. [Paywalled]","J. J. O’Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Mathematical Discovery of Planets,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, September 1996, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Neptune_and_Pluto/. [Open access]","Davor Krajnović, “The Contrivance of Neptune,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Geophysics</i> 57, no. 5 (2016): 5.28–5.34, https://doi.org/10.1093/astrogeo/atw183, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.06424. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Neptune so windy when it gets so little sunlight?","a":"Because its weather is driven partly from inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neptune has an internal heat source, and the convection currents it powers carry warm gas up through the atmosphere and form high clouds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even so, its winds of more than 2,000 kilometres per hour (1,200 miles per hour), under sunlight about 900 times fainter than at Earth, are striking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Neptune thought to have captured its largest moon?","a":"Because Triton circles Neptune in the opposite direction to the planet's rotation, the only large moon in the Solar System to do so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That backwards orbit suggests Triton may once have been an independent object that Neptune captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Voyager 2 found six smaller moons, by contrast, in circular forward orbits well inside Triton's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Neptune look a deeper blue in many famous pictures than it really is?","a":"Because the Voyager team enhanced the 1989 images to bring out clouds and other features.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the images were reprocessed in 2024, Neptune and Uranus looked much more alike than many people had thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both planets get their blue from methane in their atmospheres, which absorbs other colours and reflects blue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Neptune"},
    "astro-157": {"questions":["Gravity measurements show that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are confined to a thin weather layer no more than about 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) deep rather than reaching far into the planet.","Despite sunlight about 900 times fainter than at Earth, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> blow up to three times harder than Jupiter's, driving clouds of frozen methane around the planet."],"abstract":"The <b>winds of Neptune</b> are the strong east–west jet streams of its atmosphere, which carry clouds around the planet in bands that change with latitude, much as on Jupiter and Saturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As on the other giant planets, air flows between regions of high and low pressure and is deflected by the planet's rotation, and tracking cloud features lets astronomers measure the winds' speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These speeds are measured relative to the rotation of the planet's interior, which for Neptune is itself uncertain, since its period comes from radio and magnetic data that may not reflect the deep interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Neptune is the windiest planet in the Solar System, with winds up to three times stronger than Jupiter's and nine times stronger than Earth's that whip clouds of frozen methane around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fastest winds, near the equator, reach about 2,100 kilometres per hour (1,300 miles per hour) and approach the speed of sound in Neptune's air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That is puzzling because Neptune receives so little energy from the Sun, which shines there about 900 times more faintly than on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Part of the answer is heat from within, since Neptune, unlike Uranus, has an internal energy source whose convection currents lift warm gas high enough to form bright clouds above the main methane layer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The winds also carry great storms, and in 1989 Voyager 2 photographed the Great Dark Spot, a storm nearly 10,000 kilometres (6,200 miles) long that had vanished by the mid-1990s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Gravity measurements limit how deep the jets go, confining them to a thin weather layer no more than about 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) deep, within the outermost 0.2 per cent of the planet's mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two explanations for such jets have competed, one driving them by shallow processes near the top of the atmosphere and the other by motions deep in the planet, and this depth limit restricts how far any deep motion can reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.3 Atmospheres of the Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-3-atmospheres-of-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","“Neptune: Facts,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/facts/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Neptune Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 3 October 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/neptunefact.html. [Open access]","Yohai Kaspi et al., “Atmospheric Confinement of Jet Streams on Uranus and Neptune,” <i>Nature</i> 497, no. 7449 (2013): 344–47, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12131. [Paywalled]","Ravit Helled, Nadine Nettelmann, and Tristan Guillot, “Uranus and Neptune: Origin, Evolution and Internal Structure,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 216, no. 3 (2020): 38, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-020-00660-3, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.04891. [Open access]","E. C. Stone and E. D. Miner, “The Voyager 2 Encounter with the Neptunian System,” <i>Science</i> 246, no. 4936 (1989): 1417–21, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.246.4936.1417. [Paywalled]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are Neptune's winds so strong when the planet is so far from the Sun?","a":"Because sunlight is not the only source of energy for its weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neptune has an internal heat source, unlike Uranus, and the convection currents it powers carry warm gas upwards through the atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On all the giants except Uranus, heat from inside supplies about as much energy to the atmosphere as sunlight does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can gravity reveal how deep the winds go?","a":"Because winds that reached deep into the planet would alter its gravity field in a measurable way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The measured fourth-order harmonic of Neptune's gravity field, a fine detail of its shape, allows the winds to involve only the outermost 0.2 per cent of the planet's mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Deeper winds would have changed that harmonic by more than any interior model fitting the other gravity data allows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are Neptune's wind speeds hard to pin down exactly?","a":"Because winds on the giant planets are measured relative to the rotation of the interior, which cannot be seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neptune's rotation period of 16.11 hours comes from Voyager 2's radio and magnetic data, and those periods may not represent the rotation of the deep interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A different period would shift every measured wind speed, which is why one study proposed periods that make the winds as weak as possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-158": {"questions":["Two astronomers at the Berlin Observatory needed less than 30 minutes on the night of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to spot, with a newly printed chart, a star that was missing from the map.","Recorded as a fixed star through a small telescope in 1612 and 1613, the planet waited more than two centuries for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which turned an anomaly in an orbit into a new world."],"abstract":"The <b>discovery of Neptune</b> in 1846 made it the first planet located through mathematical prediction rather than through ordinary searching of the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Uranus, found in 1781, refused to follow its calculated orbit even after the pulls of Jupiter and Saturn were allowed for, and by 1840 the gap between where it was and where it should be had reached about 0.03 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some astronomers suspected that the law of gravity weakened over great distances, while others argued that an unseen planet beyond Uranus was pulling it off course.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Finding such a planet was new ground, since the theory of gravitation had never before been used to predict the position of a body from the effects of its gravity on another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two mathematicians took up the problem independently, John Couch Adams in England and Urbain Le Verrier in France, and neither knew that the other was working on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Le Verrier's final prediction reached the Berlin Observatory, and on 23 September 1846 Johann Galle and his assistant Heinrich d'Arrest searched the region with a newly printed star chart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In less than 30 minutes they found an eighth-magnitude star missing from the chart, less than a degree from the predicted position, and the next night confirmed that it had moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The finding was celebrated for confirming the generality of Newton's laws, though a long dispute followed over how much credit the English calculations deserved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both predictions were also lucky, since their orbits matched the real planet only around the 1840s, and the planet had been recorded long before without being recognised, first as a fixed star in 1612 and 1613.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neptune orbits the Sun at about 30 astronomical units and needs 165 years for each circuit, so it has completed only one full orbit since the night it was found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “3.6 Gravity with More Than Two Bodies,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/3-6-gravity-with-more-than-two-bodies. [Open access]","“Neptune: Facts,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/facts/. [Open access]","J. J. O’Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Mathematical Discovery of Planets,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, September 1996, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Neptune_and_Pluto/. [Open access]","Davor Krajnović, “The Contrivance of Neptune,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Geophysics</i> 57, no. 5 (2016): 5.28–5.34, https://doi.org/10.1093/astrogeo/atw183, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.06424. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Neptune Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, last updated 3 October 2024, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/neptunefact.html. [Open access]","William Herschel, “Account of a Comet,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London</i> 71 (1781): 492–501, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstl.1781.0056. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the wandering of Uranus point to an unseen planet?","a":"Because Uranus did not follow the orbit predicted for it, even after the pulls of Jupiter and Saturn were taken into account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1840 the discrepancy had reached about 0.03 degrees, larger than the likely errors in the calculations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rather than abandon the law of gravity, the mathematicians asked what mass and orbit an outer planet would need to produce the observed disturbances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the planet found so quickly once the search began?","a":"Because the prediction was good and the searchers had the right map.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Le Verrier's final paper gave a position and said the planet should show a small disc, and the Berlin astronomers compared the sky with a newly printed chart of that region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within half an hour they found an eighth-magnitude star missing from the chart, less than a degree from where it was predicted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the credit for the discovery disputed?","a":"Because a similar prediction had been made in England, but no search there found the planet first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An observer at Cambridge had recorded the planet during his search in August 1846 without comparing his records closely enough to recognise it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Historians still debate how accurate the English prediction was and when it was made, since the surviving note that gives its position is vaguely dated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Discovery of Neptune"},
    "astro-159": {"questions":["The lopsided magnetic fields of Uranus and Neptune may be generated in deep layers of electrically conducting material, perhaps including <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in which hydrogen ions carry the current.","Laser-driven shock waves that squeezed water to hundreds of gigapascals produced <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which crystallised within nanoseconds into tiny grains of ice with a lattice of oxygen."],"abstract":"<b>Superionic water</b> is a form of water ice, stable only at extreme pressures and temperatures, in which the oxygen atoms lock into a solid crystal lattice while hydrogen ions diffuse through it almost like a liquid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Simulations predicted that it should appear above about 100 gigapascals, roughly a million times the air pressure at Earth's surface, and at temperatures above 2,000 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The moving ions should make it a good conductor of electricity, with an ionic conductivity almost as high as that of a metal, and they raise the melting point of the ice to several thousand kelvin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Experiments with laser-driven shock waves have since found signs of superionic conduction and shown ice melting near 5,000 K at 190 gigapascals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Further shock experiments compressed liquid water to 100 to 400 gigapascals and saw it crystallise within a few nanoseconds into tiny grains of superionic ice, including a new phase named ice XVIII.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Such conditions are expected deep inside Uranus and Neptune, most of whose mass is thought to be water, methane and ammonia, called ices by planetary scientists even though they are hot and fluid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Superionic water is likely to make up a large fraction of these planets, and calculations suggest that it may take more than one crystal form there, with a change between them near 100 gigapascals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both planets have unusual magnetic fields, tilted and offset from their centres and far from a simple north–south pattern, which require an electrically conducting, convecting layer beneath their hydrogen-rich outer envelopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One model generates such fields in a thin convecting shell above a stable, non-convecting interior, and water in an ionic state has been suggested as the conducting material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The picture is not settled, since the gravity data can also be matched by interiors rich in rock, and other conductors such as compressed silicates mixed with hydrogen could also produce a field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Marius Millot et al., “Experimental Evidence for Superionic Water Ice Using Shock Compression,” <i>Nature Physics</i> 14, no. 3 (2018): 297–302, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-017-0017-4. [Paywalled]","Marius Millot et al., “Nanosecond X-Ray Diffraction of Shock-Compressed Superionic Water Ice,” <i>Nature</i> 569, no. 7755 (2019): 251–55, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1114-6. [Paywalled]","Hugh F. Wilson, Michael L. Wong, and Burkhard Militzer, “Superionic to Superionic Phase Change in Water: Consequences for the Interiors of Uranus and Neptune,” <i>Physical Review Letters</i> 110, no. 15 (2013): 151102, https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.110.151102, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.6482. [Open access]","Ravit Helled, Nadine Nettelmann, and Tristan Guillot, “Uranus and Neptune: Origin, Evolution and Internal Structure,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 216, no. 3 (2020): 38, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-020-00660-3, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.04891. [Open access]","Sabine Stanley and Jeremy Bloxham, “Convective-Region Geometry as the Cause of Uranus’ and Neptune’s Unusual Magnetic Fields,” <i>Nature</i> 428, no. 6979 (2004): 151–53, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature02376. [Paywalled]","“Neptune: Facts,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/facts/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can superionic water be solid and yet conduct electricity?","a":"Because only part of it is frozen in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oxygen atoms form a rigid crystal lattice, but the hydrogen ions move through the empty spaces in that lattice, diffusing almost as they would in a liquid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those moving charged particles carry an electric current, and simulations predict an ionic conductivity almost as high as that of a metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is superionic water so hard to study in a laboratory?","a":"Because it exists only when water is both extremely dense and extremely hot, at pressures above about 100 gigapascals and temperatures above 2,000 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Confining water in such a state is very challenging, so experiments use laser-driven shock waves that compress and heat a sample, which crystallises within a few nanoseconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> X-ray diffraction in those brief moments revealed the oxygen lattice of the superionic ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the magnetic fields of Uranus and Neptune point to conducting layers inside?","a":"Because a planet's magnetic field is generated by moving, electrically conducting fluid inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The fields of Uranus and Neptune are not dominated by a simple dipole, and models reproduce their shape when the field is made in a thin convecting shell above a stable interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Such a shell must lie beneath the hydrogen-rich outer envelope, and water in an ionic state is one suggested material for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-160": {"questions":["Electric currents in a deep layer of spinning <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are thought to act as a dynamo inside Jupiter, generating a magnetic field 16 to 54 times as strong as Earth's.","Shock experiments found the electrical resistance of liquid hydrogen dropping to that of a liquid metal near 140 gigapascals, evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> thought to fill much of Jupiter."],"abstract":"<b>Metallic hydrogen</b> is hydrogen compressed so strongly that its electrons are no longer firmly bound to their own nuclei and can wander freely, so that it conducts electricity like a metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the pressures inside the giant planets, a few thousand kilometres below the visible clouds of Jupiter and Saturn, hydrogen first turns from a gas into a liquid and then, deeper still, begins to act like a metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Any lattice of hydrogen atoms would have metallic properties, but a calculation published in 1935 found that such a solid needs a density many times that of ordinary solid hydrogen, beyond the pressures then available.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1996 shock experiments measured the electrical resistance of liquid hydrogen falling almost 10,000-fold between 93 and 140 gigapascals, then holding steady at a value typical of a liquid metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Exactly where the change happens in hot, dense fluid hydrogen is still debated, and different experiments and analyses have given conflicting pressures for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In Jupiter the greater part of the interior is liquid metallic hydrogen, beginning perhaps halfway to the centre, while Saturn, being less massive, has only a smaller volume of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Electric currents driven by Jupiter's fast rotation in this layer are thought to act as a dynamo, generating a magnetic field 16 to 54 times as powerful as Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Saturn too is wrapped in liquid metallic hydrogen inside a layer of ordinary liquid hydrogen, and its magnetic field, though smaller than Jupiter's, is still 578 times as powerful as Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Uranus and Neptune are too small to reach the internal pressures needed to liquefy hydrogen in this way, so they lack such a layer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jupiter's core seems to be dilute and partly dissolved, with no clear boundary between it and the metallic hydrogen that surrounds it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","“Jupiter: Facts,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/facts/. [Open access]","“Saturn: Facts,” NASA Science, accessed November 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/facts/. [Open access]","E. Wigner and H. B. Huntington, “On the Possibility of a Metallic Modification of Hydrogen,” <i>Journal of Chemical Physics</i> 3, no. 12 (1935): 764–70, https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1749590. [Paywalled]","S. T. Weir, A. C. Mitchell, and W. J. Nellis, “Metallization of Fluid Molecular Hydrogen at 140 GPa (1.4 Mbar),” <i>Physical Review Letters</i> 76, no. 11 (1996): 1860–63, https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.76.1860, record at https://www.osti.gov/biblio/284424. [Paywalled]","Michael P. Desjarlais, Marcus D. Knudson, and Ronald Redmer, “Comment on ‘Insulator-Metal Transition in Dense Fluid Deuterium,’” <i>Science</i> 363, no. 6433 (2019): eaaw0969, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaw0969, accepted manuscript at https://www.osti.gov/biblio/1507747. [Open access]","David J. Stevenson, “Jupiter’s Interior as Revealed by Juno,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 48 (2020): 465–89, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-081619-052855. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does squeezing hydrogen make it behave like a metal?","a":"Because at extreme pressure the electrons are squeezed off the hydrogen atoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a metal, electrons are not firmly attached to their parent nuclei but can wander, and that freedom is what makes metals such good conductors of electricity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deep inside Jupiter the pressure is great enough for hydrogen to reach this state, which it never does on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Jupiter have such a strong magnetic field?","a":"Because it has a huge volume of electrically conducting fluid that spins rapidly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of Jupiter's interior is liquid metallic hydrogen, and the planet's fast rotation is thought to drive electric currents in it that act as a dynamo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The resulting field is 16 to 54 times as powerful as Earth's, and its magnetosphere balloons 1 to 3 million kilometres (600,000 to 2 million miles) towards the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the pressure of the transition still uncertain?","a":"Because the conditions are extremely hard to reach and to measure in a laboratory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Shock experiments in 1996 saw liquid hydrogen reach the resistance of a liquid metal at about 140 gigapascals and 3,000 K, a continuous change rather than a sudden jump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Later experiments on dense deuterium, a heavier form of hydrogen, have been analysed in conflicting ways, and published comments dispute how their temperatures were worked out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Metallic hydrogen"},
    "astro-161": {"questions":["Jupiter's upper atmosphere is short of both helium and neon, a pattern explained if neon dissolves into helium and is carried down by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the interior while argon stays behind.","Models in which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is strong inside Saturn but modest inside Jupiter predict a helium-rich layer deep in Saturn and a helium-poor atmosphere that a future measurement could test."],"abstract":"<b>Helium rain</b> is the separation of helium from hydrogen predicted deep inside Jupiter and Saturn, where helium forms droplets that sink towards the centre of the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the crushing pressures inside these planets hydrogen becomes a liquid that conducts like a metal, and below a certain temperature helium can no longer stay mixed into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Neither the pressures nor the temperatures are easy to reach in a laboratory, so the conditions for this separation are calculated, and different calculations still place them differently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Because the falling helium sinks through the planet, it releases gravitational energy as heat, so the planet is in effect still separating into layers long after it formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One recent calculation finds that hydrogen and helium can separate across about 27.5 per cent of Jupiter's radius and 48.3 per cent of Saturn's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The firmest evidence comes from Jupiter, where the Galileo probe measured the helium in the atmosphere directly in December 1995 and found less than was present when the Sun formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same probe found Jupiter's atmosphere badly short of neon, and simulations show that neon dissolves readily into helium-rich droplets, so the rain would carry it down while leaving argon untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Helium rain also answers a long-standing puzzle about Saturn, which shines more than 50 per cent brighter than a planet of its age should if it had simply cooled since it formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Models in which helium rains out heavily inside Saturn can supply the missing energy and stretch its cooling to the age of the Solar System, and they predict a helium-rich shell or core deep inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Because those models make different predictions for the helium left in Saturn's atmosphere, measuring that amount is the test that would decide between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","U. von Zahn, D. M. Hunten, and G. Lehmacher, “Helium in Jupiter’s Atmosphere: Results from the Galileo Probe Helium Interferometer Experiment,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 103, no. E10 (1998): 22815–29, https://doi.org/10.1029/98JE00695. [Paywalled]","Hugh F. Wilson and Burkhard Militzer, “Sequestration of Noble Gases in Giant Planet Interiors,” <i>Physical Review Letters</i> 104, no. 12 (2010): 121101, https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.104.121101, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.5940. [Open access]","Jonathan J. Fortney and Nadine Nettelmann, “The Interior Structure, Composition, and Evolution of Giant Planets,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 152 (2010): 423–47, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-009-9582-x, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.0533. [Open access]","Christopher R. Mankovich and Jonathan J. Fortney, “Evidence for a Dichotomy in the Interior Structures of Jupiter and Saturn from Helium Phase Separation,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 889, no. 1 (2020): 51, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ab6210, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.01009. [Open access]","S. Howard, S. Müller, and R. Helled, “Evolution of Jupiter and Saturn with Helium Rain,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 689 (2024): A15, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202450629, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.11120. [Open access]","Xiaoju Chang et al., “Theoretical Evidence of H-He Demixing under Jupiter and Saturn Conditions,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 8543, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-52868-4, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13412. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does helium rain make Saturn shine more brightly?","a":"Because sinking helium releases energy. As helium droplets fall through the liquid hydrogen towards the core, they release gravitational energy as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saturn gives off more than 50 per cent more energy than a model of a planet that has simply cooled since it formed would predict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With strong helium rain, models can match Saturn's present brightness at the age of the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does missing neon count as evidence for helium rain?","a":"Because neon follows the helium down. The Galileo probe found Jupiter's atmosphere severely depleted in neon, while argon was not depleted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Simulations show that neon moves readily into helium-rich droplets, so droplets sinking from an immiscible layer would remove it from the atmosphere, but the same process would not affect argon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the rain stronger in Saturn than in Jupiter?","a":"Because Saturn is less massive, and the same separation conditions reach much more of its interior. Models using the same separation conditions for both planets find that helium in Jupiter has separated only modestly, while in Saturn it separates so strongly that a helium-rich shell or core forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One recent calculation finds separation possible across 48.3 per cent of Saturn's radius but 27.5 per cent of Jupiter's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-162": {"questions":["The large moons of Jupiter, Saturn and Uranus each add up to about one ten-thousandth of their planet's mass, a shared figure for the kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that grows in a planet's disc.","Only about a quarter of the outer planets' moons count as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on an orderly orbit, while the rest move backwards or on tilted, stretched paths and were probably captured."],"abstract":"A <b>regular moon</b> is a satellite that circles its planet in the same direction as the planet spins, on a nearly circular orbit close to the plane of the planet's equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Orbits like these mark moons that grew where they are, out of a disc of gas and dust around the young planet, much as the planets grew in the disc around the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most moons of the outer planets are not like this, for only about a quarter follow such orbits, while the rest are irregular, moving backwards or on stretched and tilted paths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The irregular moons mostly lie far from their planets and were probably captured from orbits around the Sun, which is why their paths bear no relation to the planet's equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jupiter's four large moons, Saturn's Titan and six other large moons, and the five largest moons of Uranus are all regular, while Neptune's big moon Triton orbits backwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The giant planets have so many moons because they could gain them in two ways, by growing them in a disc of their own and by capturing passing bodies early in the Solar System's history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saturn alone has 293 confirmed moons as of August 2026, more than any other planet, although most of them are small captured bodies rather than regular moons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regular systems share a striking pattern, for the moons of Jupiter, Saturn and Uranus each add up to about one ten-thousandth of their planet's mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One model explains this as a balance in the planet's disc between fresh material flowing in to feed the moons and moons lost by spiralling into the planet, so that their total settles at that fraction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Simulations of moons growing in such a disc can also reproduce the way Jupiter's inner large moons are locked into orbits whose periods stand in simple ratios.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.1 Ring and Moon Systems Introduced,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-1-ring-and-moon-systems-introduced. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.2 The Galilean Moons of Jupiter,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-2-the-galilean-moons-of-jupiter. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Saturn Moons,” updated September 22, 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/. [Open access]","David Jewitt and Nader Haghighipour, “Irregular Satellites of the Planets: Products of Capture in the Early Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 45 (2007): 261–95, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.astro.44.051905.092459, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0703059. [Open access]","Robin M. Canup and William R. Ward, “A Common Mass Scaling for Satellite Systems of Gaseous Planets,” <i>Nature</i> 441, no. 7095 (2006): 834–39, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04860. [Paywalled]","Masahiro Ogihara and Shigeru Ida, “N-Body Simulations of Satellite Formation around Giant Planets: Origin of Orbital Configuration of the Galilean Moons,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 753, no. 1 (2012): 60, https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-637X/753/1/60, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.0301. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a backward or tilted orbit suggest a captured moon?","a":"Because a moon that grew in its planet's disc shares the disc's motion, circling in the direction of the planet's spin close to its equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Irregular moons instead move backwards or on steeply tilted, stretched orbits, mostly far from the planet, the pattern expected of bodies picked up from orbits around the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the giant planets have so many more moons than the rocky planets?","a":"Because they had two routes to them. Their regular moons grew in discs around the young planets, and their irregular moons were captured from orbits around the Sun early in the Solar System's history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saturn alone has 293 confirmed moons as of August 2026, more than any other planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might the regular moons add up to the same share of each planet's mass?","a":"Because one model finds that the share is set by a balance. As a giant planet grows, material flowing into its disc feeds the moons, while the gas drags moons inwards until they are lost into the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two processes hold the total mass of the moons at about one ten-thousandth of the planet's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Regular moon"},
    "astro-163": {"questions":["A star seen to blink out several times on 10 March 1977, before and after the planet itself covered it, revealed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> long before any camera had photographed them.","Two small shepherd moons, Cordelia and Ophelia, hold in place the Epsilon ring, the outermost and most massive of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that circle a planet tilted on its side."],"abstract":"The <b>rings of Uranus</b> are a system of 13 known rings around the planet, most of them narrow and dark, tilted at 98° like Uranus itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every giant planet has rings, but the four systems differ greatly in mass and make-up, and those of Uranus and Neptune are nearly the reverse of Saturn's broad, bright, icy sheets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rings of Uranus are narrow ribbons with broad empty gaps between them, and most of them are less than 10 kilometres (6 miles) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their particles are nearly as black as coal, probably rich in carbon compounds, and reflect only about 5 per cent of the light that falls on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main rings are made almost entirely of particles from centimetres to metres across with little or no fine dust, although two faint outer rings found much later are dusty, one reddish and the other blue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The rings were found on 10 March 1977, when Uranus passed in front of a star and the star dimmed briefly several times before the planet reached it and again afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each dip marked a ring crossing the starlight, so the rings were mapped in detail before anyone had seen them directly, and Voyager 2 photographed two more in 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The outermost and most massive, the Epsilon ring, lies about 51,000 kilometres (32,000 miles) from the planet, is about 100 kilometres (60 miles) wide and may hold as much mass as all the others together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is held in place by two small shepherd moons, Cordelia and Ophelia, which orbit about 2,000 kilometres (1,200 miles) inside and outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other narrow rings are shaped by moons too, and the slight three-lobed wobble of the Eta ring, set by a resonance with the moon Cressida, has been used to weigh that moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.1 Ring and Moon Systems Introduced,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-1-ring-and-moon-systems-introduced. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.5 Planetary Rings and Enceladus,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-5-planetary-rings-and-enceladus. [Open access]","J. L. Elliot, E. Dunham, and D. Mink, “The Rings of Uranus,” <i>Nature</i> 267, no. 5609 (1977): 328–30, https://doi.org/10.1038/267328a0. [Paywalled]","Imke de Pater et al., “New Dust Belts of Uranus: One Ring, Two Ring, Red Ring, Blue Ring,” <i>Science</i> 312, no. 5770 (2006): 92–94, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1125110. [Paywalled]","Edward M. Molter et al., “Thermal Emission from the Uranian Ring System,” <i>Astronomical Journal</i> 158, no. 1 (2019): 47, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ab258c, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.12566. [Open access]","Robert O. Chancia, Matthew M. Hedman, and Richard G. French, “Weighing Uranus’ Moon Cressida with the η Ring,” <i>Astronomical Journal</i> 154, no. 4 (2017): 153, https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/aa880e, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.07566. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Uranus: Facts,” updated October 15, 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/uranus/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How could the rings be mapped before anyone had seen them?","a":"By watching a star pass behind them. On 10 March 1977 Uranus moved in front of a star, and the star dimmed briefly several times before and after the planet itself covered it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each dip marked a narrow ring crossing the starlight, which let the rings be mapped in detail although they could not be photographed from Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the rings stay so narrow?","a":"Because small moons hold them in. The Epsilon ring is shepherded by Cordelia and Ophelia, which orbit about 2,000 kilometres (1,200 miles) inside and outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The narrow Eta ring lies just outside a resonance with the moon Cressida, which gives it a slight three-lobed shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the ring particles so dark?","a":"Because they seem to be made largely of carbon and hydrocarbon compounds rather than clean ice. The small inner moons of Uranus have a similar make-up, which suggests that one or more moons broke up to make the rings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main rings are made of particles centimetres to metres across, with little or no fine dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Rings of Uranus"},
    "astro-164": {"questions":["Proposed in the 19th century to explain the Sun's power, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could have kept the Sun shining for only about 100 million years, though it does power newborn stars for a while.","Even a minuscule shrinkage of a planet as massive as Jupiter releases significant heat, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at work in giant planets that radiate more energy than sunlight brings them."],"abstract":"The <b>Kelvin–Helmholtz mechanism</b> is the release of heat by a large body of gas as it slowly contracts under its own gravity, its outer layers falling inwards and turning gravitational energy into heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was proposed in the 19th century as the power source of the Sun, and a contraction of only about 40 metres (130 feet) a year would supply the Sun's present output.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contraction could keep the Sun shining for only about 100 million years, however, so it cannot be the Sun's main source of energy, although it does power newborn stars for a while.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In giant planets the same process works on a smaller scale, because a body of such mass can generate significant heat by shrinking even a minuscule amount.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Models of a giant planet's history count both its cooling and its continuing gravitational contraction among the sources of the heat it radiates into space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Jupiter, Saturn and Neptune all give off more heat than sunlight alone can account for, so their interiors and atmospheres are warmer than the Sun's heating would make them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jupiter's internal source amounts to about 400 million billion watts, roughly equal to the sunlight it absorbs, and most of it is primordial heat left over from the planet's formation 4.5 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Measurements from the Cassini spacecraft later put Jupiter's internal heat at about 7.5 watts for each square metre of the planet, well above the previous best estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Saturn produces about twice as much internal energy as Jupiter for each kilogram of its mass, more than leftover heat can supply, and most of the extra probably comes from helium sinking through its interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Uranus gives off no measurable internal heat, while Neptune radiates about 2.6 times the energy it absorbs from the Sun, and why the two planets differ is not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “16.1 Sources of Sunshine: Thermal and Gravitational Energy,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/16-1-sources-of-sunshine-thermal-and-gravitational-energy. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.2 The Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-2-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","Liming Li et al., “Less Absorbed Solar Energy and More Internal Heat for Jupiter,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 9 (2018): 3709, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-06107-2. [Open access]","Jonathan J. Fortney and Nadine Nettelmann, “The Interior Structure, Composition, and Evolution of Giant Planets,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 152 (2010): 423–47, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-009-9582-x, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.0533. [Open access]","J. C. Pearl and B. J. Conrath, “The Albedo, Effective Temperature, and Energy Balance of Neptune, as Determined from Voyager Data,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i> 96, no. S01 (1991): 18921–30, https://doi.org/10.1029/91JA01087. [Paywalled]","S. Howard, S. Müller, and R. Helled, “Evolution of Jupiter and Saturn with Helium Rain,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 689 (2024): A15, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202450629, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.11120. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can't contraction be what powers the Sun today?","a":"Because it would run out too soon. A slow contraction of about 40 metres (130 feet) a year could match the Sun's present output, but over the Sun's whole life contraction could have kept it shining for only about 100 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contraction does matter for a while in newborn stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Saturn give off more heat than models of simple cooling predict?","a":"Because something besides leftover heat is at work. Saturn is over 50 per cent more luminous than a model of a uniform planet cooling since its formation predicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It produces about twice as much internal energy per kilogram as Jupiter, and most of the extra probably comes from helium droplets sinking towards the core and releasing gravitational energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do Uranus and Neptune have almost the same atmospheric temperature?","a":"Because Neptune makes up for its greater distance from the Sun with internal heat. Uranus gives off no measurable internal heat, while Neptune has a small internal source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Voyager measurements show Neptune radiating about 2.6 times the energy it absorbs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Why the two planets differ in internal heat is not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kelvin–Helmholtz mechanism"},
    "astro-165": {"questions":["The Cassini spacecraft watched the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 2010 to 2011 spawn the largest vortex ever seen in Saturn's lower atmosphere, before the storm's head ran into it and faded.","Water ice in the clouds of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that erupted on Saturn in December 2010 shows that convection lifted material from about 200 kilometres (120 miles) below the cloud tops."],"abstract":"A <b>Great White Spot</b> is a giant storm that erupts every few decades in Saturn's northern hemisphere, beginning as a small, very bright spot and spreading until it can encircle the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Six have been recorded in about 140 years, in 1876, 1903, 1933, 1960, 1990 and 2010, three at the equator, two at middle latitudes and one near the pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The gaps between them have been about 30 years, close to Saturn's 29.5-year orbit, but the planet's tilt and the shadow of its rings make such storms hard to see from Earth and may bias the record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In each case the bright spot grows to 10,000 to 20,000 kilometres (6,000 to 12,000 miles) across in about ten days and then interacts for months with the winds of its latitude band.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Storms like this belong to the weather of a giant planet, whose atmosphere has no solid surface beneath it to rub against and slow its circulation, so storms can grow bigger than Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The storm of 2010 to 2011 erupted on 5 December 2010, years before the next one was expected, and was the first to be watched by a spacecraft in orbit around Saturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its bright head drifted westwards, faster than the jet stream it erupted in, and measured up to 34,000 kilometres (21,000 miles) from east to west by January 2011, and it spawned the largest vortex ever seen in Saturn's lower atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its clouds held water ice as well as ammonia ice, material that could only have been carried up by powerful convection from about 200 kilometres (120 miles) below the visible clouds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lightning crackled through the storm until its head ran into the new vortex in June 2011, after which the activity faded and was undetectable by early August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The storm also heated the stratosphere above it, leaving a hot air mass and a disturbance that encircled the planet high in Saturn's atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “11.3 Atmospheres of the Giant Planets,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/11-3-atmospheres-of-the-giant-planets. [Open access]","Agustín Sánchez-Lavega et al., “The Great Saturn Storm of 2010–2011,” in <i>Saturn in the 21st Century</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2018), 377–416, https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316227220.013, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.07669. [Open access]","Kunio M. Sayanagi et al., “Dynamics of Saturn’s Great Storm of 2010–2011 from Cassini ISS and RPWS,” <i>Icarus</i> 223, no. 1 (2013): 460–78, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2012.12.013, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.07246. [Open access]","L. A. Sromovsky, K. H. Baines, and P. M. Fry, “Saturn’s Great Storm of 2010–2011: Evidence for Ammonia and Water Ices from Analysis of VIMS Spectra,” <i>Icarus</i> 226, no. 1 (2013): 402–18, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2013.05.043, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.05893. [Open access]","Thierry Fouchet et al., “Stratospheric Aftermath of the 2010 Storm on Saturn as Observed by the TEXES Instrument. I. Temperature Structure,” <i>Icarus</i> 277 (2016): 196–214, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2016.04.030, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.06479. [Open access]","A. Sánchez-Lavega et al., “Deep Winds beneath Saturn’s Upper Clouds from a Seasonal Long-Lived Planetary-Scale Storm,” <i>Nature</i> 475, no. 7354 (2011): 71–74, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature10203. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why might these storms recur about every 30 years?","a":"Because the interval may follow Saturn's seasons. The decades-long gap between storms is probably related to the cycle of sunlight over Saturn's 29.5-year orbit and to how slowly its atmosphere responds to heating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The record is short and biased by how well each hemisphere can be seen from Earth, so any seasonal explanation is still uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the storm of 2010 to 2011 so valuable to study?","a":"Because it was the first to be watched from a spacecraft in orbit around Saturn. It erupted about a decade early, while Cassini was still working there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cassini's cameras followed its birth, growth and end, and its radio instrument tracked the lightning until the storm's head collided with the vortex it had spawned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does water ice in the storm clouds matter?","a":"Because it shows how deep the storm reached. Water ice near the level of the visible clouds could only have been delivered by powerful convection from about 200 kilometres (120 miles) deeper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That supports the view that these storms are driven by moist convection starting in the deep water clouds, about 250 kilometres (155 miles) below the visible cloud tops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Great White Spot"},
    "astro-166": {"questions":["Formed when a body about the size of Mars struck the young Earth and threw debris into orbit, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> we call the Moon gathered from that debris about 4.5 billion years ago.","Every newly found <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first receives a number, and only the most significant later receive names, most of them taken from the myths of many different cultures."],"abstract":"A <b>natural satellite</b>, or moon, is a naturally formed body that orbits a planet, a dwarf planet or a smaller body such as an asteroid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moons are generally solid and few have atmospheres, and they range from worlds larger than the planet Mercury to bodies as small as a sports arena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As of October 2026 there were 461 officially recognised moons of the planets and Pluto, and hundreds more had been confirmed around smaller bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Every newly found moon first receives a number, the most significant receive names, and most are named after figures from the myths of many cultures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moons also let astronomers weigh what they orbit, and only after Charon was discovered in 1978 could the mass of Pluto be measured, which proved far less than Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Earth's Moon probably formed when a body about the size of Mars struck the young Earth, throwing material into orbit that gathered into the Moon about 4.5 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Such giant impacts may have been common late in the growth of rocky planets, so moons made this way could be a normal outcome rather than a rarity, and Pluto's largest moon, Charon, may have formed from the debris of a similar early collision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most planetary moons, however, probably formed from discs of gas and dust around the young planets, and the regular moons of each gas giant add up to about one ten-thousandth of their planet's mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The giant planets hold most of the Solar System's moons, and Saturn alone has 293 confirmed moons as of August 2026, more than any other planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only about a quarter of the outer planets' moons follow regular orbits, while the irregular majority, often on distant, tilted or backward orbits, were probably captured from orbits around the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Moons: Facts,” updated October 13, 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/moons/facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Saturn Moons,” updated September 22, 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.1 Ring and Moon Systems Introduced,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-1-ring-and-moon-systems-introduced. [Open access]","David Jewitt and Nader Haghighipour, “Irregular Satellites of the Planets: Products of Capture in the Early Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 45 (2007): 261–95, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.astro.44.051905.092459, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0703059. [Open access]","Robin M. Canup, “Dynamics of Lunar Formation,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 42 (2004): 441–75, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.astro.41.082201.113457. [Paywalled]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Solar System Dynamics, “Planetary Satellite Discovery Circumstances,” https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/discovery.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.4 Pluto and Charon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-4-pluto-and-charon. [Open access]","Robin M. Canup and William R. Ward, “A Common Mass Scaling for Satellite Systems of Gaseous Planets,” <i>Nature</i> 441, no. 7095 (2006): 834–39, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04860. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Saturn have so many moons?","a":"Because a giant planet gains moons in more than one way. Its regular moons grew in a disc around the young planet, while its irregular moons were captured from orbits around the Sun early in the Solar System's history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most of Saturn's 293 confirmed moons are small, and many are captured strays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might Earth's Moon not be unusual after all?","a":"Because the giant impact that probably made it may have been a common kind of event. Models of how the rocky planets grew suggest that late, giant collisions were frequent, and that impacts making satellites were probably a common process at that stage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Moon gathered from debris thrown into orbit about 4.5 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does a newly found moon get its name?","a":"It is given a number first, and only the most significant moons receive an official name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most are named after characters from the myths of many cultures, and newer moons of Saturn take names from Norse mythology, while the moons of Uranus are named after characters from Shakespeare and Alexander Pope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Natural satellite"},
    "astro-167": {"questions":["Io, Europa and Ganymede, three of the four <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Jupiter, are locked in a resonance in which Io orbits four times and Europa twice for each orbit of Ganymede.","Densest close to Jupiter and icier farther out, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> echo the pattern of the planets around the Sun, a sign of a disc that was warmer near the planet."],"abstract":"The <b>Galilean moons</b> are the four largest moons of Jupiter, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, named after the Italian astronomer Galileo Galilei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were the first moons discovered orbiting a planet other than Earth, and they stand first in the official list of the planet's satellites as moons I to IV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Europa and Io are about the size of Earth's Moon, while Ganymede, the largest moon in the Solar System, and Callisto are about the size of the planet Mercury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their densities fall with distance from Jupiter, from about 3,530 kilograms per cubic metre for Io to 1,830 for Callisto, so the inner two are mostly rock and the outer two are rich in ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That pattern mirrors the planets around the Sun, and it is thought to reflect a disc around the young Jupiter that was warmer close in, where ice could not condense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Galileo first saw three of them on 7 January 1610 as small bright stars in a line near Jupiter, and within days of watching them move he concluded that they were circling the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He called them the Medicean planets, after the powerful Medici family, and numbered them, and the names Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto were officially adopted only in the mid-1800s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He presented them as an answer to critics of the Sun-centred system, for here were four satellites circling Jupiter, as the Moon circles Earth, while the whole system travelled around the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Io, Europa and Ganymede are locked in a resonance in which Io orbits four times and Europa twice for every orbit of Ganymede.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Simulations show that moons growing in a disc with an inner edge are readily caught in such resonances, and the resonance keeps the orbits slightly stretched, which drives the tidal heating of Io and Europa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Galileo Galilei, <i>The Sidereal Messenger</i>, trans. Edward Stafford Carlos (London: Rivingtons, 1880), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/46036. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.1 Ring and Moon Systems Introduced,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-1-ring-and-moon-systems-introduced. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.2 The Galilean Moons of Jupiter,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-2-the-galilean-moons-of-jupiter. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Io: Facts,” updated April 21, 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/io/facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Europa: Facts,” updated April 21, 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/europa/europa-facts/. [Open access]","Masahiro Ogihara and Shigeru Ida, “N-Body Simulations of Satellite Formation around Giant Planets: Origin of Orbital Configuration of the Galilean Moons,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 753, no. 1 (2012): 60, https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-637X/753/1/60, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.0301. [Open access]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Jovian Satellite Fact Sheet,” https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/joviansatfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Solar System Dynamics, “Planetary Satellite Discovery Circumstances,” https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/discovery.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the moons count as evidence for a Sun-centred system?","a":"Because they answered an objection. Some critics found it impossible that the Moon could circle Earth while both travelled around the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Galileo pointed out that here were four satellites circling Jupiter, as the Moon circles Earth, while the whole system made its twelve-year orbit around the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the inner two moons rocky and the outer two icy?","a":"Because their disc was warmer near Jupiter. Denser rock and metal condense first and close in, while icy material condenses only farther out where it is colder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The densities follow that pattern, falling from about 3,530 kilograms per cubic metre for Io to 1,830 for Callisto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The planets around the Sun show the same arrangement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the resonance between Io, Europa and Ganymede matter?","a":"Because it keeps the moons flexing. The three line up again and again at the same points in their orbits, and the repeated small tugs stop the orbits from becoming circular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The changing distance from Jupiter raises tides that heat Io and Europa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Simulations suggest that such resonances arise naturally as moons form in a disc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Galilean moons"},
    "astro-168": {"questions":["Lava erupts so often on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that impact craters are filled before they can build up, and still-warm lava flows cover about a quarter of its surface.","Measurements of tidal flexing made during two Juno flybys showed that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is too rigid for a shallow magma ocean, though tides still drive its hundreds of volcanoes."],"abstract":"<b>Io</b> is the innermost of Jupiter's four large moons and the most volcanically active world in the Solar System, with hundreds of volcanoes, some throwing lava fountains dozens of kilometres high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is slightly larger than Earth's Moon and has nearly the same density, about 3,530 kilograms per cubic metre, so it is a rocky world rather than an icy one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its bright colours come from a thin coating of sulphur and sulphur dioxide, but the volcanism beneath is driven by eruptions of molten silicate rock, as on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its surface renews itself so fast that lava fills impact craters before they can build up, and its thin atmosphere is mostly sulphur dioxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jupiter's magnetic field strips about 1,000 kilograms (2,200 pounds) of Io's material every second, feeding a doughnut-shaped cloud of charged particles around the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The heat comes from tides, because the pulls of Europa and Ganymede keep Io's orbit slightly stretched, so its distance from Jupiter varies and its surface bulges up and down by as much as 100 metres (330 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A prediction published in March 1979 argued that tidal heating should have melted much of Io's interior, and Voyager 1 found active volcanism there when it flew past that same month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Maps of Io show more than 100 recently active volcanoes, including bright ones near both poles, and still-warm lava flows cover about a quarter of its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Magnetic measurements from the Galileo spacecraft were later read as evidence of a global layer of magma beneath Io's crust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Measurements of how much Io flexes, made during two close flybys of the Juno spacecraft, show it is too rigid for a shallow magma ocean and point instead to a mostly solid mantle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.2 The Galilean Moons of Jupiter,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-2-the-galilean-moons-of-jupiter. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Io: Facts,” updated April 21, 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/io/facts/. [Open access]","S. J. Peale, P. Cassen, and R. T. Reynolds, “Melting of Io by Tidal Dissipation,” <i>Science</i> 203, no. 4383 (1979): 892–94, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.203.4383.892. [Paywalled]","M. Pettine et al., “JIRAM Observations of Volcanic Flux on Io: Distribution and Comparison to Tidal Heat Flow Models,” <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 51, no. 17 (2024): e2023GL105782, https://doi.org/10.1029/2023GL105782, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05717. [Open access]","Krishan K. Khurana et al., “Evidence of a Global Magma Ocean in Io’s Interior,” <i>Science</i> 332, no. 6034 (2011): 1186–89, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1201425. [Paywalled]","R. S. Park et al., “Io’s Tidal Response Precludes a Shallow Magma Ocean,” <i>Nature</i> 638, no. 8049 (2025): 69–73, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-08442-5. [Paywalled]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Jovian Satellite Fact Sheet,” https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/joviansatfact.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Io volcanic when Earth's similar-sized Moon is not?","a":"Because Io is heated by tides. Jupiter is more than 300 times as massive as Earth, and Io's slightly eccentric orbit, kept that way by Europa and Ganymede, makes it twist and flex as it moves nearer and farther from the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The flexing heats Io much as bending a wire coat hanger heats the wire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Io look yellow, orange and white?","a":"Because its surface is coated with sulphur compounds. The orange deposits are sulphur and the white is sulphur dioxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When hot silicate lava meets frozen sulphur and sulphur dioxide, the heated deposits drive huge plumes, and the sulphur falls back as coloured snow up to a thousand kilometres (620 miles) from the vent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do scientists now doubt a shallow magma ocean inside Io?","a":"Because Io does not flex enough. A shallow global magma ocean would make its tidal deformation much larger than a mostly solid interior would.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Juno and Galileo tracking data show deformation consistent with a mostly solid mantle, so even strong tidal heating need not create a magma ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Earlier magnetic measurements had been read as evidence for one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Io (moon)"},
    "astro-169": {"questions":["A Galileo flyby in January 2000 found that the magnetic field of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> changes over time, the sign of a conducting liquid ocean beneath its shell of ice.","Launched on 14 October 2024, a NASA spacecraft is to make repeated close flybys of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to learn whether places beneath its icy surface could support life."],"abstract":"<b>Europa</b> is the smallest of Jupiter's four large moons, about 90 per cent the size of Earth's Moon, with a surface of ice over a mainly rocky interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its density of about 3,010 kilograms per cubic metre marks a mostly rocky body, but spectra of the sunlight it reflects show a surface of water ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Like Earth it is thought to have an iron core and a rocky mantle, and beneath its ice shell lies a probable ocean of salty water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its surface is crisscrossed by cracks and ridges that can run for thousands of kilometres, and it has so few craters that it must be young, perhaps only 40 to 90 million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Europa orbits Jupiter every 3.5 days in a resonance with Io and Ganymede that keeps its orbit stretched, and the changing tides flex its ice and probably create the fractures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The strongest evidence for the ocean came from the Galileo spacecraft, which found Jupiter's magnetic field disturbed around Europa as expected of an electrically conducting layer beneath the ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A pass in January 2000 showed that the moon's field changes over time, the mark of a field induced from outside rather than a permanent one, which strengthened the case for a liquid ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The shell is thought to be about 15 to 25 kilometres (10 to 15 miles) thick over an ocean 60 to 150 kilometres (40 to 100 miles) deep, which may hold twice as much water as all of Earth's oceans, although Juno has since measured a thicker outer shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Radiation from Jupiter turns water and impurities on the surface into oxidants, and if these reach the ocean they could react with chemically reducing material from the seafloor and so supply energy for life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> NASA launched the Europa Clipper spacecraft on 14 October 2024 to make repeated close flybys, arriving in 2030, and to find out whether places beneath the surface could support life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.2 The Galilean Moons of Jupiter,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-2-the-galilean-moons-of-jupiter. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Europa: Facts,” updated April 21, 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/europa/europa-facts/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Europa,” updated November 11, 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/europa/. [Open access]","Margaret G. Kivelson et al., “Galileo Magnetometer Measurements: A Stronger Case for a Subsurface Ocean at Europa,” <i>Science</i> 289, no. 5483 (2000): 1340–43, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.289.5483.1340. [Paywalled]","Samuel M. Howell and Robert T. Pappalardo, “NASA’s Europa Clipper—a Mission to a Potentially Habitable Ocean World,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 11 (2020): 1311, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-15160-9. [Open access]","NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, “Jovian Satellite Fact Sheet,” https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/joviansatfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “NASA’s Juno Measures Thickness of Europa’s Ice Shell,” January 27, 2026, https://www.nasa.gov/missions/juno/nasas-juno-measures-thickness-of-europas-ice-shell/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a changing magnetic field point to an ocean?","a":"Because only a conducting layer would produce it. Jupiter's magnetic field sweeps past Europa, and a deep layer of electrically conducting fluid would carry currents that create an induced field of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Galileo pass in January 2000 showed that Europa's field changes over time as an induced field should, rather than staying fixed like a permanent one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Europa's surface so young?","a":"Because it is constantly renewed. Very few impact craters survive in its ice, so the surface must be no more than a few million years old by one estimate and 40 to 90 million years by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tides flex the ice as Europa's distance from Jupiter changes, and water or slush from below may seep up through cracks and refreeze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might Europa's ocean support life?","a":"Because it may have the three main things life needs. It probably has abundant liquid water and the chemical elements life is built from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Energy could come from chemistry, as radiation makes oxidants at the surface that might mix with reduced material from the seafloor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ocean may also have lasted long enough for life to develop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Europa (moon)"},
    "astro-170": {"questions":["Patches of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that stand above Europa's surrounding ice may form over lenses of liquid water perched as little as 3 kilometres (2 miles) beneath the surface.","Jumbled blocks of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on Mars, Pluto and Europa differ in height, rising with block size on two worlds but not on Europa, a clue to the make-up of each crust."],"abstract":"<b>Chaos terrain</b> is ground in which an older surface has been broken up into irregular, jumbled blocks, and it is found on Mars, on Pluto and on Jupiter's moon Europa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How such landscapes form is not fully settled, and several models have been proposed for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The heights of the blocks can be used to learn about the ground beneath, from the thickness of near-surface layers on Mars to how deep the blocks reach on Pluto if they float like icebergs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The blocks behave differently from world to world, growing taller as they grow larger on Pluto and Mars but not on Europa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On Europa, chaos regions such as Conamara Chaos look like sea ice broken into floes and icebergs that slid or rotated before freezing in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> These broken regions are among the clearest surface signs of Europa's ocean, marking places where the icy crust is, or recently was, thin enough to give access to liquid or slush below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One analysis found that chaos terrains stand above the surrounding ice and proposed that they form over lenses of liquid water perched within the shell as little as 3 kilometres (2 miles) below the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same study read the sunken ground of the region called Thera Macula as active resurfacing over a lens of water comparable in volume to North America's Great Lakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ocean itself lies deeper, beneath a shell thought to be 15 to 25 kilometres (10 to 15 miles) thick, and its presence is inferred from the way Europa disturbs Jupiter's magnetic field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 2022 the Juno spacecraft measured the cold, rigid outer layer of the shell at about 29 kilometres (18 miles) thick where it looked, which would lengthen the route between the surface and the ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.2 The Galilean Moons of Jupiter,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-2-the-galilean-moons-of-jupiter. [Open access]","B. E. Schmidt et al., “Active Formation of ‘Chaos Terrain’ over Shallow Subsurface Water on Europa,” <i>Nature</i> 479, no. 7374 (2011): 502–5, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature10608. [Paywalled]","Helle L. Skjetne et al., “Morphological Comparison of Blocks in Chaos Terrains on Pluto, Europa, and Mars,” <i>Icarus</i> 356 (2021): 113866, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2020.113866, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.12033. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Europa: Facts,” updated April 21, 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/europa/europa-facts/. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “NASA’s Juno Measures Thickness of Europa’s Ice Shell,” January 27, 2026, https://www.nasa.gov/missions/juno/nasas-juno-measures-thickness-of-europas-ice-shell/. [Open access]","Margaret G. Kivelson et al., “Galileo Magnetometer Measurements: A Stronger Case for a Subsurface Ocean at Europa,” <i>Science</i> 289, no. 5483 (2000): 1340–43, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.289.5483.1340. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does chaos terrain suggest liquid water beneath Europa's ice?","a":"Because the blocks look as if they floated. In Conamara Chaos many blocks of ice seem to have slid or rotated from their original positions, like sea ice and icebergs that then froze in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One analysis proposed that chaos terrains form above lenses of liquid water perched within the ice shell as little as 3 kilometres (2 miles) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the thickness of Europa's ice shell matter?","a":"Because it sets how hard it is for material to pass between the surface and the ocean. Juno found the cold, rigid outer layer about 29 kilometres (18 miles) thick in the region it observed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A thick shell means a longer route for oxygen and nutrients, which bears on whether the ocean could be habitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Earlier estimates put the shell at 15 to 25 kilometres (10 to 15 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why compare chaos blocks on different worlds?","a":"Because block heights carry information about the crust. The height of chaotic blocks can be used to infer the thickness of the layer they formed from on Europa, how deep they reach on Pluto if they float like icebergs, and the thickness of near-surface layers on Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On Europa, block height does not generally rise with block size, unlike on Pluto and Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Chaos terrain"},
    "astro-171": {"questions":["Bright grooved terrain that has pulled old craters apart covers about three quarters of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the largest moon in the Solar System and a world with a rocky core.","Auroral ovals on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rock less than they should as Jupiter's magnetic field sweeps past, a sign that a salty, electrically conducting ocean lies beneath its ice."],"abstract":"<b>Ganymede</b> is the largest moon of Jupiter and the largest moon in the Solar System, bigger than the planet Mercury and the dwarf planet Pluto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is about 5,260 kilometres (3,270 miles) across, circles Jupiter about 1.07 million kilometres (665,000 miles) out once every 7.15 days, and has a mean density of 1,940 kilograms per cubic metre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Measurements of its gravity show that it is differentiated, with a rocky core about the size of the Earth's Moon beneath a mantle and crust of ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its surface mixes two kinds of ground, for about a quarter of it is dark, old and heavily cratered, while the rest is brighter and more lightly cratered, cut by long grooves and ridges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Crater counts put that fresher terrain at perhaps 2 to 3 billion years old, and it seems to have formed when tectonic forces cracked and compressed the icy crust, pulling some older craters apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What sets Ganymede apart is that it is the only moon known to have a magnetic field of its own, generated by a metallic iron core deep inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Galileo spacecraft found the field on close passes in 1996, measuring a dipole tilted by about 10° to the spin axis and strong enough to carve out a small magnetosphere within Jupiter's own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ganymede is also thought to hold an underground ocean of salt water that may hold more water than all the water on the Earth's surface, perhaps in several layers of ice and water stacked between its crust and core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its aurorae carry the clue, for a conducting ocean should damp the rocking of the auroral ovals as Jupiter's field sweeps past, and Hubble images showed a swing of about 2° instead of the 6° expected without one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The European Space Agency's Juice spacecraft is planned to enter orbit around Ganymede in December 2034, which would make it the first spacecraft to orbit another planet's moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["NASA, “Ganymede,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/ganymede/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Jovian Satellite Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/joviansatfact.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.2 The Galilean Moons of Jupiter,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-2-the-galilean-moons-of-jupiter. [Open access]","M. G. Kivelson et al., “Discovery of Ganymede’s Magnetic Field by the Galileo Spacecraft,” <i>Nature</i> 384, no. 6609 (1996): 537–41, https://doi.org/10.1038/384537a0. [Paywalled]","Joachim Saur et al., “The Search for a Subsurface Ocean in Ganymede with Hubble Space Telescope Observations of Its Auroral Ovals,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i> 120, no. 3 (2015): 1715–37, https://doi.org/10.1002/2014JA020778. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Juice’s Elliptical Orbit around Ganymede,” ESA Multimedia, March 2022, https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Videos/2022/03/Juice_s_elliptical_orbit_around_Ganymede. [Open access]","NASA, “Ocean Worlds: Water in the Solar System and Beyond,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/ocean-worlds/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Ganymede's surface split into dark and bright ground?","a":"Because the two kinds of terrain are of different ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dark ground is old and heavily cratered, while the bright ground has fewer and fresher craters and is perhaps 2 to 3 billion years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bright terrain seems to have formed when the icy crust cracked and was compressed into long ridges and grooves, and tidal forces from Jupiter may have heated the interior from time to time and driven that activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can a moon made largely of ice have a magnetic field?","a":"Because Ganymede is not ice all the way through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its gravity shows that rock and metal sank to form a core beneath the ice, and a magnetic field is a sign of a partly molten interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> NASA describes a metallic iron core deep inside that generates the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Galileo spacecraft passed close in 1996 it measured a field strong enough to carve out a small magnetosphere within Jupiter's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can aurorae reveal a hidden ocean?","a":"Because a salty ocean conducts electricity and weakens a changing magnetic field inside the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As Jupiter's field sweeps past Ganymede, its auroral ovals rock to and fro, and models predicted a swing of about 6° without an ocean and about 2° with one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hubble observations showed a swing of about 2°, which requires a conducting layer whose top lies no deeper than about 330 kilometres (205 miles) below the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ganymede (moon)"},
    "astro-172": {"questions":["Almost as wide as Mercury but with only a third of its mass, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is mostly ice inside and seems to have been geologically dead for more than 4 billion years.","Magnetic disturbances near <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> point to a salty ocean hidden between layers of ice beneath a crust some 200 kilometres (124 miles) thick on Jupiter's outermost large moon."],"abstract":"<b>Callisto</b> is the outermost of Jupiter's four large Galilean moons, the second largest of Jupiter's moons and the third largest moon in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is about 4,820 kilometres (3,000 miles) across, almost the size of the planet Mercury, but it has only about a third of Mercury's mass, so it must be icy through much of its interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It orbits Jupiter about 1.88 million kilometres (1.17 million miles) out once every 16.7 days, always keeping the same face towards the planet, and its density is only 1,830 kilograms per cubic metre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Callisto is the most heavily cratered object in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At noon its surface reaches only about 130 K, cold enough that water ice never evaporates from it, and at that temperature the ice is nearly as hard as rock and does not flow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Callisto is the baseline against which Jupiter's more active moons are measured, for it shows almost no sign of internal forces reshaping it and seems to have been geologically dead for more than 4 billion years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its pull on the passing Galileo spacecraft showed that it has no dense core and never fully separated into layers of rock and ice, apparently freezing before the process could finish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet the same spacecraft found the magnetic field near Callisto disturbed in a way best explained by currents in a salty, electrically conducting ocean beneath its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> NASA estimates the ice above that ocean at about 200 kilometres (124 miles) thick and the ocean itself at least 10 kilometres (6 miles) deep, lying between two layers of ice rather than on rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Callisto is counted among the known ocean worlds, though one whose surface shows no present activity, and Juice is to make a series of flybys of it on its way into orbit around Ganymede.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["NASA, “Callisto,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/callisto/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Jovian Satellite Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/joviansatfact.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.2 The Galilean Moons of Jupiter,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-2-the-galilean-moons-of-jupiter. [Open access]","K. K. Khurana et al., “Induced Magnetic Fields as Evidence for Subsurface Oceans in Europa and Callisto,” <i>Nature</i> 395, no. 6704 (1998): 777–80, https://doi.org/10.1038/27394. [Paywalled]","NASA, “Ocean Worlds: Water in the Solar System and Beyond,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/ocean-worlds/. [Open access]","Amanda R. Hendrix et al., “The NASA Roadmap to Ocean Worlds,” <i>Astrobiology</i> 19, no. 1 (2019): 1–27, https://doi.org/10.1089/ast.2018.1955. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Juice’s Elliptical Orbit around Ganymede,” ESA Multimedia, March 2022, https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Videos/2022/03/Juice_s_elliptical_orbit_around_Ganymede. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Callisto so heavily cratered?","a":"Because nothing inside it has worked to erase its craters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Callisto shows almost no sign of internal forces driving geological change and seems to have been dead for more than 4 billion years, so the scars of impacts have simply piled up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its surface ice, at about 130 K, is nearly as hard as rock and does not flow like a glacier on the Earth, so the craters keep their shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> NASA calls it the most heavily cratered object in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What does Callisto's low density tell us?","a":"It tells us what the moon is made of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Callisto is almost as wide as Mercury but has only a third of its mass, so it must contain far less rock and metal and be icy through much of its interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its density of 1,830 kilograms per cubic metre is lower than Ganymede's 1,940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gravity measurements from the Galileo spacecraft added that its rock has not sunk to form a dense core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can a dead moon have an ocean?","a":"Because an ocean can survive deep inside without changing the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Galileo's magnetometer found disturbances near Callisto best explained by currents flowing in a salty, conducting layer beneath the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> NASA puts the ice above that layer at about 200 kilometres (124 miles) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The roadmap to ocean worlds notes that Callisto's ocean lies between two layers of ice and that its surface, like Ganymede's, shows no present activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Callisto (moon)"},
    "astro-173": {"questions":["In January 2005 the Huygens probe parachuted onto <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and found a plain of water-ice boulders at 94 K, the first landing anywhere in the outer Solar System.","NASA's Dragonfly rotorcraft is meant to hop between sites on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Saturn's largest moon, studying an organic chemistry driven by sunlight acting on nitrogen and methane."],"abstract":"<b>Titan</b> is the largest moon of Saturn and the only moon in the Solar System known to have a substantial atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is about 5,150 kilometres (3,200 miles) across, nearly as big as Ganymede, and it orbits Saturn about 1.22 million kilometres (760,000 miles) out once every 15.9 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its density of 1,880 kilograms per cubic metre is close to that of Ganymede and Callisto, which suggests a body of roughly half ice and half rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Titan is larger than the planet Mercury, and it keeps the same face towards Saturn throughout its orbit, as the Earth's Moon does towards the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its atmosphere is mostly nitrogen, as the Earth's is, and the pressure at its surface is 1.6 bars, higher than on any other moon and higher even than on the Earth or Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Sunlight acting on that nitrogen and on methane builds a rich mixture of organic molecules, including propane and hydrogen cyanide, which gather in layers of orange haze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Titan is the only place besides the Earth known to have liquids on its surface, with clouds, rain, rivers, lakes and seas, though the liquid is methane and ethane rather than water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The European Space Agency's Huygens probe, carried by NASA's Cassini spacecraft, landed on 14 January 2005, the first landing in the outer Solar System, and found a plain of water-ice boulders at 94 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cassini's gravity measurements also revealed an internal ocean of water and ammonia, which NASA describes as possibly as salty as the Dead Sea and beginning about 50 kilometres (30 miles) below the ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> NASA's Dragonfly, a rotorcraft due to launch in 2028, is meant to spend three years making landings across Titan to study the chemistry that comes before life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.3 Titan and Triton,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-3-titan-and-triton. [Open access]","NASA, “Titan,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/titan/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Saturnian Satellite Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/saturniansatfact.html. [Open access]","NASA, “Titan,” Cassini mission science, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/cassini/science/titan/. [Open access]","European Space Agency, “Cassini-Huygens,” ESA Science and Exploration, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Cassini-Huygens. [Open access]","NASA, “Dragonfly,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dragonfly/. [Open access]","NASA, “Ocean Worlds: Water in the Solar System and Beyond,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/ocean-worlds/. [Open access]","NASA, “Saturn Moons,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Titan called an Earth-like world?","a":"Because it shares several features with the Earth despite its cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its atmosphere is mostly nitrogen, and its surface pressure of 1.6 bars is higher than the Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the only other place known to have liquids on its surface, with clouds, rain, rivers, lakes and seas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> NASA notes that liquid methane plays the part on Titan that water plays on the Earth, filling lakes and seas near the poles, while dunes of hydrocarbon-coated ice girdle the equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the Huygens probe find when it landed?","a":"It came down on 14 January 2005 on a flat plain strewn with boulders, and both the ground and the boulders were water ice, as hard as rock at Titan's temperature of 94 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pictures taken on the way down showed drainage channels, suggesting it had landed near the shore of an old lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It kept sending data from the surface for more than an hour until its batteries were drained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> ESA describes it as the first probe to land on a world in the outer Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Titan interest people studying the origins of life?","a":"Because its atmosphere works as a natural chemistry laboratory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sunlight acting on nitrogen and methane makes a rich mix of organic molecules, from propane to hydrogen cyanide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Titan is far too cold for liquid water at its surface, but it hides an ocean of water deep below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> NASA's Dragonfly rotorcraft is meant to land at several sites to investigate prebiotic chemistry and search for chemical signs of water-based or hydrocarbon-based life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Titan (moon)"},
    "astro-174": {"questions":["Covering almost 2% of the surface, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> include Ligeia Mare, a sea about 160 metres (525 feet) deep and made almost entirely of liquid methane.","Benzene and other insoluble organic molecules falling from a smoggy sky sink through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and settle as a sludge on the floors of their methane seas."],"abstract":"The <b>lakes of Titan</b> are bodies of liquid methane and ethane on the surface of Saturn's largest moon, the only standing liquids known anywhere beyond the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Cassini's radar first showed them clearly in July 2006, imaging more than 75 dark patches polewards of 70° north, many joined to channels and lying in hollows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of those lakes did not fill their hollows and other hollows were dry, which suggests that lakes are found in a range of states and come and go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Liquid covers more than 1.6 million square kilometres (620,000 square miles), almost 2% of Titan, in three large seas near the north pole and dozens of smaller lakes, while only one lake has been found in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kraken Mare, the largest sea, is about 30 metres (100 feet) deep near its eastern shore, and Ligeia Mare is about 160 metres (525 feet) deep at its deepest measured point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The lakes are the visible part of a weather cycle like the Earth's water cycle, in which methane evaporates, forms clouds, falls as rain and runs down valleys to the lakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Titan's seasons are long, each lasting about 7.5 Earth years, and methane rainstorms were predicted for the north pole as summer arrived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ligeia Mare is mostly pure methane, so transparent to radar that it must hold almost nothing else, which suggests it is topped up by fresh methane rain or loses its ethane somewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Organic molecules made in the atmosphere fall into the seas, where some dissolve, while insoluble compounds such as benzene sink and form a sludge on the sea floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Unlike the Earth's oceans, the liquid on Titan's surface is only a small fraction of the methane held in its atmosphere, and the seas preserve a record of how its climate and landscape have changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["E. R. Stofan et al., “The Lakes of Titan,” <i>Nature</i> 445, no. 7123 (2007): 61–64, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature05438. [Paywalled]","Alexander G. Hayes, “The Lakes and Seas of Titan,” <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 44 (2016): 57–83, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-060115-012247. [Open access]","Marco Mastrogiuseppe et al., “The Bathymetry of a Titan Sea,” <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 41, no. 5 (2014): 1432–37, https://doi.org/10.1002/2013GL058618. [Paywalled]","European Space Agency, “Profile of a Methane Sea on Titan,” ESA Science and Exploration, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Cassini-Huygens/Profile_of_a_methane_sea_on_Titan. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Plumbing Coastal Depths in Titan’s Kraken Mare,” NASA Science Photojournal, 10 November 2014, https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/plumbing-coastal-depths-in-titans-kraken-mare/. [Open access]","NASA, “Titan,” Cassini mission science, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/cassini/science/titan/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.3 Titan and Triton,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-3-titan-and-triton. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are Titan's lakes made of methane rather than water?","a":"Because Titan is far too cold for liquid water at its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At about 94 K water ice is as hard as rock, but methane and ethane can stay liquid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Methane plays the part that water plays on the Earth, evaporating, forming clouds, falling as rain and running down valleys to the lakes, so Titan has a low-temperature version of the water cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Where on Titan are the lakes found?","a":"Mostly near the north pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Radar found more than 75 lakes polewards of 70° north on a single pass, in a region where liquid methane and ethane are expected to be abundant and stable at the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three large seas all lie close to the north pole, surrounded by dozens of smaller lakes, while just one lake has been found in the southern hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Ligeia Mare's pure methane a surprise?","a":"Because ethane is produced in abundance in the atmosphere when sunlight breaks methane apart, so the sea was expected to be mostly ethane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Radar passed through the liquid with very little loss, which requires a nearly pure mixture of methane and ethane with almost nothing else dissolved or suspended in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Either fresh methane rain keeps the sea topped up or ethane is being removed, perhaps into the crust or into neighbouring Kraken Mare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lakes and rivers of Titan"},
    "astro-175": {"questions":["With the whitest, most reflective surface in the Solar System, Saturn's small moon <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stays at about −201 °C (−330 °F) yet hides a global ocean.","A wobble in the rotation of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> too large for an icy shell fixed to its core shows that its ocean spans the whole moon, not just the south pole."],"abstract":"<b>Enceladus</b> is a small icy moon of Saturn, only about 500 kilometres (310 miles) across, yet one of the most geologically active worlds in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It orbits Saturn about 238,000 kilometres (148,000 miles) out, between the moons Mimas and Tethys, once every 32.9 hours, keeping the same face towards the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has the whitest, most reflective surface in the Solar System, which keeps it extremely cold, at about −201 °C (−330 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of its terrain carries craters up to 35 kilometres (22 miles) across, but other regions have few, and the south polar region is almost free of them, a sign of resurfacing in the geologically recent past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Enceladus is held in an orbital resonance with the larger moon Dione, circling Saturn twice for each of Dione's orbits, and Dione's pull stretches its orbit into an ellipse that flexes and heats it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That heat helps keep a global ocean of liquid water beneath its icy shell, which makes it one of the Solar System's ocean worlds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The ocean is known to be global rather than a sea confined to the south pole because the moon's rotation has a slight wobble, or libration, too large for a shell rigidly joined to its core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The south polar region is an active province bounded by folded ridges, with high temperatures, extreme youth and narrow rifts from which jets of icy particles escape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those jets supply Saturn's diffuse E ring, so the little moon makes a ring of its own around the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because it sprays its ocean into space, a spacecraft can sample it without landing, and NASA judges that Enceladus has most of the chemical ingredients needed for life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["NASA, “Enceladus,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/enceladus/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Saturnian Satellite Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/saturniansatfact.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.1 Ring and Moon Systems Introduced,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-1-ring-and-moon-systems-introduced. [Open access]","P. C. Thomas et al., “Enceladus’s Measured Physical Libration Requires a Global Subsurface Ocean,” <i>Icarus</i> 264 (2016): 37–47, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2015.08.037; preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.07555. [Open access]","C. C. Porco et al., “Cassini Observes the Active South Pole of Enceladus,” <i>Science</i> 311, no. 5766 (2006): 1393–1401, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1123013. [Paywalled]","NASA, “Saturn Moons,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/. [Open access]","NASA, “Ocean Worlds: Water in the Solar System and Beyond,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/ocean-worlds/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is so small a moon still active?","a":"Because Saturn's tides keep heating it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Enceladus is locked in a resonance with the larger moon Dione, orbiting twice for each of Dione's orbits, and Dione's pull keeps its orbit stretched into an ellipse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So Enceladus is sometimes nearer to Saturn and sometimes farther away, and the changing pull heats its interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so, keeping a global ocean liquid this way is hard to explain in many thermal models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can a wobble reveal an ocean?","a":"Because a shell floating on liquid can rock more freely than one fixed to a solid interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tracking points across the surface through seven years of spacecraft observations gave a libration of about 0.12°.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is too large for the core and surface to be rigidly joined, so the icy shell must rest on a global ocean rather than on a sea confined to the south pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> NASA adds gravity measurements to the same conclusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Enceladus so bright?","a":"Because fresh ice keeps falling on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jets near the south pole throw out icy particles continuously, and while some feed Saturn's E ring, most fall back onto the moon like snow, keeping it bright white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That makes it the most reflective body in the Solar System, and because it reflects so much sunlight its surface stays at about −201 °C (−330 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The jets rise from a young, warm province at the south pole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Enceladus"},
    "astro-176": {"questions":["Salt-rich ice grains make up more than 99% of the solid mass in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, pointing to a reservoir of liquid salt water beneath the south pole of a small Saturnian moon.","Molecular hydrogen, silica grains and sodium phosphates sampled in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> suggest that an ocean beneath the ice reacts with rock on a sea floor warmed by hydrothermal activity."],"abstract":"The <b>plumes of Enceladus</b> are jets of water vapour and icy particles that erupt from fractures near the south pole of Saturn's moon Enceladus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The fractures, informally called tiger stripes, are four prominent fractures, each about 135 kilometres (84 miles) long, and the jets rise from near their hottest spots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Material leaves them at about 400 metres per second (nearly 900 miles per hour), and the eruptions appear to be continuous, wrapping the moon in a halo of fine ice dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The plume extends hundreds of kilometres into space, and while most of its ice falls back onto Enceladus as snow that keeps the moon bright white, the rest escapes to form Saturn's vast E ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Besides water vapour, the gas contains carbon dioxide, methane, perhaps a little ammonia and either carbon monoxide or nitrogen, carried along with salts and silica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The plumes matter because they are samples of a hidden ocean thrown into space, where a passing spacecraft can catch and analyse them without ever having to land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Salt-rich ice grains make up more than 99% of the solid mass leaving the vents, which points to a reservoir of salt water with a large evaporating surface rather than ice breaking down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tiny grains of silica carried out with the plume are best explained by hot-water reactions between the ocean and the rock of its floor, a sign of hydrothermal activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The plume also contains molecular hydrogen, evidence that the ocean water reacts with rock and a possible source of chemical energy like that around hydrothermal vents on the Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its ice grains carry sodium phosphates as well, implying phosphorus concentrations in the ocean at least 100 times higher than in the Earth's oceans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["NASA, “Enceladus,” Cassini mission science, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/cassini/science/enceladus/. [Open access]","NASA, “Ocean Worlds: Water in the Solar System and Beyond,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/ocean-worlds/. [Open access]","F. Postberg et al., “A Salt-Water Reservoir as the Source of a Compositionally Stratified Plume on Enceladus,” <i>Nature</i> 474, no. 7353 (2011): 620–22, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature10175. [Paywalled]","Hsiang-Wen Hsu et al., “Ongoing Hydrothermal Activities within Enceladus,” <i>Nature</i> 519, no. 7542 (2015): 207–10, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14262. [Paywalled]","J. Hunter Waite et al., “Cassini Finds Molecular Hydrogen in the Enceladus Plume: Evidence for Hydrothermal Processes,” <i>Science</i> 356, no. 6334 (2017): 155–59, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aai8703. [Paywalled]","Frank Postberg et al., “Detection of Phosphates Originating from Enceladus’s Ocean,” <i>Nature</i> 618, no. 7965 (2023): 489–93, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-05987-9. [Open access]","NASA, “Enceladus,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/enceladus/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the salty grains show that the plumes come from liquid water?","a":"Because ice that simply breaks down would not carry so much salt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Grains rich in sodium salts dominate the solid material close to the vents, making up more than 99% of its mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That composition rules out or severely limits models in which the plume forms without liquid, and points instead to a body of salt water with a large surface evaporating into the vents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> NASA describes the source as a global ocean beneath the icy shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"What does the hydrogen in the plume suggest?","a":"It suggests that the ocean water is reacting with rock through hydrothermal processes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cassini flew directly through the plume in October 2015 and detected molecular hydrogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Such reactions drive the ocean out of chemical equilibrium, much as the water around hydrothermal vents on the Earth is, and could supply chemical energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Silica grains carried up from the sea floor point the same way, to hot-water reactions between ocean and rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does phosphorus in the plume matter?","a":"Because phosphorus is one of the elements that life on the Earth needs, and the scarcest of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Before these ice grains were analysed it had never been detected in an ocean beyond the Earth, and some models suggested it would be scarce in icy moons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sodium phosphates in the grains instead imply concentrations in the ocean at least 100 times higher than in the Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> NASA counts Enceladus as having most of the chemical ingredients needed for life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-177": {"questions":["Like a wire coat hanger bent back and forth, a moon on an eccentric orbit is kneaded by its planet's changing pull, and that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can keep an ocean liquid under its ice.","Because Dione's pull keeps Enceladus's orbit stretched into an ellipse, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> flexes the little moon on every circuit of Saturn and warms its icy interior."],"abstract":"<b>Tidal heating</b> is the warming of a moon or planet by the repeated flexing that a partner's changing gravity forces upon it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tidal force comes from the unequal pull of gravity on the near and far sides of a body, which stretches it into a slightly elongated shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If a locked moon kept exactly the same face towards its planet on a perfectly circular orbit, that bulge would stay put and make no heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On an eccentric orbit, however, the moon moves nearer and farther and twists back and forth, so its interior is kneaded on every orbit, much as bending a wire coat hanger again and again makes it hot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among the giant planets' moons orbital resonances keep the orbits eccentric, as when Enceladus orbits Saturn twice for each orbit of Dione, whose pull stretches its path into an ellipse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Io is the extreme case, for tugs from Europa and Ganymede keep its orbit eccentric, and Jupiter's tides drive the highest level of volcanism in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That Io had been largely melted by tidal heating was argued in a paper published in March 1979, the month in which Voyager 1 passed and saw eight of its volcanoes erupting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Measurements of how much Io deforms, made with the Juno spacecraft, show that even this enormous heating has not made a shallow global ocean of magma, and its mantle is mostly solid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farther out, tidal heating helps keep oceans liquid beneath the ice of moons such as Europa, while for the largest moons heat left from radioactive decay in their rock may be enough on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> How Enceladus keeps a global ocean remains a puzzle for many thermal models, which may require Saturn to drain more energy from the moon's tides than expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.2 The Galilean Moons of Jupiter,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-2-the-galilean-moons-of-jupiter. [Open access]","S. J. Peale, P. Cassen, and R. T. Reynolds, “Melting of Io by Tidal Dissipation,” <i>Science</i> 203, no. 4383 (1979): 892–94, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.203.4383.892. [Paywalled]","R. S. Park et al., “Io’s Tidal Response Precludes a Shallow Magma Ocean,” <i>Nature</i> 638, no. 8049 (2025): 69–73, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-08442-5. [Open access]","NASA, “Enceladus,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/enceladus/. [Open access]","P. C. Thomas et al., “Enceladus’s Measured Physical Libration Requires a Global Subsurface Ocean,” <i>Icarus</i> 264 (2016): 37–47, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2015.08.037; preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.07555. [Open access]","Amanda R. Hendrix et al., “The NASA Roadmap to Ocean Worlds,” <i>Astrobiology</i> 19, no. 1 (2019): 1–27, https://doi.org/10.1089/ast.2018.1955. [Open access]","NASA, “Ocean Worlds: Water in the Solar System and Beyond,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/ocean-worlds/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a perfectly circular orbit produce no tidal heat?","a":"Because the tidal bulge would then stay in one place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A moon that always keeps exactly the same face towards its planet at a constant distance is stretched but not flexed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heat comes only when the orbit is eccentric, so that the moon moves nearer and farther and twists back and forth relative to its planet on each revolution, kneading its interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For Io, tugs from Europa and Ganymede keep the orbit from becoming circular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why didn't tidal heating melt Io into a magma ocean?","a":"Probably because melt escapes as fast as it forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Measurements of Io's tidal deformation from Juno and earlier Galileo tracking showed that it flexes far less than it would over a shallow global ocean of magma, so its mantle must be mostly solid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rapid rise, intrusion and eruption of melt may stop such an ocean forming, so even strong tidal heating does not guarantee one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Io is still the most volcanic body in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does tidal heating matter for life beyond the Earth?","a":"Because it can keep water liquid far from the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> NASA notes that tidal heating from Jupiter maintains the ocean beneath Europa's ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The roadmap to ocean worlds names tidal energy and the heat of radioactive decay as the ways such oceans are kept warm, adding that for the largest moons radioactive heat alone may be enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Enceladus, Dione's pull keeps the orbit stretched and the moon heated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Tidal heating"},
    "astro-178": {"questions":["Triton, Pluto, Ceres and Dione are each a candidate <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, suggested only by hints from limited spacecraft observations rather than by firm measurements.","An induced magnetic field around a moon of Jupiter, produced as the planet's field sweeps past, can mark it as an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a salty, conducting layer of liquid beneath its ice."],"abstract":"An <b>ocean world</b> is a planet or moon with a substantial body of liquid water, and in the outer Solar System such oceans lie hidden beneath shells of ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One working definition counts any body with a liquid ocean today, not necessarily a global one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By that measure Europa, Ganymede, Callisto, Titan and Enceladus are known ocean worlds, with direct evidence for their subsurface oceans gathered by the Galileo and Cassini spacecraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Triton, Pluto, Ceres and Dione are candidates, suggested so far only by hints from limited spacecraft observations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> NASA's interest in them rests on the view that life as we know it needs three ingredients, energy, organic molecules and liquid water, so the search for life is partly a search for water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because the oceans cannot be seen, they are found indirectly, and one method uses Jupiter's magnetic field, for a salty, conducting layer inside a moon produces an induced field as that field changes around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Europa's ocean is kept liquid by tidal heating from Jupiter, which may also create partly melted pockets within its outer shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Enceladus a slight wobble in the moon's rotation, too large for a shell fixed to its core, shows that the ocean is global, while gravity measurements revealed Titan's ocean of water and ammonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The oceans are kept liquid by tidal energy and by heat from radioactive decay in their rock, and for the largest moons that remnant heat may be enough on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They differ greatly in how reachable they are, for those of Titan, Ganymede and Callisto lie beneath thick ice with no obvious sign at the surface, while Enceladus vents its ocean into space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["NASA, “Ocean Worlds: Water in the Solar System and Beyond,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/ocean-worlds/. [Open access]","Amanda R. Hendrix et al., “The NASA Roadmap to Ocean Worlds,” <i>Astrobiology</i> 19, no. 1 (2019): 1–27, https://doi.org/10.1089/ast.2018.1955. [Open access]","F. Nimmo and R. T. Pappalardo, “Ocean Worlds in the Outer Solar System,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 121, no. 8 (2016): 1378–99, https://doi.org/10.1002/2016JE005081. [Open access]","K. K. Khurana et al., “Induced Magnetic Fields as Evidence for Subsurface Oceans in Europa and Callisto,” <i>Nature</i> 395, no. 6704 (1998): 777–80, https://doi.org/10.1038/27394. [Paywalled]","Joachim Saur et al., “The Search for a Subsurface Ocean in Ganymede with Hubble Space Telescope Observations of Its Auroral Ovals,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i> 120, no. 3 (2015): 1715–37, https://doi.org/10.1002/2014JA020778. [Open access]","P. C. Thomas et al., “Enceladus’s Measured Physical Libration Requires a Global Subsurface Ocean,” <i>Icarus</i> 264 (2016): 37–47, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2015.08.037; preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.07555. [Open access]","NASA, “Titan,” Cassini mission science, NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/cassini/science/titan/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How can an ocean be detected through kilometres of ice?","a":"Indirectly, by its effects on magnetic fields and on motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A salty ocean conducts electricity, so as Jupiter's magnetic field changes around a moon, currents in the ocean produce an induced field that a spacecraft can measure, as Galileo did near Europa and Callisto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The swing of Ganymede's aurorae responds to an ocean in the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Enceladus the size of a wobble in the moon's rotation shows that the icy shell floats on a global ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What keeps these oceans from freezing?","a":"Heat from two main sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tidal flexing by a parent planet warms some moons, and NASA notes that tidal heating from Jupiter keeps Europa's ocean liquid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heat from radioactive elements in the rock is the other, and for the largest moons that remnant heat may be enough on its own, depending on how much radioactive material the rock held at first and on the state of the ice above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are some ocean worlds easier to study than others?","a":"Because their oceans differ in how close they come to the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oceans of Titan, Ganymede and Callisto are expected to lie beneath thick ice shells, with no obvious surface evidence and little exchange with the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Enceladus is the opposite case, for its ocean vents into space through fractures near the south pole and feeds a plume.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The roadmap ranks Enceladus, Titan and Europa as the highest priorities for near-term exploration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ocean world"},
    "astro-179": {"questions":["Dark plumes of nitrogen gas rising about 8 kilometres (5 miles) above <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were seen by Voyager 2 in 1989 on one of the coldest surfaces any spacecraft has visited.","Found in 1846 only 17 days after its planet, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may once have been half of a binary pair like Pluto and Charon before Neptune tore the pair apart."],"abstract":"<b>Triton</b> is the largest moon of Neptune and the only large moon in the Solar System that orbits in the direction opposite to its planet's rotation, a retrograde orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is about 2,700 kilometres (1,680 miles) across and circles Neptune about 355,000 kilometres (220,000 miles) out once every 5.9 days, on an orbit steeply tilted to the planet's equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was found in 1846, just 17 days after Neptune itself, and until a second moon was found in 1949 it was known simply as the satellite of Neptune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its density of about 2,050 kilograms per cubic metre suggests roughly three quarters rock and one quarter water ice, more rock than in the icy moons of Saturn and Uranus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its bright surface of frozen nitrogen, methane, water and carbon monoxide reflects so much sunlight that it is among the coldest places any spacecraft has visited, at 35 to 40 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its backward orbit suggests that Triton once orbited the Sun and was captured by Neptune, and NASA describes it as a likely Kuiper Belt object with much in common with Pluto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One explanation is that it was half of a binary pair, perhaps like Pluto and Charon, which strayed too close to Neptune and was torn apart, its partner escaping and Triton staying behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Voyager 2, the only spacecraft to fly past, found in 1989 a young, sparsely cratered surface of smooth plains, a thin nitrogen atmosphere and dark plumes rising about 8 kilometres (5 miles) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The plumes may be driven by sunlight warming nitrogen ice beneath the surface until the pressurised gas vents explosively, carrying dark particles that drift more than 100 kilometres (60 miles) downwind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its activity and youthful surface make it the highest-priority candidate ocean world for future exploration, although the source of the energy behind that activity is still unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.3 Titan and Triton,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-3-titan-and-triton. [Open access]","NASA, “Triton,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/moons/triton/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Neptunian Satellite Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/neptuniansatfact.html. [Open access]","Craig B. Agnor and Douglas P. Hamilton, “Neptune’s Capture of Its Moon Triton in a Binary–Planet Gravitational Encounter,” <i>Nature</i> 441, no. 7090 (2006): 192–94, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04792. [Paywalled]","L. A. Soderblom et al., “Triton’s Geyser-Like Plumes: Discovery and Basic Characterization,” <i>Science</i> 250, no. 4979 (1990): 410–15, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.250.4979.410. [Paywalled]","Amanda R. Hendrix et al., “The NASA Roadmap to Ocean Worlds,” <i>Astrobiology</i> 19, no. 1 (2019): 1–27, https://doi.org/10.1089/ast.2018.1955. [Open access]","NASA, “Neptune Moons,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/neptune-moons/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Triton thought to have been captured?","a":"Because of its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Triton circles Neptune in the direction opposite to the planet's spin, and its orbit is tilted well away from Neptune's equator, which suggests that it once orbited the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also resembles Pluto, and NASA describes it as a likely Kuiper Belt object caught by Neptune's gravity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One model has it arriving as half of a binary pair that Neptune pulled apart, its partner escaping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Triton so cold?","a":"Because it reflects most of the sunlight that reaches it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its surface of frozen nitrogen, methane, water and carbon monoxide is very bright, so little solar energy is absorbed, and its temperature is only about 35 to 40 K.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At those temperatures nitrogen and methane, which are gases across most of the Solar System, are frozen solid, and only a little nitrogen vapour remains to form a thin atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What drives Triton's plumes?","a":"Sunlight rather than heat from inside, according to the leading model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nitrogen ice beneath the surface may be warmed in a kind of greenhouse, and a temperature rise of less than 4 kelvins is enough to drive the gas explosively up to a height of about 8 kilometres (5 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Each eruption may last a year or more, leaving dark clouds that drift over 100 kilometres (60 miles) downwind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Triton (moon)"},
    "astro-180": {"questions":["Retrograde orbits outnumber prograde ones among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of every giant planet, and none of them has an inclination between 60° and 130°.","Saturn's dark moon Phoebe, whose surface carries carbon dioxide, organics and water ice, is one of the best-studied of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, small moons that formed elsewhere and were captured."],"abstract":"<b>Irregular satellites</b> are moons on large orbits that are often highly eccentric, steeply inclined or retrograde, unlike the nearly circular orbits close to the equator that regular moons follow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All four giant planets have them, and their orbits reach out to about half the radius of a planet's Hill sphere, the region within which its gravity holds sway against the Sun's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only about a quarter of the moons of the outer Solar System are regular, and the majority are irregular, mostly far from their planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their count keeps growing as surveys find ever more of them, and Saturn had more than 290 confirmed moons as of August 2026, far more than any other planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Retrograde irregulars outnumber prograde ones at every giant planet, and none has been found with an inclination between 60° and 130°, a gap that is not an artefact of where surveys looked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The two kinds of orbit reflect two origins, for regular moons grew in discs around their planets, while irregulars were captured from orbits around the Sun at an early stage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How the capture happened is not settled, since none of the proposed mechanisms, from drag in a young planet's gas to three-body encounters, fits the group properties convincingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jupiter's irregulars cluster into families of similar orbits whose members tend to share a colour, consistent with fragments of single bodies broken up by collisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saturn's Phoebe, about 210 kilometres (130 miles) across and orbiting backwards about 12.9 million kilometres (8 million miles) out, has a dark surface carrying water ice, carbon dioxide and organics that suggest an origin in the outer Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neptune's irregular moons orbit outside Triton, some prograde and some retrograde, and a model of Triton's own capture holds that the planet's original moons would not have survived long afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["David Jewitt and Nader Haghighipour, “Irregular Satellites of the Planets: Products of Capture in the Early Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 45 (2007): 261–95, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.astro.44.051905.092459; preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0703059. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.1 Ring and Moon Systems Introduced,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-1-ring-and-moon-systems-introduced. [Open access]","Torrence V. Johnson and Jonathan I. Lunine, “Saturn’s Moon Phoebe as a Captured Body from the Outer Solar System,” <i>Nature</i> 435, no. 7038 (2005): 69–71, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature03384. [Paywalled]","David R. Williams, “Saturnian Satellite Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/saturniansatfact.html. [Open access]","NASA, “Saturn Moons,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/. [Open access]","Craig B. Agnor and Douglas P. Hamilton, “Neptune’s Capture of Its Moon Triton in a Binary–Planet Gravitational Encounter,” <i>Nature</i> 441, no. 7090 (2006): 192–94, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04792. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do irregular orbits point to capture?","a":"Because regular and irregular moons seem to have formed in different ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Regular moons follow nearly circular orbits close to their planet's equator, as expected for bodies that grew in a disc around the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Irregular moons travel far out on eccentric, inclined and often retrograde orbits that are incompatible with forming in such a disc, so they are thought to have been captured from orbits around the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most probably formed elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are there no irregulars on orbits tilted between 60° and 130°?","a":"Because such orbits are probably unstable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The polar regions around the planets have been searched, so the gap is not an artefact of where astronomers looked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The most likely cause is the Kozai resonance, in which the Sun's tugs on a steeply inclined orbit raise its eccentricity until the moon's closest approach to its planet becomes very small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What does Phoebe reveal about where irregular moons came from?","a":"That at least some formed far out in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cassini flew past Phoebe in June 2004 and detected iron, bound water, carbon dioxide, phyllosilicates, organics and cyanide compounds on its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its composition differs from the ice-rich material of Saturn's mid-sized moons and is consistent with the material from which Pluto and Triton formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It orbits Saturn backwards, about 12.9 million kilometres (8 million miles) out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Irregular moon"},
    "astro-181": {"questions":["Drag in a young planet's swollen gas envelope, a sudden jump in its mass and the splitting of binary pairs are proposed routes to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for small, distant moons on tilted orbits.","Mars's moons Phobos and Deimos were long explained by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of passing asteroids, though simulations show they may instead have gathered from debris thrown up by a giant impact."],"abstract":"In planetary science, <b>gravitational capture</b> is the process by which a body travelling around the Sun becomes permanently bound in orbit around a planet, the leading explanation for moons that cannot have formed where they are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temporary captures are common, since passing asteroids and comets are often held by a planet for a few decades before escaping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A permanent capture needs something to remove energy from the visitor while it is inside the planet's zone of gravitational control, such as friction with gas or a collision, and the modern Solar System offers no such process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Captures are therefore thought to have happened early, when the young planets were still wrapped in gas or crowded by leftover planetesimals, the small bodies from which planets were built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The moons thought to have been captured are mostly small and far from their planets, on orbits that are tilted, stretched or retrograde, running against the direction of the planet's spin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Three main ways of shedding the surplus energy have been suggested: drag in the swollen gas envelope of a young giant planet, a sudden growth in the planet's mass, and encounters among three bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Uranus and Neptune have about as many irregular moons as Jupiter and Saturn but very little gas, so the first two routes struggle to explain them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In one such encounter a binary pair passing a planet is split, one partner being bound while the other is flung away with the surplus energy, a likely route for Neptune's large retrograde moon Triton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Close encounters between the giant planets themselves during an early instability could also have captured passing bodies, and simulations of this give a good match to the orbits of Jupiter's irregular moons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Capture is not always the answer, since Mars's small moons Phobos and Deimos have often been called captured asteroids, yet simulations show that they could instead have formed from debris left by a giant impact on Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["David Jewitt and Nader Haghighipour, “Irregular Satellites of the Planets: Products of Capture in the Early Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 45 (2007): 261–95, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.astro.44.051905.092459, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0703059. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.1 Ring and Moon Systems Introduced,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-1-ring-and-moon-systems-introduced. [Open access]","David Nesvorný, David Vokrouhlický, and Rogerio Deienno, “Capture of Irregular Satellites at Jupiter,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 784, no. 1 (2014): 22, https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-637X/784/1/22, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0253. [Open access]","Craig B. Agnor and Douglas P. Hamilton, “Neptune’s Capture of Its Moon Triton in a Binary–Planet Gravitational Encounter,” <i>Nature</i> 441, no. 7090 (2006): 192–94, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04792. [Paywalled]","NASA Science, “Phobos,” https://science.nasa.gov/mars/moons/phobos/. [Open access]","Pascal Rosenblatt et al., “Accretion of Phobos and Deimos in an Extended Debris Disc Stirred by Transient Moons,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 9, no. 8 (2016): 581–83, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo2742. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a planet not simply capture a passing body today?","a":"Because a body falling towards a planet arrives with enough energy to leave again, and without some way of losing that energy a capture is only temporary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Passing asteroids and comets are often held for a few decades and then escape back into orbit around the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A permanent capture needs friction, a collision or a third body to carry energy away, and the present Solar System offers no such process, which is why the captures are placed early in its history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the moons of Uranus and Neptune count against capture by gas?","a":"Because the ice giants hold only about one Earth mass of hydrogen and helium each, against roughly 300 and 100 at Jupiter and Saturn, yet all four planets have similar numbers of irregular moons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gas drag and a sudden jump in mass both depend on a giant swallowing a great deal of gas, so they fit Jupiter and Saturn far better than Uranus and Neptune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Encounters among several bodies do not depend on how a planet formed, which is their great advantage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are Phobos and Deimos not certainly captured asteroids?","a":"Because no account of them yet explains the Mars system as it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The two small moons are often described as possible captured asteroids, but NASA notes that some evidence contradicts this idea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Simulations instead have them gathering from the outer part of a disc of debris thrown up by a giant impact on Mars, while larger moons formed nearer the planet later fell back onto it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That model predicts that the two moons are a mixture of Martian material and the impactor's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Gravitational capture"},
    "astro-182": {"questions":["A thin exosphere of oxygen, released when particles trapped in Saturn's magnetic field strike its icy surface, surrounds <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the second-largest of the planet's moons after Titan.","A suspected ring around <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> vanished when a dedicated search with the Cassini spacecraft's camera found no orbiting material at all around this icy moon of Saturn."],"abstract":"<b>Rhea</b> is the second-largest moon of Saturn, a cold, airless world with a mean radius of about 764 kilometres (475 miles) that circles the planet every 4.5 days at about 527,000 kilometres (327,500 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Saturn has 293 confirmed moons as of August 2026, and of its six large regular moons besides Titan, between about 400 and 1,600 kilometres (250 and 990 miles) across, Rhea is the biggest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its density, about 1.24 times that of water, suggests a body roughly three quarters ice and one quarter rock, and its surface is mostly water ice, which behaves like rock at temperatures down to −220 °C (−364 °F) in shade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its resistance to changes in its spin, measured during a close pass, suggests that the ice and rock are mixed together rather than separated into a rocky core and an icy mantle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rhea is more heavily cratered than its neighbours Dione and Tethys, perhaps because it lies too far from Saturn for tides to warm it enough to flood and smooth its plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Rhea is wrapped in an exosphere, an extremely thin atmosphere of oxygen and carbon dioxide, and it was the first oxygen atmosphere at a world other than Earth to be sampled directly by a spacecraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oxygen comes from energetic particles in Saturn's magnetic field striking the icy surface and breaking it down, while the source of the carbon dioxide is less certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Absorptions of charged particles near Rhea were read in 2008 as a sign of rings around the moon, but a dedicated imaging search found no orbiting material at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How old Rhea and its neighbours are is disputed, because the tilts of their orbits are hard to reconcile with slow tidal changes lasting since Saturn formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One model has the mid-sized moons gathering again from a disc of debris about 100 million years ago, while another builds them from the spreading edge of a massive icy ring at least 2.5 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Rhea,” https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/rhea/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Saturnian Satellite Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/saturniansatfact.html. [Open access]","Matthew S. Tiscareno et al., “Cassini Imaging Search Rules Out Rings around Rhea,” <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 37, no. 14 (2010): L14205, https://doi.org/10.1029/2010GL043663, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.1764. [Open access]","Matija Ćuk, Luke Dones, and David Nesvorný, “Dynamical Evidence for a Late Formation of Saturn’s Moons,” <i>Astrophysical Journal</i> 820, no. 2 (2016): 97, https://doi.org/10.3847/0004-637X/820/2/97, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.07071. [Open access]","Sébastien Charnoz et al., “Accretion of Saturn’s Mid-Sized Moons during the Viscous Spreading of Young Massive Rings: Solving the Paradox of Silicate-Poor Rings versus Silicate-Rich Moons,” <i>Icarus</i> 216, no. 2 (2011): 535–50, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2011.09.017, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.3360. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.1 Ring and Moon Systems Introduced,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-1-ring-and-moon-systems-introduced. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Saturn Moons,” https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Rhea more cratered than Dione and Tethys?","a":"Because it has probably had less internal warmth to erase its craters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dione and Tethys have more smooth plains, which are probably places where liquid water reached the surface, pooled in hollows such as craters and froze flat, wiping out older craters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rhea lies about 527,000 kilometres (327,500 miles) from Saturn, farther out than either, so the planet's tides flex and heat it less, although a heavier bombardment could also explain the difference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Where does Rhea's thin oxygen atmosphere come from?","a":"From its own icy surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As Saturn's magnetic field sweeps over the moon, energetic particles trapped in it strike the water ice and set off chemical reactions that break it down and release oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The result is extremely thin, about five trillion times less dense than the air at Earth's surface, yet around a hundred times denser at the surface than the exospheres of the Moon or Mercury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the carbon dioxide comes from is less certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some think Saturn's mid-sized moons are young?","a":"Because their orbits do not fit a long, slow history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Calculations of the tilts of the orbits of Tethys, Dione and Rhea suggest either that the moons are much younger than Saturn or that their tidal evolution has been extremely slow, which clashes with the strong tidal heating of Enceladus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One answer has them re-forming from a disc of debris about 100 million years ago, but another model builds them from a massive ring at least 2.5 billion years ago, so the question is open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Rhea (moon)"},
    "astro-183": {"questions":["Dust from a vast ring linked to the distant moon Phoebe drifts inwards and coats the leading face of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, setting off a runaway migration of ice that deepens the contrast.","A chain of mountains about 10 kilometres (6 miles) high runs around the equator of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Saturn's third-largest moon, and may record either a faster early spin or a ring that collapsed."],"abstract":"<b>Iapetus</b> is Saturn's third-largest moon, an icy body with a mean radius of about 736 kilometres (457 miles) that orbits at about 3.56 million kilometres (2.2 million miles) from the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of 293 confirmed moons of Saturn as of August 2026, and its density, only a little above that of liquid water, suggests that it is mostly ice with a smaller share of rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its orbit takes about 79 days and is tilted by nearly 15 degrees, and Saturn's tides have locked it so that one face always points towards the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is famous for being two-toned, because its leading hemisphere, the side facing its direction of travel, reflects only 3 to 5 per cent of sunlight while its trailing hemisphere reflects 50 to 60 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second oddity is a chain of mountains about 10 kilometres (6 miles) high around its equator, which may have formed when Iapetus spun faster or from the collapse of an ancient ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The contrast was noticed in the 1670s, when Iapetus was visible only on one side of Saturn, and was rightly read as a dark side on a moon that keeps one face to the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The darkening seems to begin with dust from outside, since Saturn's vast ring linked to its distant moon Phoebe sheds small particles that drift inwards and many of them strike the dark leading face, which is also redder than the trailing side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The dust alone is not thought to be enough, and the favoured explanation is a runaway migration of water ice that it sets off, because darkened ground absorbs more sunlight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Iapetus rotates so slowly that its days are long enough for ice in the darkened areas to evaporate and settle on colder, brighter ground, making dark areas darker and bright ones brighter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The process is thought to be unique to Iapetus because it combines a slow spin, a size small enough for water to migrate long distances and a ready source of dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Iapetus,” https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/iapetus/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Saturnian Satellite Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/saturniansatfact.html. [Open access]","John R. Spencer and Tilmann Denk, “Formation of Iapetus’ Extreme Albedo Dichotomy by Exogenically Triggered Thermal Ice Migration,” <i>Science</i> 327, no. 5964 (2010): 432–35, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1177132. [Paywalled]","Anne J. Verbiscer, Michael F. Skrutskie, and Douglas P. Hamilton, “Saturn’s Largest Ring,” <i>Nature</i> 461, no. 7267 (2009): 1098–1100, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature08515. [Paywalled]","Mario Sucerquia et al., “The Missing Rings around Solar System Moons,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 691 (2024): A74, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202449453, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.10643. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Saturn Moons,” https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/. [Open access]","Tilmann Denk et al., “Iapetus: Unique Surface Properties and a Global Color Dichotomy from Cassini Imaging,” <i>Science</i> 327, no. 5964 (2010): 435–39, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1177088. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the leading side of Iapetus so dark?","a":"Because dust and ice behave together in a runaway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small particles from a vast, faint ring linked to the distant moon Phoebe drift inwards, and many of them hit the face of Iapetus that leads in its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That darker dust absorbs more sunlight, and the warmed ground loses its ice, which settles on colder ground elsewhere, so the dark areas grow darker and the bright ones brighter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the moon's slow spin matter?","a":"Because it makes each day very long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Iapetus turns about once every 79 days, so dark ground has time to heat up in sunlight and lose its ice before night falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A faster spin would keep temperatures more even and prevent the ice from moving, which is one reason the process is thought to work only here, along with the moon's modest size and its supply of dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the equatorial ridge form?","a":"Nobody yet knows for certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ridge is a chain of mountains about 10 kilometres (6 miles) high that girdles the moon's equator and breaks up into separate peaks on the side facing away from Saturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One idea is that it formed long ago when Iapetus spun much faster, and another is that it is material from a ring that collapsed onto the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Simulations show that a ring around Iapetus could stay stable for a long time, which keeps the second idea alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Iapetus (moon)"},
    "astro-184": {"questions":["Found in telescope photographs in 1948, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has fault canyons up to 12 times as deep as the Grand Canyon, from whose highest cliff a dropped rock would fall for about 10 minutes.","Stress models matched to its ridges and grooves suggest that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may have held an ocean at least 100 kilometres (62 miles) deep beneath a thin icy crust within the last 500 million years."],"abstract":"<b>Miranda</b> is the smallest and innermost of the five major moons of Uranus, an icy body about 470 kilometres (290 miles) across that circles the planet in about 1.4 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of 29 officially recognised moons of Uranus as of August 2026, and like the others it is named for a literary character, the daughter of Prospero in Shakespeare's <i>The Tempest</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The planet's system of rings and moons is tilted by 98 degrees, like Uranus itself, and Miranda's orbit is inclined by a little over 4 degrees, far more than those of the other major moons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Like its larger neighbours, it is thought to be made of roughly equal amounts of water ice and silicate rock, with a surface darkened by carbon-rich material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was found in telescope photographs in 1948, the last moon of Uranus to be discovered before the Voyager 2 spacecraft flew through the system in 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Voyager 2's images showed one of the strangest landscapes in the Solar System, including three large, lightly cratered regions called coronae, made of ridges and valleys and cut off from older, cratered ground by sharp boundaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fault canyons are as much as 12 times as deep as the Grand Canyon, and in its weak gravity a rock dropped from the highest cliff would take about 10 minutes to fall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One old explanation had Miranda smashed apart and reassembled, but the faults at the edge of Arden Corona point instead to warm material welling up from inside, which removes the need for a shattering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mapping of its craters, ridges and grooves, compared with models of the stresses on its crust, favours an icy shell no more than about 30 kilometres (19 miles) thick when they formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same work suggests that Miranda may have held an ocean at least 100 kilometres (62 miles) deep within the last 100 to 500 million years, which makes it a possible ocean world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Miranda,” https://science.nasa.gov/uranus/moons/miranda/. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Uranian Satellite Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/uraniansatfact.html. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Uranus Moons,” https://science.nasa.gov/uranus/moons/. [Open access]","Robert T. Pappalardo, Stephen J. Reynolds, and Ronald Greeley, “Extensional Tilt Blocks on Miranda: Evidence for an Upwelling Origin of Arden Corona,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 102, no. E6 (1997): 13369–79, https://doi.org/10.1029/97JE00802. [Paywalled]","Caleb Strom et al., “Constraining Ocean and Ice Shell Thickness on Miranda from Surface Geological Structures and Stress Modeling,” <i>Planetary Science Journal</i> 5, no. 10 (2024): 226, https://doi.org/10.3847/PSJ/ad77d7. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.1 Ring and Moon Systems Introduced,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-1-ring-and-moon-systems-introduced. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Miranda once thought to have been shattered?","a":"Because its surface looks like a patchwork of mismatched parts, with lightly cratered coronae set against older, heavily cratered ground along sharp boundaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One suggestion was that a huge collision broke the moon apart and the pieces came back together haphazardly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Faulting around Arden Corona tells against this, since the faults face outwards in the pattern expected if warm material rose from inside and stretched the crust above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why would a dropped rock fall for so long?","a":"Because Miranda is small and its cliffs are enormous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The moon is only about 470 kilometres (290 miles) across, so its gravity is very weak, and its fault canyons are up to 12 times as deep as the Grand Canyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In that weak pull a rock dropped from the highest cliff would take about 10 minutes to reach the bottom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How could such a small moon have had an ocean?","a":"Because its surface records stresses that a thick, cold shell could hardly have produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mapping of its ridges and grooves, compared with models of the forces that tides, thickening ice and shifts in its spin would apply, fits best with an icy crust no more than about 30 kilometres (19 miles) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same work suggests an ocean at least 100 kilometres (62 miles) deep within the last 100 to 500 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Miranda (moon)"},
    "astro-185": {"questions":["Gas escaping from Pluto that freezes onto a cold winter pole and is altered by sunlight explains the dark red cap on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the largest of Pluto's five known moons.","Only after the discovery of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1978 could Pluto's mass be measured, revealing a world far smaller than Earth with a companion about half its own size."],"abstract":"<b>Charon</b> is the largest of Pluto's five moons, about 1,214 kilometres (754 miles) across, or roughly half the size of Pluto, which makes it the largest known moon relative to the body it orbits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The two lie about 19,640 kilometres (12,200 miles) apart and are mutually tidally locked, so that Charon's 6.4-day orbit matches Pluto's rotation and each always shows the other the same face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charon never rises or sets in Pluto's sky but hangs over one spot, where it would look as large as eight full moons seen from Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pair is sometimes called a double dwarf planet, and Pluto's four small moons, Nix, Hydra, Kerberos and Styx, orbit the two together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Charon was discovered in 1978, when telescope images of Pluto looked strangely stretched, and its orbit allowed Pluto's mass to be measured for the first time, showing it to be far less than Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The New Horizons spacecraft flew past in July 2015 and found signs of tectonics on Charon, including a belt of rough ground and valleys crossing it as if forces had tried to pull it apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its most striking feature is a dark red cap over its north pole, thought to form when gas escaping from Pluto is frozen onto Charon's cold winter pole and turned by sunlight into darker, more complex compounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pair's shared orbital axis suggests a linked history and an origin in a collision, and simulations that include the strength of rock and ice show Charon being caught largely intact in a collision in which the two bodies briefly stuck together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In that picture Charon kept its core and most of its mantle and was then pushed outwards by tides to its present wide, circular orbit, so it could be as ancient as Pluto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The small moons show how strongly the central pair shapes its system, since three of them are linked by a resonance and Nix and Hydra tumble chaotically under the pull of Pluto and Charon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Charon,” https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/pluto/moons/charon/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.4 Pluto and Charon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-4-pluto-and-charon. [Open access]","S. A. Stern et al., “The Pluto System: Initial Results from Its Exploration by New Horizons,” <i>Science</i> 350, no. 6258 (2015): aad1815, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aad1815, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.07704. [Open access]","W. M. Grundy et al., “The Formation of Charon’s Red Poles from Seasonally Cold-Trapped Volatiles,” <i>Nature</i> 539, no. 7627 (2016): 65–68, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature19340. [Paywalled]","M. R. Showalter and D. P. Hamilton, “Resonant Interactions and Chaotic Rotation of Pluto’s Small Moons,” <i>Nature</i> 522, no. 7554 (2015): 45–49, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14469. [Paywalled]","C. Adeene Denton et al., “Capture of an Ancient Charon around Pluto,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 18, no. 1 (2025): 37–43, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-024-01612-0. [Paywalled]","NASA Science, “Pluto Moons,” https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/pluto/moons/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Charon hang over one spot on Pluto?","a":"Because the two bodies are locked to each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charon takes 6.4 days to orbit Pluto, and Pluto takes the same 6.4 days to turn once, so Charon neither rises nor sets but stays above the same place on Pluto's surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same side of Charon always faces Pluto too, a state called double tidal locking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From that one spot Charon would look about as large as eight full moons seen from Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Charon's north pole red?","a":"Because Pluto keeps feeding it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gas escaping from Pluto's atmosphere can be caught and frozen onto Charon's winter pole, which grows extremely cold during the moon's long seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> While it is trapped there, light breaks the frozen gas down into more complex and less volatile compounds, and a model of the process matches the dark red cap seen by the New Horizons spacecraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might Charon be as old as Pluto?","a":"Because it may have been captured rather than built from debris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Simulations that include the strength of the colliding bodies show that a collision could leave Pluto and Charon briefly joined instead of merged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Charon would then be raised into orbit and drift outwards under tides while keeping its core and most of its mantle, so it would be an ancient body rather than a newly assembled one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Charon (moon)"},
    "astro-186": {"questions":["Dust glowing at millimetre wavelengths around the young planet PDS 70c, about 34 au from its star, revealed a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> less than about 1.2 au in radius.","A balance between material flowing in and young moons spiralling into the planet within a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may explain why each gas giant's moons add up to about one ten-thousandth of its mass."],"abstract":"A <b>circumplanetary disc</b> is a flattened disc of gas and dust around a young giant planet that is still growing, fed by material flowing in from the larger disc around the star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The regular moons of the giant planets, those on near-circular orbits close to the plane of the planet's equator, are thought to have grown by accretion inside such discs, which is why they share the direction of their planet's spin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> This sets them apart from the irregular moons, whose distant, tilted and often retrograde orbits cannot be reconciled with an origin in a disc and point instead to capture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In one model, gas and solids stream into the disc from orbit around the Sun, and moons grow from the solids while some are dragged inwards by the gas and lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That balance between supply and loss holds the total mass of the moons to about one ten-thousandth of the planet's mass, close to what is seen at Jupiter, Saturn and Uranus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The same model suggests that discs around Jupiter-mass planets elsewhere would make their largest moons no bigger than about the size of the Moon or Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A disc of this kind has been observed directly as a compact source of millimetre-wave emission from dust around the young planet PDS 70c, about 34 au from its star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The emission comes from a region less than about 1.2 au in radius, close to the size expected for a circumplanetary disc trimmed to a third of the planet's zone of gravitational control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not every regular moon need come from such a disc, since another model forms moons one by one from a massive ring spreading outwards, matching the moons of Saturn, Uranus and Neptune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A disc of debris spreading quickly from close to a planet can instead yield a single large moon, as for Earth and Pluto, and one model builds Mars's small moons Phobos and Deimos from the debris of a giant impact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["David Jewitt and Nader Haghighipour, “Irregular Satellites of the Planets: Products of Capture in the Early Solar System,” <i>Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics</i> 45 (2007): 261–95, https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.astro.44.051905.092459, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0703059. [Open access]","Robin M. Canup and William R. Ward, “A Common Mass Scaling for Satellite Systems of Gaseous Planets,” <i>Nature</i> 441, no. 7095 (2006): 834–39, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04860. [Paywalled]","Myriam Benisty et al., “A Circumplanetary Disk around PDS70c,” <i>Astrophysical Journal Letters</i> 916, no. 1 (2021): L2, https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/ac0f83, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.07123. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.1 Ring and Moon Systems Introduced,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-1-ring-and-moon-systems-introduced. [Open access]","A. Crida and S. Charnoz, “Formation of Regular Satellites from Ancient Massive Rings in the Solar System,” <i>Science</i> 338, no. 6111 (2012): 1196–99, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1226477, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.3808. [Open access]","Pascal Rosenblatt et al., “Accretion of Phobos and Deimos in an Extended Debris Disc Stirred by Transient Moons,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 9, no. 8 (2016): 581–83, https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo2742. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can the irregular moons not have formed in the disc?","a":"Because their orbits are wrong for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moons that grow in a disc around a planet should circle close to it, near the plane of its equator and in the direction of its spin, as the regular moons do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The irregular moons are far from their planets on tilted, often retrograde orbits, and neither those tilts nor the great size of the orbits can be reconciled with a disc, so they are thought to have been captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do a giant's moons add up to so little of its mass?","a":"Because a disc both builds moons and destroys them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In one model, gas and solids keep flowing into the disc while moons grow from the solids, but the gas also drags the moons inwards until some spiral into the planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The balance between that supply and that loss holds the moons' combined mass to about one ten-thousandth of the planet's, which is what Jupiter, Saturn and Uranus show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the disc around PDS 70c matter?","a":"Because it shows that such discs are real and not only a model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Observations at millimetre wavelengths found a compact patch of dust emission sitting on the young planet PDS 70c, separate from the disc around its star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The patch is less than about 1.2 au in radius, close to the size expected for a disc trimmed by the planet's gravity, and it holds dust equal to a few hundredths of an Earth mass or less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Circumplanetary disk"},
    "astro-187": {"questions":["Kept slightly oval by repeated tugs from Europa and Ganymede in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Io's orbit makes the moon flex and heat up, powering hundreds of active volcanoes.","Resonances like Jupiter's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also tie together three of Pluto's small moons and five of the six planets of the star TOI-178, whose orbital periods stand in simple ratios."],"abstract":"The <b>Laplace resonance</b> is the orbital lock that links three of Jupiter's large moons, Io, Europa and Ganymede, whose orbital periods stand very nearly in the ratio 1:2:4.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Io takes about 1.77 days to circle Jupiter, Europa about 3.55 days and Ganymede about 7.15 days, so Europa completes one orbit for every two of Io's and Ganymede one for every two of Europa's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because their periods fit together so neatly, the moons' pulls on one another arrive with precise timing, so their small effects build up instead of averaging away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Besides the two pairs of 2:1 resonances, between Io and Europa and between Europa and Ganymede, it is a resonance involving all three moons at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Similar three-body links are known elsewhere, joining three of Pluto's small moons and five of the six planets around the star TOI-178.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The resonance matters because the repeated tugs from Europa and Ganymede keep Io's orbit slightly oval instead of letting it become circular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that path Io moves nearer to and farther from Jupiter and twists back and forth, and the flexing heats its interior, the process called tidal heating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That heat makes Io the most volcanically active world in the Solar System, with hundreds of volcanoes, some of them throwing up fountains of lava dozens of kilometres high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A calculation published in 1979 argued that tidal heating had probably melted a large part of Io, and in the same year Voyager 1 saw the first signs of its volcanoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The tides also push the moons slowly outwards, and simulations suggest that Callisto will very likely be drawn into resonance in about 1.5 billion years, so the present lock should last at least that long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.2 The Galilean Moons of Jupiter,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-2-the-galilean-moons-of-jupiter. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Jovian Satellite Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/joviansatfact.html. [Open access]","G. Lari, M. Saillenfest, and M. Fenucci, “Long-Term Evolution of the Galilean Satellites: The Capture of Callisto into Resonance,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 639 (2020): A40, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202037445, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.01106. [Open access]","S. J. Peale, P. Cassen, and R. T. Reynolds, “Melting of Io by Tidal Dissipation,” <i>Science</i> 203, no. 4383 (1979): 892–94, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.203.4383.892. [Paywalled]","M. R. Showalter and D. P. Hamilton, “Resonant Interactions and Chaotic Rotation of Pluto’s Small Moons,” <i>Nature</i> 522, no. 7554 (2015): 45–49, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14469. [Paywalled]","A. Leleu et al., “Six Transiting Planets and a Chain of Laplace Resonances in TOI-178,” <i>Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics</i> 649 (2021): A26, https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202039767, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.09260. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Io,” https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/moons/io/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the resonance heat Io?","a":"Because it keeps Io's orbit from becoming a perfect circle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jupiter's gravity stretches Io into a slightly elongated shape, and if Io always kept the same face to Jupiter on a circular path, that bulge would produce no heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The timed tugs from Europa and Ganymede keep the orbit a little oval, so Io moves nearer and farther and twists back and forth on every circuit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That flexing heats the moon, much as bending a wire coat hanger back and forth warms the metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do small pulls between the moons matter so much?","a":"Because in a resonance they arrive with precise timing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Europa and Ganymede are far less massive than Jupiter, but their pulls on Io are precisely timed by the 1:2:4 pattern of their periods, so their effects build up instead of averaging away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> NASA describes Io as caught in a tug-of-war between Jupiter's gravity and these smaller, timed pulls from its two neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Will the Laplace resonance last for ever?","a":"Not necessarily, but it should survive for a very long time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tides raised between Io and Jupiter push all three linked moons slowly outwards, and as Ganymede moves out it approaches a 2:1 resonance with Callisto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In simulations Callisto was drawn into resonance every time, about 1.5 billion years from now, and afterwards the Laplace resonance was kept in some outcomes but generally broken in others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-188": {"questions":["Not designed to detect life, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> will measure the thickness of Europa's icy shell, the make-up of its ocean and any plumes venting water into space.","Named in 2017 after the swift sailing ships of the 19th century, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries nine instruments, among them an ice-penetrating radar, to study Jupiter's moon Europa."],"abstract":"<b>Europa Clipper</b> is a NASA spacecraft, launched on 14 October 2024, whose main goal is to find out whether there are places beneath the icy surface of Jupiter's moon Europa that could support life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It is due to reach Jupiter in April 2030 after a journey of about 2.9 billion kilometres (1.8 billion miles), and then to fly past Europa 49 times, typically coming within 25 to 100 kilometres (16 to 62 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rather than orbiting Europa itself it will circle Jupiter, a plan chosen for its strong science and lower cost and risk that also reduces its exposure to Jupiter's intense radiation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It carries nine science instruments, including cameras, spectrometers, an ice-penetrating radar and a magnetometer, plus a gravity experiment that uses its radio, and all of them can work at once on every pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It is not a life-detection mission, and it was named in March 2017 after the fast clipper ships that sailed Earth's oceans in the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Europa matters to astrobiology, the study of life beyond Earth, because life as known needs liquid water, chemistry and energy, and Europa may have all three and is as old as Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its salty ocean beneath the ice may hold about twice as much water as all of Earth's oceans, and the best evidence for it is a magnetic signal measured by the Galileo spacecraft, most likely produced by a global layer of salt water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Europa also seems to have a rocky seafloor where hydrothermal activity could supply chemical nutrients, and conditions there may have stayed much the same for about 4 billion years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Europa Clipper is to measure the thickness of the icy shell and how the ocean exchanges material with the surface, the ocean's composition and the moon's geology, including plumes like those the Hubble Space Telescope has probably seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A roadmap for exploring ocean worlds ranks Europa, with Enceladus and Titan, among the highest-priority targets in the near term, and Europa Clipper is the first mission designed to study it in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Europa Clipper,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/europa-clipper/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Europa Clipper Mission Science,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/europa-clipper/mission-science/. [Open access]","Robert T. Pappalardo et al., “Science Overview of the Europa Clipper Mission,” <i>Space Science Reviews</i> 220, no. 4 (2024): 40, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-024-01070-5. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Why Europa? Ingredients for Life,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/europa-clipper/why-europa-ingredients-for-life/. [Open access]","Amanda R. Hendrix et al., “The NASA Roadmap to Ocean Worlds,” <i>Astrobiology</i> 19, no. 1 (2019): 1–27, https://doi.org/10.1089/ast.2018.1955. [Open access]","NASA, “NASA Missions Provide New Insights into ‘Ocean Worlds’ in Our Solar System,” news release, 13 April 2017, https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-missions-provide-new-insights-into-ocean-worlds-in-our-solar-system/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Europa Clipper Mission Timeline,” https://science.nasa.gov/mission/europa-clipper/mission-timeline/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why send a spacecraft to Europa in particular?","a":"Because it may have every ingredient that life as known requires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Scientists look for liquid water, chemistry and energy, and for a world old enough for life to have had time to start, and Europa may have all of these and is as old as Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A roadmap for exploring ocean worlds ranks Europa, along with Enceladus and Titan, as a highest-priority target in the near term, partly because its ocean may touch a rocky seafloor and exchange material with the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why will the spacecraft orbit Jupiter rather than Europa?","a":"Because that plan offered strong science at lower cost and risk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Before the mission was selected, planners compared several concepts, among them a spacecraft orbiting Europa and a lander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The chosen design circles Jupiter in step with Europa's orbit, so it can make repeated close flybys of the moon while spending less time in Jupiter's intense radiation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It is to make 49 such flybys, typically passing within 25 to 100 kilometres (16 to 62 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can a spacecraft study an ocean it cannot see?","a":"By measuring its effects from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best evidence for the ocean so far came from the Galileo spacecraft's magnetometer, which detected a magnetic field induced within Europa as Jupiter's field swept past, most likely by a global layer of salt water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Europa Clipper carries a magnetometer too, along with an ice-penetrating radar and a gravity experiment, to study the icy shell and the ocean beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Europa Clipper"},
    "astro-189": {"questions":["Ahuna Mons on Ceres, about 4 kilometres (2.5 miles) high, and huge domes near Wright Mons on Pluto are landforms of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, built from icy material erupted from warm interiors.","Galileo images showed that many features on Europa once credited to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were better explained by faulting or by blobs of warm ice rising through the moon's frozen shell."],"abstract":"<b>Cryovolcanism</b> is volcanic activity on an icy world, in which the erupting material is not molten rock but water-rich liquid such as brine, rising from a warm interior to the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It can take the form of jets of vapour and ice grains or of slower outpourings of icy material that spread across the ground or pile up into domes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Young, smooth plains on icy moons of Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune have long been explained by cryovolcanic resurfacing, in which erupted material buries older craters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Closer looks have made that case harder, because Galileo images of Europa showed that many features once taken as cryovolcanic are better explained by faulting or by blobs of warm ice rising through the shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Effusive cryovolcanism remains a possible explanation for some of Europa's features, though strong evidence for resurfacing on a large scale is lacking, and the Hubble Space Telescope has seen probable plumes rising from the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The clearest active example is Enceladus, where jets of fine icy particles rise from warm fractures at the south pole, carried by water vapour probably venting from reservoirs of liquid beneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The eruptions appear continuous, throwing out material at about 400 metres (1,300 feet) per second and feeding a halo of ice dust that supplies Saturn's E ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On the dwarf planet Ceres, Ahuna Mons, a mountain about 4 kilometres (2.5 miles) high and 17 kilometres (11 miles) wide, is best explained as a dome built by thick, slow extrusions of icy material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pluto shows cryovolcanism of a kind seen nowhere else, with domes several kilometres high around Wright Mons that needed more than 10,000 cubic kilometres (2,400 cubic miles) of erupted material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not every icy plume comes from inside, since the nitrogen geysers seen on Neptune's moon Triton, about 10 kilometres (6 miles) high, draw their energy from sunlight warming the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sarah A. Fagents, “Considerations for Effusive Cryovolcanism on Europa: The Post-Galileo Perspective,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 108, no. E12 (2003), https://doi.org/10.1029/2003JE002128. [Paywalled]","C. C. Porco et al., “Cassini Observes the Active South Pole of Enceladus,” <i>Science</i> 311, no. 5766 (2006): 1393–1401, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1123013. [Paywalled]","O. Ruesch et al., “Cryovolcanism on Ceres,” <i>Science</i> 353, no. 6303 (2016): aaf4286, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaf4286. [Paywalled]","Kelsi N. Singer et al., “Large-Scale Cryovolcanic Resurfacing on Pluto,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 13 (2022): 1542, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-29056-3. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “12.3 Titan and Triton,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/12-3-titan-and-triton. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Enceladus,” https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/enceladus/. [Open access]","NASA, “NASA Missions Provide New Insights into ‘Ocean Worlds’ in Our Solar System,” news release, 13 April 2017, https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-missions-provide-new-insights-into-ocean-worlds-in-our-solar-system/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Galileo's images weaken the case for widespread cryovolcanism on Europa?","a":"Because sharper pictures showed other explanations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the Galileo spacecraft, Europa was thought to have been resurfaced extensively by eruptions from a global ocean, but images much more detailed than Voyager's showed that many supposed cryovolcanic features fit faulting or rising blobs of warm ice better.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Small, runny outflows are still supported by observations and models, so cryovolcanism remains possible there, but not on the scale once claimed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are Triton's plumes not driven like those of Enceladus?","a":"Because their energy comes from the Sun, not from inside the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On Triton a polar cap of frozen nitrogen appears to be evaporating, and Voyager images showed that this evaporation drives geysers of nitrogen gas rising about 10 kilometres (6 miles), darkened by dust carried up from the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Enceladus the jets are carried by water vapour probably venting from liquid reservoirs below warm fractures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was cryovolcanism on Pluto a surprise?","a":"Because a small, distant world was not expected to have enough heat for it so late in its history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One region of Pluto with very few craters is dominated by enormous rises with hummocky flanks, unlike anything else imaged in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Building them needed several eruption sites and more than 10,000 cubic kilometres (2,400 cubic miles) of material, which suggests that Pluto kept more heat, or had more of it, than was thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cryovolcano"},
    "astro-190": {"questions":["Shallow <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> up to magnitude 5.5 and over 10 minutes long come from the Moon shrinking as it cools, which cracks its crust along young thrust faults.","Reinterpreted records of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from seismometers left by Apollo astronauts show that the Moon has a small core, with a solid inner part inside a fluid outer part."],"abstract":"<b>Moonquakes</b> are the lunar equivalent of earthquakes, shakings of the Moon that were revealed by seismometers Apollo astronauts left on its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deep moonquakes, several hundred kilometres below the surface, are tidal events caused by Earth's gravity tugging and stretching the Moon's interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shallower moonquakes, 20 to 30 kilometres (12 to 19 miles) down, come from the Moon shrinking as it cools, and they can reach magnitude 5.5 and last more than 10 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others are set off by meteoroid impacts, and the shallowest of all by rock expanding and contracting as the surface swings between the frigid lunar night and the hot lunar day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seismometers at four Apollo landing sites recorded 28 shallow moonquakes between 1969 and 1977.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Moonquakes also serve as probes of the deep Moon, and the Apollo records confirmed that it lacks a large metal core like Earth's, while a later reanalysis found a small core with a solid inner part and a fluid outer part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The core reaches only about 20 per cent of the way from the centre to the surface, and in the mostly solid mantle above it a partly molten zone near the core may be where some moonquakes begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That molten layer is the usual reading of how the Moon flexes under Earth's tides, though models without melt fit the same measurements equally well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Small cliffs called lobate scarps, formed by thrust faults as the Moon contracts, are found across its surface and look younger than a billion years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Eight shallow quakes have been placed within 30 kilometres (19 miles) of young thrust faults, cliffs that form as the shrinking crust breaks, which strongly suggests that the Moon is still tectonically active.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Moonquakes,” https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moonquakes/. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Moon Composition &amp; Structure,” https://science.nasa.gov/moon/composition/. [Open access]","Renee C. Weber et al., “Seismic Detection of the Lunar Core,” <i>Science</i> 331, no. 6015 (2011): 309–12, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1199375. [Paywalled]","Thomas R. Watters et al., “Shallow Seismic Activity and Young Thrust Faults on the Moon,” <i>Nature Geoscience</i> 12, no. 6 (2019): 411–17, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-019-0362-2. [Paywalled]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “9.1 General Properties of the Moon,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/9-1-general-properties-of-the-moon. [Open access]","Thomas R. Watters et al., “Evidence of Recent Thrust Faulting on the Moon Revealed by the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter Camera,” <i>Science</i> 329, no. 5994 (2010): 936–40, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1189590. [Paywalled]","Michaela Walterová, Marie Běhounková, and Michael Efroimsky, “Is There a Semi-Molten Layer at the Base of the Lunar Mantle?,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets</i> 128, no. 7 (2023): e2022JE007652, https://doi.org/10.1029/2022JE007652, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.02476. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the Moon shake at all?","a":"Because it is still being squeezed and stretched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Earth's gravity tugs on the Moon's interior and sets off deep moonquakes, while the Moon has been cooling and shrinking since it formed nearly 4.5 billion years ago, which cracks its brittle crust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meteoroid impacts and the swing between the cold lunar night and the hot lunar day add smaller quakes near the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do shallow quakes suggest the Moon is still active?","a":"Because some of them seem to come from young faults.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Better estimates of where the Apollo-era shallow quakes began place eight of them within 30 kilometres (19 miles) of thrust faults, cliffs formed as the crust breaks while the Moon shrinks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Six of those eight happened when the Moon was near the farthest point of its orbit, when tidal stresses add to the squeeze, which points to the faults still slipping today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Moon's core so small?","a":"Probably because of how the Moon formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The core reaches only about 20 per cent of the way from the centre to the surface, a smaller share than in other well-studied rocky worlds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lunar rocks are poor in metals compared with Earth's, and the Apollo seismometers confirmed that the Moon has no large metal core, which fits a violent birth that left little iron to sink to the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-191": {"questions":["The first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to be found, Ceres, turned up in 1801 during a hunt for a planet that many astronomers thought was missing between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter.","An airless leftover of the Solar System's formation about 4.6 billion years ago, the typical <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is irregular and often cratered, and more than 150 are known to have small moons."],"abstract":"An <b>asteroid</b> is a small, airless, rocky or metallic body orbiting the Sun, a remnant of the formation of the Solar System about 4.6 billion years ago, and asteroids are also called minor planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ceres, just under 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) across, was long counted the largest of them, and below it sizes run down from Pallas and Vesta, about 500 kilometres (310 miles), to bodies less than 10 metres (33 feet) wide, and all of them together hold less than a thousandth of the mass of Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Small asteroids vastly outnumber large ones, and there are about a hundred times more of them 10 kilometres (6 miles) across than 100 kilometres (62 miles) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most are irregular in shape and pitted with craters, they rotate as they orbit, sometimes tumbling, and more than 150 are known to have small companion moons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their surfaces fall into broad classes, dark carbon-rich, stony and metallic, and the differences are linked to how far from the Sun each one formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The first, Ceres, was found on 1 January 1801 at 2.8 au from the Sun by astronomers hunting a planet thought to be missing between Mars and Jupiter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Three more turned up on similar orbits within a few years, and by 1890 more than 300 were known, when photography began to speed up the search.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each is given a number in order of discovery and sometimes a name, first from the goddesses of Greek and Roman myth and later from people, places and much else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The JPL Small-Body Database listed more than 1.5 million asteroids as of November 2026, and astronomers estimate that early work with the Vera Rubin Observatory will bring the catalogue to some 6 million within a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For nearly two centuries asteroids were thought to have formed roughly where they orbit now, but the mix of types found across the belt suggests the young planets stirred in material from all over the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Asteroids,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 21 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “13.1 Asteroids,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-1-asteroids. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Asteroid Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 21 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Solar System Dynamics, “Small-Body Database Query,” queried 12 November 2026, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_query.html. [Open access]","F. E. DeMeo and B. Carry, “Solar System Evolution from Compositional Mapping of the Asteroid Belt,” <i>Nature</i> 505, no. 7485 (2014): 629–34, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12908, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.2787. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Asteroid Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/asteroidfact.html. [Open access]","IAU Minor Planet Center, “Latest Published Data,” accessed 12 November 2026, https://minorplanetcenter.net/mpc/summary. [Open access]","Alessandro Morbidelli et al., “The Dynamical Evolution of the Asteroid Belt,” in <i>Asteroids IV</i> (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2015), https://doi.org/10.2458/azu_uapress_9780816532131-ch026, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.06204. [Open access]","Sean N. Raymond and Andre Izidoro, “The Empty Primordial Asteroid Belt,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 9 (2017): e1701138, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701138, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.04242. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were astronomers searching between Mars and Jupiter in 1801?","a":"Because many expected an additional planet to orbit in the wide gap between Mars and Jupiter, and Ceres was at first taken for that missing world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When three more small bodies were found on similar orbits within a few years, it became clear that the gap held a whole group of objects, each much smaller than the Moon, rather than a single planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the count of known asteroids keep rising?","a":"Because the number of asteroids climbs steeply as their size falls, so ever smaller and fainter bodies remain to be found: there are about a hundred times more 10 kilometres (6 miles) across than 100 kilometres (62 miles) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The JPL database had passed 1.5 million asteroids by November 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Astronomers expect the Vera Rubin Observatory, with the largest digital camera ever built, to bring the catalogue to some 6 million within a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are asteroids sorted into types such as dark, stony and metallic?","a":"Because the light they reflect reveals what their surfaces are made of: dark C-types are carbon-rich, S-types stony and M-types metallic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> These differences are linked to how far from the Sun each formed, and some asteroids were heated enough to melt, with iron sinking to the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The mixture of types now found throughout the belt suggests that material was stirred in from across the young Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Asteroid"},
    "astro-192": {"questions":["Measured from its gravitational pull on planets and spacecraft, the total mass of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between Mars and Jupiter comes to only about four ten-thousandths of Earth's.","Stony asteroids dominate the inner part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and dark carbon-rich ones its outer part, yet every type of asteroid turns up in every region of it."],"abstract":"The <b>asteroid belt</b> is the broad region between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter where most known asteroids travel, on orbits that take between 3.3 and 6 years to circle the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than three quarters of known asteroids lie in it, and the JPL Small-Body Database counted more than 1.3 million main-belt asteroids as of November 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is estimated to hold between 1.1 and 1.9 million asteroids larger than 1 kilometre (0.6 miles) across, along with millions of smaller ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its largest member is the dwarf planet Ceres, which by NASA's estimate makes up about a quarter of the belt's total mass on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The belt is not uniform in make-up, for stony S-types dominate its inner part and dark carbon-rich C-types its outer part, though every type is found in every region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Films often show a crowded field of tumbling rocks, but the belt's volume is so large that objects down to 1 kilometre (0.6 miles) in size are typically several million kilometres apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is why spacecraft such as Galileo, Cassini, Rosetta and New Horizons were able to cross it without a collision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its total mass, measured from its gravitational pull on the planets and on spacecraft, is about four ten-thousandths of the mass of Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All the asteroids together have less mass than the Moon, and the belt is thought to hold only a small fraction of the mass it had when the planets were forming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The usual explanation is that the belt began far more massive and was later strongly depleted, though a newer model proposes that it formed almost empty and was filled with planetesimals scattered in from elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “13.1 Asteroids,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-1-asteroids. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Asteroid Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 21 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/. [Open access]","E. V. Pitjeva and N. P. Pitjev, “Masses of the Main Asteroid Belt and the Kuiper Belt from the Motions of Planets and Spacecraft,” <i>Astronomy Letters</i> 44, no. 8–9 (2018): 554–66, https://doi.org/10.1134/S1063773718090050, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.05191. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Solar System Dynamics, “Small-Body Database Query,” queried 12 November 2026, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_query.html. [Open access]","F. E. DeMeo and B. Carry, “Solar System Evolution from Compositional Mapping of the Asteroid Belt,” <i>Nature</i> 505, no. 7485 (2014): 629–34, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12908, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.2787. [Open access]","Alessandro Morbidelli et al., “The Dynamical Evolution of the Asteroid Belt,” in <i>Asteroids IV</i> (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2015), https://doi.org/10.2458/azu_uapress_9780816532131-ch026, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.06204. [Open access]","Sean N. Raymond and Andre Izidoro, “The Empty Primordial Asteroid Belt,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 9 (2017): e1701138, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701138, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.04242. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Ceres: Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 22 April 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/ceres/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could spacecraft fly through the belt without hitting anything?","a":"Because the belt is enormous and its contents are thinly spread: the typical gap between objects down to about 1 kilometre (0.6 miles) across is several million kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pictures that show it crowded draw each asteroid far too large, and a traveller inside it would see far more empty space than asteroids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Galileo, Cassini, Rosetta and New Horizons all had to cross it on their way to the outer Solar System, and did so without a collision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the mass of the belt so small?","a":"Because, on the usual view, the region lost most of the material it once held, and its present mass is only a small fraction of the original.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It now amounts to about four ten-thousandths of Earth's mass, measured from the belt's pull on the planets and on spacecraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One model suggests instead that the belt formed almost empty and received its asteroids as leftovers scattered in from other parts of the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are different kinds of asteroid found in different parts of the belt?","a":"Because composition partly follows distance from the Sun, with S-types dominating the inner belt and C-types the outer belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> This pattern was once read as a frozen record of the temperature across the young disc, but surveys of tens of thousands of asteroids now find every type in every region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That mixing points to planets that migrated and scattered material across the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Asteroid belt"},
    "astro-193": {"questions":["Weighing the whole asteroid belt at about four ten-thousandths of Earth's mass undercuts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since all the supposed fragments together come to less than the Moon.","Dark carbon-rich, stony and metallic asteroids mixed throughout the belt point to many parent bodies formed in different places, not to the single world of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the early 1800s."],"abstract":"The <b>exploded planet hypothesis</b> is the idea, put forward early in the 19th century, that the asteroids are the fragments of a single planet that once orbited between Mars and Jupiter and was destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew from the discovery of Ceres in 1801 in the gap where many astronomers expected a missing planet, followed within a few years by three more small bodies on similar orbits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The discoverer of the second, Pallas, suggested that the two were pieces of one shattered world, and he found Vesta in 1807 while searching for further fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As more asteroids were found their orbits proved far too varied, and an American treatise on meteors judged in 1867 that the hypothesis had lost all claim to probability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Planetary science now treats the asteroids as leftovers of planet formation, bodies that never assembled into a planet at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The most direct objection is mass, for the whole belt, weighed by its gravitational pull on the planets and on spacecraft, holds about four ten-thousandths of Earth's mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> All the asteroids together have less mass than the Moon, far short of any of the planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their make-up also argues against a single parent, since dark carbon-rich, stony and metallic asteroids and the melted crusts of a few are mixed throughout the belt in a way that points to bodies formed in many different places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The usual view is that the gravity of the newly formed Jupiter ended planet-building in the region and made the bodies there collide and break apart, and Ceres is described as an embryonic planet that never finished forming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The belt is also thought to have lost most of its original material, so what remains is only a small fraction of what the region once held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “13.1 Asteroids,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-1-asteroids. [Open access]","NASA Science, “4 Vesta,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 23 April 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/4-vesta/. [Open access]","Daniel Kirkwood, <i>Meteoric Astronomy: A Treatise on Shooting-Stars, Fire-Balls, and Aerolites</i> (Philadelphia: J. B. Lippincott, 1867), 105–11, https://archive.org/details/meteoricastronom00kirk. [Open access]","E. V. Pitjeva and N. P. Pitjev, “Masses of the Main Asteroid Belt and the Kuiper Belt from the Motions of Planets and Spacecraft,” <i>Astronomy Letters</i> 44, no. 8–9 (2018): 554–66, https://doi.org/10.1134/S1063773718090050, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.05191. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Asteroid Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 21 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/. [Open access]","F. E. DeMeo and B. Carry, “Solar System Evolution from Compositional Mapping of the Asteroid Belt,” <i>Nature</i> 505, no. 7485 (2014): 629–34, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12908, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.2787. [Open access]","Alessandro Morbidelli et al., “The Dynamical Evolution of the Asteroid Belt,” in <i>Asteroids IV</i> (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2015), https://doi.org/10.2458/azu_uapress_9780816532131-ch026, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.06204. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Ceres: Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 22 April 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/ceres/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did finding more asteroids weaken the idea?","a":"Because each new find made the group look less like the pieces of one body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ceres was quickly followed by three more small bodies on similar orbits, showing that the gap held many small worlds rather than one missing planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1867 the orbits were known to differ so much that the closest approach to the Sun of some asteroids lay farther out than the most distant point of others, and the destroyed-planet idea was judged to have lost all claim to probability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the belt's small mass such a problem for the hypothesis?","a":"Because there is too little material: the belt's mass, measured from its gravitational pull on the planets and on spacecraft, is about four ten-thousandths of Earth's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> All the asteroids combined have less mass than the Moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The region is also thought to hold only a small fraction of the mass it had when the planets formed, which leaves even less room for a lost world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did no planet form in the belt in the first place?","a":"Because the gravity of the newly formed Jupiter is thought to have ended planet-building in the region and made the small bodies there collide and fragment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ceres, the largest object in the belt, is described as an embryonic planet that began to form but was prevented by Jupiter from finishing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The mixture of asteroid types also suggests that much of the belt's material was brought in from elsewhere by migrating planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-194": {"questions":["With fewer than a hundred asteroids known in 1867, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were spotted as the widest intervals between their orbits, falling where periods would be simple fractions of Jupiter's.","Small asteroids nudged by uneven solar heating drift into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where resonances with Jupiter swiftly eject them from the main belt towards the inner Solar System."],"abstract":"The <b>Kirkwood gaps</b> are narrow ranges of orbital size in the main asteroid belt where hardly any asteroids are found, at the distances where an asteroid's orbital period would be a simple fraction of Jupiter's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The main ones are labelled 3:1, 5:2, 7:3 and 2:1, and in the 3:1 gap an asteroid would complete three orbits of the Sun for every one of Jupiter's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These are mean-motion resonances, where repeated alignments with Jupiter at the same points of an orbit change its shape and size over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They show up as deep dips in a histogram of asteroids sorted by the size of their orbits, the semimajor axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asteroids placed in these zones follow chaotic orbits and are removed from the belt, one of the ways in which Jupiter's gravity knocks asteroids out of the main belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The gaps are named after an American astronomer who, with fewer than a hundred asteroids known, showed in 1867 that the widest intervals between their orbits fell where their periods would be commensurable with Jupiter's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He reasoned that a particle going three times round the Sun for each orbit of Jupiter would always meet the planet at the same parts of its path, so that its orbit would grow more and more eccentric and the zone would be left empty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Numerical studies later found that asteroids near the 3:1 resonance can keep nearly circular orbits for as long as a million years before suddenly becoming eccentric enough to cross the orbit of Mars, and that the edge of this chaotic zone matches the edge of the real gap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Today small asteroids, drifting slowly under the uneven heating of sunlight known as the Yarkovsky effect, keep reaching the 3:1 and 5:2 resonances, which swiftly eject them from the main belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many of these become near-Earth objects, and the 3:1 resonance is one of the two most important routes by which they leave the belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Solar System Dynamics, “Asteroid Main-Belt Distribution,” in <i>Diagrams and Charts</i>, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/diagrams/mb_hist.html. [Open access]","Daniel Kirkwood, <i>Meteoric Astronomy: A Treatise on Shooting-Stars, Fire-Balls, and Aerolites</i> (Philadelphia: J. B. Lippincott, 1867), 105–11, https://archive.org/details/meteoricastronom00kirk. [Open access]","Jack Wisdom, “Chaotic Behavior and the Origin of the 3/1 Kirkwood Gap,” <i>Icarus</i> 56, no. 1 (1983): 51–74, https://doi.org/10.1016/0019-1035(83)90127-6, author’s copy at http://groups.csail.mit.edu/mac/users/wisdom/kirkwood.pdf. [Open access]","David A. Minton and Renu Malhotra, “A Record of Planet Migration in the Main Asteroid Belt,” <i>Nature</i> 457, no. 7233 (2009): 1109–11, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature07778, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.4574. [Open access]","F. E. DeMeo and B. Carry, “Solar System Evolution from Compositional Mapping of the Asteroid Belt,” <i>Nature</i> 505, no. 7485 (2014): 629–34, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12908, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.2787. [Open access]","Mikael Granvik et al., “Debiased Orbit and Absolute-Magnitude Distributions for Near-Earth Objects,” <i>Icarus</i> 312 (2018): 181–207, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2018.04.018, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.10265. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Asteroid Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 21 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a simple ratio of periods empty part of the belt?","a":"Because an asteroid whose period is a simple fraction of Jupiter's keeps meeting the planet at the same parts of its path, so its orbit grows more and more eccentric.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The orbital elements change enough, especially the semimajor axis, to open gaps in the distribution of orbital sizes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asteroids placed in these zones follow chaotic orbits and escape from the belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the 3:1 gap hard to explain?","a":"Because the escape comes suddenly after a long quiet spell: test asteroids near the 3:1 resonance can keep low eccentricities for as long as a million years and then jump to values above 0.3, high enough to cross the orbit of Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When this chaotic zone was traced with fast calculations, its outer boundary was found to coincide with the boundary of the real gap, within the errors of the asteroids' orbits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the gaps matter for Earth?","a":"Because the resonances that keep the gaps clear also send asteroids towards the inner Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Small asteroids drift slowly through the belt under the Yarkovsky effect and, on reaching a major resonance such as the 3:1 or the 5:2, are swiftly ejected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The 3:1 resonance complex is one of the two most important routes by which near-Earth objects leave the main belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kirkwood gap"},
    "astro-195": {"questions":["Bright patches of sodium carbonate in the crater Occator suggest that salty water once rose to the surface of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the largest body in the asteroid belt.","When the Dawn spacecraft arrived in March 2015, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the first dwarf planet visited by a spacecraft, and it proved to have a rocky core under a volatile-rich shell."],"abstract":"<b>Ceres</b> is the largest object in the asteroid belt and the only dwarf planet in the inner Solar System, with a radius of 476 kilometres (296 miles) and an average distance from the Sun of 2.8 au.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the first member of the belt to be found, on 1 January 1801, and was at first taken for the planet many astronomers expected between Mars and Jupiter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was called an asteroid for many years, until in 2006 it was reclassified as a dwarf planet because it is so much bigger and so different from its rocky neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It holds a large share of the belt's total mass on its own, about a quarter by one NASA estimate and more than a third by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It takes 4.6 years to orbit the Sun and spins once every 9 hours, one of the shortest days in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> NASA's Dawn spacecraft, launched in 2007, reached Ceres on 6 March 2015, making it the first dwarf planet to be visited by a spacecraft, and found a dark, dry surface dotted with small bright areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dawn's measurements of its gravity and shape showed a partially differentiated body, a rocky core beneath a volatile-rich shell some 70 to 190 kilometres (43 to 118 miles) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The brightest areas, in the crater Occator, are rich in sodium carbonate, thought to be the solid residue of salty water that reached the surface from below and crystallised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The surface also carries ammonia-bearing clays and a lone mountain, Ahuna Mons, about 4 kilometres (2.5 miles) high, which appears to be a dome of ice pushed up from the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dawn's data suggest that Ceres has, or once had, a subsurface ocean of water, which is one reason it is counted among the places worth searching for signs of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “Ceres: Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 22 April 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/ceres/facts/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “13.1 Asteroids,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-1-asteroids. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Asteroid Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/asteroidfact.html. [Open access]","C. T. Russell et al., “Dawn Arrives at Ceres: Exploration of a Small, Volatile-Rich World,” <i>Science</i> 353, no. 6303 (2016): 1008–10, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaf4219. [Paywalled]","R. S. Park et al., “A Partially Differentiated Interior for (1) Ceres Deduced from Its Gravity Field and Shape,” <i>Nature</i> 537, no. 7621 (2016): 515–17, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature18955. [Paywalled]","M. C. De Sanctis et al., “Bright Carbonate Deposits as Evidence of Aqueous Alteration on (1) Ceres,” <i>Nature</i> 536, no. 7614 (2016): 54–57, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature18290. [Paywalled]","NASA Science, “Dawn,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 3 November 2024, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dawn/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Ceres reclassified as a dwarf planet?","a":"Because it is so much bigger than its rocky neighbours, and so different from them, that it was moved out of the asteroids in 2006.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the largest object in the belt, just under 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) across, and holds a large share of the belt's mass on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also round, while the next largest body in the belt, Vesta, is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the bright spots on Ceres so interesting?","a":"Because they appear to come from inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Spectra of the brightest areas, in the crater Occator, show a large amount of sodium carbonate, the most concentrated known deposit of carbonate beyond Earth on scales a kilometre wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The salts are thought to be the solid residue of brines that reached the surface from below, which suggests that liquid may once have existed, or may still exist, at depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do scientists think Ceres may hold a lot of water?","a":"Because its outer shell is too light to be solid rock: Dawn's measurements give it a density of 1,680 to 1,950 kilograms per cubic metre, a mixture of volatiles with denser silicates and salts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> NASA describes a probable mantle of water ice and suggests that Ceres could be as much as 25 per cent water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dawn's data also point to a subsurface ocean, now or in the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ceres (dwarf planet)"},
    "astro-196": {"questions":["Formed within two million years of the Solar System's birth, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was heated by short-lived radioactive elements until it melted and separated into crust, mantle and an iron core.","The brightest asteroid and just visible to the unaided eye, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was orbited by the Dawn spacecraft from July 2011 to September 2012 before it left for Ceres."],"abstract":"<b>Vesta</b> is the second most massive body in the main asteroid belt, holding almost 9 per cent of the mass of all the asteroids, and it orbits about 2.4 au from the Sun in the inner part of the belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is about 500 kilometres (310 miles) across and nearly spherical, close enough to round that it was almost classified as a dwarf planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With a reflectivity of almost 30 per cent it is the brightest asteroid, bright enough to be seen with the unaided eye by someone who knows just where to look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was found at Bremen on 29 March 1807, the fourth asteroid to be discovered, and named after the Roman goddess of the hearth and household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> NASA's Dawn spacecraft, launched in 2007 to visit both Vesta and Ceres, orbited it from 16 July 2011 to 5 September 2012 and then left for the dwarf planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Unlike most asteroids, Vesta separated into crust, mantle and core, because it formed within 1 to 2 million years of the birth of the Solar System, when short-lived radioactive material heated it until it melted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its surface is covered in basalt, a volcanic rock, and Dawn's measurements of its mass, volume and gravity fit an iron core with an average radius of 107 to 113 kilometres (66 to 70 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pieces of that crust reach Earth as the howardite, eucrite and diogenite meteorites, whose lava flows have been dated to 4.4 to 4.5 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were blasted off by giant impacts at the south pole, where the basin Rheasilvia, 500 kilometres (310 miles) wide and 19 kilometres (12 miles) deep, overlies half of an older basin, Veneneia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both basins are thought to be roughly 1 to 2 billion years old, and the debris they threw out also produced the Vesta family of small asteroids, whose basaltic members have inner-belt orbits consistent with an origin there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["NASA Science, “4 Vesta,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 23 April 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/4-vesta/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “13.1 Asteroids,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-1-asteroids. [Open access]","C. T. Russell et al., “Dawn at Vesta: Testing the Protoplanetary Paradigm,” <i>Science</i> 336, no. 6082 (2012): 684–86, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1219381. [Paywalled]","Paul Schenk et al., “The Geologically Recent Giant Impact Basins at Vesta’s South Pole,” <i>Science</i> 336, no. 6082 (2012): 694–97, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1223272. [Paywalled]","David R. Williams, “Asteroid Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/asteroidfact.html. [Open access]","Francesca E. DeMeo et al., “An Extension of the Bus Asteroid Taxonomy into the Near-Infrared,” <i>Icarus</i> 202, no. 1 (2009): 160–80, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2009.02.005, accepted manuscript at https://hal.science/hal-00545286. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Dawn,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 3 November 2024, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dawn/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a body only about 500 kilometres across melt inside?","a":"Because it formed very early, within 1 to 2 million years of the birth of the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Short-lived radioactive material built into bodies of that age heated them until, as on Vesta, they melted, letting dense material sink to a core and lighter material rise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The age of its lava flows, 4.4 to 4.5 billion years, fits a source of heat that was intense and short-lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How do we know that certain meteorites come from Vesta?","a":"Because their spectra match Vesta's own, which first linked the howardite, eucrite and diogenite meteorites to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dawn then found that the minerals across Vesta's surface match the composition of these meteorites, and it mapped a giant south-polar basin deep enough to have thrown them off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The younger basin, Rheasilvia, is 500 kilometres (310 miles) wide and excavated enough crust to produce both the meteorites and the small asteroids of the Vesta family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Vesta called a protoplanet?","a":"Because it is thought to be an intact survivor from the earliest epoch of the Solar System's formation, when planets were being assembled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Like Earth it is differentiated into crust, mantle and core, something most known asteroids are not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dawn's measurements of its mass, volume and gravity fit an iron core with an average radius of 107 to 113 kilometres (66 to 70 miles), confirming that it melted enough inside to separate its iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Vesta (mythology)"},
    "astro-197": {"questions":["First recognised in 1918, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> such as the Karin group, formed when a body about 40 kilometres (25 miles) across broke apart some 5.8 million years ago, record collisions in the belt.","Debris thrown off by the giant impacts at the south pole of Vesta forms one of the best-known <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the inner belt, its small basaltic members matching Vesta's crust."],"abstract":"<b>Asteroid families</b> are groups of asteroids with similar orbits that are thought to be the fragments of a larger parent body broken apart by a collision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jupiter's gravity stirred the orbits in the belt enough to make collisions fast, and over the age of the Solar System many dozen large asteroids have been shattered by impacts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fragments leave the scene at speeds close to the parent's escape speed, far below the 15 to 20 kilometres (9 to 12 miles) per second at which belt asteroids orbit the Sun, so at first their orbits are almost alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pull of the planets then makes the orbits slowly diverge, so families are found by converting each orbit into proper elements, averaged quantities that stay nearly constant over very long times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The members of most families also share similar compositions, as would be expected of pieces of one parent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The first families were recognised in 1918, together with the suggestion that each had come from the break-up of a larger body or from a collision between two asteroids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several dozen are now known, and a review published in 2015 listed 122 notable families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some are very young, and the Karin family formed only about 5.8 million years ago, when a parent about 40 kilometres (25 miles) across broke apart, leaving 541 known members by 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Vesta family is debris from the giant impacts that carved the basins at Vesta's south pole, thought to be roughly 1 to 2 billion years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dawn found that the south-polar basin was deep enough to produce the family's asteroids, and many small basaltic asteroids in the inner belt have orbits consistent with an origin on Vesta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["David Nesvorný, Miroslav Brož, and Valerio Carruba, “Identification and Dynamical Properties of Asteroid Families,” in <i>Asteroids IV</i> (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2015), https://doi.org/10.2458/azu_uapress_9780816532131-ch016, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.01628. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “13.1 Asteroids,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-1-asteroids. [Open access]","NASA Science, “4 Vesta,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 23 April 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/4-vesta/. [Open access]","C. T. Russell et al., “Dawn at Vesta: Testing the Protoplanetary Paradigm,” <i>Science</i> 336, no. 6082 (2012): 684–86, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1219381. [Paywalled]","Paul Schenk et al., “The Geologically Recent Giant Impact Basins at Vesta’s South Pole,” <i>Science</i> 336, no. 6082 (2012): 694–97, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1223272. [Paywalled]","Francesca E. DeMeo et al., “An Extension of the Bus Asteroid Taxonomy into the Near-Infrared,” <i>Icarus</i> 202, no. 1 (2009): 160–80, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2009.02.005, accepted manuscript at https://hal.science/hal-00545286. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the fragments of one collision stay recognisable for so long?","a":"Because the pieces leave at speeds far below the asteroids' orbital speed, so their orbits start out almost identical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pull of the planets makes those orbits diverge over time, but converting them into proper elements, which stay nearly constant, keeps the group visible even billions of years after the break-up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are young families especially useful?","a":"Because collisions and slow changes to their orbits have had little time to alter them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Karin family formed only about 5.8 million years ago, and the age of such a family can be found by running its members' orbits backwards until they converge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That method works only for families younger than about 10 million years, because chaos, encounters with large asteroids and forces other than gravity blur older orbits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How was the Vesta family linked to Vesta?","a":"Because Dawn found a giant basin at Vesta's south pole whose excavation was large enough to produce the Vesta-family asteroids and the howardite, eucrite and diogenite meteorites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The basins there are roughly 1 to 2 billion years old and threw off enough crust to create these small asteroids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Small basaltic asteroids in the inner belt have orbits consistent with an origin on Vesta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Asteroid family"},
    "astro-198": {"questions":["Since the planets remove them within about a hundred million years, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must be resupplied constantly, chiefly by fragments escaping the main belt through resonances with Jupiter.","A NASA survey begun in 1998 set out to find nine in ten of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> larger than a kilometre across, those able to cause damage across the whole globe."],"abstract":"<b>Near-Earth asteroids</b> are asteroids whose orbits bring them within 1.3 au of the Sun at their closest approach, so that their paths come near Earth's orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Together with a smaller number of comets they make up the near-Earth objects, the great majority of which are asteroids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are divided by orbit into Atiras, which stay inside Earth's orbit, Atens and Apollos, which cross it, and Amors, which approach it from outside without crossing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those that pass within 0.05 au of Earth's orbit and are larger than about 140 metres (460 feet) are classed as potentially hazardous asteroids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The JPL Small-Body Database listed more than 43,000 near-Earth asteroids as of November 2026, and the Minor Planet Center's census gave a similar figure, fewer than a thousand of them larger than 1 kilometre (0.6 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Their orbits can be predicted over a few centuries but are unstable over longer spans, because the planets keep tugging on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Within about a hundred million years each either strikes a terrestrial planet or the Sun or is thrown out of the inner Solar System, the two fates being about equally likely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since that is short beside the age of the Solar System, the population must be constantly resupplied, mostly from the main belt, where collisions knock fragments onto Earth-crossing orbits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Small asteroids drift slowly under the uneven heating of sunlight until they reach a strong resonance with Jupiter, which swiftly ejects them, and models find the 3:1 resonance and the ν6 secular resonance to be the most important escape routes, with comets adding a few per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A NASA survey begun in 1998 aimed to find 90 per cent of those larger than 1 kilometre (0.6 miles), the size able to cause global damage, and it reached that goal in 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Center for Near Earth Object Studies, “NEO Groups,” NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/about/neo_groups.html. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “13.2 Asteroids and Planetary Defense,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-2-asteroids-and-planetary-defense. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Asteroid Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 21 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/. [Open access]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Solar System Dynamics, “Small-Body Database Query,” queried 12 November 2026, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_query.html. [Open access]","Mikael Granvik et al., “Debiased Orbit and Absolute-Magnitude Distributions for Near-Earth Objects,” <i>Icarus</i> 312 (2018): 181–207, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2018.04.018, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.10265. [Open access]","IAU Minor Planet Center, “Latest Published Data,” accessed 12 November 2026, https://minorplanetcenter.net/mpc/summary. [Open access]","F. E. DeMeo and B. Carry, “Solar System Evolution from Compositional Mapping of the Asteroid Belt,” <i>Nature</i> 505, no. 7485 (2014): 629–34, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12908, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.2787. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why don't near-Earth asteroids last?","a":"Because their orbits are unstable over long spans as the planets tug on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Within about a hundred million years each will either hit a terrestrial planet or the Sun or be ejected from the inner Solar System, with roughly equal odds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only about a quarter of the present Earth-approaching asteroids are expected to end up colliding with Earth itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was 1 kilometre chosen as the survey's target size?","a":"Because an impact by an object that large could blast so much dust into the atmosphere that sunlight would be dimmed for months, causing crop failures across the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> NASA estimates that an asteroid about 1,000 metres (3,300 feet) across strikes Earth roughly once every 700,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The goal of finding 90 per cent of such asteroids was reached in 2012, when nearly a thousand had been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What makes an asteroid potentially hazardous?","a":"Its orbit and its size: it must come within 0.05 au of Earth's orbit and be bright enough to suggest a body roughly 140 metres (460 feet) across or larger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The label means only that a threatening close approach is possible, not that an impact will happen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> None of the near-Earth asteroids found so far is on a course to strike Earth in the foreseeable future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "astro-199": {"questions":["Orbits tilted by up to about 40 degrees suggest that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sharing Jupiter's path formed far out in the Solar System and were captured when the giant planets migrated.","Launched in October 2021, NASA's Lucy spacecraft is due to visit eight of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that lead and trail Jupiter, beginning with Eurybates in August 2027."],"abstract":"<b>Trojan asteroids</b> are small bodies that share a planet's orbit around the Sun, gathered near two stable points 60 degrees ahead of and behind the planet, the L4 and L5 Lagrange points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was realised in 1772 that small bodies could share a planet's orbit stably near these points, long before any was seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first was found near Jupiter's leading point in early 1906, and the first three were named after heroes of the Trojan War, which gave the whole class its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jupiter's Trojans are by far the largest group, but trojans also accompany Mars and Neptune, and in 2011 an asteroid called 2010 TK7 was shown to lead Earth around its leading point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The JPL Small-Body Database listed more than 16,000 Jupiter Trojans as of November 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Jupiter's two swarms are named on a pattern, Greek heroes for the group that leads the planet and Trojan heroes for the group that follows, and the leading swarm holds noticeably more objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are among the darkest bodies in the Solar System, and their reddish surfaces fall into two distinct spectral groups, one redder than the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were long thought to have formed near Jupiter and been captured as it grew, but their orbits, tilted by up to about 40 degrees, are hard to explain that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A newer view holds that they formed much farther out, in the primordial Kuiper Belt, and were captured into Jupiter's orbit while the giant planets migrated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> NASA's Lucy spacecraft, launched on 16 October 2021, is due to fly past its first Trojan, Eurybates, on 11 August 2027 and to visit eight Trojans in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Joshua P. Emery et al., “The Complex History of Trojan Asteroids,” in <i>Asteroids IV</i> (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2015), https://doi.org/10.2458/azu_uapress_9780816532131-ch011, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.01658. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Asteroid Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 21 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/. [Open access]","Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “13.1 Asteroids,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-1-asteroids. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Lucy,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 8 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/mission/lucy/. [Open access]","Martin Connors, Paul Wiegert, and Christian Veillet, “Earth’s Trojan Asteroid,” <i>Nature</i> 475, no. 7357 (2011): 481–83, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature10233. [Paywalled]","A. Morbidelli et al., “Chaotic Capture of Jupiter’s Trojan Asteroids in the Early Solar System,” <i>Nature</i> 435, no. 7041 (2005): 462–65, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature03540. [Paywalled]","NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Solar System Dynamics, “Small-Body Database Query,” queried 12 November 2026, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_query.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can an asteroid share Jupiter's orbit without hitting it?","a":"Because it gathers near one of two special points in the orbit, 60 degrees ahead of or behind the planet, the L4 and L5 Lagrange points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There the gravitational pull of the Sun and the planet balance the asteroid's tendency to fly out of that orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some trojan populations have stability timescales longer than the age of the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are they called Trojans?","a":"Because the first three found in Jupiter's Lagrange regions, in 1906 and 1907, were later named after heroes of the Trojan War, Achilles, Patroclus and Hektor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name eventually came to be used for any object trapped near the L4 or L5 point of any body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among Jupiter's Trojans the leading swarm takes the names of Greek heroes and the trailing swarm those of Troy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does it matter where the Trojans formed?","a":"Because they may be remnants of the same material that formed the outer planets, time capsules from the birth of the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> If they were captured from distant regions while the giant planets migrated, their orbits preserve a record of that migration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ice has not yet been detected on them directly, though the low density of the binary Patroclus is consistent with ice inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Trojan (celestial body)"},
    "astro-200": {"questions":["Spectra alone make metal hard to identify, so radar was needed to confirm Psyche as metallic, one limit of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that astronomers assign from reflected sunlight.","Stony asteroids dominate the inner belt and carbon-rich ones the outer, yet surveys sorting asteroids into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now find every class in every region of the main belt."],"abstract":"<b>Asteroid spectral types</b> are the classes into which asteroids are sorted by the spectrum of the sunlight they reflect, as a guide to what their surfaces are made of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three broad classes are dark, carbon-rich C-types, stony S-types and metallic M-types, and the C-types are the most common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> C-types reflect only 3 to 4 per cent of the light that falls on them, like coal, and are primitive bodies little changed since the Solar System began, while S-types reflect about 15 per cent and show clearer signs of silicate minerals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rarer types include asteroids with basaltic surfaces like volcanic plains, and the very dark, reddish D and P types that are common among Jupiter's Trojans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A widely used modern scheme defines 24 classes from spectra running from visible light into the near-infrared, building on systems first devised in the 1980s and 1990s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A spectral type is read from colour and absorption features that offer clues to composition, and the first interpretations could be wrong, for reddish asteroids were once taken to be melted igneous bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Grains returned by a spacecraft from the reddish S-type Itokawa showed that it had been heated a little but was relatively primitive, not melted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Metal is hard to identify from spectra, so radar was used to confirm the largest M-type, Psyche, as metallic, since metal reflects radar far better than stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The types are spread unevenly through the belt, with S-types dominating its inner part and C-types its outer part, a pattern once read as the temperature gradient of the young disc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Surveys now show every type in every region of the main belt, so the classes help trace how the movements of the giant planets mixed material across the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Fraknoi, David Morrison, and Sidney C. Wolff, “13.1 Asteroids,” in <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-1-asteroids. [Open access]","NASA Science, “Asteroid Facts,” National Aeronautics and Space Administration, last updated 21 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/. [Open access]","Francesca E. DeMeo et al., “An Extension of the Bus Asteroid Taxonomy into the Near-Infrared,” <i>Icarus</i> 202, no. 1 (2009): 160–80, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.icarus.2009.02.005, accepted manuscript at https://hal.science/hal-00545286. [Open access]","F. E. DeMeo and B. Carry, “Solar System Evolution from Compositional Mapping of the Asteroid Belt,” <i>Nature</i> 505, no. 7485 (2014): 629–34, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12908, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.2787. [Open access]","Sean N. Raymond and Andre Izidoro, “The Empty Primordial Asteroid Belt,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 9 (2017): e1701138, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701138, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.04242. [Open access]","Joshua P. Emery et al., “The Complex History of Trojan Asteroids,” in <i>Asteroids IV</i> (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2015), https://doi.org/10.2458/azu_uapress_9780816532131-ch011, preprint at https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.01658. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are C-type asteroids so dark?","a":"Because their silicate minerals are mixed with dark, organic carbon compounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They reflect only 3 to 4 per cent of the light falling on them, like a lump of coal, and they are among the most ancient objects in the Solar System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can't a spectrum simply tell us what an asteroid is made of?","a":"Because a spectrum shows colour and absorption features, which offer clues to composition, age and alteration but must be interpreted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Reddish S-types were once thought to be melted bodies, until grains from the S-type Itokawa showed it to be relatively primitive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Metal is especially hard to identify from a spectrum, which is why radar was needed to confirm Psyche as metallic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the types change across the belt?","a":"Because different classes formed in different places, and S-types now dominate the inner belt and C-types the outer belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That trend once seemed a record of the young disc's temperature, but surveys now find all types in every region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In one model, C-types were scattered into the belt from the region of the giant planets and S-types from where the terrestrial planets formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Asteroid spectral types"}
  };
  (window.CARD_EXTRA_IN = window.CARD_EXTRA_IN || []).push({ CARD_EXTRA: CARD_EXTRA });
})();
